window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "l10", "title": "Lecture 10: Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l10 Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System n n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. n Session Lecture 10 n n 1. ANS Organization Slide 2-16 n nThe Autonomic Nervous System ANS maintains homeostasis through two opposing divisions Sympathetic Fight or Flight and Parasympathetic Rest and Digest . Both systems utilize a two-neuron chain preganglionic and postganglionic . n n Sympathetic Division n Origin Thoracolumbar T1-L2 lateral horn. n Neuron Lengths Short Preganglionic Long Postganglionic. n Neurotransmitters n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic receptors . n Postganglionic Norepinephrine Adrenergic receptors mostly. n Exceptions Sweat glands ACh/Muscarinic and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly into blood . n n Parasympathetic Division n Origin Craniosacral CN III VII IX X and S2-S4 . n Neuron Lengths Long Preganglionic Short Postganglionic ganglia near target . n Neurotransmitters n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic . n Postganglionic Acetylcholine Muscarinic . n n 2. Clinical Correlates of ANS Dysfunction n n Horner s Syndrome Slide 14 nLesion of the sympathetic pathway to the head/neck. n Triad Ptosis droopy eyelid Miosis constricted pupil Anhidrosis lack of sweating . n n Autonomic Dysreflexia Slide 28 n Cause Spinal Cord Injury above T6 . n Mechanism A noxious stimulus e.g. full bladder triggers a massive sympathetic surge below the lesion. n Consequence Widespread vasoconstriction causes life-threatening Hypertension . n Reflex Baroreceptors detect high BP → Vagus nerve slows heart → Bradycardia . n Result Hypertension + Bradycardia. n n Bladder Dysfunction Slide 31-32 n Spastic UMN Bladder Lesion above Pontine Micturition Center PMC but below cortex or between PMC and cord. Reflex is hyperactive. Detrusor contracts against closed sphincter. n Atonic LMN Bladder Lesion of Sacral cord or Cauda Equina. Reflex arc destroyed. Bladder fills but cannot contract overflow incontinence . n n 3. Enteric Nervous System ENS Slide 33-37 n nThe Brain of the Gut capable of functioning independently. n Plexuses Myenteric Auerbach s - Motility and Submucosal Meissner s - Secretion . n n correlate n Hirschsprung s Disease n Pathophysiology Congenital failure of Neural Crest Cells to migrate to the distal colon. n Presentation Newborns fail to pass meconium. n Result Aganglionic Megacolon . The affected segment lacks ENS plexuses and remains constantly constricted cannot relax causing obstruction and dilation upstream. n Treatment Surgical resection of the aganglionic segment. n n n 4. Cardiovascular Reflexes Slide 27 n n Carotid Sinus Massage Mechanically stimulates baroreceptors → mimics high BP → increases Vagal tone → slows Heart Rate. Used to terminate SVT. n highyield n Contraindication Do not perform Carotid Massage in patients with carotid stenosis or recent stroke as it may dislodge an embolus. n Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System Neuroscience questions A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury develops a pounding headache and profuse sweating. Their blood pressure is 210/110 mmHg and heart rate is 50 bpm. What is the most likely physiological mechanism? Hypovolemic Shock Autonomic Dysreflexia Septic Shock Orthostatic Hypotension Vasovagal Syncope 1 This is classic Autonomic Dysreflexia a noxious stimulus triggers a sympathetic surge below the lesion Hypertension which triggers a baroreceptor reflex to slow the heart Bradycardia . Which neurotransmitter is released by sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating sweat glands? Norepinephrine Epinephrine Acetylcholine Dopamine Serotonin 2 Sweat glands are the exception to the rule. Sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating them release Acetylcholine acting on Muscarinic receptors. A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours. Imaging reveals a dilated colon proximal to a narrowed distal segment. Biopsy of the narrowed segment would likely show Hypertrophy of the muscularis externa Absence of Myenteric and Submucosal ganglia Inflammation of the mucosa Excessive Neural Crest Cell migration Presence of Lewy Bodies 1 Hirschsprung s Disease is characterized by the absence of ganglia aganglionosis in the distal colon due to failed neural crest migration. Horner s Syndrome is characterized by which constellation of signs? Mydriasis Ptosis Anhidrosis Miosis Exophthalmos Hyperhidrosis Miosis Ptosis Anhidrosis Mydriasis Exophthalmos Anhidrosis Ptosis Miosis Hyperhidrosis 2 Horner s Syndrome presents with Miosis constriction Ptosis drooping and Anhidrosis dryness on the ipsilateral side. Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons located? Brainstem Nuclei Sacral Spinal Cord S2-S4 Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Dorsal Root Ganglia Paravertebral Ganglia 2 The sympathetic division is Thoracolumbar meaning preganglionic neurons originate in the lateral horn of the spinal cord from T1 to L2. Location of Sympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Anatomy Location of Parasympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Craniosacral Brainstem + S2-S4 Anatomy Neurotransmitter of ALL Preganglionic Neurons Acetylcholine Nicotinic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Sympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Norepinephrine Adrenergic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Parasympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Acetylcholine Muscarinic Receptor Physiology Triad of Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Pathology Condition causing Aganglionic Megacolon Hirschsprung s Disease Pathology Signs of Autonomic Dysreflexia Hypertension + Bradycardia + Headache/Sweating Pathology Spinal cord level risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia Above T6 Pathology Bladder type in Lower Motor Neuron Sacral lesion Atonic Flaccid Bladder Pathology BandB Neurology > Autonomic Nervous System > ANS Pharmacology FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic vs. Parasympathetic Lengths Sympathetic Short Pre Long Post. n Parasympathetic Long Pre Short Post. Horner s Triad Ptosis Muller s muscle paralysis Miosis Dilator pupillae paralysis Anhidrosis Sudomotor loss . Autonomic Dysreflexia Triad In SCI > T6 Hypertension Sympathetic surge + Bradycardia Baroreceptor reflex + Headache . Hirschsprung s Mechanism Failed migration of Neural Crest Cells . Result Aganglionic segment that cannot relax constricted causing upstream dilation megacolon . Sympathetic Exceptions Most sympathetic postganglionics release NE. Exceptions Sweat glands ACh and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly ."}, {"id": "l11", "title": "Lecture 11: Hypothalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l11 Lecture 11 Hypothalamus n n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. n Session Lecture 11 n n 1. Hypothalamus Overview Slide 6-8 n nThe Hypothalamus is the master control center for Homeostasis regulating the Autonomic Nervous System Endocrine System and Limbic System. nMnemonic for Functions TAN HATS n T hirst and Water Balance n A denohypophysis Control n N eurohypophysis Control n H unger n A utonomic Regulation n T emperature Regulation n S exual urges / S leep n n 2. Major Nuclei and Functions Slide 9-16 n n Paraventricular Nucleus PVN Produces Oxytocin ADH TRH CRH. Projects to autonomic centers. n Supraoptic Nucleus SON Produces mainly ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin. n Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Master Circadian Clock . Regulated by light input from retina. n Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion → Hyperthermia. A/C = Anterior Cooling . n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion → Hypothermia Poikilothermia . n Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory. Input from Hippocampus Fornix . n n correlate n Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome n Cause Thiamine Vitamin B1 deficiency often due to chronic alcoholism. n Pathology Degeneration of the Mammillary Bodies and Medial Dorsal Thalamus. n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Wernicke s Encephalopathy . n Sequelae Korsakoff s Psychosis Anterograde/Retrograde Amnesia + Confabulation if untreated. n n n 3. Feeding Regulation Slide 19-22 n n Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Center . Lesion → Anorexia Lateral makes you Lean . n Ventromedial Nucleus Satiety Center . Lesion → Hyperphagia and Obesity Ventromedial makes you Very Massive . n Hormones n Leptin From adipose tissue. Signals satiety inhibits Lateral stimulates Ventromedial . n Ghrelin From stomach. Signals hunger. n n 4. Water Balance Disorders Slide 12 n n Diabetes Insipidus DI n Neurogenic Lack of ADH secretion Hypothalamic/Posterior Pituitary damage . n Sxs Polyuria Polydipsia Dilute Urine. n SIADH Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH n Cause Ectopic production Small Cell Lung Cancer CNS disorders Drugs. n Sxs Water retention Hyponatremia Cerebral Edema. n n highyield n Mnemonic Feeding Centers n L ateral = L ean Hunger Center; lesion causes starvation . n V entromedial = V ery M assive Satiety Center; lesion causes obesity . n Lecture 11 Hypothalamus Neuroscience questions A chronic alcoholic presents with confusion ataxia and nystagmus. MRI reveals atrophy of the Mammillary Bodies. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Wernicke s Encephalopathy Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 2 This is the classic presentation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy due to Thiamine deficiency which damages the mammillary bodies. A patient develops hyperphagia and significant obesity after a brain tumor resection. Which hypothalamic nucleus was most likely damaged? Lateral Hypothalamus Supraoptic Nucleus Ventromedial Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Anterior Hypothalamus 2 The Ventromedial Nucleus is the Satiety Center. Damage leads to unchecked hunger and obesity Very Massive . Which hypothalamic nucleus is responsible for circadian rhythms? Arcuate Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Paraventricular Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus Mammillary Nucleus 1 The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN regulates sleep-wake cycles and circadian rhythms. Damage to the Anterior Hypothalamus would result in Hypothermia Hyperthermia Diabetes Insipidus Amnesia Insomnia 1 The Anterior Hypothalamus regulates Cooling . Damage prevents cooling resulting in Hyperthermia . In neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus there is a lack of which hormone? Oxytocin Prolactin ADH Vasopressin TSH GH 2 Neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus causes polyuria due to a failure to secrete ADH Vasopressin from the posterior pituitary/hypothalamus. Function of Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Feeding Center Physiology Function of Ventromedial Hypothalamus Satiety Physiology Function of Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic Physiology Function of Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic/Shivering Physiology Function of Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Circadian Rhythms Physiology Function of Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory Papez Circuit Physiology Lesion of Mammillary Bodies causes Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Amnesia/Confabulation Pathology Lesion of Lateral Hypothalamus causes Anorexia Failure to eat Pathology Lesion of Ventromedial Hypothalamus causes Hyperphagia Obesity Pathology Major hormones of Posterior Pituitary ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin Physiology BandB Endermo > Hypothalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Hypothalamus Pathoma CNS Hypothalamic Feeding Centers Lateral Hypothalamus LH Hunger Center. Lesion = Starvation Lateral makes you Lean . n Ventromedial Nucleus VMN Satiety Center. Lesion = Obesity VMN makes you Very Massive . Temperature Regulation Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion = Hyperthermia. n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion = Hypothermia. Wernicke-Korsakoff Anatomy Caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency leading to necrosis of the Mammillary Bodies . Circadian Rhythm Controlled by the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN which receives direct input from the retina. ADH Production ADH is synthesized in the Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei and released from the Posterior Pituitary ."}, {"id": "l12", "title": "Lecture 12: Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l12 Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 12 n n 1. Thalamic Organization Slide 12-18 n nThe Thalamus is the Gateway to the Cortex . Most sensory information except olfaction must synapse here before reaching the cortex. n n Relay Nuclei Specific n VPL Ventral Posterolateral n Input DCML and Spinothalamic Tract Body . n Output Somatosensory Cortex Postcentral Gyrus . n Function Vibration Proprioception Pain Temp from Body . n VPM Ventral Posteromedial n Input Trigeminal Face and Gustatory Solitary . n Output Somatosensory Cortex. n Function Sensation from Face + Taste . n LGN Lateral Geniculate Nucleus n Input Optic Tract. n Output Visual Cortex V1 . n Function Vision L ight . n MGN Medial Geniculate Nucleus n Input Inferior Colliculus. n Output Auditory Cortex. n Function Hearing M usic . n VA/VL Ventral Anterior/Lateral n Input Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum. n Output Motor Cortex. n Function Motor planning and coordination. n Anterior Nucleus n Input Mammillothalamic Tract. n Output Cingulate Gyrus. n Function Memory and Emotion Papez Circuit . n n 2. Thalamocortical States Slide 23-26 n Tonic Mode Linear transmission. High fidelity. Awake state depolarized by ARAS NE ACh 5-HT Histamine . n Burst Mode Oscillatory. Low fidelity. Sleep state hyperpolarized . Blocks sensory input preventing waking. Absence Seizures occur in this mode. n n 3. Internal Capsule Slide 35-39 nWhite matter highway carrying fibers to/from cortex. n Anterior Limb Thalamocortical fibers Anterior/DM . n Genu Corticobulbar Tract Motor to face/brainstem . n Posterior Limb n Corticospinal Tract Motor to body . n Somatosensory Radiations VPL/VPM to Cortex . n Visual/Auditory Radiations Retrolenticular/Sublenticular . n Blood Supply Primarily MCA Lenticulostriate and Anterior Choroidal . Thalamus is PCA . n n 4. Clinical Syndromes Slide 29-32 n n Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Thalamic Pain Syndrome n Cause Stroke PCA damaging VPL/VPM. n Presentation Contralateral hemianesthesia followed by agonizing burning pain Allodynia induced by touch or even emotion. n n Artery of Percheron Infarct n Variant Single artery arising from PCA supplying Bilateral paramedian thalamus + midbrain. n Stroke Bilateral thalamic infarct. n Presentation Coma RAS damage Memory loss Vertical gaze palsy. Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus Neuroscience questions A patient suffers a stroke and develops severe burning pain on the right side of their body that is exacerbated by light touch. Which nucleus was most likely infarcted? Right VPL Left VPL Right VPM Left MGN Left Anterior Nucleus 1 Thalamic Pain Syndrome Dejerine-Roussy is contralateral. A Left VPL infarct affects the Right side of the body. VPL handles body sensation. Visual information from the optic tract synapses in which thalamic nucleus? Medial Geniculate Nucleus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus Ventral Posterolateral Nucleus Pulvinar Ventral Anterior Nucleus 1 LGN = L ight Vision . MGN = Music Hearing . Which limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract? Anterior Limb Genu Posterior Limb Retrolenticular Part Sublenticular Part 2 The Posterior Limb of the Internal Capsule carries the Corticospinal tract Motor to body and Somatosensory radiations. Which thalamic nucleus is part of the Papez circuit involved in memory? Ventral Lateral Anterior Nucleus Pulvinar Lateral Posterior Centromedian 1 The Anterior Nucleus receives input from the Mammillary Bodies and projects to the Cingulate Gyrus forming part of the Papez circuit for memory/emotion. The Burst Mode of thalamocortical firing is associated with Focused attention REM Sleep Slow Wave Sleep Panic attacks Active learning 2 Burst Mode is an oscillatory state that disconnects the cortex from sensory input characteristic of Slow Wave Sleep and Absence Seizures . Input to VPL Nucleus Spinothalamic & DCML Body Sensation Neuroanatomy Input to VPM Nucleus Trigeminal & Solitary Tract Face & Taste Neuroanatomy Input to LGN Optic Tract Vision Neuroanatomy Input to MGN Inferior Colliculus Hearing Neuroanatomy Input to VA/VL Nuclei Basal Ganglia & Cerebellum Motor Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Genu Corticobulbar Tract Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Posterior Limb Corticospinal Tract & Sensory Radiations Neuroanatomy Artery supplying the Thalamus Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy Cause of Thalamic Pain Syndrome Stroke in VPL/VPM Dejerine-Roussy Pathology Thalamic mode during Deep Sleep Burst Mode Physiology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Thalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Thalamus Pathoma CNS VPM vs VPL Mnemonic VPM = M akeup on the Face Face sensation + Taste . n VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . Geniculate Nuclei Mnemonic L GN = L ight Visual . n M GN = M usic Auditory . Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Central Post-Stroke Pain. Thalamic lesion causes initial numbness followed by hypersensitivity and severe burning pain on the contralateral side. Internal Capsule Genu The Genu knee contains the Corticobulbar tract Face motor . A lesion here affects CN motor nuclei e.g. UMN facial droop . Thalamic Blood Supply Principally the Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA . The Artery of Percheron is a rare variant supplying bilateral thalami."}, {"id": "l13", "title": "Lecture 13: Clinical Decision Making", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l13 Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 13 n n 1. Dual Process Theory Slide 4-5 n nClinical reasoning involves two cognitive systems n System 1 Intuitive Fast automatic reflexive pattern recognition. Used by experts for common cases. Prone to Heuristic Biases . n System 2 Hypothetico-Deductive Slow analytical deliberate. Used for complex or unclear cases. Less prone to bias but resource-intensive. n n 2. Cognitive Biases Heuristics Slide 5 26-28 n n Anchoring Locking onto salient features in the patient s initial presentation too early and failing to adjust. n Availability Bias Judging a diagnosis as more likely because it is easily recalled e.g. you just saw 3 cases of Appendicitis so the next belly pain must be Appendicitis . n Confirmation Bias Seeking evidence to support your hypothesis while ignoring evidence that refutes it. n Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process before verifying the diagnosis or considering other possibilities. When the diagnosis is made the thinking stops. n n 3. The Diagnostic Framework n n VINDICATE Mnemonic Slide 15 nA checklist to generate a broad Differential Diagnosis DDx . n V ascular n I nfectious n N eoplastic n D rug / Degenerative n I nflammatory / Idiopathic n C ongenital n A utoimmune / Allergic n T rauma / Toxic n E ndocrine / Metabolic n n Illness Scripts Slide 18 22 nAn organized mental summary of a disease including n Epidemiology/Pathophysiology Who gets it? Why? n Time Course Acute Subacute Chronic? n Salient Symptoms/Signs Key features Prototypical presentation . n Diagnostics/Treatment Labs Imaging Meds. n n 4. Problem Representation Slide 12 35 nA one-sentence summary of the case used to communicate and reason. n Structure Patient Age/Sex/Risk Factors presents with Time Course Chief Complaint characterized by Key Features . n Example A 3yo male with no PMH presents with 2 days of fever and ear pulling found to have a bulging erythematous TM. n n correlate n Case Study Acute Otitis Media AOM n Presentation Fever Ear Pain Otalgia Bulging Tympanic Membrane TM Loss of benchmarks. n Pathogens Strep pneumoniae Haemophilus influenzae Moraxella catarrhalis . n DDx n Serous Otitis Media Fluid effusion but no active infection/bulging. n Otitis Externa Swimmer s ear. Canal inflammation. Pain with tragal motion. flesh-colored canal makes this unlikely. n Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making Neuroscience questions A resident diagnoses a patient with Influenza because everyone coming into the ER today has the flu despite the patient having neck stiffness and a rash. Which cognitive bias is at play? Anchoring Availability Bias Confirmation Bias Premature Closure Sunk Cost Fallacy 1 Availability Bias is judging frequency or probability by how easily examples come to mind e.g. a recent run of flu cases . When generating a differential diagnosis for a 60-year-old male with back pain you use the mnemonic VINDICATE. The N stands for Neurologic Nutritional Neoplastic Nociceptive Neonatal 2 N in VINDICATE stands for Neoplastic Cancer a critical category to consider in older adults with new back pain. Which of the following best describes System 2 thinking? Fast and reflexive Pattern recognition Slow and analytical Prone to emotional influence Automatic association 2 System 2 is the slow deliberate analytical mode of thinking used for complex problems. A 3-year-old presents with ear pain. Exam reveals a bulging erythematous tympanic membrane with loss of bony landmarks. The most likely diagnosis is Otitis Externa Serous Otitis Media Mastoiditis Acute Otitis Media Suppurative Foreign Body 3 Bulging and erythema of the TM are hallmark signs of Acute Suppurative Otitis Media. You suspect a patient has a Pulmonary Embolism. You order a D-dimer which is negative but you dismiss the result as a lab error because you are so sure of your diagnosis. This is an example of Confirmation Bias Anchoring Availability Bias Visceral Bias Gambler s Fallacy 1 Confirmation Bias involves interpreting evidence to support your existing beliefs the PE diagnosis while dismissing contradictory evidence the negative D-dimer . System 1 Thinking Fast Intuitive Automatic Clinical Reasoning System 2 Thinking Slow Analytical Deliberate Clinical Reasoning Availability Bias Diagnosing based on what is readily recalled recent cases Clinical Reasoning Anchoring Bias Sticking to initial impressions despite new data Clinical Reasoning Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process too early Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE V Vascular Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE I first Infectious Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE N Neoplastic Clinical Reasoning Illness Script Components Pathophys Epidemiology Time Course Symptoms Diagnostics Treatment Clinical Reasoning Key sign of Acute Otitis Media Bulging Tympanic Membrane Clinical Reasoning BandB Infectious Disease > HEENT FirstAid Public Health > Epidemiology > Bias Pathoma General Principles System 1 vs System 2 System 1 Fast Intuitive Gut feeling . Prone to bias. n System 2 Slow Analytical Math problem . More accurate. VINDICATE Mnemonic Vascular Infectious Neoplastic Drug/Degenerative Inflammatory/Idiopathic Congenital Autoimmune Trauma/Toxic Endocrine/Metabolic. Confirmation Bias The tendency to look only for evidence that confirms your hunch and ignore evidence that contradicts it. Availability Bias Diagnosing something because you recently saw it or it s top of mind e.g. flu during flu season . Premature Closure The most common fatal error Accepting a diagnosis before it is fully verified and stopping the search for alternatives."}, {"id": "l15", "title": "Lecture 15: General Sensory Systems", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l15 Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 15 n n 1. Sensory Receptors & Fibers Slide 5-13 n n Fiber Types n A-beta A u03b2 Large Myelinated. Fast 30-70 m/s . Carries Touch Vibration Proprioception . n A-delta A u03b4 Small Myelinated. Medium speed. Carries Sharp Pain First Pain Cold . n C fibers Unmyelinated. Slow 0.5-2 m/s . Carries Dull/Burning Pain Second Pain Warmth Itch . n n Mechanoreceptors Touch n Meissner s Corpuscle Fast adapting Small field. Light Touch/Grip . Glabrous skin. n Merkel Disc Slow adapting Small field. Static Pressure/Texture Braille . n Pacinian Corpuscle Fast adapting Large field. Vibration . n Ruffini Ending Slow adapting Large field. Skin Stretch Grip shape . n n 2. Ascending Pathways Slide 26-30 n n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML nCarries Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception from the body. n1. 1st Order DRG. Ascend in Dorsal Columns Gracilis=Lower Cuneatus=Upper . n2. 2nd Order Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus Medulla . Decussate as Internal Arcuate Fibers to form Medial Lemniscus . n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → Primary Somatosensory Cortex S1 . n n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System nCarries Pain Temperature Crude Touch . n1. 1st Order DRG. Synapse in Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . n2. 2nd Order Dorsal Horn. Decussate immediately in Anterior White Commissure. Ascend in anterolateral funiculus. n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → S1 Cortex. n n 3. Trigeminal System Face Slide 31-33 n Touch/Proprioception Trigeminal Ganglion → Principal Sensory Nucleus Pons → Decussate → VPM. n Pain/Temp Trigeminal Ganglion → Descend to Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Medulla → Decussate → VPM. n Proprioception Jaw Mesencephalic Nucleus Midbrain - UNIQUE Cell bodies inside CNS . n n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 38-43 n n correlate n Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome n Pathology Hemisection of the spinal cord e.g. stabbing/trauma . n Presentation n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor Corticospinal and Touch/Vibration/Proprioception DCML below lesion. n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temperature Spinothalamic 1-2 segments below lesion due to Lissanuer s tract ascent before crossing . n n n correlate n Phantom Limb Pain n Pathology Cortical reorganization after amputation. n Mechanism The cortical area for the amputated limb e.g. arm loses input and is invaded by adjacent areas e.g. Face . Touching the face is perceived as touching the missing arm. n Nature Often burning/painful resistant to meds. n n n correlate n Thalamic Lesion Dejerine-Roussy n Lesion VPL/VPM. n Presentation Contralateral Hemianesthesia loss of all sensation . Later develops into severe burning pain Thalamic Pain Syndrome . n n n 5. Pain Modulation Slide 18-20 n Gate Control Theory A u03b2 fibers rubbing activate inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn blocking C fiber pain transmission. n Descending Pathway PAG → RVM → Dorsal Horn Inhibits pain via Serotonin/NE . Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems Neuroscience questions A patient cannot feel temperature or sharp pain in their right leg but vibration sense is intact. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Fasciculus Gracilis Left Spinothalamic Tract Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Medial Lemniscus Right Dorsal Horn 1 Pain/Temp from the Right leg travels to the left side of the spinal cord immediately. A lesion in the Left Spinothalamic Tract causes loss of pain/temp on the Right side. Which receptor is responsible for sensing vibration? Merkel Disc Meissner Corpuscle Pacinian Corpuscle Ruffini Ending Free Nerve Ending 2 Pacinian Corpuscles are rapidly adapting and specialized for sensing Vibration . A hemisection of the spinal cord at T10 on the left side Brown-S u00e9quard would result in Loss of pain/temp on the left leg Loss of vibration/proprioception on the right leg Loss of motor function on the right leg Loss of pain/temp on the right leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Hemisection on the Left causes Contralateral Right loss of Pain/Temp STT crosses immediately and Ipsilateral Left loss of Touch/Motor. The cell bodies of the primary afferent neurons for the face Trigeminal are located in the Dorsal Root Ganglion Trigeminal Ganglion Principal Sensory Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus VPM Nucleus 1 Primary sensory neurons for the face are in the Trigeminal Gasserian Ganglion . Which fibers carry slow burning pain? A-alpha A-beta A-delta B fibers C fibers 4 C fibers are unmyelinated and slow conducting responsible for the delayed dull burning second pain . Pathway for Fine Touch and Proprioception Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Neuroanatomy Pathway for Pain and Temperature Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System Neuroanatomy Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuroanatomy Where does Spinothalamic Tract decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuroanatomy Function of Pacinian Corpuscle Vibration Physiology Function of A-delta fibers Fast Sharp Pain Cold Physiology Function of C fibers Slow Dull Pain Warmth/Itch Physiology Brown-S u00e9quard Contralateral Deficit Pain and Temperature STT Pathology Brown-S u00e9quard Ipsilateral Deficit Motor CS & Vibration/Proprioception DCML Pathology Thalamic Nucleus for Body Sensation VPL Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord > Spinal Cord Tracts FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Cord Pathoma CNS DCML vs STT Decussation DCML crosses in the Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic crosses in the Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure . VPL vs VPM VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . n VPM = M akeup/Face Face sensation . Fiber Speed Rule A-beta Touch > A-delta Fast Pain > C Slow Pain . Myelination and diameter determine speed. Brown-S u00e9quard Deficits Ipsilateral Motor & Touch. Contralateral Pain & Temp. Phantom Limb Mechanism Maladaptive Cortical Reorganization Neuroplasticity where face area invades hand area."}, {"id": "l16", "title": "Lecture 16: Visual System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l16 Lecture 16 Visual System n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 16 n n 1. Eye Functional Anatomy Slide 4-10 n nThe eye focuses light onto the retina to transduce it into neural signals. n Refraction Most focusing power comes from the Cornea air-water interface not the lens. n Accommodation The Lens changes shape to focus near objects. n Near Vision Reflex Ciliary muscle Contracts → Zonules Relax → Lens becomes Rounder . + Pupil constriction + Convergence . n Far Vision Ciliary muscle Relaxes → Zonules Tighten → Lens flattens. n Aqueous Humor Produced by Ciliary Body. Drains via Canal of Schlemm . Blockage = Glaucoma . n n Retina Organization n Fovea Center of Macula. All Cones . Highest acuity. No blood vessels/layers in light path. n Periphery Mostly Rods . High sensitivity low light low acuity. n Blind Spot Optic Disc Where optic nerve exits. No photoreceptors. n n 2. Visual Pathway Slide 26-30 n n1. Retina Light hits Photoreceptors → Bipolar Cells → Ganglion Cells. n2. Optic Nerve CN II Ganglion cell axons. n3. Optic Chiasm Nasal fibers temporal field CROSS . Temporal fibers nasal field stay ipsilateral. n4. Optic Tract Contains ipsilateral Temporal fibers + contralateral Nasal fibers. Projects to LGN . n5. Lateral Geniculate Nucleus LGN Thalamic relay. n Magno M layers Motion/Depth. n Parvo P layers Color/Form. n6. Geniculocalcarine Tract Optic Radiations n Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe Loops around lateral ventricle. Carries Superior Visual Field Pie in the Sky . n Dorsal/Parietal Radiations Carries Inferior Visual Field Pie on the Floor . n7. Primary Visual Cortex V1 Occipital Lobe. Upper bank Cuneus = Inferior Field. Lower bank Lingual = Superior Field. n n 3. Visual Field Lesions Slide 26-29 32 n Monocular Blindness Lesion of Optic Nerve . n Bitemporal Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Chiasm . n Cause Pituitary Adenoma compressing chiasm centrally. n Effect Loss of peripheral vision Tunnel Vision . n Homonymous Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Tract or PCA Stroke . n Effect Loss of same field in both eyes e.g. Left field perception lost in both eyes . n Macular Sparing PCA strokes often spare the fovea supplied by MCA collaterals . n Quadrantanopsia n Meyer s Loop Temporal Pie in the Sky Contralateral Superior Quadrant . n Parietal Radiations Pie on the Floor Contralateral Inferior Quadrant . n n correlate n Papilledema Slide 34 n Pathophysiology Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP transmitted through the subarachnoid space surrounding the Optic Nerve. n Sign Swelling of the Optic Disc visible on fundoscopy. Blurred margins. n Context Brain tumor Meningitis Pseudotumor Cerebri. n n n correlate n Hemispatial Neglect Slide 43-44 n Lesion Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant hemisphere . n Presentation Patient ignores the Left side of the world/body. n Starts eating from only right side of plate. n Shaves only right face. n Draws only right half of clock. n Note This is an Attention deficit not a visual one. n n n mnemonic n Visual Field Defects n Tem poral Loop = Pie in the Sky Temporal lobe lesion → Superior field defect . n Parietal Path = Pie on the Floor Parietal lobe lesion → Inferior field defect . n Lecture 16 Visual System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with tunnel vision bumping into objects on their periphery. MRI reveals a mass compressing the optic chiasm. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Optic Nerve Compression Pituitary Adenoma Temporal Lobe Tumor Parietal Lobe Tumor Occipital Lobe Stroke 1 A Pituitary Adenoma compresses the specialized crossing fibers in the Optic Chiasm causing Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . A patient cannot see the upper left quadrant of their visual field Pie in the Sky . Where is the lesion located? Right Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Right Parietal Lobe Left Parietal Lobe Right Occipital Cortex 0 A Left upper field defect maps to the Right brain Contralateral . In the Sky Superior implies the inferior fibers Meyer s Loop in Temporal Lobe . So Right Temporal Lobe . Accommodation for near vision involves which action of the ciliary muscle? Relaxation Contraction Hyperpolarization Atrophy No change 1 The ciliary muscle Contracts loosening the zonules allowing the lens to spring into a rounder shape for near focus. Papilledema is a clinical sign indicative of Retinal Detachment Glaucoma Increased Intracranial Pressure Cataracts Macular Degeneration 2 Papilledema swollen optic disc is caused by transmitted Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . A patient with a Right Parietal Lobe stroke only eats food on the right side of their plate and ignores the left side. This is called Homonymous Hemianopsia Agnosia Hemispatial Neglect Apraxia Prosopagnosia 2 Hemispatial Neglect is an attention deficit classically due to a non-dominant Right Parietal Lobe lesion. Visual Deficit Optic Chiasm Lesion Bitemporal Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Right Optic Tract Lesion Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Meyer s Loop Temporal Lesion Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky Neurology Visual Deficit Parietal Radiation Lesion Contralateral Inferior Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor Neurology Lesion causing Left Hemispatial Neglect Right Parietal Lobe Neurology Sign of Increased ICP on Eye Exam Papilledema Pathology Function of Ciliary Muscle Contraction Accommodation Near Vision Physiology Area of Retina with highest acuity Fovea Macula Physiology Blood Supply to Visual Cortex Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy What is spared in PCA stroke visual field defect? Macula Macular Sparing Pathology BandB Neurology > The Eye > Visual Fields FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Pituitary Adenoma Deficit Compresses Optic Chiasm → Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . Meyer s Loop Deficit Temporal Lobe lesion → Superior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky . Parietal Radiation Deficit Parietal Lobe lesion → Inferior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor . Macular Sparing Occipital Cortex PCA infarcts often spare the Macula because it has dual blood supply MCA collaterals . Right Parietal Lobe Lesion Causes Left Hemispatial Neglect . The Right parietal lobe attends to both sides while the Left only attends to the Right. Loss of Right = Loss of Left attention."}, {"id": "l17", "title": "Lecture 17: Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l17 Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 17 n n 1. Auditory Anatomy Slide 4-6 10-14 n n Outer Ear Funnels sound. n Middle Ear Ossicles Malleus Incus Stapes amplify sound to transfer it from air tympanic membrane to fluid oval window . n Impedance Matching Necessary because fluid differs from air. Stapes pushes on Oval Window. n Protective Reflex Stapedius CN VII and Tensor Tympani CN V3 contract to dampen loud sounds. n Inner Ear Cochlea n Tonotopy Base = High Frequencies Narrow/Stiff . Apex = Low Frequencies Wide/Floppy . n Ion Gradients Endolymph Scala Media is high in K+ . Perilymph is low in K+. n n 2. Mechanotransduction Organ of Corti Slide 15-18 nThe Organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane. n Inner Hair Cells IHC The primary sensory receptors 95% of afferents . n Outer Hair Cells OHC Cochlear amplifiers. Damage = Tinnitus . n Transduction Vibration bends stereocilia → Tip links open K+ channels → K+ influx depolarization → Ca2+ influx → Glutamate release. n n 3. Auditory Pathway Slide 20-21 n E. C. O. L. I. M. mnemonic. n1. E ighth Nerve Spiral Ganglion . n2. C ochlear Nuclei Medulla . n3. S uperior O live Pons - Sound Localization Time/Intensity diffs . First bilateral processing. n4. L ateral Lemniscus. n5. I nferior Colliculus Midbrain . n6. M edial Geniculate Nucleus M GN Thalamus . n7. Auditory Cortex Temporal Lobe/Heschl s Gyrus . n n 4. Hearing Loss Types Slide 27-29 n Conductive Middle/Outer ear issue Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis . n Rinne Bone > Air. n Weber Localizes to Affected ear. n Sensorineural Inner ear/Nerve issue Presbycusis Noise Drugs . n Rinne Air > Bone Normal pattern but reduced . n Weber Localizes to Unaffected ear. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33-37 n n correlate n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma n Pathology Benign tumor of Schwann cells on CN VIII Vestibulocochlear . n CPA Angle Compression of Cerebellopontine Angle structures. n Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo . Can compress CN VII Facial palsy if large. n Diagnosis MRI. n n n correlate n Usher Syndrome n Genetics Autosomal Recessive. n Pathology Mutation in stereocilia tip-link proteins e.g. Cadherin 23 . n Presentation Congenital Deafness + Retinitis Pigmentosa Progressive Blindness . Major cause of deaf-blindness. n n n correlate n EAST Syndrome n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy. n Pathology Mutation in KCNJ10 Kir4.1 K+ channel . n Mechanism Failure to maintain High K+ in Endolymph prevents hair cell depolarization. n n n correlate n Presbycusis n Definition Age-related sensorineural hearing loss. n Pattern Loss of High Frequencies first Base of cochlea wears out . n Mechanism Hair cell loss. n Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System Neuroscience questions A 65-year-old male complains of difficulty hearing women s voices and birds chirping high frequencies in noisy environments. Otoscopy is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Otosclerosis Presbycusis Meniere s Disease Cholesteatoma Otitis Media 1 Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss affecting High Frequencies first due to hair cell loss at the cochlear base. A patient presents with unilateral hearing loss tinnitus and facial weakness. MRI reveals a mass at the Cerebellopontine Angle. What is the likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Glioblastoma Multiforme Vestibular Schwannoma Acoustic Neuroma Meningioma Craniopharyngioma 2 Vestibular Schwannoma arises on CN VIII causing hearing loss/tinnitus and can compress the adjacent CN VII Facial nerve at the CPA. In the auditory pathway where does sound localization computing time/intensity differences first occur? Cochlear Nucleus Superior Olivary Complex Inferior Colliculus Medial Geniculate Nucleus Auditory Cortex 1 The Superior Olivary Complex is the first site receiving Bilateral input allowing it to compare timing and intensity data for localization. EAST Syndrome is caused by a mutation affecting the transport of which ion in the inner ear? Sodium Calcium Chloride Potassium Magnesium 3 EAST Syndrome is a K+ channelopathy Kir4.1 preventing the maintenance of high Potassium in the endolymph. During a Weber test the sound lateralizes to the patient s Left ear. Bone conduction is greater than Air conduction in the Left ear. This suggests Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber lateralizing to the affected ear + Rinne showing Bone > Air is the classic pattern for Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear Left . Auditory Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM Eighth nerve Cochlear nuc Olive Lat Lemniscus Inf Colliculus MGN Neuroanatomy Frequency map of Cochlea Base = High. Apex = Low. Physiology Ion concentrated in Endolymph Potassium K+ Physiology Function of Superior Olivary Complex Sound Localization Neuroanatomy Muscle innervated by CN VII in ear Stapedius Anatomy Muscle innervated by CN V3 in ear Tensor Tympani Anatomy Usher Syndrome Presentation Deafness + Blindness Retinitis Pigmentosa Pathology Acoustic Neuroma Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo Pathology Weber Test Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the Bad ear Clinical Skills Rinne Test Sensorineural Loss Air > Bone Normal pattern Clinical Skills BandB Neurology > The Ear > Hearing FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Auditory Pathoma Head & Neck Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM E ighth Nerve → C ochlear Nucleus → O live Superior → L ateral Lemniscus → I nferior Colliculus → M GN. Cochlear Tonotopy Base = High Frequency Stiff/Narrow . n Apex = Low Frequency Floppy/Wide . Rinne & Weber Rules Conductive Weber to Bad Ear. Rinne Bone > Air. n Sensorineural Weber to Good Ear. Rinne Air > Bone. Acoustic Reflex Mediated by CN VII Stapedius and CN V3 Tensor Tympani to dampen loud sounds. EAST Syndrome Mechanism K+ Channel mutation Kir4.1 . No K+ gradient in Endolymph = No Hearing."}, {"id": "l18", "title": "Lecture 18: Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l18 Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 18 n n 1. Peripheral Anatomy Slide 4-15 nThe Vestibular Labyrinth detects head motion via hair cells mechanotransduction . n Otolith Organs Utricle & Saccule n Function Detect Linear Acceleration e.g. gravity elevator car acceleration . n Mechanism Hair cells embedded in Macula covered by Otoconia crystals . Gravity pulls crystals → bends hairs. n Mnemonic U tricle = U pright Horizontal plane . S accule = S ideways Vertical plane . n Semicircular Canals Anterior Posterior Horizontal n Function Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration head turning . n Mechanism Crista Ampullaris covered by Cupula gelatin . n Fluid Dynamics When head turns Left Endolymph lags moves Right → bends Cupula. n n 2. Vestibular Pathways & Reflexes Slide 16-20 n Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR Stabilizes gaze during head movement. n Path Head Left → Left SCC active → Vestibular Nuclei → Right CN VI Abd & Left CN III MR → Eyes move Right . n Vestibulo-Spinal Tracts n Lateral Extensor muscles Anti-gravity/Balance . n Medial Neck muscles Head stabilization . n n 3. Peripheral Vertigo Disorders Slide 26-30 n n correlate n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV n Mechanism Otoconia crystals dislodge from Utricle and float into a Semicircular Canal usually Posterior . n Presentation Brief <1 min episodes of severe vertigo triggered by Head Position changes rolling over in bed . n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike Maneuver elicits nystagmus. n Treatment Epley Maneuver repositions crystals . n n n correlate n Meniere s Disease n Mechanism Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid pressure . n Presentation Triad of Episodic Vertigo mins-hours + Tinnitus + Sensorineural Hearing Loss . n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics. n n n correlate n Labyrinthitis n Mechanism Inflammation/Infection of the inner ear. n Presentation Continuous severe vertigo + Hearing Loss + Nausea. Often follows a viral infection. n n n 4. Central Vertigo Disorders Slide 31-34 n n correlate n Wallenberg Syndrome Lateral Medullary Syndrome n Pathology Infarct of PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . n Signs n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus. n Nucleus Ambiguus dysphagia hoarseness. n Spinothalamic Tract Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp Body . n Spinal Trigeminal Ipsilateral loss of Pain/Temp Face . n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis . n n n mnemonic n Peripheral vs Central Vertigo n Peripheral Severe vertigo Hearing Loss common Fatigable nystagmus. BPPV Meniere s . n Central Mild vertigo No Hearing Loss usually Non-fatigable nystagmus CNS signs Ataxia Dysphagia . Stroke MS . n Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with episodes of room-spinning vertigo lasting 30 seconds triggered only when rolling over in bed. Hearing is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Wallenberg Syndrome 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min position-dependent vertigo with normal hearing caused by dislodged otoconia. Which structure detects linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity? Crista Ampullaris Semicircular Canals Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Cochlea Organ of Corti 2 The Otolith Organs Utricle and Saccule contain the macula and otoconia specifically designing them to detect Linear Acceleration and gravity. A patient presents with vertigo dysphagia hoarseness ipsilateral facial numbness and contralateral body numbness. Examination reveals an ipsilateral Horner s syndrome. Which artery is occluded? Anterior Spinal Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery PICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery AICA Middle Cerebral Artery MCA 2 This is Wallenberg Lateral Medullary Syndrome caused by occlusion of the PICA . The constellation of Vertigo Vestibular Dysphagia Ambiguus and Crossed Sensory loss is classic. What is the primary mechanism of Meniere s Disease? Otoconia dislodgement Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid Demyelination Bacterial Infection Tumor Compression 1 Meniere s Disease is caused by Endolymphatic Hydrops swelling/excess pressure of the endolymphatic compartment . The Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR allows for Hearing high frequencies Maintaining upright posture Stabilizing gaze during head movement Localizing sound in space Detecting gravity 2 The VOR generates eye movements equal and opposite to head movements to stabilize gaze on a fixed target. Function of Semicircular Canals Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration Physiology Function of Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Detect Linear Acceleration & Gravity Physiology Diagnosis Brief positional vertigo no hearing loss BPPV Pathology Diagnosis Vertigo + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease Pathology Maneuver to treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Clinical Skills Artery involved in Lateral Medullary Syndrome PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Pathology Key signs of Wallenberg Syndrome Dysphagia Vertigo Horner s Crossed Pain/Temp loss Pathology Where are Otoconia normally located? Utricle and Saccule Macula Anatomy Symptoms of Acoustic Neuroma Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo CPA angle Pathology Fluid with high Potassium in Inner Ear Endolymph Physiology BandB Neurology > The Ear > Vestibular System FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Otoliths vs Canals Otoliths Utricle/Saccule = Linear/Gravity. n Canals SCC = Angular/Rotation. BPPV Pathophysiology Dislodged Otoconia Otoliths floating in the Semicircular Canals. Meniere s Triad 1. Vertigo n2. Tinnitus n3. Hearing Loss Fluctuating . Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . Causes Lateral Medullary Syndrome. Dix-Hallpike Diagnostic maneuver for BPPV . Induces nystagmus if positive."}, {"id": "l19", "title": "Lecture 19: Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l19 Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 19 n n 1. Cholinergic Receptors Slide 9-11 n Muscarinic GPCRs Located on effector organs Heart Smooth Muscle Glands . n M1 M3 M5 Gq Increase Ca2+ Contraction/Secretion . n M2 M4 Gi Decrease cAMP Inhibition/Bradycardia . n Nicotinic Ion Channels Located at Ganglia Nn and Neuromuscular Junction Nm . n Ligand-gated Na+/K+ channels. n n 2. Muscarinic Agonists Slide 30-33 n Direct-Acting means they bind directly to the receptor unlike AChE inhibitors . n n Choline Esters Quaternary Amines n Poorly absorbed do not cross BBB. n Acetylcholine ACh Rapidly hydrolyzed. Used only for Ophthalmic Surgery Miosis . n Methacholine n use Bronchoprovocation Test to diagnose Asthma. Induces bronchospasm in hyperreactive airways. n Bethanechol n Use Urinary Retention Post-op Post-partum Neurogenic bladder . n Mnemonic Bethany call chol me if you want to pee. n Carbachol Glaucoma rarely used . Mixed M and N activity. n n Alkaloids Tertiary Amines n Well absorbed Cross BBB. n Pilocarpine n Use Glaucoma Opens meshwork via miosis Xerostomia Sjogren s Radiation . n Mnemonic Pile on the sweat and tears. n Cevimeline Synthetic M3 agonist. Used for Sjogren s Syndrome Dry mouth . n n 3. Nicotinic Agonists Slide 36-39 n Nicotine n Action Biphasic. Low dose = Stimulation. High dose = Depolarizing Blockade Paralysis . n Use Smoking Cessation NRT . n Varenicline Chantix n Action Partial Agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ neuronal receptors. n Benefit Reduces cravings agonist effect AND blocks nicotine from bindings antagonist effect reducing reward. n n 4. Adverse Effects & Contraindications Slide 34 n Muscarinic Toxicity SLUDGE n S alivation L acrimation U rination D efecation G I Upset E mesis. n DANGER Bradycardia and Bronchoconstriction . n Contraindications n Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction . n Peptic Ulcer Disease Increased Acid . n GI/Urinary Obstruction Risk of rupture . n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33 n n correlate n Sj u00f6gren s Syndrome n Pathology Autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands Salivary Lacrimal . n Presentation Xerostomia Dry mouth Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca Dry eyes . n Treatment Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Muscarinic Agonists to stimulate secretion. n n n correlate n Asthma Diagnosis n Drug Methacholine . n Test Inhaled methacholine challenge. n Result A patient with asthma will bronchoconstrict at a much lower dose than a healthy person. n Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old male with post-operative urinary retention needs medication to help void. Examination confirms no obstruction. Which drug is most appropriate? Methacholine Atropine Bethanechol Pilocarpine Nicotine 2 Bethanechol is a direct muscarinic agonist with selectivity for the bladder/GI used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention. A patient being treated for Sjogren s syndrome complains of excessive sweating and diarrhea. This is likely due to which medication? Pilocarpine Atropine Scopolamine Ipratropium Varenicline 0 Pilocarpine is used to increase salivation in Sjogren s but is non-selective causing systemic muscarinic side effects like sweating diaphoresis and diarrhea. Which drug acts as a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors to aid in smoking cessation? Bupropion Varenicline Nicotine Patch Methacholine Carbachol 1 Varenicline is a partial agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ nicotinic receptors reducing cravings and withdrawal symptoms. Methacholine is primarily used clinically for Treating Glaucoma Diagnosing Asthma Treating Urinary Retention Smoking Cessation Treating Alzheimer s 1 Methacholine is used in the bronchoprovocation challenge test to diagnose asthma . Direct-acting cholinomimetics should be avoided in patients with which condition? Glaucoma Xerostomia Peptic Ulcer Disease Constipation Hypertension 2 Muscarinic agonists increase gastric acid secretion which can exacerbate Peptic Ulcer Disease . Drug for Post-op Urinary Retention Bethanechol Pharmacology Drug for Diagnosis of Asthma Methacholine Pharmacology Drug for Sjogren s Syndrome Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Pharmacology Mechanism of Varenicline Partial Agonist at Nicotinic Receptors Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists Lung Asthma/COPD Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists GI Peptic Ulcer Disease or Obstruction Pharmacology SLUDGE Mnemonic Salivation Lacrimation Urination Defecation GI upset Emesis Toxicology Pilocarpine Class Muscarinic Agonist Tertiary Amine Pharmacology Bethanechol Class Muscarinic Agonist Quaternary Amine - No CNS Pharmacology Receptors on Sweat Glands Muscarinic Sympathetic Cholinergic Physiology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Cholinomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Bethanechol Use Non-obstructive Urinary Retention . Bethany call me to pee . Pilocarpine Use Open Angle Glaucoma and Sjogren s Syndrome Sweat/Saliva . Methacholine Challenge Used to diagnose Asthma via induced bronchoconstriction. Varenicline Mechanism Partial Agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ Nicotinic receptors. Reduces withdrawal + Blocks reward. Cholinomimetic Contraindications Asthma causes bronchospasm Peptic Ulcers causes acid Obstruction causes rupture ."}, {"id": "l1a", "title": "Lecture #1a: Basics of Diagnosis: Innominates and Pubic Bone", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1a Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone n n Lecturer To Shan Li D.O. n Session Lecture 1a n n Introduction and Objectives Slide 2 nThis lecture introduces the structural examination of the pelvis focusing on the diagnosis of somatic dysfunctions of the innominates and pubic bone. n Key Goals n Understand pelvic anatomy and biomechanics. n Perform and interpret Standing Flexion Test StFT ASIS Compression Test and Seated Flexion Test SeFT . n Differentiate between Iliosacral Pelvic and Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunctions. n n--- n n Anatomy of the Pelvis Slide 5-6 n Bones n Innominate Composed of three parts that fuse at the acetabulum n Ilium n Ischium n Pubis n Sacrum Articulates with the ilia. n n Joints n Sacroiliac Joint SIJ n L-shaped or C-shaped. n Allows for mobility though limited Slide 6 . n Mobility decreases with age. n Pubic Symphysis Fibrocartilaginous joint. n n Ligaments Slide 7 15 n Ligaments e.g. sacrotuberous sacrospinous provide stability and create foramina for neurovascular structures. n Clinical Relevance They guide motion and can become tender/restrictive in somatic dysfunction. n n--- n n Biomechanics and Function n n Gait and Motion Slide 17 n Physiologic motion of the sacrum and ilia occurs during walking. n Somatic Dysfunction Affects gait efficiency and increases energy expenditure. n n correlate n Pregnancy-Related Changes Slide 18 n Pathophysiology Horizontalization of sacrum increased laxity due to Relaxin . n Presentation Anterior pelvic tilt increased lumbar lordosis potential for pain low back pelvic . n Lecturer s Point Center of gravity shifts anteriorly... Sacroiliac joint and pubic symphysis become more lax. n n n Clinical Implications Slide 20 n Pelvic dysfunction often presents as n Low back pain hip/groin pain. n Sciatic neuralgia piriformis syndrome . n Visceral issues GI GU via autonomic relationships. n n--- n n Structural Examination Screening Tests n n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT Slide 23-28 n Purpose Diagnoses Iliosacral Dysfunction Ilio-sacral = Ilium moving on Sacrum = Pelvic SD . n Execution n 1. Patient stands feet 6-8 inches apart. n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally eyes at level . n 3. Patient bends forward slowly. n Positive Result One PSIS moves First and Farthest superiorly. n Interpretation The side that moves superiorly is the Dysfunctional Side restricted side . The ilium is locked to the sacrum on that side and moves with it. n n pearl n StFT Mnemonic Standing = S ide of I lium Iliosacral . n n n 2. Seated Flexion Test SeFT Slide 32-35 n Purpose Diagnoses Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacro-iliac = Sacrum moving on Ilium = Sacral SD . n Execution n 1. Patient seated feet flat stabilizing the ilia . n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally. n 3. Patient bends forward. n Positive Result One PSIS moves First and Farthest . n Interpretation Indicates the side of sacral dysfunction. n Note The standing vs seated distinction isolates the ilia standing = mobile vs sacroiliac mechanics seated = ilia locked by tuberosities . n n highyield n StFT vs SeFT Summary Slide 36 n StFT + Iliosacral Dysfunction → Look for Innominate/Pubic shears. n Axis Inferior Transverse Axis. n SeFT + Sacroiliac Dysfunction → Look for Sacral Torsions/Shears. n Axis Middle Transverse Axis. n n n 3. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock Slide 30-31 n Purpose Lateralization Determines which side is dysfunctional . n Helps confirm StFT/SeFT findings or distinguish when tests are equivocal. n Execution n 1. Patient supine. n 2. Physician applies posterior-medial pressure on ASIS towards the SI joint. n 3. Assess for spring or resistance. n Positive Result The side with Restriction less spring board-like feel is the positive side. n n--- n n Static Landmarks Examination Slide 40-42 nAfter lateralizing finding the side check landmarks to classify the dysfunction e.g. Anterior/Posterior Rotation Upslip/Downslip . n n Anterior Landmarks n ASIS Heights Compare superior/inferior. n Pubic Tubercles Check for superior/inferior shears. n Medial Malleoli Assess functional leg length. n n Posterior Landmarks n PSIS Heights Compare. n Iliac Crest Heights Compare. n Ischial Tuberosities Compare heights. n Sacral Sulci Depth. n n pearl n Diagnosis Flow n1. Screen StFT Pelvic vs SeFT Sacral . n2. Lateralize ASIS Compression confirms side . n3. Diagnose Check landmarks ASIS PSIS etc. on the dysfunctional side relative to the other. n n n--- n n Review Questions Slide 44-52 n nSee the Questions tab for interactive board-style practice. Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone lecturer To Shan Li D.O. OMM session Lecture 1a prev next l1b pdf pdfs/Lecture 1a. T. Li D.O. PPT .pdf Basics of Pelvic Diagnosis n Anatomy n Innominate Bones Ilium Ischium Pubis n Joints Sacroiliac SIJ Pubic Symphysis n Ligaments Sacrotuberous Sacrospinous Stability n Biomechanics n Motion Occurs during gait n Pregnancy Relaxin → Increased laxity → Anterior Tilt n Screening Tests n Standing Flexion StFT n Diagnoses Iliosacral Innominate Dysfunction n Axis Inferior Transverse n Positive restricted side moves Superior n Seated Flexion SeFT n Diagnoses Sacroiliac Sacral Dysfunction n Axis Middle Transverse n Mechanism Ischial tuberosities lock ilia n ASIS Compression n Purpose Lateralization confirms side n Positive Resistance/Rigidity n Landmarks Static Exam n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci questions 1. A 32-year-old female presents with right-sided low back pain. Structural examination reveals a positive Standing Flexion Test on the right and a negative Seated Flexion Test. The ASIS Compression Test is positive on the right. What is the most likely category of somatic dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral SD Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate SD Lumbar Somatic Dysfunction Psoas Syndrome Pubic Shear 1 A positive Standing Flexion Test StFT indicates Iliosacral Innominate dysfunction. The Seated Flexion Test is negative ruling out primary Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunction. The ASIS compression confirms the right side is involved. Therefore the diagnosis lies within the iliosacral/innominate category B . 2. While performing the Standing Flexion Test the physician notes the right PSIS moves more superiorly than the left. Which biomechanical principle explains this finding? The right iliosacral joint is hypermobile The right iliosacral joint is restricted hypomobile The left sacrum is torsioned The right hamstrings are tight The left quadratus lumborum is tight 1 In the flexion tests the PSIS on the dysfunctional side moves first and farthest superiorly. This is because the joint is restricted hypomobile and locks early carrying the ilium up with the sacrum as the patient bends forward. The normal side maintains motion longer and doesn t rise as high initially. 3. A pregnant patient in her third trimester presents with anterior pelvic tilt and increased lumbar lordosis. Which hormone is primarily responsible for the increased laxity of her sacroiliac joints and pubic symphysis? Estrogen Progesterone Relaxin Oxytocin Prolactin 2 Relaxin is the hormone secreted during pregnancy that increases ligamentous laxity particularly in the pelvis SI joints pubic symphysis to accommodate childbirth. This laxity contributes to the structural changes described Slide 18 . 4. The Seated Flexion Test is designed to isolate the motion of the sacrum by eliminating the influence of which structures? The lumbar spine The lower extremities/Ilia The thoracic cage The cervical spine The abdominal muscles 1 By sitting the weight is borne by the ischial tuberosities. This effectively locks the ilia and minimizes the influence of the lower extremities and by extension the iliosacral mechanics on the ilium side allowing the physician to assess sacroiliac sacral motion more specifically Slide 23 . 5. Which axis of motion is primarily associated with Iliosacral Pelvic motion as tested by the Standing Flexion Test? Superior Transverse Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Oblique Axis Vertical Axis 2 Iliosacral motion ilium moving on sacrum occurs primarily around the Inferior Transverse Axis Slide 36 . Sacroiliac motion sacrum moving on ilium occurs around the Middle Transverse Axis . Standing Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate/Pelvic Concept Seated Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral Concept What does a positive StFT indicate biomechanically? The PSIS on the positive side moves first and farthest because the Ilio-sacral joint is restricted hypomobile . Concept What is the purpose of the ASIS Compression Test? Lateralization identifying which side involved . Clinical Which hormone causes pelvic ligament laxity in pregnancy? Relaxin Glossary Axis of motion for Iliosacral Dysfunction? Inferior Transverse Axis Concept Axis of motion for Sacroiliac Dysfunction? Middle Transverse Axis Concept Anatomy What 3 bones fuse to form the Innominate? Ilium Ischium Pubis Glossary In StFT if Right PSIS moves higher which side is dysfunctional? Right side Clinical Normal result for ASIS Compression Test? Springy/Resilient feel Restrictions feel rigid/board-like . Clinical"}, {"id": "l1b", "title": "Lecture #1b: Diagnosis of the Pelvis (Lab Prep)", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1b Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep n n Lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. n Session Lab 15 Prep n n Introduction nThis lecture prepares students for the Pelvis Lab 15 . It reinforces the diagnostic concepts from Lecture 1a and introduces specific Innominate and Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions. n Goal Efficiently examine name and record somatic dysfunctions of the pelvis. n n--- n n Review of Diagnostic Tests Slide 4-6 8-13 n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT n Significance Lateralizes Innominate Iliosacral dysfunction. n Positive PSIS moves first and farthest superiorly. n Rule Detailed diagnosis is named for the side of the positive StFT . n n 2. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock n Significance Lateralizes dysfunction; useful screening but less specific can be Iliosacral or Sacroiliac . n Positive Resistance to posterior-medial compression Hard end-feel . n n 3. Static Landmarks n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli. n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci. n n--- n n Innominate Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 26-36 n n Innominate Rotations nMotion of the innominate on the sacrum around the Inferior Transverse Axis . n n Anterior Rotation Slide 28-29 n Mechanism Innominate rotates anteriorly. n Landmarks on dysfunctional side n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Superior n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg n Symptoms Hamstring tightness Sciatica Piriformis . n n Posterior Rotation Slide 30-31 n Mechanism Innominate rotates posteriorly. n Landmarks on dysfunctional side n ASIS Superior n PSIS Inferior n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg n Symptoms Inguinal/Groin pain Rectus Femoris Medial Knee pain Sartorius . n n Innominate Shears nTranslatory motion of the entire innominate usually traumatic . n n Superior Shear Upslip Slide 33-34 n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts superiorly e.g. landing on one leg . n Landmarks n ASIS Superior n PSIS Superior n Pubic Tubercle Superior n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg n Motion Laxity in superior translation. n n Inferior Shear Downslip Slide 35-36 n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts inferiorly e.g. getting leg caught in stirrup . n Landmarks n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Inferior n Pubic Tubercle Inferior n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg n n pearl n Shear Tip In a shear ALL landmarks move in the same direction e.g. Superior Shear = ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High . In rotations ASIS and PSIS move in opposites . n n n Innominate Flares Slide 37 n Outflare ASIS further from midline associated with Posterior Rotation . n Inflare ASIS closer to midline associated with Anterior Rotation . n n--- n n Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 38-42 n n Pubic Shears Slide 38 n Superior Shear Pubic tubercle high on dysfunctional side. n Inferior Shear Pubic tubercle low on dysfunctional side. n Clinical Trauma childbirth. n n Pubic Symphysis Dysfunctions nDysfunction at the joint itself without asymmetry of height. n n correlate n Pubic Compression Adducted Pubis Slide 39-40 n Pathophysiology Pubic bones forced together Trauma . n Presentation n Bilateral tenderness. n Bulging symphyseal cartilage. n Restricted Pelvic Ring motion Bilaterally restricted ASIS Compression . n Urinary symptoms frequency/urgency . n Diagnosis No height asymmetry but restricted motion and tenderness. n n n correlate n Pubic Gapping Abducted Pubis Slide 41-42 n Pathophysiology Pubic bones pulled apart Childbirth Fractures . n Presentation n Deep Sulcus at symphysis. n Bilateral tenderness. n Urinary symptoms. n Often very painful. n n n--- n n Case Study Slide 45 n Patient 20F Equestrian fell from horse foot caught in stirrup. n Findings Right Side n StFT + Right n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Inferior n Pubic Tubercle Inferior n Medial Malleolus Inferior n Diagnosis Right Inferior Innominate Shear . n Reasoning All landmarks are inferior on the right consistent with the mechanism of injury traction on the leg . n n Lab Prep Pearls n Order of Exam n 1. Screen StFT/SeFT . n 2. Lateralize ASIS Compression . n 3. Reseat the Pelvis! Bridge . n 4. Static Landmarks Anterior then Posterior . n 5. Diagnose. Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. OMM session Lab Prep Lecture prev l1a next l2 pdf pdfs/Lecture 1b. M. Terzella D.O. PPT .pdf Pelvic Somatic Dysfunctions n Innominate Rotations n Anterior Rotation n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Superior n Leg Long n Association Inflare Hamstring tightness n Posterior Rotation n ASIS Superior n PSIS Inferior n Leg Short n Association Outflare Groin pain Rectus n Innominate Shears n Superior Shear Upslip n All Landmarks Superior ASIS PSIS Pubes n Cause Vertical impact landing on leg n Inferior Shear Downslip n All Landmarks Inferior n Cause Traction stuck in stirrup n Pubic Dysfunctions n Shears Sup/Inf asymmetry named for StFT side n Compression Adduction n Sign Bulging rigidity n Cause Trauma n Gapping Abduction n Sign Deep Sulcus n Cause Childbirth questions 1. A patient presents with right-sided pelvic pain. Examination reveals Right ASIS is Superior Right PSIS is Inferior and Right Medial Malleolus is Superior. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Anterior Innominate Rotation Right Posterior Innominate Rotation Right Superior Innominate Shear Left Anterior Innominate Rotation Left Superior Innominate Shear 1 In a Posterior Rotation the ASIS moves up Superior and the PSIS moves down Inferior . This also pulls the leg up causing a short leg Superior Medial Malleolus . This matches the findings exactly. 2. Which of the following findings is most consistent with a Superior Innominate Shear Upslip ? ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle High ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubic Tubercle Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle Low 2 In a Shear Upslip the entire bone translates superiorly. Therefore ALL landmarks on that bone ASIS PSIS Pubic Tubercle Ischial Tuberosity will be found Superior compared to the healthy side. 3. A postpartum female presents with pubic pain and urinary urgency. Examination reveals bilateral pubic tenderness and a deep palpable sulcus at the pubic symphysis. ASIS and PSIS heights are symmetric. What is the diagnosis? Superior Pubic Shear Inferior Pubic Shear Pubic Compression Adducted Pubic Gapping Abducted Bilateral Innominate Outflare 3 The key finding is the deep sulcus at the symphysis and history of childbirth which suggests Pubic Gapping Abduction . Compression would present with a bulging cartilage. Shears would present with height asymmetry. 4. In an Anterior Innominate Rotation which muscle group is most likely to be tight/hypertonic? Hamstrings Quadriceps/Rectus Femoris Gluteus Maximus Biceps Femoris External Obliques 1 This is a bit distinct check Slide 29 vs 31 . Actually Hamstrings are tight in Anterior rotation Lecturer slide 28 notes Subjective complaints Hamstring tightness for Anterior . Wait biomechanically Rectus pulls it Anterior. Let s check slide 29 Anteriorly Rotated... Hamstring tightness . Why? Because the ischial tuberosity moves superiorly/posteriorly putting tension on the hamstrings. Conversely Quadriceps Rectus dysfunction is associated with Posterior rotation Slide 31 because it attaches to AIIS/ASIS and pulls it down. 5. What is the critical first step before performing the static landmark examination? Treat the lumbar spine Reseat the pelvis Bridging Measure True leg length Perform the Seated Flexion Test Check cranial rhythmic impulse 1 Reseating the pelvis asking patient to bend knees lift hips and set them down is crucial to ensure the patient is straight and soft tissues are relaxed preventing False positives in landmark asymmetry Slide 15 . Landmarks for Anterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High Leg Long Clinical Landmarks for Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS High PSIS Low Leg Short Clinical Landmarks for Superior Innominate Shear? ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High All Up Clinical Landmarks for Inferior Innominate Shear? ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubes Low All Down Clinical Key finding for Pubic Compression? Bulging cartilage restricted pelvic ring bilateral tenderness. Clinical Key finding for Pubic Gapping? Deep sulcus history of delivery/trauma. Clinical Muscle associated with Anterior Innominate pain? Hamstrings tightness Concept Muscle associated with Posterior Innominate pain? Rectus Femoris / Sartorius Groin/Knee pain Concept Innominate Inflare is associated with which rotation? Anterior Rotation Concept Innominate Outflare is associated with which rotation? Posterior Rotation Concept"}, {"id": "l2", "title": "Lecture #2: Overview of Neuroembryology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "l2 Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology n n Lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. n Session Lecture 2 n Topic Embryonic development of the Nervous System n n 1. Gastrulation and Neurulation Slides 3-8 n n Gastrulation Week 3 nThe process begins with Gastrulation establishing the three germ layers n Ectoderm Gives rise to the Nervous System and skin epidermis . n Mesoderm Forms muscle bone connective tissue and the Notochord . n Endoderm Forms the gut tube and internal organs. n n Notochord Induction nThe Notochord a rod-like mesodermal structure is the key inducer. It secretes signaling molecules including Sonic Hedgehog SHH and BMPs that signal the overlying ectoderm to thicken and differentiate into the Neural Plate Slide 4 17 . n n Neurulation nThe transformation of the neural plate into the neural tube is the fundamental event of the nervous system. n1. Neural Plate Thickened ectoderm Day 19 . n2. Neural Groove The midline invaginates Day 20 . n3. Neural Folds The lateral edges elevate. n4. Neural Tube The folds fuse in the midline creating a hollow tube. This fusion begins in the cervical region and zips rostrally and caudally. n n Neuropore Closure nThe tube is initially open at both ends. Timing is critical n Rostral Anterior Neuropore Closes around Day 25 . n Caudal Posterior Neuropore Closes around Day 27-28 . n n 2. Neural Crest Cells Slides 13-15 nAs the neural tube closes cells at the crest of the neural folds detach and migrate away. These are the Neural Crest Cells often termed the 4th Germ Layer due to their wide variation. n PNS Derivatives Dorsal Root Ganglia Sensory Autonomic Ganglia Schwann Cells. n Other Derivatives Melanocytes Adrenal Medulla Craniofacial bones/cartilage face Odontoblasts teeth . n n 3. Neural Tube Differentiation Slides 16-19 nThe wall of the neural tube organizes into distinct layers n Ventricular Zone Inner layer neuroepithelium becomes Ependymal cells lining ventricles. n Mantle Layer Middle layer becomes Gray Matter cell bodies . n Marginal Layer Outer layer becomes White Matter axons . n n Dorsal-Ventral Patterning Slide 17 28 n Alar Plate Dorsal Induced by BMPs/TGF- u03b2 . Becomes Sensory regions Dorsal Horn . n Basal Plate Ventral Induced by SHH from Notochord . Becomes Motor regions Ventral Horn . n Sulcus Limitans The groove separating Alar sensory and Basal motor plates. n n 4. Brain Development Vesicles Slides 21-25 nThe rostral neural tube expands into vesicles. n 3 Primary Vesicles Week 4 n1. Prosencephalon Forebrain n2. Mesencephalon Midbrain n3. Rhombencephalon Hindbrain n n 5 Secondary Vesicles Week 5 n Prosencephalon divides into n Telencephalon Cerebral Hemispheres Lateral Ventricles. n Diencephalon Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Third Ventricle. n Mesencephalon Remains Mesencephalon Midbrain Cerebral Aqueduct . n Rhombencephalon divides into n Metencephalon Pons Cerebellum Upper 4th Ventricle. n Myelencephalon Medulla Lower 4th Ventricle. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Congenital Disorders n n correlate n Neural Tube Defects NTDs nCaused by failure of neuropore closure. Strongly associated with Folate Vitamin B9 deficiency. n Anencephaly Meroencephaly Failure of Rostral neuropore closure. Forebrain and skull vault fail to form. Frog-like appearance. Incompatible with life. n Spina Bifida Failure of Caudal neuropore closure. n Occulta Defect in vertebral arch only. Tuft of hair/dimple. Asymptomatic. n Meningocele Meninges herniate through defect. Fluid-filled sac. n Myelomeningocele Meninges + Spinal Cord herniate. Severe neurologic deficit paralysis sensory loss . n Encephalocele Herniation of brain tissue through a skull defect often occipital . n n n correlate n Holoprosencephaly HPE Slide 26-27 n Pathology Failure of the Prosencephalon Forebrain to cleavage/divide into two hemispheres. n Mechanism Defect in midline induction often SHH pathway . n Presentation Spectrum of midline facial defects Cleft lip/palate hypotelorism Cyclopia single eye and proboscis tube nose in severe cases. n Associations Patau Syndrome Trisomy 13 Fetal Alcohol Syndrome. n n n correlate n Dandy-Walker Syndrome Slide 32 n Pathology Agenesis or hypoplasia of the Cerebellar Vermis . n Mechanism Failure of the Rhombic Lip to develop Metencephalon . n Imaging Large posterior fossa cyst dilated 4th ventricle + small/absent cerebellum. n Symptoms Hydrocephalus Ataxia Delayed motor development. n n n correlate n Microcephaly Slide 38 n Pathology Small brain → Small skull Calvaria grows in response to brain growth . n Causes n Genetics Autosomal recessive. n Infections Zika Virus Cytomegalovirus CMV Rubella Toxoplasmosis. n Environment Fetal Alcohol Sulfate Radiation. n n n correlate n Hydrocephalus Slide 44 n Pathology Accumulation of CSF in ventricles due to obstruction or impaired absorption. n Congenital Cause Aqueductal Stenosis narrowing of Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius is the most common cause. Blocks flow from 3rd to 4th ventricle. n Signs Rapidly enlarging head circumference bulging fontanelles Sunset eyes . n Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. Neuro session Lecture 2 prev l1b next l3 pdf pdfs/Lecture 2. K. Poole Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf questions 1. Which of the following cells are derived from the Neural Crest? Motor neurons of the ventral horn Astrocytes of the CNS Dorsal Root Ganglion DRG cells Oligodendrocytes Microglia 2 Neural Crest cells migrate to form the DRGs autonomic ganglia Schwann cells and adrenal medulla. CNS glial cells astrocytes oligos come from the neural tube neuroectoderm . Microglia come from mesoderm. 2. A newborn has a tuft of hair over the lumbar spine but no neurologic deficits. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spina Bifida Occulta Meningocele Myelomeningocele Anencephaly Rachischisis 0 Spina Bifida Occulta is a defect in the vertebral arch mesoderm fusion often marked by a tuft of hair or dimple. The meninges and cord remain in place. 3. Which vitamin deficiency is strongly associated with Neural Tube Defects? Vitamin B12 Vitamin C Folic Acid B9 Vitamin D Vitamin A 2 Folic Acid supplementation prior to conception and during early pregnancy significantly reduces the risk of NTDs. 4. The Metencephalon gives rise to which adult structures? Medulla Midbrain Thalamus and Hypothalamus Pons and Cerebellum Cerebral Hemispheres 3 The Metencephalon develops into the Pons and Cerebellum . The Myelencephalon becomes the Medulla. 5. Which structure induces the formation of the Neural Plate from the ectoderm? Primitive Streak Notochord Yolk Sac Intermediate Mesoderm Neural Crest 1 The Notochord derived from mesoderm secretes signaling molecules e.g. Sonic Hedgehog that induce the overlying ectoderm to differentiate into the Neural Plate . Tissue origin of CNS? Neuroectoderm Neural Tube Embryology Tissue origin of PNS neurons/glia? Neural Crest Embryology Structure inducing neural plate? Notochord Embryology Adult derivative of Telencephalon? Cerebrum Cerebral Hemispheres Embryology Adult derivative of Diencephalon? Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Embryology Failure of rostral neuropore closure? Anencephaly Pathology Failure of caudal neuropore closure? Spina Bifida Pathology Vitamin to prevent NTDs? Folic Acid B9 Prevention Closure day of Rostral Neuropore? Day 25 Embryology Closure day of Caudal Neuropore? Day 27-28 Embryology BandB Neurology > Embryology > Nervous System Development FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Pathoma CNS > Congenital Malformations Physeo Anatomy > Neuroanatomy > Embryology Germ Layer Rule Ectoderm = Skin + Brain. n Mesoderm = Muscle + Bone + Notochord. n Endoderm = Gut. Neural Crest Mnemonic MOTEL PASS M elanocytes O dontoblasts T racheal cartilage E nterochromaffin cells L aryngeal cartilage P arafollicular cells A drenal medulla S chwann cells S piral septum. Neuropore Dates Rostral closes Day 25 . Caudal closes Day 27-28 . Folate is critical before this window. Alar vs Basal Plate Alar = Dorsal = Sensory Top . n Basal = Ventral = Motor Bottom . Holoprosencephaly Failed forebrain cleavage. Spectrum Cleft lip → Cyclopia. Associated with Trisomy 13 Patau and SHH mutations."}, {"id": "l20", "title": "Lecture 20: Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics (Cholinesterase Inhibitors)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l20 Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 20 n n 1. Mechanism & Classification Slide 9 16 nIndirect-acting agents inhibit Acetylcholinesterase AChE increasing the concentration of endogenous Acetylcholine in the synapse. This stimulates both Muscarinic and Nicotinic receptors. n Quaternary Amines No CNS entry Neostigmine Pyridostigmine. n Tertiary Amines Enter CNS Physostigmine Donepezil Organophosphates. n n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 20 36 n n Neuromuscular Junction Disorders n Myasthenia Gravis MG n Pathology Autoantibodies against Nicotinic receptors Nm . n Treatment Pyridostigmine Oral long-acting or Neostigmine . n Diagnosis Edrophonium Tensilon test. Rapid short-acting. Improvement = +MG. Historical . n Reversal of NMJ Blockade n Neostigmine is used to reverse non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers e.g. Rocuronium after surgery. n n CNS Disorders n Alzheimer s Disease n Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . n Mechanism Increase CNS ACh to improve cognition/memory. n Anticholinergic Toxicity Atropine Overdose n Drug Physostigmine . n Why? It crosses the BBB to reverse central delirium unlike Neostigmine . n n Glaucoma n Echothiophate Organophosphate Long acting . n n 3. Toxicity & Poisoning Slide 24-28 n n Organophosphates Insecticides Nerve Gas n Mechanism Irreversible inhibition of AChE via phosphorylation . Aging makes the bond permanent. n Muscarinic Signs DUMBELS D iarrhea U rination M iosis B radycardia/Bronchospasm E mesis L acrimation S alivation. n Nicotinic Signs Fasciculations → Paralysis Depolarizing blockade . n Treatment n 1. Atropine Blocks Muscarinic effects life-saving for lungs/heart . n 2. Pralidoxime 2-PAM Regenerates AChE IF given before aging. Relieves neuromuscular paralysis Atropine does not . n n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 22 n n correlate n Myasthenic vs. Cholinergic Crisis n Myasthenic Crisis Low ACh. Respiratory failure due to undertreatment of MG. Action Give more Pyridostigmine. n Cholinergic Crisis Excess ACh. Depolarizing blockade due to overtreatment. Action Stop drug give Atropine. n differentiation Edrophonium test improves Myasthenic crisis but worsens Cholinergic crisis. n n n correlate n Ogilvie Syndrome n Condition Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction massive dilation without physical blockage . n Treatment Neostigmine stimulates GI motility . n Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neuroscience questions A farmer is brought to the ER with pinpoint pupils excessive salivation difficulty breathing and muscle fasciculations. Exposure to an insecticide is suspected. Which drug should be administered immediately to treat the respiratory secretions and bradycardia? Physostigmine Pralidoxime Atropine Neostigmine Pilocarpine 2 Atropine is the first-line antidote for the life-threatening muscarinic effects bronchorrhea bronchospasm bradycardia of organophosphate poisoning. Which drug is an appropriate treatment for Alzheimer s disease? Neostigmine Pyridostigmine Donepezil Edrophonium Pilocarpine 2 Donepezil is a centrally acting AChE inhibitor used for Alzheimer s. Neostigmine/Pyridostigmine do not cross the BBB . A child ingests his grandmother s eye drops Atropine and develops hot red skin dilated pupils and delirium. Which antidote can reverse the CNS delirium? Neostigmine Physostigmine Pralidoxime Donepezil Edrophonium 1 The patient has Anticholinergic toxicity Mad as a hatter . Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the BBB to reverse CNS toxicity. What is the mechanism of action of Pralidoxime 2-PAM ? Blocks muscarinic receptors Blocks nicotinic receptors Regenerates Acetylcholinesterase Inhibits Acetylcholine release Directly activates GABA receptors 2 Pralidoxime is a cholinesterase regenerator. It breaks the bond between the organophosphate and the enzyme if given before aging . Which drug is preferred for long-term oral management of Myasthenia Gravis? Edrophonium Physostigmine Pyridostigmine Echothiophate Acetylcholine 2 Pyridostigmine has a longer duration of action 4-6h and is oral making it ideal for maintenance therapy in Myasthenia Gravis . Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Muscarinic Atropine Toxicology Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Enzyme Regenerator Pralidoxime 2-PAM Toxicology Antidote for Atropine Overdose Physostigmine Toxicology Drug for Myasthenia Gravis Maintenance Pyridostigmine Pharmacology Drug for Alzheimer s Disease AChE Inhibitor Donepezil Pharmacology Why doesn t Neostigmine treat Alzheimer s? It is a Quaternary amine Charged → Doesn t cross BBB Pharmacology Symptoms of Muscarinic Excess DUMBELS Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Toxicology Drug used to diagnose Myasthenia Gravis Historical Edrophonium Pharmacology Mechanism of Organophosphates Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase Toxicology Treatment for Ogilvie Syndrome Neostigmine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Physostigmine vs. Neostigmine Physostigmine crosses BBB Treats Atropine OD . n Neostigmine does NOT cross BBB Treats MG urinary retention . DUMBELS Mnemonic Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Muscarinic excess . Organophosphate Antidotes 1. Atropine Muscarinic symptoms . n2. Pralidoxime Nicotinic/Muscle symptoms - regenerates enzyme . Myasthenia Gravis Treatment Pyridostigmine Long-acting oral . Increases ACh at NMJ. Alzheimer s Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . Increase CNS ACh."}, {"id": "l21", "title": "Lecture 21: Muscarinic Antagonists (Anticholinergics)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l21 Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 21 n n 1. Class Overview Slide 7-8 n Mechanism Competitive antagonists or inverse agonists at Muscarinic receptors M1-M5 . n Structure n Tertiary Amines Cross BBB Atropine Scopolamine Benztropine. n Quaternary Ammonium No CNS Ipratropium Tiotropium Glycopyrrolate. n n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 21-23 n Atropine n Bradycardia Blocks vagal tone M2 → Increases HR. n Antidote For Organophosphate poisoning. n Pre-op Reduces metabolic secretions. n Note Low doses can cause Paradoxical Bradycardia blocks presynaptic M2 autoreceptors . n Scopolamine n Motion Sickness Transdermal patch bypasses first-pass . n Respiratory COPD/Asthma n Ipratropium Short & Tiotropium Long . Block bronchoconstriction + decrease secretions. n Urinary Incontinence Overactive Bladder n Oxybutynin . Relaxes detrusor muscle. n Parkinson s Disease n Benztropine . Treats tremor restores ACh/Dopamine balance . n Ophthalmology n Tropicamide/Cyclopentolate . Induce Mydriasis Dilation & Cycloplegia Paralysis of accommodation for exams. n n 3. Toxicity Anticholinergic Toxidrome Slide 26-28 n Red as a Beet Cutaneous flushing vasodilation to dissipate heat . n Dry as a Bone No sweating Anhidrosis dry mouth. n Hot as Hades Hyperthermia cant sweat . n Blind as a Bat Mydriasis dilated pupils + Cycloplegia blurry near vision . n Mad as a Hatter Delirium Hallucinations Central toxicity . n Full as a Flask Urinary Retention. n Treatment Physostigmine crosses BBB - Use with caution! n n 4. Contraindications Slide 25 n Narrow-Angle Glaucoma Dilation blocks outflow → Acute crisis. n BPH Prostatic Hyperplasia Worsens urinary retention. n Elderly Risk of falls confusion delirium BEERS criteria . n GI Obstruction/Ileus Worsens constipation. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 31 n n correlate n Drug Interactions n Oxybutynin + Macrolides Erythromycin Macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 . Oxybutynin is metabolized by CYP3A4. Result → Anticholinergic Toxicity . n n n correlate n Use in Special Populations n Children High risk of Hyperthermia Hot as Hades . n Elderly High risk of Delirium Mad as a Hatter and Falls. Avoid if possible. n Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics Neuroscience questions A 75-year-old male with benign prostatic hyperplasia BPH takes an over-the-counter cold medication and develops acute urinary retention. Which ingredient likely caused this? Guaifenesin Acetaminophen Diphenhydramine Anticholinergic Ibuprofen Pseudoephedrine 2 Diphenhydramine has strong anticholinergic properties which can precipitate urinary retention in patients with BPH. Why is Atropine contraindicated in patients with Narrow-Angle Glaucoma? It causes miosis It increases aqueous humor production It causes mydriasis which blocks the outflow angle It causes cataract formation It lowers intraocular pressure too much 2 Atropine causes Mydriasis dilation . In narrow-angle glaucoma the bunched-up iris blocks the trabecular meshwork dangerously increasing pressure. To prevent motion sickness a patient applies a Transderm Scop patch. What is the active drug and its mechanism? Scopolamine; Muscarinic Antagonist Scopolamine; Nicotinic Antagonist Meclizine; H1 Antagonist Ondansetron; 5-HT3 Antagonist Metoclopramide; D2 Antagonist 0 Scopolamine is a Muscarinic Antagonist used transdermally for motion sickness prevention. Which drug is used to treat tremors in Parkinson s disease by restoring the balance between Acetylcholine and Dopamine? Atropine Ipratropium Benztropine Oxybutynin Succinylcholine 2 Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic used to treat Parkinsonian tremors Parkinson s = Low Dopamine + High ACh . A child presents with flushed skin dry mouth dilated pupils and tachycardia after eating berries from a garden plant. The most likely plant is Foxglove Digitalis Jimson Weed Datura/Atropine Oleander Poison Ivy Mushrooms Muscarine 1 Jimson Weed Datura stramonium contains belladonna alkaloids Atropine/Scopolamine causing the classic anticholinergic toxidrome. Anticholinergic Toxidrome Mad as a Hatter Delirium / Hallucinations Toxicology Anticholinergic Toxidrome Blind as a Bat Mydriasis + Cycloplegia Blurred vision Toxicology Drug for Overactive Bladder Oxybutynin Pharmacology Inhaled Muscarinic Antagonist for COPD Tiotropium Long or Ipratropium Short Pharmacology Why Atropine causes Tachycardia Blocks M2 receptors on SA node Removes vagal brake Physiology Mechanism of Paradoxical Bradycardia Blockade of Presynaptic Autoreceptors M2 → Increased ACh release Physiology Contraindication Narrow Angle Glaucoma Muscarinic Antagonists Atropine Pharmacology Contraindication BPH Muscarinic Antagonists Causes Retention Pharmacology Drug for Motion Sickness Patch Scopolamine Pharmacology Treats Parkinson s Tremor Benztropine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Muscarinic Antagonists FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergic Toxidrome Red Flush Dry No sweat Hot Hyperthermia Blind Mydriasis Mad Delirium Full Retention . Paradoxical Bradycardia Low dose Atropine blocks Presynaptic M2 autoreceptors → Increases ACh releases → Slows Heart. Reversal Agents Atropine reverses AChE Inhibitors. n Physostigmine reverses Atropine. Contraindications Glaucoma Angle Closure BPH Retention Elderly Delirium . Ipratropium/Tiotropium Quaternary amines No CNS . Inhaled for COPD/Asthma ."}, {"id": "l22", "title": "Lecture 22: Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l22 Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 22 n n 1. Ganglionic Blockers Slide 7-9 n Target Neuronal Nicotinic Receptors Nn at autonomic ganglia. n Mecamylamine n Mechanism Blocks output of BOTH Sympathetic and Parasympathetic systems. n Effect Predictable based on Dominant Tone n Arterioles Symp dominant Vasodilation → Hypotension Orthostatic . n Heart Para dominant Tachycardia. n Gut/Bladder Para dominant Constipation Urinary Retention. n Eye Para dominant Mydriasis Cycloplegia. n n 2. Neuromuscular Blockers NMBAs Slide 19-23 nTarget Nicotinic Nm receptors at the Neuromuscular Junction NMJ . n n Depolarizing NMBA n Succinylcholine SCh n Structure Two ACh molecules. n Mechanism Agonist. Causes sustained depolarization → Fasciculations → Flaccid paralysis Phase I . Desensitization Phase II . n Metabolism Plasma Cholinesterase Pseudocholinesterase . n Duration Very short <8 min . Prolonged in patients with genetic deficiency. n Adverse Effects n Malignant Hyperthermia MH Triggered by SCh + Inhaled Anesthetics e.g. Halothane . Muscle rigidity hyperthermia. Treat with Dantrolene . n Hyperkalemia Life-threatening in Burn/Trauma patients due to receptor upregulation/rhabdomyolysis . n n Non-Depolarizing NMBAs Competitive Antagonists n Isoquinolines n Atracurium Degraded by Hofmann Elimination spontaneous no organ weakness . Side Effect Histamine release Hypotension + Laudanosine accumulation Seizures . n Cisatracurium Hofmann elimination. No histamine. Safe for organ failure. n Aminosteroids n Rocuronium Fast onset can replace SCh for intubation . Biliary excretion. n Vecuronium Renal/Biliary excretion. n n 3. Reversal Slide 27 n Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neostigmine Increase ACh to compete with NMBAs. Always give with Atropine to prevent muscarinic bradycardia. n Sugammadex Specialized chelator. Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium for rapid reversal. No muscarinic side effects. n n 4. Clinical Correlates n n correlate n Malignant Hyperthermia n Trigger Succinylcholine or Volatile Anesthetics. n Genetic Mutation in Ryanodine Receptor RyR1 . n Pathology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from SR → Rigidity → Heat/Acidosis. n Cure Dantrolene Blocks RyR1 . n n n correlate n Hofmann Elimination n Drug Atracurium / Cisatracurium. n Concept Breaks down spontaneously in plasma based on pH and temperature. n Pearl Ideal for patients with multisystem organ failure Kidney/Liver don t matter . n Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers Neuroscience questions A patient undergoes surgery and develops muscle rigidity tachycardia and a temperature of 105 u00b0F shortly after induction. Which drug is the most likely trigger? Rocuronium Succinylcholine Cisatracurium Propofol Fentanyl 1 Succinylcholine is a classic trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia along with volatile anesthetics . Which neuromuscular blocker undergoes spontaneous Hofmann elimination and is therefore safe in patients with renal and hepatic failure? Pancuronium Rocuronium Vecuronium Cisatracurium Succinylcholine 3 Cisatracurium and Atracurium degrades spontaneously in plasma Hofmann elimination independent of organ function. What is the mechanism of action of Sugammadex? Inhibits Acetylcholinesterase Blocks Ryanodine Receptors Chelates/Encapsulates Aminosteroid NMBAs Stimulates Nicotinic Receptors Blocks Muscarinic Receptors 2 Sugammadex forms a complex with aminosteroids Rocuronium/Vecuronium to reverse blockade rapidly. A patient with severe burns requires intubation. Which neuromuscular blocker is CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of hyperkalemia? Rocuronium Cisatracurium Vecuronium Atracurium Succinylcholine 4 Succinylcholine causes depolarization and K+ efflux. In burn patients upregulated receptors this can cause massive hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. The use of Neostigmine to reverse neuromuscular blockade can cause bradycardia. Which drug is co-administered to prevent this? Pralidoxime Physostigmine Atropine Epinephrine Dantrolene 2 Neostigmine increases ACh everywhere Muscarinic + Nicotinic . Atropine is given to block the Muscarinic effects Bradycardia while allowing Nicotinic effects Muscle strength . Contraindication for Succinylcholine Burns Trauma Hyperkalemia Hx of Malignant Hyperthermia Pharmacology Treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia Dantrolene Pharmacology NMBA metabolized by Plasma Cholinesterase Succinylcholine Pharmacology NMBA eliminated by Hofmann Elimination Cisatracurium / Atracurium Pharmacology Depolarizing Neuromuscular Blocker Succinylcholine Pharmacology Dominant Autonomic Tone Heart Parasympathetic Vagal Physiology Dominant Autonomic Tone Blood Vessels Sympathetic Physiology Sugammadex reverses which drugs? Rocuronium and Vecuronium Pharmacology Why give Atropine with Neostigmine reversal? To prevent Muscarinic side effects Bradycardia Pharmacology Ganglion Blocker Drug Name Mecamylamine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Neuromuscular Blockers FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > NMBAs Succinylcholine & Potassium Causes K+ efflux. Contraindicated in Burns Trauma Crush injuries and Denervation risk of Cardiac Arrest . Dantrolene Mechanism Blocks Ryanodine Receptors RyR1 on Sarcoplasmic Reticulum. Treats Malignant Hyperthermia. Hofmann Elimination Atracurium/Cisatracurium . Spontaneous degradation. Safe in renal/hepatic failure. Dominant Tone Concept Ganglionic blockers remove the dominant tone. Vessels = Symp Block → Hypotension . Heart = Para Block → Tachycardia . Sugammadex Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium . Reversal without cholinergic side effects."}, {"id": "l23", "title": "Lecture 23: Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l23 Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System n n Lecturer A. Reno D.O. n Session Lecture 23 n n 1. Approach to Dizziness Slide 6-27 n Vertigo Illusion of movement Spinning . n Peripheral Inner Ear n Nystagmus Unidirectional usually horizontal/rotary . n Fixation Extinguishes nystagmus. n Walking Patient can usually walk. n Hearing Often affected Tinnitus/Loss . n Central Brainstem/Cerebellum - Stroke/MS n Nystagmus Bidirectional or Vertical . n Fixation Does NOT extinguish . n Walking Patient often cannot stand/walk Ataxia . n Hearing Usually normal. n Red Flags Comparison to Stroke HINTS Exam . n n 2. Key Vestibular Pathologies n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV n Duration Seconds < 1 minute . n Trigger Head movement Rolling over in bed . n Mechanism Dislodged Otoconia Calcium Carbonate crystals in posterior semicircular canal. n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike maneuver Rotary nystagmus . n Treatment Epley Maneuver . n n Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops n Duration Hours 20 min - 24 hrs . n Classic Triad n 1. Episodic Vertigo . n 2. Tinnitus Roaring . n 3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss Fluctuating low frequency . n Mechanism Excess Endolymph fluid. n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics HCTZ/Triamterene . n n Vestibulo-Cochlear Infections n Vestibular Neuritis Vertigo only No hearing loss . Duration Days . Usually viral. Rx Corticosteroids. n Labyrinthitis Vertigo + Hearing Loss . Viral or Bacterial. n n 3. Hearing Loss Evaluation Slide 40-47 n Conductive CHL n Anatomy Outer/Middle ear Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis . n Weber Lateralizes to BAD ear. n Rinne BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction . n n Sensorineural SNHL n Anatomy Cochlea/Nerve Presbycusis Noise Meniere s Acoustic Neuroma . n Weber Lateralizes to GOOD ear. n Rinne AC > BC Normal pattern but reduced . n n 4. Clinical Correlates n correlate n The HINTS Exam nUsed to distinguish Central Stroke from Peripheral vertigo in acute vestibular syndrome. n1. H ead I mpulse Normal impulse = Central bad . Peripheral has corrective saccade . n2. N ystagmus Direction changing = Central . n3. T est of S kew Vertical Skew = Central . n Pearl INFARCT Impulse Normal Fast-phase Alternating Refixation on Cover Test . n n n correlate n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma n Unilateral Sensorineural Hearing Loss + Tinnitus. n Slow growing. n Diagnose with MRI . n Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A 45-year-old woman complains of room-spinning dizziness that lasts for 30 seconds when she rolls over in bed. It is associated with nausea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Multiple Sclerosis 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min episodes of vertigo triggered by head position changes. A patient presents with vertigo tinnitus and hearing loss in the right ear. The episodes last for about 4 hours. Which treatment is most appropriate for long-term management? Epley Maneuver Antibiotics Low salt diet and Diuretics Carbamazepine Chemotherapy 2 The patient has Meniere s Disease Triad + Duration . Treatment involves reducing endolymph fluid with salt restriction and diuretics . Weber test lateralizes to the LEFT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the LEFT. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber to the Left + Rinne BC > AC on Left = Left Conductive Hearing Loss . In CHL bone conduction is better than air and it lateralizes to the affected ear . Which finding on physical exam strongly suggests a CENTRAL cause of vertigo e.g. Stroke ? Nystagmus that suppresses with fixation Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus Positive Head Impulse Test Corrective Saccade Vertical Nystagmus Hearing Loss 3 Vertical nystagmus or direction-changing nystagmus is a red flag for Central pathology. Peripheral nystagmus is usually horizontal/rotary and suppresses with fixation. A patient has sudden onset of severe vertigo lasting 3 days with nausea and vomiting but NO hearing loss. Findings include horizontal nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis? Labyrinthitis Vestibular Neuritis Meniere s Disease BPPV Otosclerosis 1 Vestibular Neuritis presents with continuous vertigo for days often post-viral with preserved hearing . Labyrinthitis would include hearing loss . Diagnosis Vertigo < 1 minute triggered by movement BPPV Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Hours + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + No Hearing Loss Vestibular Neuritis ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + Hearing Loss Labyrinthitis ENT Weber Test Lateralization Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the BAD affected ear Diagnosis Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural Loss Lateralizes to the GOOD unaffected ear Diagnosis Maneuver to Diagnose BPPV Dix-Hallpike Diagnosis Maneuver to Treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Treatment Mechanism of Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid in inner ear Pathology Central vs Peripheral Nystagmus Fixation Peripheral suppresses with fixation. Central does NOT. Diagnosis AMBOSS Neurology > Vertigo and Dizziness FirstAid Neurology > Otology BandB Neurology > The Ear Vertigo Duration Clues < 1 min BPPV. n Hours Meniere s Disease. n Days Vestibular Neuritis / Labyrinthitis. Peripheral vs. Central Nystagmus Peripheral Extinguishes with fixation. Horizontal/Rotary. n Central Does NOT extinguish. Vertical or Direction-Changing. Meniere s Triad 1. Episodic Vertigo n2. Tinnitus Roaring n3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss. Weber/Rinne Rules Conductive Bad ear hears Weber louder. BC > AC. n Sensorineural Good ear hears Weber louder. AC > BC. Treatment of BPPV Epley Maneuver Repositioning of otoconia ."}, {"id": "l24", "title": "Lecture 24: Olfaction and Taste", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l24 Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 24 n n 1. Olfaction Smell n Receptors Bipolar neurons in olfactory epithelium. n Turnover Every 7 days stem cells . n Receptors GPCRs. n Pathway n 1. Olfactory Nerves CN I pass through Cribriform Plate of Ethmoid bone. n 2. Synapse in Glomeruli of Olfactory Bulb. n 3. Mitral/Tufted Cells Second order form Olfactory Tract. n 4. Project directly to Primary Olfactory Cortex Piriform Cortex Amygdala Entorhinal Cortex. n Note Olfaction is the ONLY sense that bypasses the Thalamus before reaching the cortex. n n 2. Taste Gustation n Modalities n Salty Na+ influx Amiloride-sensitive channels . n Sour H+ ions Block K+ channels . n Sweet/Bitter/Umami GPCRs G-protein coupled . n Innervation n Anterior 2/3 CN VII Facial - Chorda Tympani . n Posterior 1/3 CN IX Glossopharyngeal . n Epiglottis/Pharynx CN X Vagus . n Pathway n 1. Nerves project to Nucleus Tractus Solitarius NTS in Medulla. n 2. Project to VPM of Thalamus. n 3. Project to Gustatory Cortex Insula/Frontal Operculum . n n 3. Clinical Correlates n correlate n Kallmann Syndrome n Triad Anosmia + Delayed Puberty Hypogonadism . n Mechanism Failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from olfactory placode to hypothalamus. n n n correlate n Foster-Kennedy Syndrome n Cause Meningioma of the Olfactory Groove. n Triad n 1. Ipsilateral Anosmia Tumor compresses olfactory tract . n 2. Ipsilateral Optic Atrophy Tumor compresses Optic Nerve . n 3. Contralateral Papilledema Increased intracranial pressure . n n n correlate n Uncinate Fits Temporal Lobe Epilepsy n Seizures originating in the Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe . n Aura Hallucination of foul smells Cacosmia . n n n correlate n Neurodegenerative Diseases n Parkinson s / Alzheimer s Hyposmia decreased smell is often the earliest clinical sign appearing years before motor or cognitive symptoms. n Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste Neuroscience questions A 16-year-old male presents with delayed puberty. Physical exam reveals small testes and a complete inability to smell since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis? Klinefelter Syndrome Kallmann Syndrome Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Turner Syndrome Uncinate Epilepsy 1 Kallmann Syndrome is characterized by hypogonadotropic hypogonadism delayed puberty and anosmia due to migration failure of GnRH neurons. A patient presents with seizures preceded by a hallucination of a foul smell burning rubber . Where is the seizure focus likely located? Postcentral Gyrus Occipital Lobe Uncus Temporal Lobe Frontal Lobe Cerebellum 2 Uncinate fits are temporal lobe seizures involving the Uncus primary olfactory cortex area resulting in olfactory hallucinations Cacosmia . Which cranial nerve is responsible for taste sensation on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue? Trigeminal V Facial VII Glossopharyngeal IX Vagus X Hypoglossal XII 1 The Facial Nerve CN VII carries taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue via the Chorda Tympani . A 55-year-old woman presents with loss of smell in her right ear and vision loss in her right eye. Fundoscopy reveals right optic atrophy and left papilledema. What is the most likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Multiple Sclerosis Occipital Stroke Glioblastoma Multiforme 1 Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma causes ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy direct compression and contralateral papilledema ICP . Loss of olfaction is often an early prodromal sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 1 Hyposmia loss of smell frequently precedes motor symptoms in Parkinson s Disease and Alzheimer s by several years. Diagnosis Anosmia + No Puberty Kallmann Syndrome Neurology Diagnosis Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Neurology CN for Taste Anterior 2/3 Tongue CN VII Facial Anatomy CN for Taste Posterior 1/3 Tongue CN IX Glossopharyngeal Anatomy Taste Modalities using Ion Channels Salty Na+ and Sour H+ Physiology Taste Modalities using GPCRs Sweet Bitter Umami Physiology Seizure aura with foul smell Cacosmia Uncinate Fit Temporal Lobe/Uncus Neurology Unique feature of Olfactory Pathway Does not relay in Thalamus before Cortex Anatomy Primary Olfactory Cortex location Piriform Cortex Temporal Lobe Anatomy Thalamic Nucleus for Taste VPM Ventral Posteromedial Anatomy AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Otology/Ophthalmology BandB Neurology > The Chemical Senses Olfactory Pathway Uniqueness Olfaction is the only sensory modality that projects directly to the cortex without relaying through the Thalamus first. Kallmann Syndrome Teenager with No Smell + No Puberty . GnRH neuron migration failure . Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema . Taste Innervation Anterior 2/3 = CN VII . nPosterior 1/3 = CN IX . nThroat = CN X . Taste Transduction Salty/Sour = Ion Channels . nSweet/Bitter/Umami = GPCRs ."}, {"id": "l25", "title": "Lecture 25: Limbic System - Neuroanatomy, Regional Function and Limbic Pathology", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l25 Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. session Lecture 25 Neuroscience prev l24 next l26 pdf pdfs/Lecture 25. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Faculty J. Xie Ph.D..srt Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. Session Lecture 25 1. Introduction to the Limbic System Slide 5-7 The Limbic System Latin limbus = border is a complex network of cortical and diencephalic structures located on the medial border of the cerebral hemisphere. It serves as a bridge linking the conscious intellectual functions of the cerebral cortex with the unconscious autonomic regulation of the brainstem and hypothalamus . Primary Function Regulates primitive emotional drives memory storage/retrieval and homeostasis. The 5 F s Mnemonic mnemonic Functions of the Limbic System The 5 F s F eeding Satiety/Hunger F leeing Fear response F ighting Aggression F eeling Emotion F ornicating Sexual behavior Key Structures While the exact composition is debated the Big 3 universally agreed-upon components are 1. Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing of sensory input. 2. Hippocampal Formation Memory consolidation and spatial navigation. 3. Amygdala Fear anger and emotional association. Other components include the Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus Anterior Thalamic Nuclei Septal Area Reward and Orbitofrontal Cortex Social behavior . 2. Cingulate Gyrus Slide 9-10 The Cingulate Gyrus wraps around the corpus callosum and acts as a gateway between the cortex and limbic system. Function It adds emotional coloring to sensory perceptions e.g. how pain feels emotionally . Clinical Relevance Cingulotomy A surgical lesioning of the anterior cingulate cortex. Historically used to treat intractable pain or severe depression/anxiety. Patients report they still feel the pain but it doesn t bother them anymore loss of the affective/suffering component . 3. Hippocampal Formation & Memory Slide 13-17 The Hippocampus Latin for Seahorse is critical for learning memory consolidation and spatial navigation. Structure Composed of the Dentate Gyrus Hippocampus Proper Cornu Ammonis CA1-CA4 and Subiculum . Vulnerability The CA1 pyramidal cells are exceptionally sensitive to hypoxia/ischemia . They will die within minutes of oxygen deprivation Sommer s Sector . Lateralization Right Hippocampus Spatial Orientation and navigation e.g. London Taxi Drivers have enlarged right posterior hippocampi . Left Hippocampus Verbal memory. The Fornix Slide 16-17 The major output tract of the hippocampus. Pathway Hippocampus → Fimbria → Crus → Body → Columns of Fornix → Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus . correlate Temporal Lobe Epilepsy Pathophysiology The medial temporal lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala has the lowest seizure threshold in the brain. Presentation Complex Partial Psychomotor Seizures . Preceded by an Aura e.g. burning rubber smell/uncinate fits hallucinations deja vu . Automatisms Lip smacking chewing swallowing or fumbling of hands. Altered consciousness dreamy state . correlate Herpes Simplex Encephalitis Pathophysiology HSV-1 has a specific tropism for the limbic cortex Medial Temporal Lobes/Orbitofrontal cortex . Presentation Acute onset of fever headache seizures and bizarre behavior. Sequelae Bilateral necrosis of the medial temporal lobes leads to profound Anterograde Amnesia inability to form new memories and Kluver-Bucy-like symptoms. Imaging Hyperintensity in temporal lobes on MRI Slide 12 . 4. The Papez Circuit Slide 30-31 Discovered by James Papez 1937 this is the fundamental circuit for Episodic Memory Consolidation and emotional expression. highyield The Papez Circuit Pathway 1. Hippocampus Subiculum 2. $ xrightarrow text Fornix $ Mammillary Bodies 3. $ xrightarrow text Mammillothalamic Tract $ Anterior Thalamic Nucleus 4. $ xrightarrow text Internal Capsule Ant. Limb $ Cingulate Gyrus 5. $ xrightarrow text Cingulum bundle $ Entorhinal Cortex / Hippocampus The circuit closes 5. Amygdala & Emotion Slide 19-23 The Amygdala Almond is located deep in the uncus of the temporal lobe anterior to the hippocampus. Function Mediates Fear Anger Aggression and the emotional significance of memories e.g. fear conditioning . Connections Hypothalamus Triggers autonomic responses to emotion tachycardia sweating . Olfactory System Why smells trigger strong emotional memories Proustian memory . correlate Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Pathophysiology Bilateral lesion of the Amygdala . associated with HSV encephalitis trauma or lobectomies. Presentation 1. Placidity Loss of fear and aggression docile . 2. Hyperorality Putting inappropriate objects in mouth. 3. Hypersexuality Indiscriminate sexual behavior. 4. Hypermetamorphosis Compulsive visual exploration of objects. 5. Visual Agnosia Psychic blindness inability to recognize emotional significance of objects . 6. Memory Disorders Slide 36-39 Classification of Memory 1. Explicit Declarative Facts Semantic and Events Episodic . Requires Medial Temporal Lobe Hippocampus & Diencephalon. 2. Implicit Procedural Skills habits conditioning. Skills e.g. riding a bike Basal Ganglia Cerebellum Motor Cortex. Emotional Conditioning Amygdala. Skeletal Conditioning Cerebellum. Clinical Cases correlate Patient H.M. History Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Deficit Severe Anterograde Amnesia cannot form new explicit memories . Retrograde memory pre-surgery was largely intact. Key Finding His Procedural Memory was intact he could learn to solve the Tower of Hanoi puzzle but couldn t remember practicing it . Implication Proved that hippocampus is essential for consolidation of new declarative memories but not for procedural skills. correlate Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathophysiology Thiamine Vitamin B1 Deficiency intimately associated with chronic Alcoholism . Lesion Sites Mammillary Bodies hemorrhagic necrosis Dorsomedial Thalamus Periaqueductal gray. Wernicke s Encephalopathy Acute/Reversible Triad C onfusion A taxia N ystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of beer . Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic/Irreversible Severe Anterograde Amnesia . Confabulation Making up stories to fill memory gaps lack of insight . Treatment IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose Glucose first precipitates the encephalopathy . correlate Alzheimer s Disease Pathophysiology Widespread cortical atrophy specifically Hippocampal Atrophy Early memory loss. Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Degeneration of Cholinergic neurons. Neurochemistry Deficiency of Acetylcholine ACh . Treatment AChE Inhibitors Aricept to increase synaptic ACh Slide 38 . 7. Septal Area & Reward Slide 25 Located near the anterior commissure. Contains the Nucleus Accumbens . Function The Pleasure Center . Mediates reward and reinforcement. Neurotransmitter Dopamine Mesolimbic pathway . Clinical Implicated in Addiction . The 5 F s of the Limbic System Feeding Fleeing Fighting Feeling and Sex. Sommer s Sector Vulnerability CA1 Pyramidal cells in the hippocampus are the most sensitive neurons to hypoxia . First to die in ischemic stroke or cardiorespiratory arrest. Papez Circuit Sequence Hippocampus → Fornix → Mammillary Bodies → Ant. Thalamic Nucleus → Cingulate Gyrus → Hippocampus. Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Bilateral Amygdala lesion. Hyper sexuality Hyper orality Placidity No fear . Wernicke s Triad Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer . Caused by B1 Thiamine deficiency harming Mammillary Bodies . Procedural vs. Declarative Memory Hippocampal damage Patient H.M. destroys Declarative New facts/events but spares Procedural Skills/Habits - Basal Ganglia/Cerebellum . HSV Encephalitis Target HSV-1 loves the Medial Temporal Lobe . Causes hemorrhagic necrosis fever seizures and bizarre behavior. questions A chronic alcoholic is brought to the ER with confusion ataxia and ophthalmoplegia. MRI reveals degeneration of the mammillary bodies. Which nutrient deficiency is responsible? Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Vitamin C Ascorbic Acid 1 Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome is caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency common in alcoholics. It damages the Mammillary Bodies and medial thalamus. The triad is Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus. A patient undergoes bilateral temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Post-operatively he interacts with objects by putting them in his mouth displays inappropriate sexual behavior toward staff and appears completely fearless. Which structure s absence explains these symptoms? Hippocampus Cingulate Gyrus Amygdala Mammillary Bodies Septal Nuclei 2 This is Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Hyperorality Hypersexuality Placidity . It is caused by bilateral lesions to the Amygdala . A 65-year-old male with a history of cardiac arrest 2 weeks ago now presents with severe short-term memory loss. He cannot remember what he ate for breakfast or the names of his new doctors. Which specific neuronal population was likely damaged by hypoxia? Purkinje cells of the Cerebellum Anterior Horn cells of Spinal Cord CA1 Pyramidal cells of the Hippocampus Dopaminergic neurons of Substantia Nigra Cholinergic neurons of Nucleus Basalis 2 The CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Sommer s sector are the most sensitive cells in the brain to hypoxic-ischemic injury . Damage here causes anterograde amnesia. In the Papez circuit fibers travel from the mammillary bodies to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus via which tract? Fornix Cingulum Stria Terminalis Mammillothalamic Tract Medial Forebrain Bundle 3 The Mammillothalamic Tract connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Nucleus of the Thalamus . Patient H.M. had his medial temporal lobes removed. He could no longer form new memories of events Episodic but he could learn to solve puzzles Procedural . Which structure is primarily responsible for the preserved procedural memory? Hippocampus Basal Ganglia Amygdala Septal Nuclei Mammillary Bodies 1 Procedural Memory skills/habits is processed by the Basal Ganglia Cerebellum and Motor Cortex which were intact in H.M. The Hippocampus removed drives Declarative/Episodic memory. Which limbic structure is responsible for spatial navigation? Right Hippocampus Physiology What is the classic triad of Wernicke s Encephalopathy? Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer Pathology Bilateral lesions of the Amygdala cause what syndrome? Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Placidity Hyperorality Hypersexuality Pathology What is the major output tract of the Hippocampus? The Fornix Anatomy Which neurons are most sensitive to cerebral hypoxia? CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Pathology What is the primary neurotransmitter deficiency in Alzheimer s Disease? Acetylcholine ACh due to loss of Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Pathology HSV-1 Encephalitis typically affects which brain region? Medial Temporal Lobes Limbic Cortex Pathology Damage to the Mammillary Bodies is seen in whom? Alcoholics Thiamine/B1 deficiency → Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathology What connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Thalamus? Mammillothalamic Tract Anatomy What defines Anterograde Amnesia? Inability to form NEW memories Old memories intact Pathology BandB Neurology > Limbic System FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Limbic System Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Diseases Limbic System Components The Big 3 Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing Pain affect Hippocampus Memory Spatial nav Right Amygdala Fear Anger Kluver-Bucy Papez Circuit Hippocampus → Fornix Mammillary Bodies → Mammillothalamic Tract Ant. Thalamus → Internal Capsule Cingulate → Cingulum Pathology Kluver-Bucy Bilateral Amygdala. Hypersex/oral. Wernicke-Korsakoff B1 Def. Mammillary Bodies. Confabulation. HSV Encephalitis Medial Temporal Lobe. Bizarre behavior. Alzheimer s Hippocampal atrophy. Low ACh. Patient H.M. Medial Temporal removal. Anterograde Amnesia."}, {"id": "l26", "title": "Lecture 26: Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l26 Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status n n Lecturer L. Mark D.O. n Session Lecture 26 n n 1. Mental Status & History n Mental Status Exam Appearance Behavior Speech Mood Cognition. n Glasgow Coma Scale GCS n Score 3 Coma to 15 Normal . n Components E ye opening V erbal response M otor response. EVM-456 . n n 2. Cranial Nerve Examination n CN I Olfactory n Test Smell Coffee Cloves one nostril at a time. n Deficit Anosmia. DDx COVID-19 Kallmann Syndrome Trauma . n n CN II Optic n Visual Acuity Snellen Chart 20/20 . n Visual Fields Confrontation test Static finger wiggle . detects Hemianopsia. n Fundoscopy n Red Reflex Orange glow. n Optic Disc Check for Papilledema blurred margins or Cupping Glaucoma . n n CN III IV VI Oculomotor Trochlear Abducens n Pupils CN III n Direct Constriction in illuminated eye. n Consensual Constriction in opposite eye. n Near Triad Convergence Constriction Accommodation. n EOM H-Pattern. n CN VI Deficit Eye cannot look out Lateral deviation failure . n CN III Deficit Eye is Down and Out + Ptosis + Dilated Pupil. n n CN V Trigeminal n Sensory V1 Opthalmic V2 Maxillary V3 Mandibular - Pinprick/Light touch. n Motor Muscles of Mastication Temporalis/Masseter clench . n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Senses touch → Efferent VII Blink . n n CN VII Facial n Motor Facial expression Smile Frown Puff cheeks Close eyes tight . n Central Lesion Stroke Spares forehead. n Peripheral Lesion Bell s Palsy Affects entire half of face Forehead + Lower . n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3. n n CN VIII Vestibulocochlear n Gross Hearing Whisper test / Finger rub. n Weber Test Tuning fork on vertex. n Lateralizes to BAD ear Conductive Loss. n Lateralizes to GOOD ear Sensorineural Loss. n Rinne Test Mastoid BC vs. Air AC . n AC > BC Normal or Sensorineural. n BC > AC Conductive Loss. n n CN IX X Glossopharyngeal Vagus n Palate Say Ah . Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. n Gag Reflex Sensory IX Motor X. n n CN XI Accessory n Motor Shrug shoulders Trapezius Turn head SCM . n n CN XII Hypoglossal n Motor Protrude tongue. Tongue deviates TOWARDS lesion Licks the lesion . Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status Neuroscience questions A patient presents with right-sided hearing loss. Weber test lateralizes to the RIGHT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the right. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 1 Weber lateralizing to the affected bad ear + Rinne BC > AC indicates a Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear. On physical exam a patient s tongue deviates to the LEFT when protruded. Which cranial nerve is lesioned? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN IX 1 The tongue deviates towards the side of the CN XII lesion weak side . Left deviation = Left CN XII lesion. When asking a patient to say Ah the uvula deviates to the RIGHT. This suggests a lesion of which cranial nerve? Right CN X Left CN X Right CN IX Left CN IX Right CN XII 1 The uvula deviates away from the CN X lesion pulled by the intact strong side . Right deviation = Left CN X lesion. Which component of the corneal reflex is carried by the Facial Nerve CN VII ? Sensory Afferent Pain Sensory Afferent Touch Motor Efferent Blink Lacrimation only None 2 CN V1 provides the sensory Afferent limb. CN VII provides the Motor Efferent limb to close the eyelid Orbicularis Oculi . A patient has a dilated right pupil that is unresponsive to light and the right eye is positioned down and out . Which cranial nerve is affected? CN II CN III CN IV CN VI CN VII 1 These are classic signs of a CN III Oculomotor Palsy Ptosis Mydriasis dilation and Down-and-Out deviation. Tongue Deviation Direction Towards the Lesion CN XII Neurology Uvula Deviation Direction Away from the Lesion CN X Neurology Corneal Reflex Afferent Limb CN V1 Ophthalmic Neurology Corneal Reflex Efferent Limb CN VII Facial Neurology Rinne Test Conductive Loss Result BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction Ent CN III Palsy Signs Down and Out eye Ptosis Dilated Pupil Neurology Glasgow Coma Scale Components Eyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 Emergency Test for CN XI Shoulder Shrug Trapezius & Head Turn SCM Neurology CN VII Test Facial Expressions Smile Frown Puff Cheeks Neurology Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural To the GOOD ear Ent AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves BandB Neurology > The Brainstem > Cranial Nerves Tongue Deviation CN XII Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARDS the side of the lesion Licks the lesion . Uvula Deviation CN X Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from the side of the lesion pulled by the strong side . Corneal Reflex Afferent CN V1 Sensation . n Efferent CN VII Blink . Rinne Interpretation BC > AC = Conductive Hearing Loss Abnormal . n AC > BC = Normal or Sensorineural . GCS Scoring EVM-456 . nEyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 . nMax 15 Min 3."}, {"id": "l27", "title": "Lecture 27: OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l27 Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA n n Lecturer T. Li D.O. n Session Lecture 27 n n 1. Counterstrain CS Overview n Definition Indirect Passive technique. Fold and Hold . n Mechanism Shortens muscle to silence proprioceptive feedback Muscle Spindles/Gamma Gain . n Safety Very gentle. Good for acute pain. n Reaction 30-50% experience soreness for 1-2 days Warn patient! . n n 2. Steps of Counterstrain n1. Find Point Locate tenderpoint. n2. Scale Call it 10/10 . n3. Position Move to position of ease reduce to 0/10 or $ le$ 3/10 . n4. Hold 90 Seconds . n5. Return Passive return to neutral Slowly! . n6. Reassess Check tenderness again. n n 3. Pelvic Counterstrain Points n Iliacus Deep in iliac fossa. n Tx Flexion + External Rotation Frog Leg . n Psoas Medial to ASIS. n Tx F-S T -R A Flex Sidebend Toward Rotate Away . n Inguinal Lateral to pubic tubercle. n Tx F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Internal Rotate . n Low Ilium Superior pubic ramus. n Tx Flexion. n n 4. Innominate HVLA n Diagnosis Reminder n Anterior Rotation ASIS Low PSIS High. n Posterior Rotation ASIS High PSIS Low. n Superior Shear All landmarks high. n HVLA Setup n Post. Rotation Extend the hip to rotate anteriorly . n Ant. Rotation Flex the hip to rotate posteriorly . n Superior Shear Traction leg Abduct + IR + Pull . n n 5. Clinical Correlates n correlate n Counterstrain Contraindications n Absolute No Somatic Dysfunction No Consent Lack of Cooperation. n Relative Severe Osteoporosis Acute Illness Instability. n Note Recent trauma or fracture would depend on location but Osteoporosis is the classic relative contraindication. n Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA Neuroscience questions A patient feels soreness the day after a counterstrain treatment. How should this be managed? Immediate ER referral Repeat the treatment immediately Reassurance and hydration Normal reaction Prescribe Opioids Apply Heat only 2 Post-treatment soreness occurs in 30-50% of patients and lasts 1-2 days. It is a normal reaction due to metabolic release. Reassurance and water are key. What is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to Counterstrain? Osteoporosis Acute Pain Pregnancy Refusal of treatment No Consent Pediatric patient 3 Lack of consent and absence of somatic dysfunction are the main Absolute contraindications. Osteoporosis is a Relative contraindication. For a Psoas tenderpoint medial to ASIS what is the correct treatment position? Extension Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Toward Rotate Away Extension only Flexion only 2 Psoas = F-ST-RA . Flexion Sidebend Toward the point Rotate Away from the point. Which finding suggests a Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Shear only 1 Posterior rotation pulls the ASIS up High and pushes the PSIS down Low . What is the goal pain score reduction during Counterstrain positioning? Reduce by 10% Reduce to 5/10 Reduce to 7/10 Reduce to 0/10 or at least <= 3/10 Increase pain to provoke release 3 The goal is 100% reduction 0/10 but any reduction to 3/10 or less is considered acceptable to proceed with the hold. Psoas Counterstrain Setup F-ST-RA Flex SB Toward Rot Away OMM Iliacus Counterstrain Setup Frog Leg Flex + External Rotation OMM Inguinal Counterstrain Setup F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Int Rotate OMM Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds OMM Counterstrain Mechanism Silent Muscle Spindles Gamma Gain OMM Anterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS Low PSIS High OMM Posterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS High PSIS Low OMM Absolute Contraindication to CS No Consent / No Dysfunction OMM Relative Contraindication to CS Osteoporosis / Severe Illness OMM Post-CS Soreness Duration 1-2 Days Normal OMM BandB Musculoskeletal > Spine FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Anatomy Psoas Counterstrain Position F-S T -R A n F lexed S idebent T oward R otated A way. Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds is the standard hold time for Counterstrain. Inguinal Counterstrain F-ADD-IR n F lexed ADD ucted I nternally R otated. Common crossing legs position . Anterior Innominate Findings ASIS Lower PSIS Higher on the affected side. Posterior Innominate Findings ASIS Higher PSIS Lower on the affected side."}, {"id": "l28", "title": "Lecture 28: Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l28 Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 28 n n 1. Frontal Lobe & Executive Function n Functions Planning Inhibition Personality Filter Social norms . n Pathology n Frontotemporal Dementia Pick s Disease n Gene MAPT mutation Tau protein . n Symptoms Disinhibition Apathy Personality changes e.g. gambling rude comments early onset 45-65y . n Classic Case Phineas Gage Iron rod through frontal lobe . n Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Controls biological/voluntary saccades rapid eye movements to contralateral side. n n 2. Parietal Lobe & Attention n Right Parietal Lobe Non-Dominant n Function Spatial attention to BOTH sides of the world. n Lesion Right MCA Stroke Hemispatial Neglect . Patient interacts only with the Right side eats half plate draws half clock . n Left Parietal Lobe Dominant - Angular Gyrus n Lesion Gerstmann Syndrome . n Tetrad n 1. Agraphia Can t write . n 2. Acalculia Can t do math . n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t ID fingers . n 4. Left-Right Disorientation . n n 3. Temporal Lobe & Recognition n Fusiform Face Area FFA Inferior temporal gyrus. n Function Facial recognition. n Lesion Prosopagnosia Face Blindness . n Medial Temporal Lobe MTL Hippocampus/Memory. n Alzheimer s Tau tangles Amyloid plaques. Atrophy of MTL. n n 4. Insula & Cingulate n Insula Buried deep in lateral sulcus. n Functions Gustatory Taste Interoception feeling inside body Disgust Addiction/Craving Urge to smoke . n Stimulation Elicits taste sensations or disgust . n Cingulate Gyrus Part of Limbic System. n Cingulotomy Surgical lesioning used for intractable Chronic Pain or severe OCD . n n 5. Histology Highlights n Betz Cells Giant pyramidal cells in Layer 5 of Primary Motor Cortex Output to Corticospinal tract . n Cytoarchitecture Neocortex has 6 layers. Layer 4 = Input Sensory Layer 5 = Output Motor . Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old man presents with significant personality changes. He has become sexually inappropriate with strangers and has lost his life savings gambling. He has no memory loss. Genetic testing reveals a MAPT mutation. What is the diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia Parkinson s Disease 1 The combination of Disinhibition gambling inappropriate behavior + Personality change + MAPT mutation + Early onset 55y points to Frontotemporal Dementia . A patient cannot distinguish their left hand from their right hand and has lost the ability to perform simple addition. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Parietal Lobe Left Inferior Parietal Lobe Angular Gyrus Right Temporal Lobe Left Frontal Lobe Occipital Lobe 1 This is Gerstmann Syndrome Acalculia L-R confusion . The lesion is in the dominant usually Left Angular Gyrus Inferior Parietal Lobule . Electrical stimulation of which brain region is most likely to elicit a sensation of bad taste or disgust? Primary Motor Cortex Fusiform Gyrus Insular Cortex Broca s Area Wernicke s Area 2 The Insula processes Gustatory taste and visceral information including feelings of disgust . A patient recovering from a stroke combs only the right side of their hair and eats only food on the right side of their tray. Which artery was likely occluded? Left MCA Right MCA Left ACA Right ACA Basilar Artery 1 This is Hemispatial Neglect ignoring the Left side . The lesion is in the Right Parietal Lobe supplied by the Right MCA . Damage to Frontal Area 8 would most likely result in a deficit of Language comprehension Face recognition Voluntary saccadic eye movements Hearing Fine motor control of the hand 2 Frontal Area 8 corresponds to the Frontal Eye Fields which control voluntary rapid eye movements saccades . Role of Insula Taste Gustatory & Disgust / Interoception Cortex Lesion causing Hemispatial Neglect Right Posterior Parietal Lobe Right MCA Neurology Lesion causing Gerstmann Syndrome Left Angular Gyrus Dominant Parietal Neurology Symptoms of Gerstmann Syndrome Agraphia Acalculia Finger Agnosia L-R Disorientation Neurology Gene associated with Frontotemporal Dementia MAPT Tau protein Pathology Symptoms of Frontotemporal Dementia Disinhibition Apathy Personality Change Pathology Function of Fusiform Face Area Facial Recognition Lesion = Prosopagnosia Cortex Function of Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Voluntary Saccades Physiology Layer of Neocortex with Betz Cells Layer 5 Output to Spinal Cord Histology Cingulotomy is a treatment for... Chronic Pain or OCD Psychiatry AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebral Cortex FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Lobes BandB Neurology > Cerebrum Gerstmann Syndrome Tetrad Lesion of Left Angular Gyrus . n1. Agraphia n2. Acalculia n3. Finger Agnosia n4. L-R Disorientation Hemispatial Neglect Lesion of Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant . nPatient neglects the Left side of the world. Frontotemporal Dementia FTD Behavioral variant. Disinhibition Apathy Personality change. n MAPT gene Tau . Insula Functions Taste Disgust Interoception Addiction Cravings Smoking . Prosopagnosia Face blindness. Lesion of Fusiform Face Area Temporal Lobe ."}, {"id": "l29", "title": "Lecture 29: Neocortical Development", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l29 Lecture 29 Neocortical Development n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 29 n n 1. The 3 P s of Brain Development n1. Production Proliferation Making neurons. n2. Placement Migration Moving neurons to correct layers. n3. Performance Synaptic function. n n 2. Production Proliferation n Location Ventricular Zone VZ . n Mechanism Radial Glial cells divide to produce neurons. n Pathology n Primary Microcephaly Small brain . Reduced proliferation. n Genes ASPM Abnormal Spindle-like Microcephaly Citron Kinase . n Hemi-megalencephaly One hemisphere is too large. excess proliferation. Risk of Epilepsy . n n 3. Placement Migration n Pattern Inside-Out . Oldest neurons are deep Layer 6 Youngest are superficial Layer 2 . n Guidance Radial Glia fibers guide neurons. Reelin secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in Layer 1 tells them when to stop. n Pathology neuronal migration disorders n Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH Neurons fail to leave VZ. Nodules lining ventricles. n Gene Filamin A . n Subcortical Band Heterotopia Double Cortex Neurons stuck in white matter Intermediate Zone . n Genes DCX LIS1 . n Lissencephaly Smooth Brain No gyri Agyria . n Gene Reelin mutations. n Molecular Layer Heterotopia Neurons migrate too far past Layer 2 into Layer 1 . Associated with Dyslexia and Epilepsy . n n 4. Gyrification n Timeline Large fissures Sylvian form first 14w . Frontal gyri form last. Lecture 29 Neocortical Development Neuroscience questions A newborn MRI shows a smooth brain surface with no gyri or sulci. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hemi-megalencephaly Lissencephaly Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Double Cortex Syndrome Porencephaly 1 Lissencephaly literally means Smooth Brain . It is a severe migration disorder where gyri fail to form Agyria . Which cell type serves as the scaffold for neuronal migration in the developing neocortex? Astrocytes Oligodendrocytes Radial Glia Microglia Schwann Cells 2 Radial Glia extend processes from the ventricle to the pial surface acting as train tracks for migrating neurons. An MRI reveals nodules of gray matter lining the lateral ventricles. Which gene mutation is most strongly associated with this finding? Reelin Filamin A DCX ASPM MAPT 1 This describes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH . The classic association is Filamin A an actin-binding protein needed for migration initiation . In the Inside-Out pattern of cortical development which layer is formed LAST? Layer 6 Layer 5 Layer 4 Layer 2 Subplate 3 Deep layers 6 form first. Superficial layers 2 form last. Layer 1 is the marginal zone and exists early but the cortical plate neurons fill in 6→2 . Molecular Layer Heterotopia neurons migrating too far is often found in the brains of patients with Parkinson s Disease Dyslexia ALS Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis 1 Small clusters of neurons in Layer 1 Molecular Layer Heterotopia are seen in Dyslexia and Epilepsy . Inside-Out Migration meaning Deep layers 6 formed first Superficial 2 formed last Embryology Radial Glia function Progenitor cells + Scaffold for migration Embryology Gene for Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Filamin A Pathology Gene for Double Cortex Syndrome DCX / LIS1 Pathology Gene for Microcephaly ASPM / Citron Kinase Pathology Cells that secrete Reelin Cajal-Retzius Cells Layer 1 Embryology Lissencephaly means... Smooth Brain Agyria Pathology Molecular Layer Heterotopia association Dyslexia / Epilepsy Pathology Ventricular Zone function Site of neuronal proliferation Embryology 3 P s of Brain Dev Production Placement Performance Embryology AMBOSS Pediatrics > Neurology > Congenital anomalies FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Inside-Out Migration Neocortex forms Inside-Out . Layer 6 is born first. Layer 2 is born last. Double Cortex Syndrome Also called Subcortical Band Heterotopia . A band of gray matter is stuck in the white matter between the cortex and ventricle. Reelin Function Secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in the Marginal Zone Layer 1 . Acts as a Stop signal for migrating neurons. Primary Microcephaly Genes ASPM and Citron Kinase . Defects in mitosis/cytokinesis. Filamin A Mutation Causes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Neurons stuck at the starting line ."}, {"id": "l3", "title": "Lecture #3: Neuroanatomy: Meninges, BBB, CSF, Ventricles", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l3 Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles n n Lecturers Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Central Nervous System Barriers Slides 6-9 nThe CNS is an immunologically privileged site protected from the fluctuating chemical environment of the blood by three major barriers n n Blood-Brain Barrier BBB n Location Capillaries within the brain parenchyma. n Structure n Endothelial Cells The physical barrier connected by Tight Junctions Zona Occludens . n Pericytes Contractile cells that wrap around endothelial cells. n Astrocyte End-feet Induce and maintain the barrier properties. n Function Highly selective. n Permeable Lipid-soluble substances O2 CO2 Ethanol Steroid hormones . n Impermeable Polar/Large molecules Glucose Amino acids Dopamine require specific transporters. n n correlate n Parkinson s Disease & Levodopa Slide 12 n Problem Parkinson s is due to dopamine depletion. Dopamine is polar and cannot cross the BBB . n Solution Levodopa L-DOPA is a precursor that can cross the BBB via amino acid transporters. Once inside neurons it is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase. n n n Other Barriers n Blood-CSF Barrier Formed by Tight Junctions between the Epithelial Cells of the Choroid Plexus . n Arachnoid Barrier Avascular arachnoid epithelium seals the CSF from the extracellular fluid of the dura. n n Circumventricular Organs CVOs Slide 14-16 nSpecific brain regions that lack a BBB . Their capillaries are fenestrated to allow communication between the blood and brain for sensory or secretory functions. n Sensory CVOs n Area Postrema Located in the caudal medulla floor of 4th ventricle . Functions as the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone CTZ to detect toxins and induce vomiting . n Subfornical Organ SFO & OVLT Detect plasma osmolarity and Angiotensin II. n Secretory CVOs n Posterior Pituitary Neurohypophysis Releases Oxytocin and ADH. n Pineal Gland Secretes Melatonin. n Median Eminence Releases hypothalamic hormones. n n correlate n Levodopa-Induced Nausea Slide 16 nBecause the Area Postrema lacks a BBB circulating Levodopa can stimulate dopamine receptors there before entering the brain causing severe nausea/vomiting. n Prevention Co-administration of Carbidopa a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the BBB. n n n--- n n 2. The Meninges Slides 20-25 nThe brain is encased in three layers PAD n1. Dura Mater Hard Mother Tough outer layer. n Periosteal Layer Attached to the skull. n Meningeal Layer Inner layer. Separates from periosteal layer to form Dural Venous Sinuses and Dural Folds Falx Cerebri Tentorium Cerebelli . n2. Arachnoid Mater Spider Mother Wispy intermediate layer. n Arachnoid Granulations One-way valves that drain CSF into venous sinuses. n3. Pia Mater Soft Mother Delicate inner layer adherent to the brain surface follows gyri/sulci . n n Meningeal Spaces & Hemorrhages n Epidural Space Potential space between Skull and Dura. n Vessel Middle Meningeal Artery MMA . n Pathology Epidural Hematoma Trauma to Pterion → Lens-shaped bleed . n Subdural Space Potential space between Dura and Arachnoid. n Vessel Bridging Veins . n Pathology Subdural Hematoma Shearing forces/Shaken Baby → Crescent-shaped bleed . n Subarachnoid Space Real space between Arachnoid and Pia. n Contents CSF and major cerebral arteries/veins. n Pathology Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm → Thunderclap Headache . n n--- n n 3. Brain Herniations Slides 26-29 nIncreased Intracranial Pressure ICP can force brain tissue through compartments. n n correlate n A. Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation n Displacement Cingulate gyrus herniates under the Falx Cerebri . n Sequelae Can compress the Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA → Contralateral leg weakness. n n n correlate n B. Transtentorial Uncal Herniation n Displacement Medial Temporal Lobe Uncus herniates past the Tentorium Cerebelli . n Sequelae n Compresses CN III Oculomotor → Ipsilateral Blown Pupil Fixed & Dilated Down and Out eye. n Compresses Cerebral Peduncle → Contralateral Hemiparesis. n Compresses PCA → Cortical blindness. n n n correlate n C. Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation n Displacement Cerebellar Tonsils herniate through the Foramen Magnum . n Sequelae Compresses the Medulla Cardiorespiratory centers → Respiratory Arrest and death. n n n--- n n 4. Ventricular System & CSF Slides 35-43 n CSF Flow Pathway n1. Lateral Ventricles via Foramen of Monro → n2. Third Ventricle via Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius → n3. Fourth Ventricle via Foramina of Luschka Lateral and Magendie Medial → n4. Subarachnoid Space → n5. Arachnoid Granulations → n6. Superior Sagittal Sinus Venous blood . n n Hydrocephalus nAccumulation of CSF resulting in ventricular enlargement. n Non-Communicating Obstructive Blockage within the ventricles. n Common Cause Aqueductal Stenosis Slide 44 . Lateral & 3rd ventricles dilate; 4th remains normal. n Communicating Blockage of absorption Arachnoid Granulations or flow in subarachnoid space. All ventricles dilate. n Causes Meningitis Subarachnoid Hemorrhage scarring . n Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH Chronic communicating hydrocephalus in elderly. n Triad Wet Wobbly Wacky Urinary Incontinence Ataxia/Magnetic Gait Dementia . n Hydrocephalus Ex Vacuo Apparent enlargement of ventricles due to brain atrophy e.g. Alzheimer s Huntington s . ICP is normal . n n Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT Slide 26 n Thrombosis in dural sinuses e.g. Superior Sagittal Sinus . n Risk Factors Hypercoagulable states Pregnancy OCPs Infection Mastoiditis . n Signs Headache Papilledema Seizures due to venous congestion/infarction . Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles lecturer Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew Neuroanatomy session Lecture 3 prev l2 next l4 pdf pdfs/Lecture 3. V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf Neuroanatomy BBB & Meninges n Barriers n BBB n Tight Junctions Zona Occludens n Permeable Lipids O2 n Blocked Polar molecules Dopamine n CVOs No BBB n Area Postrema Vomiting n Posterior Pituitary n Meninges n Dura Outer n Epidural Space MMA n Subdural Space Bridging Veins n Arachnoid Middle n Subarachnoid Space CSF Arteries n Pia Inner n Herniations n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus Leg weak n Uncal Temporal lobe Blown Pupil n Tonsillar Cerebellum Resp Arrest n Ventricles n Flow Lat → 3rd → 4th n Hydrocephalus NPH Wet Wobbly Wacky questions 1. A patient presents with neurological symptoms secondary to increased blood-brain barrier BBB permeability after a traumatic brain injury. Damage to which of the following proteins essential in tight junctions would most likely likely disrupt BBB integrity? Connexin-43 Laminin Zona Occludens ZO-1 Integrin Fibronectin 2 From Slide 11 Zona Occludens ZO proteins along with Claudins and Occludins form the tight junctions between endothelial cells. Damage to these compromises the barrier. 2. Levodopa is treated for Parkinson s disease because unlike Dopamine it can cross the Blood-Brain Barrier. However it frequently causes nausea. This is due to its interaction with which brainstem structure that lacks a BBB? Nucleus Tractus Solitarius Pineal Gland Area Postrema Subfornical Organ Median Eminence 2 From Slide 18 The Area Postrema is a Circumventricular Organ CVO in the medulla vomiting center that lacks a BBB. It detects emetic agents like circulating Levodopa/Dopamine in the blood before they enter the protected CNS. 3. A 26-year-old man presents with a dilated unresponsive right pupil blown pupil after a head injury. CT shows a subdural hematoma with displacement of the medial temporal lobe. What is the specific type of herniation? Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation Transtentorial Uncal Herniation Central Herniation Upward Cerebellar Herniation 2 From Slide 32 The Uncas medial temporal lobe herniating past the tentorium compresses CN III causing an ipsilateral blown pupil. This is a classic sign of Uncal Herniation. 4. Which layer of the meninges is highly vascular and contains the Middle Meningeal Artery rupture of which leads to an Epidural Hematoma? Pia Mater Arachnoid Mater Meningeal Dura Periosteal Dura / Epidural Space interface Subarachnoid Space 3 The Middle Meningeal Artery runs in the Epidural space between the skull and the periosteal layer of the dura . Trauma to the pterion often ruptures this artery. 5. In the ventricular system CSF flows from the Third Ventricle to the Fourth Ventricle via which structure? Foramen of Monro Foramen of Magendie Foramen of Luschka Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius Central Canal 3 CSF flows Lateral → Monro → 3rd → Aqueduct of Sylvius → 4th → Luschka/Magendie. Which proteins form Tight Junctions in the BBB? Occludin Claudin Zona Occludens ZO Concept Which brain area lacks BBB and triggers vomiting? Area Postrema Anatomy Why is Levodopa used instead of Dopamine? Dopamine is polar blocked by BBB ; Levodopa crosses and converts. Pharm Structure separating cerebral hemispheres? Falx Cerebri Anatomy Structure separating cerebrum and cerebellum? Tentorium Cerebelli Anatomy Contents of Epidural Space? Middle Meningeal Artery MMA Clinical Contents of Subdural Space? Bridging Veins Clinical Sign of Uncal Herniation? Ipsilateral Blown Pupil CN III compression Pathology Sign of Subfalcine Herniation? Contralateral leg weakness ACA compression Pathology Triad for Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH ? Wet Incontinence Wobbly Ataxia Wacky Dementia Clinical BandB Neurology > Meninges & CSF BBB Rule Lipid-soluble substances O2 Alcohol cross freely. Polar substances Dopamine Glucose need transport. Tight Junctions are key. Parkinson s Rx Give Levodopa not Dopamine. Levodopa crosses BBB via AA transporter and converts to Dopamine inside. Median Eminence vs Area Postrema Both lack BBB CVOs . Area Postrema = Vomiting CTZ . Median Eminence = Hormones. Epidural vs Subdural Epidural = Lens shaped MMA Trauma . n Subdural = Crescent shaped Bridging Veins Elderly . Uncal Herniation Medial temporal lobe pushes on CN III . First sign = Ipsilateral Blown Pupil . NPH Triad Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus n1. Wet Incontinence n2. Wobbly Ataxia n3. Wacky Dementia"}, {"id": "l30", "title": "Lecture 30: Supraspinal Motor Systems (Cortex & Brainstem)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l30 Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 30 n n 1. Pyramidal System Upper Motor Neurons n Definition UMNs originate in the Cortex M1 Premotor SMA and project to LMNs in the Brainstem or Spinal Cord. n Corticospinal Tract CST n Lateral CST 85% Decussates at Medullary Pyramids. Controls Distal Limbs Fine motor . n Ventral CST 15% Descends ipsilaterally decussates in spinal cord. Controls Axial/Proximal muscles Posture . n Corticobulbar Tract n Controls Cranial Nerves Head/Neck . n Innervation Most CN nuclei receive Bilateral input. n Exceptions n CN VII Face Lower Face receives only Contralateral input. Upper Face is Bilateral . n CN XII Tongue Tongue deviates AWAY from the UMN lesion towards the weak side . n n 2. UMN vs. LMN Lesions n n| Feature | UMN Lesion Cortex/Stem | LMN Lesion Nerve/Root | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased | n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia | Hyporeflexia | n| Babinski | Present + Toe up | Absent Toe down | n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation | n| Fasciculations | No | Yes | n n 3. Brainstem Tracts & Posturing n Rubrospinal Tract Originates in Red Nucleus Midbrain . Flexor tones of upper limbs. n Vestibulospinal Tract Extensor tone Antigravity . n Decorticate Posture Mummy n Lesion ABOVE Red Nucleus e.g. Cerebral Hemispheres . n Presentation Arms Flexed Red nucleus intact Legs Extended. n Decerebrate Posture n Lesion BELOW Red Nucleus e.g. Pons/Midbrain . n Presentation Arms Extended Legs Extended Rubrospinal lost Vestibulospinal unopposed . n n 4. Specific Clinical Syndromes n Locked-In Syndrome n Lesion Pons Basilar Artery . n Effect Bilateral CST/Corticobulbar damage. Quadriplegia + Anarthria can t speak . n Spared Vertical eye movements/Blinking Midbrain/CN III spared . Consciousness intact. n Bell s Palsy LMN CN VII Paralysis of Entire Hemi-face Forehead + Chin . n Stroke UMN CN VII Paralysis of Lower Face Only Forehead spared . Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem Neurology questions A patient presents with right-sided facial weakness involving the forehead and chin. What is the most likely location of the lesion? Left Motor Cortex Face area Left Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve CN VII Left Facial Nucleus Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Weakness of the entire hemiface forehead + chin indicates an LMN lesion Bell s Palsy or CN VII injury on the same side . A comatose patient exhibits extension of both the upper and lower extremities when stimulated. This posturing suggests a lesion located Above the Red Nucleus Below the Red Nucleus e.g. Pons In the Spinal Cord In the Thalamus In the Cerebellum 1 Extension of all limbs is Decerebrate posturing which occurs with lesions below the Red Nucleus rubrospinal tract lost . Which of the following is a classic sign of an Upper Motor Neuron UMN lesion? Fasciculations Severe Atrophy Hyporeflexia Babinski Sign Flaccidity 3 Babinski sign extensor plantar response is a hallmark of UMN dysfunction. The others are LMN signs. A patient suffers a stroke affecting the Left Primary Motor Cortex. The tongue deviates to which side upon protrusion? Left Right Does not deviate Upward Downward 1 With a UMN lesion Left Cortex the tongue deviates AWAY from the lesion towards the weak side . So it points to the Right . n Wait let me double check my pearl... LMN = Toward. UMN = Away. Correct. So Left Cortex lesion → Right Genioglossus weak → Tongue points Right. Correct Answer is Right. Options Left/Right . Which tract is primarily responsible for voluntary fine motor control of the distal upper extremities? Ventral Corticospinal Tract Lateral Corticospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract Reticulospinal Tract Tectospinal Tract 1 The Lateral Corticospinal Tract controls distal muscles hands/fingers . The Ventral tract controls axial muscles. UMN Lesion Signs Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + Neurology LMN Lesion Signs Flaccidity Atrophy Fasciculations Neurology Decorticate Posture Arms Flexed Mummy . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. Neurology Decerebrate Posture Arms Extended. Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s LMN = Whole Face. Stoke UMN = Lower Face. Neurology Lateral Corticospinal Tract Decussation Medullary Pyramids Neuroanatomy Rubrospinal Tract Origin Red Nucleus Midbrain Neuroanatomy Locked-In Syndrome Lesion Pons Basilar Artery Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Pyramidal & Extrapyramidal FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Tracts UMN vs LMN Face UMN Stroke Spares forehead Look for wrinkles! . n LMN Bell s Affects whole face Forehead smooth . Decorticate vs Decerebrate De-COR-ticate Arms like C s Flexed to Cor e . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. n De-cere-brate Arms like E s Extended . Lesion Below Red Nucleus Worse prognosis . Tongue Deviation Lick your lesions. n LMN Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion. n UMN Tongue deviates AWAY from lesion. Locked-In Syndrome Pontine Stroke. Quadriplegia. Only eyes move Vertical . Consciousness preserved. Pyramidal Decussation Occurs at the Cervicomedullary Junction Lower Medulla ."}, {"id": "l31", "title": "Lecture 31: Basal Ganglia", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l31 Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 31 n n 1. Basal Ganglia Anatomy n Striatum Input Caudate + Putamen. Releases GABA . n Pallidum Output GPi Internal & GPe External . Releases GABA . n Subthalamic Nucleus STN Releases Glutamate Excitatory . n Substantia Nigra pars Compacta SNc Releases Dopamine . n n 2. The Pathways Albin-DeLong Model n1. Direct Pathway GO n Promotes movement. n Striatum inhibits GPi → Disinhibition of Thalamus. n D1 Receptors Dopamine excites this pathway. n2. Indirect Pathway NO-GO n Inhibits movement. n Striatum inhibits GPe → STN active → GPi active → Thalamus inhibited. n D2 Receptors Dopamine inhibits this pathway Releases the brake . n3. Hyperdirect Pathway Fast suppression Freeze! . Cortex → STN → GPi. n n 3. Parkinson s Disease Hypokinetic n Pathology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in SNc . Lewy Bodies $ alpha$-synuclein . n Symptoms TRAP n T remor Resting Pill-rolling . n R igidity Cogwheel . n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked face . n P ostural Instability. n History MPTP toxin caused acute Parkinsonism in drug users Frozen Addicts . n n 4. Huntington s Disease Hyperkinetic n Pathology Atrophy of Caudate Nucleus Boxy ventricles on MRI . loss of GABA/ACh neurons. n Genetics CAG trinucleotide repeat >40 . Autosomal Dominant . Anticipation worse in next gen . n Symptoms Chorea Dance-like Dementia Depression. Signs appear age 30-50. n n 5. Other Disorders n Hemiballismus n Lesion Subthalamic Nucleus STN lacunar stroke . n Symptoms Wild ballistic flailing of contralateral arm/leg. n Tardive Dyskinesia n Cause Chronic use of Antipsychotics D2 blockers . n Symptoms Repetitive lip smacking tongue rolling. Late onset. Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia Neurology questions A patient presents with wild uncontrollable flailing movements of the right arm and leg. An MRI would most likely show a lacunar stroke in which structure? Left Substantia Nigra Left Subthalamic Nucleus Right Subthalamic Nucleus Left Caudate Right Putamen 1 Hemiballismus contralateral flailing is caused by a lesion in the Subthalamic Nucleus STN . Right movements = Left STN. Which neurotransmitter is primarily released by the neurons of the Subthalamic Nucleus? GABA Dopamine Glutamate Acetylcholine Serotonin 2 The STN is the only intrinsic BG nucleus that is excitatory releasing Glutamate . All others Striatum GPe GPi SNr release GABA. In Huntington s Disease which brain structure undergoes the most significant atrophy leading to ventriculomegaly? Substantia Nigra Caudate Nucleus Thalamus Hippocampus Cerebellum 1 Huntington s causes atrophy of the Striatum specifically the Caudate Nucleus leading to boxy dilated ventricles. A 65-year-old male presents with a resting tremor masked facies and a shuffling gait. Histology would likely reveal Neurofibrillary Tangles Lewy Bodies Amyloid Plaques Negri Bodies Pick Bodies 1 This is Parkinson s Disease. The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein in the Substantia Nigra. Dopamine binding to D2 receptors in the striatum results in Excitation of the Indirect Pathway Inhibition of the Indirect Pathway Excitation of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Thalamus 1 D2 receptors are inhibitory . They are on the Indirect No-Go pathway. So Dopamine inhibits the Indirect pathway essentially turning off the stop signal which promotes movement. Hemiballismus Lesion Contralateral Subthalamic Nucleus STN Neurology Parkinson s Pathology Loss of Dopamine in SNc + Lewy Bodies Pathology Huntington s Genetic Defect CAG Repeat >40 on Ch 4 Genetics Direct Pathway Function Promotes Movement Disinhibits Thalamus Physiology Neurotransmitter of Striatum GABA Neuroscience Neurotransmitter of STN Glutamate Neuroscience Tardive Dyskinesia Cause Chronic D2 Blockers Antipsychotics Pharmacology MPTP causes... Parkinsonism Destroys SNc Pathology AMBOSS Neurology > Pathology > Degenerative diseases FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Movement Disorders Parkinson s TRAP T remor Resting n R igidity n A kinesia n P ostural Instability Huntington s CAG Mnemonic C audate lost n A Ch lost n G ABA lost n Also CAG Repeats Hemiballismus Lesion Damage to the Subthalamic Nucleus STN causes contralateral flailing. Dopamine Receptors D1 = Direct Go . n D2 = Indirect No-Go . nDopamine stimulates D1 and inhibits D2 both resulting in Movement . MPTP Toxin Destroys SNc neurons causing rapid permanent Parkinsonism. Synthetic heroin case ."}, {"id": "l32", "title": "Lecture 32: Cerebellum", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l32 Lecture 32 Cerebellum n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 32 n n 1. Neuroanatomy n Organization Cortex 3 layers covering Deep Nuclei. n Deep Nuclei Lateral to Medial D on t E at G reasy F ood . n D entate. n E mboliform. n G lobose. n F astigial. n Circuits n Inputs n Mossy Fibers From Spinal cord/Brainstem. Excitatory Glutamate . n Climbing Fibers From Inferior Olive . Excitatory Glutamate . n Processing n Purkinje Cells The sole output of the cerebellar cortex. Releases GABA Inhibitory onto Deep Nuclei. n Output n Deep Nuclei send final signal to Thalamus/Brainstem. n n 2. Functional Divisions & Pathology n n| Division | Anatomy | Function | Lesion Symptoms | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Vestibulo-cerebellum | Flocculonodular Lobe | Balance Eye movements | Vertigo Nystagmus Wide-based gait Truncal Ataxia . | n| Spino-cerebellum | Vermis & Paravermis | Posture Trunk/Limb Gait | Ataxic Gait Drunk walk . Alcoholism damages Anterior Lobe Vermis . | n| Cerebro-cerebellum | Lateral Hemispheres | Fine motor planning | Intention Tremor Dysmetria Dysdiadochokinesia . | n n 3. Clinical Syndromes n Essential Tremor n Most common movement disorder. Familial Autosomal Dominant . n Postural/Action Tremor worse when holding a cup . n Note Improves with Alcohol . Often self-medicated . n Intention Tremor n Cerebellar lesion. Tremor worsens as you approach target . n Resting Tremor n Basal Ganglia Parkinson s . n n 4. DANISH Mnemonic Cerebellar Signs n D ysdiadochokinesia Can t do rapid alternating movements . n A taxia. n N ystagmus. n I ntention Tremor. n S peech Slurred/Scanning . n H ypotonia. Lecture 32 Cerebellum Neurology questions A chronic alcoholic presents with a wide-based staggering gait but normal arm coordination. Which region of the cerebellum is most likely degenerated? Flocculonodular Lobe Anterior Lobe Vermis Lateral Hemisphere Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 1 Chronic alcoholism typically causes atrophy of the Anterior Lobe Vermis leading to Truncal Ataxia and gait issues while sparing the limbs Lateral Hemispheres . Which of the following describes the output of Purkinje cells? Excitatory Glutamate to Deep Nuclei Inhibitory GABA to Deep Nuclei Excitatory Glutamate to Thalamus Inhibitory GABA to Thalamus Excitatory Acetylcholine to Spinal Cord 1 Purkinje Cells provide Inhibitory GABA input to the Deep Cerebellar Nuclei modulating the final output. A patient cannot rapidly supinate and pronate their hand Dysdiadochokinesia on the left side. Where is the lesion? Right Cerebellar Hemisphere Left Cerebellar Hemisphere Vermis Right Motor Cortex Right Basal Ganglia 1 Cerebellar symptoms are Ipsilateral . Dysdiadochokinesia limb coordination maps to the Lateral Hemisphere . So Left hand issue = Left Cerebellar Hemisphere . A child presents with frequent falls nystagmus and vomiting. An MRI reveals a tumor in the roof of the 4th ventricle compressing the flocculonodular lobe. What is the most likely diagnosis? Glioblastoma Multiforme Meningioma Medulloblastoma Schwannoma Pituitary Adenoma 2 Medulloblastoma is a common pediatric tumor that often arises in the Vermis/Flocculonodular lobe causing truncal ataxia and hydrocephalus vomiting . Climbing fibers which provide powerful excitatory input to Purkinje cells originate from which structure? Spinal Cord Pontine Nuclei Inferior Olive Red Nucleus Vestibular Nuclei 2 Climbing Fibers originate exclusively from the Inferior Olive Medulla . Deep Cerebellar Nuclei Lat to Med Dentate Emboliform Globose Fastigial Neuroanatomy Purkinje Cell Neurotransmitter GABA Inhibitory Neuroscience Climbing Fibers Origin Inferior Olive Neuroanatomy Lesion causing Dysdiadochokinesia Ipsilateral Lateral Cerebellar Hemisphere Neurology Classic sign of Cerebrocerebellar lesion Intention Tremor Neurology Sign of Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk Walk Neurology Essential Tremor characteristics Action/Postural. Improves w/ Alcohol. Neurology DANISH meaning Dysdiadochokinesia Ataxia Nystagmus Intention Tremor Slurred Speech Hypotonia Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebellum FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cerebellum Deep Nuclei Mnemonic D on t E at G reasy F ood Lateral to Medial n D entate E mboliform G lobose F astigial. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral lesions affect Limbs fall Toward side of lesion . n Medial Vermis lesions affect Trunk Truncal Ataxia fall anywhere . Alcoholic Cerebellar Degeneration Chronic alcohol use specifically damages the Anterior Lobe Vermis causing a wide-based ataxic gait. Essential Tremor Action tremor eating/writing . Improves with Alcohol. Tx Beta-blockers. Output of Cerebellar Cortex Purkinje Cells are the ONLY output of the cortex. They are Inhibitory GABA ."}, {"id": "l33", "title": "Lecture 33: Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l33 Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 33 n n 1. Receptors & Physiology n $ alpha 1$ Gq Vasoconstriction Skin/Splanchnic Mydriasis Pupil Dilation Urinary Sphincter Contraction. n $ alpha 2$ Gi Presynaptic Inhibition Decreases NE release . n $ beta 1$ Gs Heart $ uparrow$ HR $ uparrow$ Contractility Kidney $ uparrow$ Renin . n $ beta 2$ Gs Bronchodilation Vasodilation Skeletal Muscle Uterine Relaxation Tocolysis . n $ beta 3$ Gs Bladder Detrusor Relaxation. n n 2. Direct-Acting Sympathomimetics n n| Drug | Receptors | Action | Clinical Use | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Epinephrine | $ alpha beta$ | Low Dose $ beta 2$ Hypotension . High Dose $ alpha 1$ Hyperstension . | Anaphylaxis DOC Cardiac Arrest. | n| Norepinephrine | $ alpha 1 = alpha 2 > beta 1$ | Potent Vasoconstriction. Reflex Bradycardia. | Septic Shock DOC . | n| Dopamine | D1 > $ beta 1$ > $ alpha 1$ | Low Renal Vasodilation. Med Inotropy. High Vasoconstriction. | Shock Renal sparing theoretically Heart Failure. | n| Dobutamine | $ beta 1 > beta 2$ | $ uparrow$ Inotropy > Chronotropy. | Heart Failure Decompensated Stress Testing. | n| Phenylephrine | $ alpha 1$ | Vasoconstriction. | Nasal Decongestant Hypotension Mydriasis. | n| Isoproterenol | $ beta 1 = beta 2$ | $ uparrow$ HR Vasodilation $ downarrow$ DBP . | Bradycardia Heart block Rarely used . | n n 3. Hemodynamics Key Concepts n Mean Arterial Pressure MAP $ text CO times text TPR $. n Pulse Pressure $ text Systolic - text Diastolic $. n Epinephrine Reversal Giving an $ alpha$-blocker before Epinephrine converts its pressor effect $ alpha 1$ to a depressor effect $ beta 2$ unmasked → Hypotension. n Reflex Bradycardia Pure $ alpha 1$ agonists Phenylephrine/Norepinephrine cause massive vasoconstriction → $ uparrow$ BP → Baroreceptors fire → Reflex $ downarrow$ HR. n n 4. Clinical Pearls n Anaphylaxis Epinephrine is the ONLY first-line drug. Prevents shock $ alpha 1$ opens airway $ beta 2$ stops mast cells. n Septic Shock Norepinephrine is first-line to restore MAP. n Tachyphylaxis Rapidly diminishing response to successive doses seen with Indirect agonists like Tyramine/Ephedrine less so with direct . Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists Neurology questions A patient in septic shock requires a vasopressor to maintain Mean Arterial Pressure MAP . Which agent is the first-line choice due to its potent alpha-1 and beta-1 activity with minimal beta-2 effects? Epinephrine Dopamine Norepinephrine Phenylephrine Isoproterenol 2 Norepinephrine is the Drug of Choice DOC for Septic Shock. It provides strong vasoconstriction alpha-1 and some inotropy beta-1 without the hypotension associated with beta-2 stimulation. A patient is given a drug that increases Systolic BP decreases Diastolic BP and increases Heart Rate. Which drug fits this profile? Phenylephrine Norepinephrine Isoproterenol Clonidine Propranolol 2 Isoproterenol is a non-selective Beta agonist Beta-1 and Beta-2 . Beta-1 increases SBP and HR. Beta-2 causes vasodilation dropping DBP. Phenylephrine is administered intravenously. What represents the expected cardiovascular response? Increased BP Increased HR Increased BP Decreased HR Decreased BP Increased HR Decreased BP Decreased HR No change in BP Increased HR 1 Phenylephrine is a pure Alpha-1 agonist. It increases TPR and BP. The body responds with Reflex Bradycardia Decreased HR . Which receptor subtype is primarily responsible for the bronchodilatory effects of Epinephrine used in anaphylaxis? Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Dopamine-1 3 Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation . Alpha-1 mediates the vasoconstriction that supports BP. Dobutamine is primarily used in the setting of Anaphylactic Shock Septic Shock Decompensated Heart Failure Hypertension Asthma 2 Dobutamine Beta-1 > Beta-2 is an inotrope used to increase cardiac output in Decompensated Heart Failure or cardiogenic shock. Epinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Pharmacology Norepinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 > Alpha-2 > Beta-1 NO Beta-2 Pharmacology Phenylephrine Receptor Profile Pure Alpha-1 Agonist Pharmacology Isoproterenol Receptor Profile Beta-1 = Beta-2 Non-selective Beta Pharmacology Dobutamine Use Heart Failure Inotropy Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Anaphylaxis Epinephrine Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Septic Shock Norepinephrine Pharmacology Reflex response to Phenylephrine Bradycardia Decreased HR Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Epinephrine Dosage Effects Low Dose = Beta-2 Dominant Vasodilation/Hypotension . n High Dose = Alpha-1 Dominant Vasoconstriction/Hypertension . Norepinephrine vs Epinephrine Norepinephrine has NO Beta-2 activity. It causes pure vasoconstriction and reflex bradycardia. Epinephrine has Beta-2 activity Bronchodilation . Dopamine Dosing Low Renal D1. n Medium Cardiac Beta-1. n High Pressor Alpha-1. Phenylephrine Reflex A pure Alpha-1 agonist Phenylephrine increases BP but causes Reflex Bradycardia lowers HR . Beta-1 Selectivity Dobutamine is a Beta-1 agonist used for Heart Failure to increase contractility without massively increasing Heart Rate Chronotropy ."}, {"id": "l34", "title": "Lecture 34: Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l34 Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 34 n n 1. Beta-2 Agonists Lungs & Uterus n SABA Short-Acting Albuterol Levalbuterol. n Use Acute Asthma/COPD exacerbation Rescue . n LABA Long-Acting Salmeterol Formoterol. n Use Chronic maintenance. Always combine with Steroids ICS in asthma to prevent mortality. n Tocolytics Terbutaline . n Use Relaxes uterine smooth muscle to delay preterm labor allows time for corticosteroids to mature fetal lungs . n n 2. Beta-3 Agonists Bladder n Mirabegron n Action Relaxes Detrusor muscle. n Use Overactive Bladder OAB . Alternative to Anticholinergics . n n 3. Alpha-2 Agonists Central Sympatholytics nReduces sympathetic outflow from the brainstem. n Clonidine n Uses Hypertension resistant Opioid Withdrawal ADHD. n Adverse Rebound Hypertension if stopped abruptly. dry mouth sedation. n Methyldopa n Use Hypertension in Pregnancy Safe for fetus . n Adverse Coombs+ Hemolytic Anemia Autoimmune Drug-induced Lupus. n n 4. Indirect & Mixed Acting n Amphetamines Adderall n Mechanism Displaces NE/DA from vesicles into synapse Reverse transport . n Use ADHD Narcolepsy. n Cocaine n Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT . n Effect Vasoconstriction Local Anesthetic + Pressor . n Ephedrine n Mechanism Mixed Direct Agonist + Indirect Release . n Use Hypotension during Anesthesia. n Pseudoephedrine n Use Nasal Decongestant. Precursor for Meth hence restricted . n n 5. MAO Inhibitors The Cheese Effect n Mechanism Inhibits Monoamine Oxidase enzyme that breaks down NE/DA/Serotonin . n Tyramine Interaction Tyramine found in Aged Cheese Wine Cured Meats releases stored NE. Commonly degraded by GI MAO. n Crisis If on MAO-I Tyramine is NOT degraded → Massive NE release → Hypertensive Crisis . Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting Neurology questions A pregnant woman presents with chronic hypertension. Which centrally acting alpha-2 agonist is considered safe and appropriate for management? Clonidine Methyldopa Lisinopril Losartan Propranolol 1 Methyldopa is the classic safe antihypertensive for pregnancy. ACE inhibitors Lisinopril and ARBs are teratogenic. A patient on a Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor MAOI for depression consumes a charcuterie board with aged cheese and wine. They present to the ER with a severe headache and BP of 220/120. mechanism is Direct Alpha-1 agonism by Tyramine Displacement of stored Norepinephrine by Tyramine Blockade of Norepinephrine reuptake Allergic reaction to wine Serotonin Syndrome 1 Tyramine acts as an indirect sympathomimetic by displacing stored NE from vesicles. Usually broken down by MAO but persists in MAO-I patients. Which drug works by relaxing the detrusor muscle via Beta-3 agonism to treat overactive bladder? Oxybutynin Mirabegron Bethanecol Tamsulosin Finasteride 1 Mirabegron is a Beta-3 agonist. Oxybutynin is an Anticholinergic which also works but via M3 blockade . Amphetamines exert their stimulant effect primarily by Directly activating Alpha-1 receptors Blocking the degradation of Acetylcholine Displacing Norepinephrine and Dopamine from vesicles into the synapse Blocking the reuptake of Serotonin only Directly activating Beta-2 receptors 2 Amphetamines enter the nerve terminal via uptake and then displace NE/DA from vesicles reversing the transporter to pump NE/DA OUT into the synapse. Salmeterol is classified as a Short-Acting Beta Agonist SABA Long-Acting Beta Agonist LABA Alpha-1 Antagonist Muscarinic Antagonist Corticosteroid 1 Salmeterol is a LABA used for asthma maintenance. Albuterol is a SABA. Methyldopa Indication Hypertension in Pregnancy Pharmacology Methyldopa Adverse Effect Coombs + Hemolytic Anemia Pharmacology Clonidine Mechanism Central Alpha-2 Agonist Decreases Sympathetic Outflow Pharmacology Mirabegron Target Beta-3 Receptor Bladder Relaxation Pharmacology Terbutaline Use Tocolysis Delay Labor Pharmacology Tyramine Mechanism Displaces Stored NE Indirect Agonist Pharmacology Cocaine Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT Pharmacology Albuterol vs Salmeterol Albuterol = SABA Acute . Salmeterol = LABA Maintenance . Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Methyldopa in Pregnancy Methyldopa is the classic choice for Hypertension in Pregnancy Preeclampsia prophylaxis . Watch for Hemolytic Anemia . Clonidine Rebound Never stop Clonidine abruptly. Causes massive Rebound Hypertension due to upregulated receptors. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS reuptake NET blocker . n Amphetamine REVERSES transporter Displaces NE . Both increase synaptic NE . Cheese Reaction MAO Inhibitors + Tyramine Wine/Cheese = Hypertensive Crisis. Tyramine displaces NE . Terbutaline Tocolysis Beta-2 agonist used to Stop Labor Relax Uterus . Turbulence delays the flight birth ."}, {"id": "l35", "title": "Lecture 35: Cranial Nerves I, II, III, IV, VI (Eye Movements)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l35 Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 35 n n 1. CN I Olfactory & CN II Optic n CN I Visceral Sensory. Bypasses Thalamus. n Pathology Anosmia Early sign of Parkinson s - Lewy bodies in Olfactory Bulb . n CN II Special Sensory. n Reflex Pupillary Light Reflex Afferent II → Pretectal Area → Bilateral Edinger-Westphal → Efferent III . n n 2. CN III Oculomotor n Motor Medial Superior Inferior Recti; Inferior Oblique; Levator Palpebrae. n Parasympathetic Edinger-Westphal Nucleus → Pupil Constriction Miosis & Lens Accommodation. n Lesion CN III Palsy n Down and Out eye Unopposed SO4/LR6 . n Ptosis Droopy eyelid . n Mydriasis Blown pupil - loss of parasympathetics . n Note The Superior Rectus and Levator are innervated by the Contralateral nucleus but the nerve fibers cross inside the complex so a NERVE lesion affects the ipsilateral eye. n n 3. CN IV Trochlear n Motor Superior Oblique Depresses/Intorts eye . n Unique Features n 1. Only CN to exit Dorsally . n 2. Only CN to Decussate Completely Right Nucleus → Left Superior Oblique . n Lesion n Vertical Diplopia Worse when looking down e.g. stairs/reading . n Head Tilt Patient tilts head AWAY from the affected side to compensate/align eyes. n n 4. CN VI Abducens & Gaze Control n Motor Lateral Rectus Abducts eye . n Horizontal Gaze Circuit n To look RIGHT Right PPRF → Right Abducens Nucleus . n Right Abducens Nucleus does TWO things n 1. Contracts Ring Lateral Rectus Abducts Right Eye . n 2. Sends signal via MLF Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus to Left Oculomotor Nucleus → Contracts Left Medial Rectus Adducts Left Eye . n Pathology n CN VI Palsy Esotropia Eye turns in . n INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia n Lesion of MLF . n Ipsilateral eye cannot Adduct Medial Rectus palsy . n Contralateral eye has Nystagmus. n Convergence is SPARED because CN III is intact . n Classic Cause Multiple Sclerosis Bilateral INO . n n 5. Summary Table n n| Nerve | Muscles | Lesion Sign | n| --- | --- | --- | n| CN III | All others + Levator + Pupil | Down & Out Ptosis Blown Pupil | n| CN IV | Superior Oblique | Head tilt AWAY Vertical Diplopia stairs | n| CN VI | Lateral Rectus | Esotropia Eye turns In | Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements Neurology questions A patient presents with vertical diplopia that worsens when walking down stairs. They are observed tilting their head to the left. Which cranial nerve is likely injured? Left CN IV Right CN IV Left CN III Right CN VI Left CN VI 2 Vertical diplopia stairs problem suggests CN IV Trochlear palsy. The head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Left tilt = Right CN IV lesion. A patient cannot look to the right with the right eye. When attempting to look right the left eye also fails to adduct. Convergence is intact. Where is the lesion? Right Abducens Nerve Right Abducens Nucleus Right MLF Left MLF Right PPRF 2 This is a Right Abducens Nucleus lesion. It knocks out the Right LR Can t look right AND the signal to the Left MR via MLF causing a gaze palsy to that side. A simple nerve lesion would only affect the Right LR . In Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia INO the lesion is located in the Abducens Nerve Oculomotor Nerve Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Superior Colliculus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus 2 INO is caused by damage to the MLF disconnecting the Abducens nucleus from the contralateral Oculomotor nucleus. Which cranial nerve contains the parasympathetic fibers responsible for pupillary constriction? Optic II Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Trigeminal V Abducens VI 1 CN III Oculomotor carries parasympathetics from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion for miosis and accommodation. Anosmia is an early and often overlooked clinical sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease ALS Multiple Sclerosis 1 Parkinson s Disease is strongly associated with early Anosmia due to Lewy Body deposition in the olfactory bulb. Eye Muscles Innervation Mnemonic SO4 LR6 All Others 3 Neuroanatomy CN IV Exit Point Dorsal Brainstem Midbrain Neuroanatomy CN III Palsy Signs Down & Out Ptosis Mydriasis Neurology INO Lesion Location Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Neurology Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Away from Lesion Neurology Vertical Diplopia Cause CN IV Palsy Neurology Parasympathetic Nucleus for Eye Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Neuroanatomy Abducens Nucleus Function Abducts Ipsilateral Eye + Adducts Contralateral Eye via MLF Neurophysiology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves SO4 LR6 All The Rest 3 S uperior O blique = CN 4 . n L ateral R ectus = CN 6 . nAll other eye muscles = CN 3 . CN IV Trochlear Trivia The only cranial nerve to exit from the BACK Dorsal of the brainstem. Also the only one to cross completely! INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia Lesion of MLF . Eye lags when looking towards nose Adduction deficit . The other eye shakes Nystagmus . Seen in MS . Parkinson s Early Sign Anosmia Loss of smell is often the FIRST sign of Parkinson s appearing years before motor symptoms. Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Right CN IV palsy = Left Head Tilt ."}, {"id": "l36", "title": "Lecture 36: Cranial Nerves V, VII-XII (Brainstem Nuclei)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l36 Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 36 n n 1. CN V Trigeminal n Sensory Face n Mesencephalic Nucleus Proprioception Jaw jerk . n Principal Pontine Nucleus Discriminative Touch Face . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain & Temperature Face . Extends down to C-spine . n Motor V3 Muscles of Mastication Temporalis Masseter Pterygoids . n Clinical n Trigeminal Neuralgia Suicide disease . Sharp shooting pain. n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Ophthalmic → Efferent VII Facial . n n 2. CN VII Facial n Motor Muscles of Facial Expression Stapedius dampens sound Stylohyoid Digastric Post . n Parasympathetic Superior Salivatory Nucleus → Lacrimal Tears Submandibular/Sublingual Saliva . n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3 of Tongue. n Clinical n Bell s Palsy LMN Ipsilateral paralysis of ENTIRE face Forehead involved . Hyperacusis Loss of taste. n UMN Lesion Stroke Contralateral paralysis of LOWER face only Forehead Spared due to bilateral innervation . n n 3. CN IX Glossopharyngeal & CN X Vagus n CN IX n Sensory Post 1/3 Tongue Taste/Feel Carotid Body/Sinus. n Motor Stylopharyngeus. n Reflex Gag Reflex Afferent . n CN X n Motor Nucleus Ambiguus → Pharynx/Larynx/Palate. n Visceral Dorsal Motor Nucleus → GI/Heart Parasymp . n Reflex Gag Reflex Efferent . n Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. Hoarseness. n n 4. CN XI Accessory & CN XII Hypoglossal n CN XI Trapezius Shrug SCM Turn head . Lesion Shoulder droop cannot turn head away from lesion. n CN XII Tongue muscles. Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion Lick your wounds . n n 5. Brainstem Syndromes n Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . n Nuclei n Ambiguus Dysphagia Hoarseness. n Vestibular Vertigo Nystagmus. n Spinal V Ipsilateral Facial Pain/Temp loss. n Spinothalamic Contralateral Body Pain/Temp loss. n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome. n Medial Medullary Syndrome n Artery ASA Anterior Spinal Artery . n Nuclei n Hypoglossal XII Tongue deviates to lesion. n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis. n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral Proprioception/Vibration loss. Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei Neurology questions A patient presents with sudden onset of right-sided facial drooping involving the forehead and the lower face. They also complain that sounds seem abnormally loud in the right ear. The most likely location of the lesion is Left Motor Cortex Right Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve Left Facial Nerve Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Right Facial Nerve LMN lesion causes Bell s Palsy Whole hemiface paralysis and Hyperacusis loss of Stapedius . A patient sticks out their tongue and it deviates to the LEFT. There is atrophy of the left side of the tongue. Which nerve is injured? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN VII 1 The tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . Left deviation + Atrophy = Left CN XII LMN lesion. In Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg which nucleus is responsible for the characteristic dysphagia and hoarseness? Solitary Nucleus Nucleus Ambiguus Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus Hypoglossal Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus 1 Nucleus Ambiguus provides motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx via IX and X . Damage causes dysphagia and hoarseness. Loss of the afferent limb of the corneal reflex would result from damage to which cranial nerve? CN II CN III CN V1 CN VII CN VIII 2 The afferent sensing limb of the corneal reflex is CN V1 Ophthalmic division of Trigeminal . The efferent blinking is CN VII. A patient has loss of pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of the face and the LEFT side of the body. This crossed sensory deficit suggests a lesion in the Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Right Medial Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Thalamus 0 Crossed findings Ipsi Face Contra Body are a hallmark of brainstem lesions. Right Lateral Medulla hits Right Spinal V Face and Right Spinothalamic carrying Left Body info . Corneal Reflex Limbs In V1. Out VII. Neurology Gag Reflex Limbs In IX. Out X. Neurology Tongue Deviation Rule Deviates TOWARD the lesion Lick your wounds Neurology Uvula Deviation Rule Deviates AWAY from the lesion Neurology Nucleus Ambiguus Function Motor to Pharynx/Larynx Swallowing/Speech - CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s = Whole Face. Stroke = Lower Face only Forehead spared . Neurology Hyperacusis Nerve CN VII Stapedius muscle paralysis Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Bell s LMN Can NOT wrinkle forehead Whole face paralyzed . n Stroke UMN CAN wrinkle forehead Forehead spared . Uvula vs Tongue Uvula CN X Deviates AWAY from lesion. n Tongue CN XII Deviates TOWARD lesion. Corneal Reflex In CN V1 Feels the cotton . n Out CN VII Blinks the eye . Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg PICA stroke. Specific sign Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus + Crossed Pain/Temp Face same side Body opposite side . Gag Reflex In CN IX Glossopharyngeal . n Out CN X Vagus ."}, {"id": "l37", "title": "Lecture 37: Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l37 Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 37 n n 1. Motor System n Grading 0-5 Scale n 0 No contraction. n 1 Trace / Flicker. n 2 Active movement with gravity eliminated horizontal . n 3 Active movement against gravity only . n 4 Active movement against resistance but weak . n 5 Normal strength. n Key Roots n C5/6 Biceps Deltoid Brachioradialis. n C7 Triceps Wrist Extension. n C8/T1 Hand intrinsics Grip . n L2/3/4 Iliopsoas Hip Flex Quads Knee Ext . n L5 Tibialis Ant Dorsiflex/Heel walk . n S1 Gastroc Plantarflex/Toe walk . n n 2. Reflexes DTRs n Grading 0-4 Scale n 0 Absent. n 2 Normal. n 4 Hyperactive with Clonus UMN . n Roots n Biceps C5 C6. n Brachioradialis C6. n Triceps C7. n Patellar L4. n Achilles S1. n Reinforcement Jendrassik Maneuver hook fingers and pull distracts patient to elicit hard-to-find reflexes. n n 3. UMN vs LMN Findings n n| Feature | UMN Upper Motor Neuron | LMN Lower Motor Neuron | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased | n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia +Clonus | Hyporeflexia / Absent | n| Babinski | Positive Upgoing toe | Negative Downgoing | n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation | n| Fasciculations | Absent | Present | n n 4. Coordination & Gait Cerebellum n Dysdiadochokinesis Inability to perform Rapid Alternating Movements RAM . n Dysmetria Overshooting/Undershooting Finger-to-Nose . n Ataxia Unsteady gait. Tandem Walking Heel-to-toe brings out subtle ataxia. n Romberg Test n Patient stands feet together eyes CLOSED . n Positive Patient sways/falls. n Meaning Loss of Proprioception Dorsal Columns . Note Cerebellar ataxia occurs with eyes OPEN too. n n 5. Other Signs n Pronator Drift Eyes closed arms out palms up. If one arm pronates and drops → Contralateral UMN Corticospinal weakness. n Primitive Reflexes Grasp Rooting Snout. Seen in Frontal Lobe lesions or babies . Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes Neurology questions A patient presents with weakness in the right arm. On examination they have increased tone spasticity and hyperactive reflexes 3+ in the right biceps and triceps. There is no atrophy. This pattern suggests a lesion in the Lower Motor Neuron e.g. Brachial Plexus Upper Motor Neuron e.g. Corticospinal Tract Neuromuscular Junction Cerebellum Muscle fiber directly 1 UMN signs include Spasticity Hyperreflexia and preserved muscle bulk no atrophy . LMN signs would be flaccidity and atrophy. Which of the following describes muscle strength Grade 3/5? Active movement against gravity and resistance Active movement against gravity only Active movement with gravity eliminated Flicker of contraction only Normal strength 1 Grade 3 is movement against gravity but NOT resistance. Grade 2 is gravity eliminated. Grade 4 is against resistance. A positive Romberg test swaying significantly only when eyes are closed primarily indicates dysfunction of The Cerebellum The Motor Cortex Proprioception Dorsal Columns The Basal Ganglia The Thalamus 2 Romberg tests Proprioception . If vision eyes open compensates but balance fails when eyes close it s a sensory ataxia Dorsal Columns . The Achilles tendon reflex is mediated primarily by which spinal nerve root? L3 L4 L5 S1 S2 3 Achilles reflex is S1 . Patellar is L4. Dysdiadochokinesis inability to perform rapid alternating movements is a hallmark sign of damage to the Cerebellum Frontal Lobe Spinal Cord Basal Ganglia Cranial Nerves 0 Incoordination of rapid movements RAM is a classic Cerebellar sign. S1 Reflex Achilles Ankle Jerk Neurology L4 Reflex Patellar Knee Jerk Neurology C7 Reflex Triceps Neurology C5/C6 Reflex Biceps / Brachioradialis Neurology Grade 3 Strength Against Gravity Only Neurology Grade 2 Strength Gravity Eliminated Neurology Jendrassik Maneuver Use Reinforce/Distract for Reflexes Neurology Positive Romberg Meaning Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Column Proprioception Loss Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Neurological Examination FirstAid Neurology Reflex Roots Mnemonic S1 2 Buckle my shoe - Ankle . n L3 4 Kick the door - Knee . n C5 6 Pick up sticks - Biceps . n C7 8 Lay them straight - Triceps . Romberg is Sensory A positive Romberg falling only when eyes CLOSED indicates a Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Columns/Proprioception NOT Cerebellum. Pronator Drift Highly specific sign for subtle UMN Weakness . The supinator muscles are weaker than pronators in UMN lesions. Babinski Sign Stroke sole of foot. Normal = Toes curl DOWN. UMN Lesion = Big toe goes UP Dorsiflex + fanning . Jendrassik Maneuver Can t get a knee jerk? Have them hook hands and PULL. Distraction unmasks the reflex."}, {"id": "l38", "title": "Lecture 38: OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l38 Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis n n Lecturer P. Noto D.O. n Session Lecture 38 n n 1. Axes of Motion n Oblique Axes Named for the superior pole. n Right Oblique Axis Runs from Right Superior to Left Inferior pole. n Left Oblique Axis Runs from Left Superior to Right Inferior pole. n Usage Walking cycle Torsions . n Transverse Axes n Superior S2 Respiratory / Craniosacral motion. n Middle S2 Postural / Flexion-Extension. n Inferior S3 Innominate rotation Ilial shear . n n 2. Diagnosing Torsions vs Shears n L5 Rules n 1. L5 rotates OPPOSITE to the sacrum. n 2. L5 sidebends to the SAME side as the Oblique Axis. n Example If L5 is Rotated Right Sacrum is Rotated Left. n Seated Flexion Test SFT n Positive on the side of Dysfunction Blocked motion . n The axis is on the Opposite side of the positive SFT. n Physiologic Forward vs Non-Physiologic Backward n Physiologic Neutral mechanics Type I . Forward torsions L on L R on R . n Spring Test Negative It springs / Good motion . n Sphinx Test Negative Symmetry improves with extension . n Non-Physiologic Non-neutral Type II . Backward torsions L on R R on L . n Spring Test Positive Hard end feel / No spring . n Sphinx Test Positive Asymmetry gets worse / Pain . n n 3. The 4-Step Diagnosis n1. Seated Flexion Test Determines the side of dysfunction and thus the axis is on the opposite side . n2. Spring/Sphinx Determines if it is Forward Negative or Backward Positive . n3. L5 Confirms rotation. n4. Deep Sulcus / Post ILA n Forward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides. n Backward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides. n Wait simplify n Deep Sulcus = Side of Forward Rotation Sacral Base Anterior . n Posterior ILA = Side of Backward Rotation Sacral Base Posterior . n n 4. Specific Diagnoses n Left on Left Forward Torsion n Facing Left. n Axis Left. n L5 Rotated Right. n Spring Negative Good . n Right on Left Backward Torsion n Facing Right. n Axis Left. n L5 Rotated Left. n Spring Positive Bad . n Unilateral Shears n Not an axis dysfunction. Whole side is stuck forward or backward. n SFT + on same side as deep sulcus/low ILA if flexion or shallow sulcus/high ILA if extension . Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis Neurology questions A patient has a positive Seated Flexion Test on the RIGHT. The Spring Test is NEGATIVE. L5 is Rotated Right. What is the diagnosis? Right on Right Sacral Torsion Left on Left Sacral Torsion Right on Left Sacral Torsion Left on Right Sacral Torsion Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion 1 Step 1 SFT Right + → Axis is on the Left . So it s ? on Left . n Step 2 Spring Negative → Forward Torsion L on L . n Check Forward torsions have SAME letters. L on L fits. Which of the following findings is consistent with a Non-Physiologic Backward Sacral Torsion? Negative Sphinx Test Negative Spring Test Positive Spring Test L5 Rotated opposite to Sacrum Symmetrical Sacral Sulci 2 Positive Spring Test Hard/Stiff indicates the sacral base is stuck backward Posterior which is a non-physiologic backward torsion. In a Right on Right sacral torsion L5 will be Rotated Left Sidebent Left Rotated Left Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Left Neutral 1 R on R Axis is Right → L5 Sidebends Right . Rotation is Right → L5 Rotates Left Opposite . Pattern Rot Left SB Right Type I neutral mechanics . The Superior Transverse Axis S2 is associated with which motion? Postural Flexion/Extension Innominate Rotation Respiratory / Craniosacral Motion Walking Cycle Disc Herniation 2 Superior Axis Respiratory/Craniosacral. n Middle Axis Postural. n Inferior Axis Innominate Ilial . If the Seated Flexion Test is positive on the LEFT which oblique axis is engaged? Left Oblique Axis Right Oblique Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Vertical Axis 1 SFT is positive on the side of dysfunction. The functioning axis is on the OPPOSITE side. Positive Left → Axis is Right . SFT Positive Side Meaning Side of Dysfunction Axis is Opposite OMM Spring Test Positive Meaning Stuck Backward Non-physiologic / Backward Torsion OMM L5 Rotation Rule Rotates OPPOSITE to Sacrum OMM L5 Sidebending Rule Sidebends SAME side as Axis OMM Same Letters e.g. L on L Forward Torsion Physiologic OMM Different Letters e.g. R on L Backward Torsion Non-Physiologic OMM AMBOSS OMM > Sacrum FirstAid OMM > Sacrum L5 Rule Mnemonic L5 acts like a Bully . If L5 rotates Right the Sacrum turns away Left . Sidebending side = Axis side. Spring Test Rules If it Springs it s a Functional Physiologic/Forward torsion. nIf it s Stiff Positive it s a Stuck Non-physiologic/Backward torsion. Naming Torsions Rotation on Axis . n R on R Forward Physiologic . n L on L Forward Physiologic . n R on L Backward Non-Physiologic . n L on R Backward Non-Physiologic . n Hint Same letters = Forward. Different letters = Backward. Seated Flexion Test Finds the side of ILIOSACRAL dysfunction. The axis is on the OPPOSITE side of the positive test. Sphinx Test Logic Extension Sphinx makes a forward sacrum happy more symmetrical . It makes a backward sacrum sad more asymmetrical ."}, {"id": "l39", "title": "Lecture 39: Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l39 Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 39 n n 1. Alpha-Adrenergic Antagonists Alpha Blockers n Non-Selective Alpha-1 & Alpha-2 n Phenoxybenzamine Irreversible Covalent bond . Long duration. Used pre-op for Pheochromocytoma prevents hypertensive crisis . n Phentolamine Competitive Reversible . Used for Epinephrine Reversal local anesthetic overdose and Pheochromocytoma diagnosis/surgery. n Side Effects Severe Orthostatic Hypotension Reflex Tachycardia due to Alpha-2 block increasing NE release . n Selective Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins n Prazosin Terazosin Doxazosin Used for HTN add-on and BPH. n Tamsulosin Flomax Alpha-1A selective Prostate specific . Used for BPH less hypotension . n Adverse Effects First-Dose Effect Syncope/Severe Orthostatis → Take at bedtime! Floppy Iris Syndrome Cataract surgery risk . n Selective Alpha-2 Antagonist n Yohimbine Increases NE release blocks feedback . Aphrodisiac . Causes anxiety/tremor. n n 2. Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists Beta Blockers n Non-Selective Beta-1 & Beta-2 n Propranolol Lipophilic CNS effects nightmares/sedation . Used for Migraine prophylaxis Performance Anxiety Stage fright Thyroid Storm. n Contraindication Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction via B2 . n Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective A-M - Atenolol Metoprolol Esmolol . n Safer in lung disease. n Combined Alpha/Beta Blockers n Labetalol Carvedilol Block Alpha-1 Vasodilation + Beta-1/2. n Use CHF Carvedilol decreases mortality Hypertensive Emergencies Labetalol . n n 3. Key Pharmacology Concepts n Epinephrine Reversal n Normal Epi Increases BP Alpha-1 vasoconstriction > Beta-2 dilation . n Epi + Alpha Blocker Decreases BP Alpha blocked unmasks Beta-2 vasodilation . n Diabetes & Beta Blockers n Beta blockers MASK symptoms of hypoglycemia tachycardia tremors . n Non-selectives delay recovery from hypoglycemia block liver gluconeogenesis . n Withdrawal n NEVER stop abruptly. Chronic use UPREGULATES receptors. n Sudden stop → Rebound HTN Angina MI Sudden Death. n n 4. Therapeutic Uses Summary n n| Indication | Preferred Agents | Mechanism | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Hypertension | Metoprolol Atenolol | Decrease CO Renin inhibition. | n| Angina/MI | Metoprolol Carvedilol | Decrease O2 demand HR/Contractility . | n| CHF | Carvedilol Metoprolol Succinate Bisoprolol | Block sympathetic remodeling. Increase survival. | n| BPH | Tamsulosin Doxazosin | Relax prostate smooth muscle Alpha-1 . | n| Pheochromocytoma | Phenoxybenzamine | Irreversible Alpha block before surgery. | n| Glaucoma | Timolol | Decrease aqueous production. | Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists Neurology questions A patient with Pheochromocytoma is scheduled for surgery. Which drug is most appropriate for preoperative management to prevent hypertensive crisis? Propranolol Phenoxybenzamine Atenolol Prazosin Yohimbine 1 Phenoxybenzamine is an Irreversible alpha blocker. It is the gold standard for pheochromocytoma pre-op to prevent catecholamine surges. A 65-year-old male is started on Terazosin for BPH. What important counseling point must be provided? Take on an empty stomach because food reduces absorption. Take with grapefruit juice to enhance effect. Take the first dose at bedtime to avoid syncope. Discontinue immediately if nasal congestion occurs. Expect heart rate to decrease significantly. 2 First-Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers cause significant orthostatic hypotension/syncope initially. Taking it at bedtime mitigates the risk of falling. Which of the following beta blockers also possesses Alpha-1 blocking activity providing additional vasodilation? Metoprolol Atenolol Carvedilol Timolol Esmolol 2 Carvedilol and Labetalol trigger Combined blockade Beta-1 Beta-2 and Alpha-1 . This provides vasodilation + heart rate control. A diabetic patient taking insulin is prescribed a beta blocker. They should be warned that the drug might mask which symptom of hypoglycemia? Sweating Hunger Tachycardia Confusion Fatigue 2 Tachycardia and tremors are mediated by the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia. Beta blockers mask these. Sweating cholinergic is usually preserved. The phenomenon known as Epinephrine Reversal describes a drop in blood pressure when Epinephrine is administered in the presence of A Beta Blocker An Alpha Blocker A Muscarinic Agonist A Ganglionic Blocker A Calcium Channel Blocker 1 An Alpha Blocker like Phentolamine blocks the vasoconstriction Alpha-1 of Epi leaving the vasodilation Beta-2 unopposed → BP drop. Phenoxybenzamine Mechanism Irreversible Alpha Blocker Pharmacology First Dose Effect Drug Class Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins Pharmacology A-M Beta Blockers Atenolol Metoprolol Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective Pharmacology Carvedilol / Labetalol Mechanism Mixed Alpha-1 and Beta Blockers Pharmacology Epinephrine Reversal Cause Epi + Alpha Blocker Unmasks Beta-2 Vasodilation Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Signs Masked by Beta Blockers Tachycardia Tremors Anxiety Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Sign SPARED by Beta Blockers Sweating Cholinergic Pharmacology Pheochromocytoma Pre-op Drug Phenoxybenzamine Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Autonomic Drugs > Adrenergic Antagonists alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs resource AMBOSS chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic nervous system Epinephrine Reversal Giving an Alpha Blocker Phentolamine before Epinephrine turns Epi s Pressor effect into a Depressor effect BP drops due to unopposed Beta-2 vasodilation . First Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers Prazosin/Terazosin cause severe orthostatic hypotension on the first dose. Counseling Take the first dose at Bedtime . Beta Blocker Withdrawal Chronic beta blockade causes receptor Upregulation . Abrupt withdrawal leads to supersensitivity = Rebound Hypertension / MI . Always TAPER. Diabetes & Beta Blockers Beta blockers mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia palpitations tremors anxiety . Sweating Cholinergic is the only sign that remains. Pheochromocytoma Rule Always give Alpha blockers BEFORE Beta blockers. A before B . Giving Beta first leaves Alpha unopposed → Hypertensive Crisis."}, {"id": "l4", "title": "Lecture #4: Blood Supply of the CNS", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l4 Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Overview of Cerebral Circulation Slides 3-6 nThe brain represents only 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of the body s oxygen and glucose . nBlood enters the cranium via two major systems n1. Anterior Circulation Internal Carotid Arteries Supplies ~80% of the brain Telencephalon Diencephalon . n2. Posterior Circulation Vertebral Arteries Supplies ~20% of the brain Brainstem Cerebellum Occipital lobes . n n--- n n 2. Anterior Circulation ICA Slides 8-14 nThe Internal Carotid Artery ICA enters the skull via the Carotid Canal passes through the Cavernous Sinus and gives off 4 key Intradural branches OPAM n n 1. Ophthalmic Artery Slide 14 n First branch. Supplies the eye/retina. n Clinical Occlusion causes Amaurosis Fugax transient monocular blindness described as a curtain coming down . n n 2. Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Slide 15 n Connects the Anterior ICA and Posterior PCA circulations. n Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysms . Aneurysm here can compress CN III causing an ipsilateral Down and Out eye with blown pupil. n n 3. Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Slides 16-17 20 n Supplies the Medial surface of the Frontal and Parietal lobes. n Function Motor/Sensory to the Lower Limb Leg/Foot and trunk. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Leg weakness and sensory loss. n n 4. Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Slides 18-19 20 n Largest branch. Runs laterally through the Sylvian Fissure. n Supplies the Lateral surface of Frontal Temporal and Parietal lobes. n Function Motor/Sensory to the Face and Upper Limb Arm/Hand . Also supplies language centers in the dominant hemisphere. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Face/Arm weakness Face/Arm sensory loss. n Left MCA Dominant Aphasia Broca s or Wernicke s . n Right MCA Non-dominant Hemineglect. n n correlate n Lenticulostriate Arteries Slide 19 n Small deep perforating branches of the MCA supplying the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. n Pathology High pressure Hypertension causes lipohyalinosis and microaneurysms Charcot-Bouchard Aneurysms . n Result Rupture leads to Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage . Occlusion leads to Lacunar Infarcts pure motor strokes . n n n--- n n 3. Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar Slides 23-26 n Vertebral Arteries VA n Ascend through transverse foramina of C6-C1. Enter skull via Foramen Magnum. n Branches n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Supplies anterior 2/3 of spinal cord. n PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Medulla and Inferior Cerebellum. Occlusion → Wallenberg Syndrome . n n Basilar Artery BA n Formed by fusion of VAs. Runs along the Pons. n Branches n AICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Pons. n Pontine Arteries Supply the Pons. Occlusion → Locked-in Syndrome . n SCA Superior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Superior Cerebellum. n n Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA n Terminal bifurcation of the Basilar Artery. n Supplies Occipital Lobe Visual Cortex and Inferior Temporal lobe. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field loss often with Macular Sparing . n n pearl n CN III Rule The Oculomotor Nerve CN III acts as a sandwich filling between the SCA Superior Cerebellar and PCA Posterior Cerebral arteries. Aneurysms here compress CN III. n n n--- n n 4. Collateral & Venous Circulation n n Circle of Willis Slides 29-30 n Anastomotic ring at the base of the brain providing collateral flow. n Formed by ACom ACA ICA PCom PCA. n Note The circle is incomplete in ~50% of people. n Watershed Zones Border areas between ACA/MCA/PCA territories. Susceptible to ischemia during Global Hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension → Man in a Barrel syndrome proximal weakness . n n Venous Drainage Slides 32-42 nVeins drain into Valveless Dural Venous Sinuses n Superior Sagittal Sinus SSS Drains superficial veins + CSF via arachnoid granulations . n Cavernous Sinus A venous plexus surrounding the Sella Turcica. n Contents ICA CN VI freely inside and CN III IV V1 V2 in the wall . n Pathology Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis or Fistula can cause multiple cranial nerve palsies. n n Presentation Thunderclap Headache Worst headache of life meningeal signs. n Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 4 prev l3 next l5 pdf pdfs/Lecture 4. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf CNS Blood Supply n Anterior Circ ICA n Ophthalmic Eye Amaurosis Fugax n ACA Medial Brain Legs n MCA Lateral Brain Face/Arms + Speech n Lenticulostriate Lacunar stroke n PCom Connects to Posterior n Posterior Circ Vert-Basilar n Vertebral A. → PICA n Basilar A. → AICA SCA PCA n PCA Occipital Vision n Circle of Willis n ACom Connects ACAs n PCom Connects ICA-PCA n Venous n Sup. Sagittal Sinus n Cavernous Sinus CN III IV V VI n Transverse/Sigmoid → IJV questions 1. A 65-year-old man presents with sudden difficulty understanding language. He speaks fluently but uses nonsensical words Word Salad . MRI shows an ischemic stroke. Which artery is most likely involved and which area is affected? ACA - Broca s Area MCA - Broca s Area MCA - Wernicke s Area PCA - Occipital Lobe PICA - Lateral Medulla 2 From Slide 46 Wernicke s Area comprehension is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus supplied by the MCA . Damage leads to Receptive Aphasia Fluent but meaningless . Broca s Expressive is also MCA but frontal. 2. A patient experiences a curtain coming down over their right eye which resolves after a few minutes Amaurosis Fugax . This symptom is a warning sign for a blockage in which artery? Ophthalmic Artery Branch of ICA Middle Cerebral Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery Basilar Artery External Carotid Artery 0 From Slide 14 The Ophthalmic Artery is the first branch of the Intradural ICA. Transient occlusion causes temporary monocular blindness Amaurosis Fugax a TIA warning sign. 3. A patient with chronic hypertension presents with a pure motor stroke. MRI reveals a small lacunar infarct in the internal capsule. Which small arteries are likely responsible? Callosomarginal Arteries Lenticulostriate Arteries Anterior Choroidal Arteries Posterior Communicating Arteries Pontine Arteries 1 From Slide 19 Lenticulostriate Arteries deep branches of MCA supply the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. They are prone to lipohyalinosis and Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms in hypertension. 4. In the Circle of Willis which artery connects the Anterior Circulation ICA system to the Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar system ? Anterior Communicating Artery ACom Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Anterior Choroidal Artery Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Basilar Artery 1 The Posterior Communicating Artery PCom connects the ICA to the PCA linking the two major circulatory systems. 5. Contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory? MCA ACA PCA PICA AICA 1 From Slide 20 The Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA supplies the medial surface of the cortex where the Leg/Foot area of the motor/sensory homunculus is located. Branches of Intradural ICA? OPAM Ophthalmic PCom ACA MCA Anatomy Which artery supplies the visual cortex? Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Anatomy Clinical sign of ACA stroke? Contralateral Leg Weakness Clinical Clinical sign of MCA stroke? Contralateral Face/Arm Weakness + Aphasia Clinical Artery passing through Cavernous Sinus? Internal Carotid Artery ICA Anatomy Transient monocular vision loss Curtain ? Amaurosis Fugax Ophthalmic Artery ischemia Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysm causing CN III palsy? Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Pathology Vessels prone to rupture in hypertension Basal Ganglia ? Lenticulostriate Arteries Charcot-Bouchard Pathology What nerve passes between SCA and PCA? CN III Oculomotor Anatomy Risk factors for Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT ? Pregnancy OCPs Hypercoagulability Clinical BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord Anatomy CNS Blood Flow Rule The brain is 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of oxygen/glucose. It has zero reserve. Stroke Homunculus Rule ACA = Medial aspect = Legs/Feet . n MCA = Lateral aspect = Face/Arms/Hands + Speech. Amaurosis Fugax Transient monocular blindness Curtain coming down is a TIA of the Ophthalmic Artery warning of impending ICA stroke. CN III Compression Aneurysms of the PCom or between SCA and PCA often compress CN III causing a Down and Out eye with a blown pupil . Lenticulostriate Arteries These small vessels supply the Basal Ganglia. They are the primary site of hypertensive lipohyalinosis and Lacunar Infarcts . Aphasia Localization Aphasia is usually Left MCA. n Broca s Frontal = Broken speech. n Wernicke s Temporal = Word salad."}, {"id": "l40", "title": "Lecture 40: Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l40 Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 40 n n 1. Spinal Cord Anatomy Review n Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Cross in Brainstem . n Spinothalamic Tract Pain Temperature Crude Touch. Cross immediately/1-2 levels up . n Corticospinal Tract Motor. Cross in Medulla . n n 2. Spinal Cord Syndromes n Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia n Lesion Center of cord crossing spinothalamic fibers . n Presentation Cape-like loss of pain/temperature across shoulders/arms. Upper extremities > Lower. n Causes Syringomyelia Chiari I Trauma Hyperextension Tumor. n Posterior Column Syndrome Tabes Dorsalis n Lesion Dorsal Columns. n Presentation Loss of Proprioception/Vibration → Sensory Ataxia Stomping Gait +Romberg . Lhermitte Sign Shock down back . n Causes Neurosyphilis Tabes Dorsalis - Triad Lightning pains Ataxia Argyll Robertson Pupil B12 Deficiency. n Posterolateral Column Syndrome Subacute Combined Degeneration n Lesion Dorsal Columns + Corticospinal Tract. n Presentation Ataxia/Numbness + Spastic Paresis. n Causes Vitamin B12 Deficiency Pernicious anemia Gastric bypass Vegans Nitrous Oxide abuse Whippets . n Anterior Cord Syndrome n Lesion Anterior 2/3 of cord Spares Dorsal Columns . n Presentation Complete Motor Paralysis + Loss of Pain/Temp. Proprioception/Vibration SPARED . n Causes Anterior Spinal Artery ASA infarct Aortic aneurysm repair/surgery . n Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome Hemicord n Lesion Half of the cord Hemisection . n Presentation n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor CST + Proprioception/Vibration DC . n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temp Spinothalamic starting 1-2 levels below . n Causes Stab wound MS Tumor. n Cauda Equina Syndrome n Lesion Nerve roots L2-S5 Horse s tail . n Presentation Saddle Anesthesia Bowel/Bladder dysfunction Retention/Incontinence Radicular pain. n Management Surgical Emergency Decompression within 48hrs . n n 3. Common Mononeuropathies n Median Nerve Carpal Tunnel . Numbness 1st 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy. n Ulnar Nerve Cubital Tunnel . Numbness 4th/5th digits. Claw Hand weak interossei . n Radial Nerve Saturday Night Palsy . Spiral groove compression. Wrist Drop . n Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Unilateral Foot Drop Steppage gait . Compression at Fibular Head. n Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Meralgia Paresthetica . Numbness of lateral thigh. Caused by tight belts obesity pregnancy. Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies Neurology questions A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms. Vibration and proprioception are intact. This pattern is consistent with Anterior Cord Syndrome Central Cord Syndrome Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome Posterior Column Syndrome Cauda Equina Syndrome 1 Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia expands from the central canal disrupting the crossing Spinothalamic fibers first causing the cape-like dissociation. Following a stab wound to the right side of the spinal cord Hemisection at T10 which finding would be expected? Loss of pain and temperature on the Right leg Loss of vibration and proprioception on the Left leg Paralysis of the Left leg Loss of pain and temperature on the Left leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Brown-S u00e9quard Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp. Right lesion → Left leg Pain/Temp loss. Right leg would lose Motor/Vibe . A patient recovering from abdominal aortic aneurysm repair develops bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain sensation but preserves vibration sense. The most likely diagnosis is Posterior Spinal Artery Infarct Anterior Spinal Artery Infarct Vitamin B12 Deficiency Tabes Dorsalis Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome 1 Anterior Spinal Artery supplies the anterior 2/3 Motor + Spinothalamic . Infarct causes Anterior Cord Syndrome Paralysis + Pain loss with Dorsal Column sparing Vibe intact . Which of the following creates a Saturday Night Palsy causing wrist drop? Median Nerve compression at carpal tunnel Ulnar Nerve compression at cubital tunnel Radial Nerve compression at spiral groove Common Peroneal compression at fibular head Sciatic Nerve compression 2 Radial Nerve compression at the spiral groove humerus often from falling asleep with arm over a chair Saturday Night causes Wrist Drop . Subacute Combined Degeneration of the spinal cord involves demyelination of the Posterior Columns and Corticospinal Tracts due to deficiency of Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin E Copper 2 Vitamin B12 deficiency or Nitrous Oxide toxicity causes Subacute Combined Degeneration. Central Cord Syndrome Pattern Cape-like Pain/Temp Loss Neurology Tabes Dorsalis Cause Neurosyphilis Tertiary Neurology Brown-Sequard Pain/Temp loss side Contralateral Opposite side Neurology Brown-Sequard Motor/Vibe loss side Ipsilateral Same side Neurology Wrist Drop Nerve Radial Nerve Neurology Foot Drop Nerve Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Neurology Saddle Anesthesia + Incontinence Cauda Equina Syndrome Emergency Neurology Subacute Combined Degeneration Cause B12 Deficiency / Nitrous Oxide Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Lesions resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Spinal Cord resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Brown-S u00e9quard Rules You lose Motor & Vibe on the SAME side Ipsi . nYou lose Pain & Temp on the OPPOSITE side Contra . Anterior Cord Spare Anterior Spinal Artery stroke kills everything EXCEPT the Dorsal Columns . So they can still feel Vibration/Proprioception but can t move or feel pain. Syringomyelia Sign Cape-like distribution of numbness Pain/Temp loss across the shoulders and back. Often associated with Chiari Malformation . Whippets & B12 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes B12 leading to Subacute Combined Degeneration Posterolateral cord syndrome . Look for ataxia + numbness in a party-goer. Cauda Equina Flags New onset Incontinence or retention + Saddle Anesthesia . This is an MRI + Surgery EMERGENCY."}, {"id": "l41", "title": "Lecture 41: From Myelopathy to Myopathy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l41 Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 41 n n 1. Localization Approach n Myelopathy Spinal Cord UMN signs below Sensory level Bowel/Bladder. n Anterior Horn Cell LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations . Sensory SPARED . n Radiculopathy Root Radiating pain Dermatomal sensory loss. n Plexopathy Multiple nerves/roots involved. Painful e.g. Parsonage-Turner . n Peripheral Neuropathy Stocking-glove sensory loss Distal weakness. n NMJ Fatigable weakness Diplopia/Ptosis. Sensory SPARED . n Myopathy Proximal muscle weakness. Sensory SPARED . n n 2. Anterior Horn Cell Disorders n ALS Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis n UMN + LMN Signs in same segment. n Asymmetric limb weakness Bulbar signs Dysarthria/Dysphagia . n Sensory Spared . n Polio & West Nile Virus n Acute Asymmetric flaccid paralysis LMN . n Fever/Meningeal signs. n Spinal Muscular Atrophy SMA n Genetic SMN1 . Symmetric LMN weakness. n n 3. Peripheral Nerve Disorders n Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome AIDP n Acute Ascending Paralysis. Post-infectious Campylobacter Zika . n LP Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells . n Tx IVIG or Plasma Exchange. NO Steroids . n CIDP Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy n Chronic >8 weeks . Chronic GBS . n Tx Steroids IVIG. n Leprosy Hansen s Disease. Mutilating neuropathy Armadillos . n n 4. Neuromuscular Junction NMJ n Myasthenia Gravis MG n Post-synaptic ACh Receptor Antibodies. n Clinical Ptosis Diplopia Bulbar weakness. Fatigue Worse at end of day . n Association Thymoma . n Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome LEMS n Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channel Antibodies. n Clinical Proximal leg weakness. Facilitation Strength IMPROVES with use . Dry mouth. n Association Small Cell Lung Cancer . n Botulism n Toxin blocks ACh release SNARE proteins . n Descending Paralysis. Pupils dilated/fixed. Honey infants . n n 5. Myopathies n Polymyositis/Dermatomyositis n Proximal symmetric weakness difficulty rising from chair . n Pain is uncommon . n Elevated CK. Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy Neurology questions A 35-year-old male presents with ascending weakness starting in his legs and moving up. Reflexes are absent. Two weeks ago he had a diarrheal illness. CSF shows protein of 150 High and WBC of 2 Normal . What is the diagnosis? Multiple Sclerosis Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome Myasthenia Gravis ALS Polio 1 GBS presents with acute ascending paralysis and areflexia after an infection Campylobacter . CSF shows Albuminocytologic Dissociation . Which of the following findings is classic for Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS ? Pure sensory loss in a stocking-glove distribution Symmetric proximal muscle weakness with pain Combination of UMN and LMN signs with sensory sparing Diplopia and ptosis worsening at night Ascending paralysis with bladder dysfunction 2 ALS affects Anterior Horn Cells LMN and Corticospinal Tracts UMN . The hallmark is mixed UMN/LMN signs. Sensation is typically preserved. A patient with suspected Myasthenia Gravis is tested. Which antibody is most specific for this condition? Anti-Voltage Gated Calcium Channel Anti-Acetylcholine Receptor AChR Anti-Jo-1 Anti-GM1 Anti-Hu 1 AChR antibodies are pathogenic in Myasthenia Gravis Post-synaptic . Calcium channel antibodies are for Lambert-Eaton. Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome is most commonly associated with which underlying malignancy? Thymoma Small Cell Lung Cancer Renal Cell Carcinoma Lymphoma Melanoma 1 Small Cell Lung Cancer is found in ~50% of patients with LEMS. Thymoma is associated with Myasthenia Gravis . Which feature distinguishes Polio from ALS? Polio causes LMN signs only; ALS causes UMN & LMN signs. Polio is progressive; ALS resolves spontaneously. Polio affects sensation; ALS spares sensation. Polio causes spasticity; ALS causes flaccidity. Polio is autoimmune; ALS is viral. 0 Polio destroys Anterior Horn Cells LMN only → Flaccid paralysis. ALS destroys AHCs LMN + CSTs UMN → Mixed Spastic/Flaccid. ALS Finding Mixed UMN + LMN Signs Neurology GBS CSF Finding Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Mechanism Antibodies to Post-synaptic ACh Receptors Neurology Lambert-Eaton Mechanism Antibodies to Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channels Neurology Lambert-Eaton Association Small Cell Lung Cancer Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Association Thymoma Neurology Botulism Presentation Descending Paralysis + Dilated Pupils Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular resource Pathoma chapter Musculoskeletal > Neuromuscular Junction resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular & Peripheral Nerve UMN vs LMN Signs UMN Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + No atrophy. n LMN Flaccidity Hyporeflexia Fasciculations Atrophy. ALS Hallmark Presence of UMN and LMN signs in the same limb/segment. e.g. Atrophied muscle has a brisk reflex . Sensory is Intact . GBS vs CIDP Treatment Both get IVIG/PLEX. n GBS Steroids do NOT work. n CIDP Steroids DO work. MG vs LEMS MG Eyes Post-synaptic. Worse with use. Thymoma. n LEMS Legs Pre-synaptic Ca channels . BETTER with use. Small Cell Ca. Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein but Normal WBCs in CSF. Classic finding in Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome ."}, {"id": "l42", "title": "Lecture 42: Diseases of Myelin", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l42 Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin n n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. n Session Lecture 42 n n 1. Demyelinating Diseases Myelin Destruction n Multiple Sclerosis MS n Autoimmune destruction of CNS myelin Oligodendrocytes . n Pathology Periventricular Plaques Dawson s Fingers . Reactive Gliosis. Preservation of Axons early . n CSF Oligoclonal IgG Bands Intrathecal synthesis . n Clinical Relapsing-Remitting course. Optic Neuritis Painful vision loss INO MLF lesion Lhermitte Sign Electric shock with neck flexion Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens symptoms . n Epidemiology Women > Men. Far from equator Vitamin D . n Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome GBS n Autoimmune destruction of PNS myelin Schwann Cells . n Pathology Segmental demyelination + Lymphocytic infiltration. n Clinical Acute Ascending Paralysis + Areflexia. n Trigger Campylobacter jejuni CMV EBV Molecular Mimicry . n CSF Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells . n Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination n Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia . From Low to High your Pons will Die . n Clinical Locked-In Syndrome Quadriplegia + Preserved Consciousness/Eye movements . n n 2. Dysmyelinating Diseases Leukodystrophies n Definition Genetic defects in myelin formation or turnover . n Metachromatic Leukodystrophy MLD n Deficiency Arylsulfatase A . n Accumulation Sulfatides. n Path Metachromasia change in dye color . n Krabbe Disease Globoid Cell Leukodystrophy n Deficiency Galactocerebrosidase . n Accumulation Galactosylsphingosine Psychosine - Toxic. n Path Globoid Cells Engorged Macrophages . n Adrenoleukodystrophy ALD n Genetics X-Linked Recessive ABCD1 gene . n Defect Peroxisomal transporter. n Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA . n Clinical Adrenal Insufficiency + CNS deterioration in young boys. n n 3. Infectious Demyelination n Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML n Pathogen JC Virus Reactivation . n Host Immunosuppressed AIDS CD4 < 200 . n Path Lytic infection of Oligodendrocytes. Enlarged nuclei with Ground Glass viral inclusions. n Clinical Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits. Death. Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin Neurology questions A 28-year-old female presents with sudden loss of vision in her right eye associated with pain on eye movement. MRI shows periventricular white matter plaques. Analysis of her CSF would most likely reveal Albuminocytologic Dissociation Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neutrophilic Pleocytosis Decreased Glucose Positive India Ink Stain 1 Oligoclonal Bands found in the CSF but not serum indicate intrathecal IgG production a hallmark of Multiple Sclerosis . A 45-year-old alcoholic is brought to the ER with severe hyponatremia Na = 102 . The residents rapidly correct his sodium to 135 over 6 hours. Two days later he is quadriplegic but can blink and move his eyes vertically. What happened? Stroke in the MCA territory Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Central Pontine Myelinolysis Wernicke s Encephalopathy Herniation 2 Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome is caused by rapid correction of hyponatremia . It destroys the Pons causing Locked-In Syndrome . Which pattern of weakness is characteristic of Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome? Descending paralysis with pupillary dilation Hemiparesis sparing the face Ascending flaccid paralysis with areflexia Proximal muscle weakness with preserved sensation Waxing and waning weakness improving with rest 2 GBS causes an acute Ascending paralysis toes to nose with loss of reflexes Areflexia . Adrenoleukodystrophy involves the accumulation of which substance due to a peroxisomal defect? Sphingomyelin Sulfatides Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Galactocerebroside Copper 2 ALD is a defect in the ABCD1 transporter preventing the breakdown of Very Long Chain Fatty Acids in peroxisomes. Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML is caused by the reactivation of which virus in immunocompromised patients? Herpes Simplex Virus 1 HSV-1 Cytomegalovirus CMV JC Virus Epstein-Barr Virus EBV Varicella Zoster Virus VZV 2 JC Virus causes PML. Mnemonic J ohn C unningham virus destroys oligodendoctyes in AIDS . Multiple Sclerosis CSF Finding Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neurology Lhermitte Sign Electric shock down back with neck flexion MS/Spinal Cord Neurology Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens MS symptoms Neurology Central Pontine Myelinolysis Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia Neurology Metachromatic Leukodystrophy Enzyme Arylsulfatase A Neurology Krabbe Disease Hallmark Globoid Cells Neurology Adrenoleukodystrophy Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Neurology PML Virus JC Virus Neurology primarySource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Demyelinating Disorders alternatives resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Demyelinating Disorders resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Demyelinating resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Demyelinating MS Triad Charcot 1. Scanning Speech Dysarthria n2. Intention Tremor Cerebellar n3. Nystagmus INO/MLF Uhthoff s Phenomenon MS symptoms get WORSE with HEAT Hot bath exercise fever . Heat slows conduction in demyelinated axons. CPM Rule From Low to High your Pons will Die Hyponatremia → Rapid Correction → CPM/Locked-in . n From High to Low your Brain will Blow Hypernatremia → Rapid Correction → Cerebral Edema . Krabbe Findings Look for Globoid Cells Multinucleated Giant Cells and optic atrophy. Adrenoleukodystrophy Think Adrenal glands + Leuko White matter + Dystrophy Bad growth . Young boy with bronzing skin Addison s and behavioral changes."}, {"id": "l43", "title": "Lecture 43: Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l43 Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis n n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. n Session Lecture 43 n n 1. Acute Exacerbations Flare-ups n Glucocorticoids High Dose IV Methylprednisolone n Mechanism Induces Lipocortin → Inhibits Phospholipase A2 → Decreased Prostaglandins/Leukotrienes. n Use Shortens duration of acute attacks e.g. Optic Neuritis . Does NOT change long-term prognosis. n Adverse Effects HPA axis suppression Taper needed Osteoporosis Hyperglycemia Insomnia. n n 2. Disease Modifying Therapies DMTs n Interferon Beta 1a & 1b n Mechanism Cytokine JAK/STAT ; Inhibits T-cell activation/migration across BBB. n Adverse Effects Flu-like symptoms Fever chills aches Depression Liver toxicity. n Glatiramer Acetate n Mechanism Synthetic polypeptide Myelin Basic Protein decoy . Shifts immune response from Th1 Pro-inflammatory to Th2 Anti-inflammatory . n Adverse Effects Injection site reactions Post-injection systemic reaction Flushing Chest chest tightness - benign but scary . n Note Safest in Pregnancy . n Natalizumab n Mechanism Monoclonal antibody against $ alpha$4-integrin . Blocks leukocytes from binding VCAM-1 and entering the CNS. n Adverse Effects PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy due to JC Virus reactivation. n Fingolimod n Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator . Sequesters lymphocytes in lymph nodes can t exit . n Adverse Effects Bradycardia Monitor EKG at first dose Macular Edema. n Dimethyl Fumarate n Mechanism Activates Nrf2 Antioxidant pathway . n Adverse Effects Flushing GI upset. n Teriflunomide n Mechanism Inhibits Pyrimidine synthesis Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase . n Adverse Effects Hepatotoxicity Teratogenicity Contraindicated in pregnancy . n Mitoxantrone n Mechanism Anthracycline analog. Intercalates DNA / Inhibits Topoisomerase II. n Use Secondary Progressive MS. n Adverse Effects Cardiotoxicity Dilated Cardiomyopathy - monitor EF Acute Myeloid Leukemia AML . Blue discoloration of urine/sclera. n Anti-CD20 Ocrelizumab Rituximab n Mechanism B-cell depletion. n Adverse Effects Infusion reactions Hepatitis B reactivation. n n 3. Symptomatic Treatment n Spasticity n Baclofen GABA-B agonist . n Tizanidine $ alpha$2-adrenergic agonist . n Walking Speed n Dalfampridine K+ Channel Blocker - Enhances conduction. n Urinary Incontinence n Oxybutynin Muscarinic Antagonist . n Botulinum Toxin Cleaves SNARE proteins . Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis Neurology questions A 32-year-old female with Multiple Sclerosis is started on a new medication. Since starting she complains of flushed skin and profound gastrointestinal distress. Which medication mechanism is most likely responsible? Inhibition of Dihydroorotate Dehydrogenase Activation of Nrf2 pathway Sequestration of lymphocytes in lymph nodes Blockade of alpha-4 integrin Depletion of CD20 B cells 1 Dimethyl Fumarate activates the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. Its most common side effects are Flushing and GI upset. A patient being treated for MS develops Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML . Which drug mechanism is associated with this complication? Interferon beta-1a Glatiramer Acetate Natalizumab Baclofen Methylprednisolone 2 Natalizumab blocks alpha-4 integrin preventing immune surveillance in the CNS allowing JC Virus reactivation PML . Which medication helps improve walking speed in MS patients by blocking potassium channels? Tizanidine Dalfampridine Oxybutynin Modafinil Amantadine 1 Dalfampridine blocks voltage-gated Potassium channels increasing action potential duration and improving conduction. Before starting a patient on Fingolimod which baseline test is most critical? Liver Function Tests Echocardiogram Electrocardiogram EKG Pulmonary Function Tests Thyroid Stimulating Hormone 2 Fingolimod can cause significant Bradycardia and heart block after the first dose. Observation and EKG monitoring are required. Which agent treats spasticity in MS by acting as an agonist at GABA-B receptors? Diazepam Dantrolene Baclofen Tizanidine Cyclobenzaprine 2 Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist used centrally for spasticity. Tizanidine is an alpha-2 agonist; Dantrolene works on muscle Ryanodine receptors . Natalizumab Mechanism Blocks Alpha-4 Integrin prevents CNS entry Pharmacology Natalizumab Serious AE PML JC Virus Pharmacology Fingolimod Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator Sequesters lymphocytes Pharmacology Fingolimod AE Bradycardia First dose Pharmacology Teriflunomide Contraindication Pregnancy Teratogenic Pharmacology Glatiramer Acetate Mechanism Myelin Basic Protein Decoy Th1 → Th2 Pharmacology Interferon Beta AE Flu-like symptoms Depression Pharmacology Dalfampridine Mechanism Potassium Channel Blocker Improves walking Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Smooth Muscle & CNS Drugs > Multiple Sclerosis Drugs alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Neurologic resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Multiple Sclerosis > Management PML Risk Natalizumab is highly effective but carries a risk of PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy if the patient is JC Virus positive. Natalizumab says NO to leukocytes entering the brain . Fingolimod & The Heart Fingolimod causes Bradycardia . You must monitor the patient s heart rate/EKG for 6 hours after the First Dose . Mitoxantrone Toxicity Like Doxorubicin Mitoxantrone is Cardiotoxic Dilated Cardiomyopathy . Must monitor Ejection Fraction. Pregnancy & MS Teriflunomide is Teratogenic . Glatiramer Acetate is generally considered the safest during pregnancy. Walking Drug Dalfampridine is the Walking Drug . It blocks Potassium Channels to prolong the action potential and improve conduction in demyelinated nerves."}, {"id": "l44", "title": "Lecture 44: Introduction to Ophthalmology", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l44 Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology n n Lecturer A. Nattis D.O. n Session Lecture 44 n n 1. The Red Eye n Conjunctivitis n Viral Pink Eye Watery discharge. Adenovirus. Highly contagious. n Bacterial Purulent pus discharge. Glued shut in AM. n Allergic Itching Pruritus . Bilateral. n Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma n Presentation Severe eye pain Headache Nausea/Vomiting Haloes around lights . n Exam Mid-dilated fixed pupil . Cloudy cornea. High IOP. Emergency . n Uveitis Iritis n Presentation Deep pain Photophobia Light sensitivity Redness. n Exam Cells and Flare in anterior chamber. Irregular pupil Synechiae . n Association Autoimmune HLA-B27 Sarcoidosis . n Scleritis n Presentation Deep boring pain. Wakes patient at night. n Association Rheumatoid Arthritis. n n 2. Neuro-Ophthalmology Pupils n Horner s Syndrome n Triad Ptosis Droopy lid Miosis Small pupil Anhidrosis No sweat . n Cause Sympathetic pathway lesion Pancoast tumor Carotid dissection . n CN III Palsy n Presentation Eye is Down and Out . Ptosis. n Pupil If Dilated Blown it s a PCom Aneurysm Surgical Emergency . If pupil spared it s Ischemic Diabetes . n Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD / Marcus Gunn Pupil n Test Swinging Flashlight Test. n Result Pupil appears to Dilate when light is swung to the bad eye because it senses less light than the good eye . n Cause Optic Nerve lesion Optic Neuritis . n Argyll Robertson Pupil n Sign Accommodates but does not react to light Prostitute s Pupil . n Cause Neurosyphilis. n n 3. Retina & Optic Nerve n Retinal Detachment n Symptoms Flashes and Floaters . Curtain coming down over vision. n Tx Surgical Emergency. n Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO n Sign Cherry Red Spot on macula. Pale retina. Sudden painless vision loss. n Cause Embolus Stroke of the eye . n Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO n Sign Blood and Thunder Diffuse hemorrhages . n Optic Neuritis n Sign Painful vision loss. RAPD + . Associated with MS . n Giant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis n Sign Ischemic Optic Neuropathy AION . Jaw claudication. Scalp tenderness. high ESR. n Tx High Dose Steroids immediately to save the other eye. Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden painless loss of vision in his right eye. Fundoscopy reveals a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Central Retinal Vein Occlusion Retinal Detachment Central Retinal Artery Occlusion Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Optic Neuritis 2 Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO presents with a pale retina edema and a Cherry Red Spot thin fovea showing choroid . It is an embolic event. A patient presents with a painful red eye nausea and halos around lights. The pupil is mid-dilated and non-reactive. What is the immediate concern? Viral Conjunctivitis Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Uveitis Scleritis Corneal Abrasion 1 Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma is an ocular emergency characterized by high pressure pain halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Which finding is part of the classic triad of Horner s Syndrome? Mydriasis Dilated Pupil Exophthalmos Bulging Eye Anhidrosis Lack of sweating Diplopia Double vision Hyperhidrosis Excess sweating 2 Horner s Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis on the ipsilateral face . Does the pupil in a CN III palsy due to a PCom aneurysm typically present as dilated or constricted? Constricted Miotic Dilated Mydriatic Normal Irregular Pear-shaped 1 Parasympathetic fibers run on the outside of CN III. Compressive lesions like an PCom Aneurysm compress these fibers first causing a Dilated pupil. A patient with MS presents with pain on eye movement and decreased color vision. Swinging flashlight test shows a Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD . Diagnosis? Papilledema Optic Neuritis Glaucoma Cataract Macular Degeneration 1 Optic Neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve common in MS. It causes painful vision loss and an RAPD . Horner s Syndrome Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Ophthalmology Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Symptoms Pain Haloes Nausea Fixed Mid-dilated Pupil Ophthalmology Cherry Red Spot Diagnosis Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO Ophthalmology Blood and Thunder Fundus Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO Ophthalmology Retinal Detachment Symptoms Flashes Floaters Curtain falling Ophthalmology CN3 Palsy Appearance Down and Out Ophthalmology Painful Vision Loss + MS Optic Neuritis Ophthalmology Painless Vision Loss + Jaw Claudication Giant Cell Arteritis AION Ophthalmology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Ophthalmology > Red Eye / Retina alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Ophthalmology resource Pathoma chapter Eye Pathology resource BandB chapter Neurology > The Eye > Ophthalmology Basics Horner s vs CN3 Palsy Horner s Pupil is Small Miosis . Ptosis is mild. n CN3 Palsy Pupil is Large Dilated . Ptosis is severe Eye shut . The Painful Red Eye Sudden pain + Haloes + Hard eye = Acute Glaucoma . nPhotophobia + Autoimmune Hx = Uveitis . nContact Lens + Sleeping in them = Corneal Ulcer Pseudomonas . Retinal Detachment Warning New onset Flashes and Floaters is a Retinal Detachment until proven otherwise. Papilledema Bilateral Optic Disc Swelling due to increased Intracranial Pressure. In an obese young female think Pseudotumor Cerebri IIH . Hutchinson s Sign Shingles vesicle on the tip of the nose predicts ocular involvement Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus ."}, {"id": "l45", "title": "Lecture 45: Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l45 Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions n n Lecturers J. Xie Ph.D. & K. Poole Ph.D. n Session Lecture 45 n n 1. Brainstem Vascular Syndromes n Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery or Vertebral Artery. n Key Structures & Symptoms n Nucleus Ambiguus CN 9 10 Dysphagia Hoarseness Decreased Gang Reflex. Specific to Lateral . n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus Vomiting. n Sympathetic Tract Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Loss of Pain/Temp on Ipsilateral Face . n Spinothalamic Tract Loss of Pain/Temp on Contralateral Body . n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Ataxia Fall toward lesion . n Mnemonic Don t PICA horse hoarseness that can t eat dysphagia . n Medial Medullary Syndrome n Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA . n Key Structures & Symptoms n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis Upper & Lower limbs . n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral loss of Proprioception/Vibration. n CN XII Hypoglossal Tongue deviates Toward the lesion Lick your wounds . n Medial Pontine Syndrome n Artery Paramedian branches of Basilar Artery. n Key Contralateral Hemiparesis + CN VI Abducens palsy Eye can t look out . n n 2. Cranial Nerve & Vessel Associations n CN III Oculomotor n Runs between Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA and Superior Cerebellar Artery SCA . n Susceptible to PCom Aneurysms → Dilated Pupil + Down & Out Eye. n n 3. Cerebellar Lesions n Lateral Cerebellum Hemispheres n Affects Limbs . n Signs Dysdiadochokinesia Intention Tremor Dysmetria. n Side Ipsilateral fall/ataxia. n Medial Cerebellum Vermis/Flocculonodular n Affects Trunk . n Signs Truncal Ataxia Wide-based gait Nystagmus. n Alcohol Specific damage to Vermis . n n 4. Spinal Cord Tracts Review n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML n Function Touch Vibration Proprioception. n Crossing Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic Tract STT n Function Pain Temperature. n Crossing Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure 1-2 levels up . n Corticospinal Tract CST n Function Voluntary Motor. n Crossing Medulla Pyramids . Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset vertigo vomiting and difficulty swallowing dysphagia . Exam shows a droopy left eyelid small left pupil loss of pain sensation on the left face and loss of pain sensation on the right body. Where is the lesion? Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Medial Pons Left Lateral Pons 1 Left Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg Ipsilateral Horner s Ipsilateral Face sensory loss Contralateral Body sensory loss Dysphagia Nucleus Ambiguus . Lesion is on the Left . A patient presents with right-sided hemiparesis arm and leg and the tongue deviates to the left upon protrusion. Sensation of pain and temperature is intact. Which artery is likely occluded? Left PICA Right PICA Left Anterior Spinal Artery Right Anterior Spinal Artery Basilar Artery 2 Medial Medullary Syndrome Tongue deviates Toward lesion Left CN XII . Hemiparesis is Contralateral Right side . Artery is the Anterior Spinal Artery . Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration primarily affects which structure resulting in a wide-based ataxic gait? Flocculus Lateral Hemispheres Vermis Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 2 Chronic alcohol use causes atrophy of the Anterior Vermis leading to truncal ataxia and gait instability legs/trunk often sparing the arms. A cavernous sinus thrombosis would most likely affect which cranial nerve traveling through the sinus? Optic Nerve CN II Abducens Nerve CN VI Facial Nerve CN VII Vestibulocochlear Nerve CN VIII Hypoglossal Nerve CN XII 1 CN VI Abducens travels inside the cavernous sinus along with the Carotid Artery . CN III IV V1 V2 are in the lateral wall. CN VI is often the first affected. In Brown-S u00e9quard syndrome spinal cord hemisection pain and temperature sensation are lost Ipsilateral to the lesion at the level of the lesion Contralateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Ipsilateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Bilaterally at the level of the lesion only Completely preserved 1 Spinothalamic tract crosses almost immediately. A hemisection causes Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp starting 1-2 segments below the lesion. Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Wallenberg Syndrome Key Symptom Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus Neurology Medial Medullary Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Neurology Medial Medullary Key Sign Tongue deviation to lesion side + Contralateral Hemiparesis Neurology Cerebellar Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk walk Neurology Cerebellar Hemisphere Lesion Limb Ataxia Finger-to-Nose Neurology CN 3 Palsy + Dilated Pupil PCom Aneurysm Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Brainstem Syndromes alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Brainstem resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Brainstem resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Brainstem Rule of 4s Medial vs Lateral Medial structures Motor pathway CST Medial Lemniscus MLF Motor CNs 3 4 6 12 . n Lateral structures Spinothalamic Spinocerebellar Spinal Trigeminal Sympathetic Sensory CNs 8 . Wallenberg Hallmark The ONLY brainstem stroke with Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus is Lateral Medullary PICA . Tongue Deviation CN XII lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . This is the hallmark of Medial Medullary Syndrome . PCom Aneurysm A Blown Pupil Dilated with a CN III palsy is a PCom Aneurysm until proven otherwise. Ischemic CN III palsy Diabetes spares the pupil. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral = L imbs Arm/Leg ataxia . n Medial Ver M is = Midline Trunk/Gait ataxia ."}, {"id": "l46", "title": "Lecture 46: Sensory System & Special Techniques", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l46 Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 46 n n 1. Sensory Pathways Review n Spinothalamic Tract n Modality Pain Temperature Crude Touch. n Fibers Small Unmyelinated/Thinly myelinated. n Posterior Columns DCML n Modality Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. n Fibers Large Heavily myelinated. n Testing Always test Distal first Toes/Fingers . If normal distally proximal is likely normal. n n 2. Cortical Sensation Discriminative n Requires Intact primary sensation DCML/STT + Intact Parietal Lobe Sensory Cortex . n Stereognosis Identify common object key coin in hand with eyes closed. n Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm. n Two-Point Discrimination Distinguish two points vs one. n Extinction Touch both sides simultaneously. If patient only feels one side suspects contralateral parietal lesion Neglect . n n 3. Meningeal Signs Meningitis/SAH n Brudzinski Sign n Maneuver Flex patient s neck chin to chest . n Positive Involuntary flexion of Hips and Knees . n Kernig Sign n Maneuver Flex hip/knee to 90 degrees then slowly Extend the knee. n Positive Pain/Resistance in hamstring. n n 4. Special Musculoskeletal Tests n Cervical Spine Radiculopathy n Spurling Test Extend and Rotate head to affected side + Axial Compression. Positive = Radiation of pain down arm. n Distraction Test Lift head Traction . Positive = Relief of pain. n Lumbar Spine Sciatica/Disc Herniation n Straight Leg Raise SLR Passive leg raise. Positive = Radicular pain between 30-70 degrees. n Bragard s Test SLR + Dorsiflexion of foot. Increases nerve stretch. n Crossed Well SLR Raise the Unaffected leg. Positive = Pain in the Affected leg. Highly specific for Disc Herniation . n Malingering / Functional n Hoover Test Cup both heels. Ask patient to lift the paralyzed leg. n Positive for Malingering You do NOT feel downward pressure from the normal heel. Normal physiology requires pushing down with the opposite leg to lift one leg . Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques Neurology questions You are performing a neurological exam on a patient claiming to have a paralyzed right leg. You cup your hands under both heels and ask him to lift his right leg. He strains but his leg doesn t move. You feel no pressure in your left hand under his left heel. What is the interpretation? True Right Leg Paralysis Left Leg Paralysis Positive Hoover Sign Lack of Effort Sciatic Nerve Impingement Cauda Equina Syndrome 2 Positive Hoover Sign Lack of downward pressure in the contralateral normal heel indicates the patient is not making a genuine effort to lift the affected leg. Which physical exam finding is most specific for a lumbar disc herniation? Positive Straight Leg Raise Ipsilateral Positive Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise Paraspinal muscle tenderness Decreased range of motion Negative Brudzinski sign 1 While the Ipsilateral SLR is sensitive the Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise pain in the affected leg when lifting the healthy leg is highly Specific for disc herniation. A patient presents with fever and severe headache. When you flex the patient s neck their hips and knees spontaneously flex. This is a positive Kernig Sign Brudzinski Sign Babinski Sign Lhermitte Sign Hoffmann Sign 1 Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion causes involuntary hip/knee flexion. It suggests meningeal irritation Meningitis . A patient complains of shooting pain down their right arm. You extend and rotate their head to the right and apply downward pressure. This maneuver reproduces the pain. What is the test? Distraction Test Spurling Test Tinel Sign Phalen Test Lhermitte Sign 1 Spurling Test Cervical compression with extension/rotation narrows the neural foramen. Reproduction of radicular pain indicates Cervical Radiculopathy. Which sensory modality is carried by the Dorsal Columns Posterior Columns ? Pain Temperature Crude Touch Vibration and Proprioception Itch 3 Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Spinothalamic = Pain Temp Crude Touch . Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion → Hip/Knee flexion Meningitis Neurology Kernig Sign Knee extension → Pain Meningitis Neurology Spurling Test Cervical Compression → Radicular Pain Radiculopathy Neurology Hoover Test Test for Malingering Lack of contralateral heel pressure Neurology Crossed Straight Leg Raise Lift Good Leg → Pain in Bad Leg Specific for Disc Herniation Neurology Stereognosis Identify object in hand Parietal Cortex Neurology Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm Parietal Cortex Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neurological Examination alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Exam resource BandB chapter Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Sensory Exam Hoover Test Logic In a True effort to lift one leg the other leg MUST push down. If you don t feel downward pressure from the good leg when they try to lift the bad leg they aren t really trying. BrudZinski vs Kernig B rudzinski = B rain Neck flexion causes knees to bend . n K ernig = K nee Knee extension causes pain . Spurling Test think Spurling crushes the Spine . Compression reproduces radicular pain. Distraction relieves it. Crossed SLR The Crossed Straight Leg Raise lifting the good leg causes pain in the bad leg is very Specific for a Herniated Disc. Stocking-Glove The classic pattern of Diabetic Polyneuropathy. Starts in toes/feet longest nerves and ascends. Sensory loss is symmetrical."}, {"id": "l48", "title": "Lecture 48: Pharmacology: Sedative Hypnotics", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l48 Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics Neurology Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics n n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. n Session Lecture 48 n n 1. Mechanisms of CNS Depression n Story Mode nTo induce sedation or sleep we typically need to calm down the brain s excitatory pathways. The most effective way to do this is by enhancing the activity of GABA Gamma-Aminobutyric Acid the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. When GABA binds to its receptor it opens a chloride channel allowing negatively charged chloride ions $Cl^-$ to flow into the neuron. This causes hyperpolarization making the neuron less likelytor complex but interact with it in distinct ways that determine their safety profile. n n 1.1 GABA-A Receptor Interactions n GABA Dependent vs. Independent n Benzodiazepines & Z-Drugs n Bind to a specific site between $ alpha$ and $ gamma$ subunits . n Require GABA to be present to open the channel. n Effect Increase the frequency of channel opening. n Safety Exhibit a Ceiling Effect — once receptors are saturated they can t open more channels without GABA. Less risk of fatal respiratory depression alone. n Barbiturates & Ethanol n Bind to a distinct site Barbiturates between $ alpha$ and $ beta$ . n Do NOT require GABA at high doses can open the channel independently . n Effect Increase the duration of channel opening. n Safety No Ceiling Effect . Can cause profound CNS depression coma and fatal respiratory depression as dose increases. n n 1.2 Dose-Response Relationship n Linear Slope Drug A - Barbiturates/Alcohol n Steep linear increase in CNS depression with dose. n Sedation $ rightarrow$ Hypnosis $ rightarrow$ Anesthesia $ rightarrow$ Coma $ rightarrow$ Death. n Plateau Effect Drug B - Benzodiazepines n Curve flattens out. n Significant sedation but reduced risk of lethal respiratory depression unless combined with other depressants . n n correlate n Drug-Drug Interactions & Respiratory Depression n The Lethal Combo nWhile Benzodiazepines rarely cause fatal respiratory depression on their own due to the ceiling effect combining them with other CNS depressants removes this safety net. n Mechanism Synergistic effects on GABA/respiratory centers. n Combinations to Avoid n Benzodiazepines + Alcohol n Benzodiazepines + Opioids Fentanyl Heroin Oxycodone n Benzodiazepines + Barbiturates n Clinical Consequence A patient taking Xanax Alprazolam who drinks alcohol at a party runs a significantly higher risk of stopping breathing. n n n--- n n 2. Barbiturates n Story Mode nOnce the mainstay of sedation Barbiturates -barbital have largely been replaced by Benzodiazepines due to their narrow therapeutic index and high abuse potential. However they retain niche uses in neurology epilepsy and anesthesia. Their ability to induce liver enzymes also makes them practically troublesome for drug interactions. n n 2.1 Key Agents & Uses n Phenobarbital n Use Seizure disorders Epilepsy . First-line for neonatal seizures . n Thiopental n Use Induction of anesthesia rapid onset . n Mechanism Highly lipid-soluble $ rightarrow$ crosses Blood-Brain Barrier BBB instantly. n Primidone Metabolized to Phenobarbital. n n 2.2 Pharmacokinetics n Metabolism n Hepatic metabolism. n CYP Inducers especially CYP3A4 Increases metabolism of other drugs e.g. Oral Contraceptives $ rightarrow$ unwanted pregnancy; Warfarin $ rightarrow$ clotting risk . n Redistribution Thiopental n Rapid onset due to high blood flow to the brain. n Short duration not because of metabolism but because the drug redistributes to less vascular tissues muscle fat . n n 2.3 Adverse Effects n Respiratory Depression Fatal in overdose supported ventilation required; alkalize urine to trap and excrete . n Sedation/Hangover Especially in elderly. n Tolerance & Dependence n Metabolic Tolerance Enzyme induction leads to faster breakdown. n Pharmacodynamic Tolerance Receptor downregulation. n Contraindication Porphyria induces ALA synthase $ rightarrow$ heme synthesis $ rightarrow$ toxic porphyrin accumulation . n n correlate n Porphyria Exacerbation n The Mechanism nBarbiturates induce Cytochrome P450 enzymes. The synthesis of these enzymes requires heme . The body responds by ramping up heme production via ALA synthase. n In Porphyria Patients They have a genetic block in the heme pathway. Ramping up synthesis upstream leads to a toxic buildup of porphyrin intermediates. n Symptoms Abdominal pain psychiatric symptoms madness red/brown urine. n Rule Never give Barbiturates to a patient with acute intermittent porphyria. n n n--- n n 3. Benzodiazepines n Story Mode nBenzodiazepines -zolam -zepam are the modern workhorses for anxiety acute seizure control and alcohol withdrawal. By requiring GABA to work they offer a safer profile. Choice of agent often depends on pharmacokinetics half-life and metabolic pathway especially in patients with fragile livers like the elderly. n n 3.1 Agents & Classification n Long-Acting n Diazepam Valium Chlordiazepoxide n Use Alcohol withdrawal smoother taper seizures muscle spasm. n Metabolism Converted to active metabolites e.g. desmethyldiazepam with very long half-lives 40-100 hours . n Intermediate/Short-Acting n Lorazepam Ativan Status epilepticus anxiety. n Alprazolam Xanax Panic disorder rapid onset high abuse potential. n Midazolam Versed Procedural sedation/anesthesia colonoscopy . Causes Anterograde Amnesia . n n 3.2 Metabolism & The Elderly n The Problem Elderly patients have reduced Phase I oxidative metabolism and renal function. Long-acting benzos Diazepam accumulate $ rightarrow$ falls confusion pseudodementia . n The Solution OTL Use agents that undergo only Phase II Conjugation/Gluronidation . n O xazepam n T emazepam n L orazepam n n 3.3 Antidote Flumazenil n Mechanism Competitive antagonist at the Benzodiazepine binding site. n Use Reversal of Benzodiazepine overdose e.g. post-surgery sedation . n Warning Can precipitate acute withdrawal seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users. Administer with extreme caution. n n correlate n Floppy Baby Syndrome n Benzos in Pregnancy nBenzodiazepines cross the placenta. n Effect Neonatal CNS depression. n Presentation Hypotonia rag doll appearance lethargy difficulty sucking/feeding respiratory depression. n Contraindication Avoid benzos in pregnancy and breastfeeding if possible. n n n--- n n 4. Z-Drugs & Insomnia n Story Mode nFor patients who just need sleep without the anxiolytic muscle relaxant or anticonvulsant effects of benzos we use Z-Drugs . They are structurally different but bind to a specific subtype of the benzodiazepine receptor BZ1/omega-1 that mediates sedation. n n 4.1 Agents n Zolpidem Ambien n Zaleplon Sonata n Eszopiclone Lunesta n n 4.2 Key Features n Mechanism Agonist at BZ1 receptor subtype. n Effect Induces sleep with minimal effect on sleep architecture REM/Slow Wave Sleep preserved better than benzos . n Pharmacokinetics Short half-life $ rightarrow$ less daytime hangover . n Adverse Effects Sleep-walking/driving/eating behaviors. n Reversal Can be reversed by Flumazenil . n n 4.3 Melatonin Agonists n Ramelteon Tasimelteon n Mechanism Agonists at MT1/MT2 receptors in Suprachiasmatic Nucleus. n Use Sleep-onset insomnia Non-24 blind patients . n Benefit No abuse potential non-controlled no withdrawal. n n--- n n 5. Ethanol Alcohol n Story Mode nEthanol is a dirty drug that hits multiple targets. It potentiates GABA sedation and inhibits NMDA/Glutamate loss of memory/excitation . Chronic use leads to a compensatory upregulation of NMDA receptors which explains the dangerous hyperexcitability seen in withdrawal. n n 5.1 Metabolism n1. Ethanol $ xrightarrow Alcohol Dehydrogenase $ Acetaldehyde Toxic flushing nausea n2. Acetaldehyde $ xrightarrow Aldehyde Dehydrogenase $ Acetate Excreted n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymatic pathways saturate easily. Rate of elimination is constant regardless of concentration. n n 5.2 Alcohol Withdrawal n Mechanism Chronic suppression of NMDA + upregulation of receptors. Abrupt stop $ rightarrow$ unopposed glutamate excitation. n Symptoms n 6-12h Tremors anxiety insomnia. n 12-48h Seizures hallucinosis. n 48-96h Delirium Tremens DTs - Autonomic instability BP/HR spikes delirium fatal if untreated. n Treatment n Benzodiazepines Long-acting Chlordiazepoxide/Diazepam to taper Lorazepam if liver failure . n n 5.3 Alcohol Use Disorder Treatments n Disulfiram Inhibits Aldehyde Dehydrogenase $ rightarrow$ Acetaldehyde buildup upon drinking $ rightarrow$ sickness negative reinforcement . n Naltrexone Opioid antagonist $ rightarrow$ reduces reward /craving properties of alcohol dopamine link . n Acamprosate NMDA antagonist/GABA modulator $ rightarrow$ reduces craving/withdrawal dysphoria. n n correlate n Fetal Alcohol Syndrome FAS n Mechanism Maternal alcohol assumption creates teratogenic accumulation of acetaldehyde and ROS. n Presentation n Growth Retardation IUGR . n Facial Dysmorphia Smooth philtrum thin upper lip small eye openings microphthalmia . n CNS Microcephaly intellectual disability behavioral issues. n Safe Limit There is no known safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy. n n n--- n n 6. Narcolepsy & Stimulants n Story Mode nIf the problem is too much sleep Narcolepsy or inability to focus ADHD we need to ramp up Norepinephrine and Dopamine. Note that one unique treatment Sodium Oxybate is actually a profound depressant used to force deep sleep at night to improve daytime wakefulness. n n 6.1 Stimulants n Amphetamines Adderall / Methylphenidate Ritalin n Mechanism Block reuptake AND increase release of NE/DA. n Use ADHD Narcolepsy. n SE Tachycardia hypertension anxiety appetite suppression weight loss growth stunting in kids. n Modafinil n Mechanism Unclear likely increases DA release; effects on Orexin/Histamine. n Use Narcolepsy Shift-Work Sleep Disorder. n Benefit Wakefulness promoting with less sympathomimetic jittery side effects than amphetamines. n n 6.2 Sodium Oxybate GHB n Mechanism GABA-B agonist. n Use Narcolepsy with Cataplexy sudden loss of muscle tone . n Effect Consolidates deep sleep at night $ rightarrow$ improved daytime alertness. n Safety Date Drug potential. highly restricted REMS program . CNS depression risk. n n--- n n 7. Transcript-Slide Gap Analysis n| Topic | Transcript Emphasis Included | Slide Only Excluded | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Elderly & Metabolism | Strong emphasis on why Lorazepam is preferred conjugation only and Diazepam is avoided active metabolites . | Simply listing metabolic pathways without the clinical why . | n| Redistribution | Detailed explanation of Thiopental kinetics Brain $ rightarrow$ Muscle/Fat is why you wake up NOT metabolism. | Charts of distribution kinetics. | n| Situational Anxiety | Specific mention of Propranolol for stage fright violinist example to block peripheral symptoms without sedation. | General list of beta-blocker uses. | n| Fomepizole | Mentioned as antidote for Methanol/Ethylene Glycol antifreeze poisoning primarily distinct from Ethanol treatment. | Chemical structures of alcohols. | n| Date Drugs | Context on Flunitrazepam Rohypnol and GHB causing anterograde amnesia as a mechanism for assault. | Generic list of illicit substances. | n n--- n n 8. AnKing & Resources n Primary n Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology Sedative-Hypnotics n Alternatives n Boards and Beyond Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics The Relaxing Barbershop for Barbs Ben s Diner for Benzos . n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Alcohol Drugs Al-Kohol s Bar . n First Aid CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics & Alcohol. n n--- n n 9. High Yield Slides n Mechanism of Action Diagrams Showing the GABA-A receptor and binding sites for Benzodiazepines vs. Barbiturates. n Dose-Response Curves The linear line Barbs/Coma vs. the plateau line Benzos/Safety . n Metabolic Pathways Diagram showing Phase I vs Phase II highlighting the OTL benzos skipping Phase I. n Alcohol Withdrawal Timeline Timeline showing Tremors $ rightarrow$ Seizures $ rightarrow$ DTs. n Narcolepsy Treatment Algorithm Flowchart involving Modafinil/Stimulants/Sodium Oxybate. questions A 75-year-old female requires a benzodiazepine for anxiety. Which agent is most appropriate to minimize the risk of accumulation falls? Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam Clorazepate Flurazepam 2 Lorazepam is an OTL benzo that only undergoes Phase II metabolism conjugation which is preserved in aging. Others have long-acting metabolites. Which sedative-hypnotic drug overdose can be reversed with Flumazenil? Phenobarbital Ethanol Zolpidem Thiopental Sodium Oxybate 2 Zolpidem a Z-drug acts at the Benzodiazepine binding site and CAN be antagonized by Flumazenil. Barbiturates Alcohol and GHB work at different sites/mechanisms. Which Benzodiazepines are safe in liver failure/elderly? OTL Oxazepam Temazepam Lorazepam Pharmacology Mechanism of Barbiturates Potentiate GABA-A Increase Duration of opening Pharmacology Mechanism of Benzodiazepines Potentiate GABA-A Increase Frequency of opening Pharmacology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics Sketchy CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics OTL for Elderly The OTL benzos O xazepam T emazepam L orazepam bypass Phase I oxidation and only undergo Phase II conjugation. This makes them safer for the elderly. Barbs vs Benzos BarbiDURATes increase DURATion . n Frenzodiazepines increase FREQquency . Redistribution You wake up from Thiopental NOT because it s metabolized but because it leaves the brain to go into fat/muscle Redistribution ."}, {"id": "l49", "title": "Lecture 49: Sleep and Sleep Disturbances", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l49 Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances Neurology Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances n n 1. Sleep Physiology n Story Mode nSleep isn t just a passive off switch; it s a highly active metabolic state essential for clearing neurotoxins like amyloid-beta and tau and consolidating memory. It follows a circadian rhythm 24-hour cycle driven by internal biological clocks. Interestingly without external cues sunlight our natural rhythm drifts to about 25 hours. This cycle orchestrates a shift from the catabolic/feeding state of wakefulness to the anabolic/fasting state of sleep prioritizing growth hormone secretion and oxidative repair. n n 1.1 Sleep Stages & EEG Architecture Slide 97 n Wakefulness n EEG Alpha waves 8-13 Hz when awake at rest; Beta waves >13 Hz when active. n Tone High muscle tone. n N1 Light Sleep n EEG Theta waves 4-7 Hz . Slowing of brain activity. n Signs Eyelids droop pupils constrict slow roving eye movements. n N2 Intermediate Sleep n EEG Sleep Spindles bursts of fast activity and K-complexes on a background of Theta. n Significance Most of total sleep time. n N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep n EEG Delta waves <4 Hz slow and high amplitude . n Function Physical restoration clearance of neurotoxins. Hardest to wake from. n REM Rapid Eye Movement n EEG Sawtooth/Erratic waves looks like wakefulness - Paradoxical Sleep . n Physiology Slide 154 n Brain Vivid illogical dreaming. n Body Atonia near-complete paralysis to prevent acting out dreams. n Autonomic Instability irregular HR/BP loss of thermoregulation. n Extraocular Muscles The only muscles that move rapid bursts . n n 1.2 Sleep Architecture & Latency n Cycles We do NOT cycle 1->2->3->REM->3->2->1 sequentially. Cycles vary; REM predominates later in the night early morning . n Sleep Latency Time from lights out to N1. n REM Latency Time from Sleep Onset to first REM period. n Clinical Relevance Short REM latency is a hallmark of Narcolepsy and Depression . n n--- n n 2. Dyssomnias Disorders of Timing/Quality n Story Mode nDyssomnias are problems with getting to sleep staying asleep or waking up. They can be intrinsic primary or extrinsic secondary to environment/illness . The most common offender is Insomnia which is often a symptom of poor habits sleep hygiene rather than a primary disease. n n 2.1 Insomnia Slide 192 n Definition Difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep resulting in daytime fatigue. n Acute Common 95% of adults often stress-related. n Chronic >3 months affects 10% of adults. n Secondary Causes 90% n Psychiatric Depression Anxiety PTSD. n Medical COPD Heart Failure PND GERD Pain. n Substances Caffeine Alcohol disrupts deep sleep Steroids Diuretics nocturia . n Treatment Hierarchy n 1. Treat Underlying Cause e.g. control pain stop caffeine . n 2. Sleep Hygiene Gold Standard n Same wake/sleep time daily. n Bedroom = Sleep & Sex ONLY no screens/stress . n Avoid late meals/alcohol. n 3. Pharmacology n First Line Z-Drugs Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone or Melatonin agonists Ramelteon . n Second Line Benzodiazepines Avoid in elderly due to falls; decreases N3 sleep . n Last Resort Antipsychotics/Antidepressants High side effects . n n 2.2 Narcolepsy Slide 315 n Story Mode nImagine REM sleep—with its paralysis and vivid dreams—suddenly intruding into your waking life. That is Narcolepsy. You might collapse while laughing Cataplexy because the REM paralysis switch got flipped while you were awake. n Pathophysiology Loss of Orexin Hypocretin producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. n Genetic Link Strong association with HLA-DQB1 0602 . n Tetrad of Symptoms n 1. Excessive Daytime Sleepiness Sleep Attacks . n 2. Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion laughter surprise . n 3. Sleep Paralysis Inability to move upon waking/falling asleep. n 4. Hallucinations n Hypnagogic Go-ing to sleep. n Hypnopompic Pop-ping out of bed waking . n Diagnosis Polysomnogram showing Short REM Latency Sleep Onset REM periods . n Treatment Stimulants Modafinil Methylphenidate + Venlafaxine/SSRI for Cataplexy . n n 2.3 Obstructive Sleep Apnea OSA Slide 349 n Mechanism Soft tissue tongue/palate collapses during sleep $ rightarrow$ Airway obstruction $ rightarrow$ Hypoxia $ rightarrow$ Arousal. Prevents deep sleep. n Risk Factors Obesity Large Neck Circumference Male Gender Retrognathia small jaw . n Presentation Snoring witness apnea partner usually reports it morning headaches daytime somnolence. n Diagnosis Polysomnogram Sleep Study . n Treatment n CPAP Continuous Positive Airway Pressure Pneumatic splint to keep airway open. n Weight loss. n Surgery rarely . n n 2.4 Restless Leg Syndrome RLS Slide 268 n Symptom Uncomfortable sensation creeping/crawling in legs at rest relieved by movement . n Timing Worse at night/evening. n Secondary Causes Iron Deficiency Pregnancy Renal Failure. n Treatment n Check Iron Ferritin levels first! n Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Gabapentin/Pregabalin. n n correlate n Circadian Rhythm Disorders n Jet Lag n Mismatch between internal clock and external time. n Treatment Melatonin; adapt to destination light/dark cycle immediately. n n Delayed Sleep Phase n Night Owls . Sleep 2am - 10am. Common in adolescents. n Treatment Light therapy in morning. n n Advanced Sleep Phase n Early Birds . Sleep 8pm - 4am. Common in elderly. n n n--- n n 3. Parasomnias Disorders of Behavior n Story Mode nParasomnias are unwanted behaviors occurring during sleep. The key distinction is WHEN they occur Non-REM N3 vs. REM. N3 parasomnias are disorders of arousal where the brain is half-awake but memory is off. REM parasomnias are disorders of disinhibition where the paralysis that usually protects us fails. n n 3.1 Non-REM Parasomnias Stage N3 n Sleep Terrors Pavor Nocturnus n Who Children 1% . n Event Child screams looks terrified autonomic markers high HR sweating . Cannot be consoled . n Outcome Goes back to sleep. Amnesia for the event in the morning. n Tx Reassurance grow out of it . n Sleepwalking Somnambulism n Who Children up to 30% rarely adults. n Event Motor activity walking eating driving while asleep. n Risk Injury/Safety. n Tx Safety measures locks/alarms . Benzodiazepines if severe/refractory. n n 3.2 REM Parasomnias n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD n Mechanism Loss of normal REM atonia paralysis . n Event Patient acts out vivid/violent dreams punching kicking yelling . n Risk Injury to self or bed partner. n Clinical Pearl Highly predictive of future Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Disease Lewy Body Dementia . n Tx Clonazepam Melatonin. n n--- n n 4. Sleep Deprivation & Safety n Story Mode nA tired doctor is a dangerous doctor. Sleep deprivation specifically impairs the Prefrontal Cortex the center for judgment and executive function. This leads to rigid thinking inability to adapt to new info and increased risk-taking—a recipe for medical errors. n n 4.1 Consequences n1. Cognitive 4-5 hours sleep/night causes cumulative impairment equal to intoxication. Caffeine does NOT fix judgment errors. n2. Immune <7 hours sleep = 3x risk of Common Cold. Reduced vaccine response. n3. Fatal Familial Insomnia Prion disease. Progressive insomnia $ rightarrow$ Death. Proves sleep is vital for life . n n highyield n The Intern Studies n House Staff & Driving nResidents working frequent 24+ hour shifts are a high-risk population. n 49% reported falling asleep at the wheel. n 90% of these events occurred post-call . n Result Increased MVAs and Near-Misses. n Takeaway You cannot train yourself to not need sleep. It is a biological imperative for safety. n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a complaint from his wife that he fights in his sleep. She reports he punches and yells and recently fell out of bed. When awakened he vividly recalls a dream where he was fighting off an attacker. What is the most likely diagnosis? Sleep Terrors Somnambulism REM Sleep Behavior Disorder Obstructive Sleep Apnea Nocturnal Seizures 2 The patient acts out dreams due to a loss of REM atonia. Key features are the violent nature occurring in an older male and recall of the dream . This is distinct from Sleep Terrors children no recall and is a risk factor for Parkinson s. A 22-year-old medical student reports difficulty staying awake in class. She reports falling asleep instantly when she lies down. She also describes episodes of sudden weakness in her knees when she laughs at a joke. Which of the following is likely deficient in her CNS? Dopamine Serotonin Acetylcholine Orexin Hypocretin GABA 3 This is Narcolepsy . The presentation of Excessive Daytime Sleepiness + Cataplexy muscle weakness with emotion is classic. It is caused by the autoimmune destruction of Orexin-producing neurons in the hypothalamus. Which of the following sleep stages is correctly matched with its characteristic EEG waveform? Stage N1 - Delta Waves Stage N2 - Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes Stage N3 - Alpha Waves REM Sleep - Delta Waves Wakefulness Active - Theta Waves 1 Stage N2 is characterized by Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes on a Theta background. N1 has Theta. N3 has Delta Slow waves . REM has Sawtooth or Beta-like waves. Wakefulness has Alpha rest or Beta active . A 45-year-old female complains of an uncomfortable creeping sensation in her legs every evening that makes it hard to relax. Walking around the room relieves the feeling. What is the most appropriate initial diagnostic step? Polysomnogram Nerve Conduction Study Serum Ferritin level MRI of Lumbar Spine Trial of Benzodiazepines 2 This is Restless Leg Syndrome RLS . It is strongly associated with Iron Deficiency . Checking iron stores Ferritin is mandatory before starting symptomatic treatment with dopamine agonists or gabapentin. What is the effect of chronic sleep deprivation on the immune system? Increased antibody response to vaccines Decreased risk of upper respiratory infections Increased susceptibility to common cold Rhinovirus Enhanced T-cell proliferation No significant effect 2 Study data shows that <7 hours of sleep significantly increases the risk of contracting the common cold Rhinovirus and lowers antibody responses to vaccinations. What sleep stage do Sleep Terrors and Sleepwalking occur in? Stage N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep Neurology What sleep stage do Nightmares occur in? REM Sleep Patient remembers them Neurology Mechanism of Narcolepsy Loss of Orexin Hypocretin neurons in lateral hypothalamus Neurology What genetic allele is associated with Narcolepsy? HLA-DQB1 0602 Neurology Clinical Tetrad of Narcolepsy EDS Cataplexy Sleep Paralysis Hypnagogic/pompic Hallucinations Neurology REM Sleep Behavior Disorder is a precursor to which diseases? Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Lewy Body Dementia Neurology First-line pharmacotherapy for Insomnia after hygiene Z-Drugs Zolpidem etc. or Melatonin agonists Pharmacology Secondary causes of Restless Leg Syndrome Iron Deficiency Check Ferritin! Pregnancy Renal Failure Neurology Effect of Benzodiazepines on Sleep Architecture Decreases N3 Deep Sleep Pharmacology Diagnosis of frequent snoring + daytime sleepiness in obese male Obstructive Sleep Apnea Diagnose with Polysomnogram Neurology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Neurology > Sleep > Sleep Disorders Sketchy Path Neurology > Sleep First Aid Psychiatry > Sleep Disorders REM vs N3 Parasomnias N3 Deep Sleep Terrors Walking. NO Memory. Children. n REM Behavior Disorder Acting out dreams . Vivid Memory. Elderly Parkinson s risk . Cataplexy Trigger Cataplexy sudden loss of tone in Narcolepsy is specifically triggered by strong emotion most commonly laughter . Ferritin & RLS Always check Iron/Ferritin in clear-cut Restless Leg Syndrome. Iron deficiency is a reversible cause. Sleep Latency Definitions Sleep Latency Time to fall asleep High in Insomnia Low in Sleep Deprivation . n REM Latency Time to first dream Low in Narcolepsy & Depression . Z-Drugs Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone. They bind the BZ1 receptor subtype to induce sleep without the muscle relaxant/anticonvulsant effects of Benzos."}, {"id": "l5", "title": "Lecture #5: Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l5 Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging n n Lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Computed Tomography CT Slides 5-18 n Mechanism n Uses Helical X-ray beams to measure the attenuation density of tissues. n Hounsfield Units HU The scale of density. n Hyperdense White Bone +1000 HU Acute Hemorrhage Calcifications. n Isodense Gray Brain parenchyma. n Hypodense Black Air -1000 HU Fat CSF Water. n Windows Different software settings to highlight specific tissues e.g. Bone Window for fractures Brain Window for strokes . n n Clinical Indications n Neurologic Emergencies The workhorse of the ER. n Acute Hemorrhage Stroke/Trauma First-line test. Fast <30s . n Trauma Skull fractures. n Initial Stroke To rule out hemorrhage before giving tPA. n n CT Contrast Iodinated n Uses Infection Abscess Tumors Vascular pathology CTA . n Contraindications/Risks n 1. Contrast-Induced Nephropathy CIN Check Creatinine/GFR. Hydrate. n 2. Allergy Mild hives to Anaphylaxis. Pre-medicate with Steroids/Benadryl. n 3. Metformin Risk of Lactic Acidosis . Discontinue for 48 hours. n n--- n n 2. Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI Slides 19-27 n Mechanism n Uses strong magnetic fields to align protons. No Ionizing Radiation. n Sequences n T1 Fat is bright Anatomy . Good for structure. n T2 Water/CSF is bright Pathology . Good for Edema. n Diffusion Weighted Imaging DWI Most sensitive for Acute Ischemic Stroke shows cytotoxic edema within minutes . n n Clinical Indications n Soft Tissue Pathology Superior resolution for brain parenchyma. n Tumors Multiple Sclerosis Plaques Infections. n Posterior Fossa/Brainstem CT has bone artifact here; MRI does not. n n MRI Contrast Gadolinium n Risk Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . n Rare but serious fibrosis of skin/organs. n Contraindicated if GFR < 30 Severe Renal Failure . n n Summary CT vs. MRI n n| Feature | CT | MRI | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Speed | Fast < 1 min | Slow 30-45 mins | n| Cost | Cheaper | Expensive | n| Radiation | Yes X-ray | No | n| Bone | Excellent | Poor | n| Soft Tissue | Fair | Excellent | n| Contraindications | Pregnancy relative | Metal Implants Pacemakers | n n--- n n 3. Vascular Imaging Slides 28-38 n Carotid Duplex Ultrasound n Indication Screening for Carotid Artery Stenosis bruit TIA . n Pros Non-invasive No radiation Portable. n Cons Operator dependent. n n Conventional Angiography Digital Subtraction Angiography - DSA n Gold Standard for vascular pathology. n Procedure Catheter inserted femoral/radial → dye injected directly into vessels. n Usage n Diagnostic Aneurysms AVMs Vasculitis. n Therapeutic Coiling aneurysms Thrombectomy clot removal . n Risks Invasive stroke risk 1% access site bleeding. n n--- n n 4. Clinical Correlates Intracranial Hemorrhages Slides 47-53 n n correlate n Epidural Hematoma n Vessel Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery MMA branch of Maxillary . n Cause Trauma to Pterion Temple . Rarely crosses sutures. n CT Appearance Lens-shaped Biconvex hyperdensity. n Clinical Lucid Interval Knocked out → Fine → Rapid Decline/Death . n n n correlate n Subdural Hematoma n Vessel Rupture of Bridging Veins . n Cause Shearing forces falls in elderly alcoholics shaken baby . n CT Appearance Crescent-shaped hyperdensity. Crosses sutures. n Clinical Gradual onset Chronic or Acute. n n n correlate n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH n Vessel Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis or Trauma. n CT Appearance Hyperdensity in Sulci and Cisterns Starburst . n Clinical Thunderclap Headache Worst headache of my life Meningismus. n n n--- n n 5. Enhancement Patterns Slide 15 n Ring-Enhancing Lesion Central necrosis with peripheral enhancement. n Differential M.A.G.I.C. D.R. n R adiation necrosis Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 5 prev l4 next l6 pdf pdfs/Lecture 5. S. Zakhary D.O. PPT .pdf Neuroimaging n CT Scan n Physics X-ray Attenuation n Uses Acute Hemorrhage Bone Trauma n Contrast Iodine Risk Allergy Kidney n MRI n Physics Magnets T1/T2 n Uses Ischemia DWI Tumors MS n Contrast Gadolinium Risk NSF n Contra Metal Pacemaker n Angiography n DSA Gold Standard Vascular n Therapeutic Coiling n Ultrasound Carotid Stenosis screening n Pathology Signs n Ring Enhancement Abscess GBM n Hyperdense CT Acute Blood questions 1. A 65-year-old male presents to the ER with sudden onset right-sided weakness and aphasia starting 30 minutes ago. What is the most appropriate INITIAL imaging modality? Non-contrast CT Head MRI Brain with Gadolinium Carotid Ultrasound Conventional Angiography Lumbar Puncture 0 From Slide 48 Non-contrast CT is the first-line study for suspected stroke Code Stroke . It is fast <30s and rules out Hemorrhage . If no blood is seen the patient may be a candidate for tPA. MRI takes too long for the initial decision. 2. A diabetic patient taking Metformin is scheduled for a CT scan with IV contrast. What is the recommended management regarding their medication? Take Metformin as usual Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours BEFORE the scan Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours AFTER the scan Double the dose of Metformin Replace Metformin with Insulin permanently 2 From Slide 46/Slide 134 Metformin must be discontinued for 48 hours after contrast administration or before in elective cases to prevent the risk of Lactic Acidosis if contrast-induced nephropathy occurs. 3. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication for an MRI of the brain? Pregnancy 1st trimester Shellfish Allergy Cardiac Pacemaker Non-compatible Renal Failure GFR < 60 Claustrophobia 2 From Slide 23/Slide 57 Metallic Implants like older Pacemakers are an absolute contraindication due to the strong magnetic field. Allergy is an issue for Contrast not the MRI itself. Renal failure is an issue for Gadolinium not the MRI itself. 4. A patient with severe renal failure GFR < 30 requires neuroimaging. You specifically avoid Gadolinium-based contrast due to the risk of which condition? Anaphylaxis Lactic Acidosis Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Acute Tubular Necrosis Stevens-Johnson Syndrome 2 From Slide 27 Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 is associated with Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF a rare but serious scleroderma-like fibrosing disease. 5. Ring- Enhancing Lesion on a contrast CT or MRI is classically associated with which of the following pathologies? Multiple Sclerosis Brain Abscess Ischemic Stroke Acute Meningioma Subdural Hematoma 1 From Slide 15 The classic differential for a Ring-Enhancing Lesion is Abscess or Glioblastoma/Metastasis . Meningiomas typically show diffuse homogeneous enhancement dural tail . First-line imaging for acute stroke? Non-contrast CT Head Radiology What does hyperdense white material on CT usually represent? Bone or Acute Blood Radiology Risk of Gadolinium in renal failure? Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Pathology Classic appearance of Abscess on contrast MRI? Ring-Enhancing Lesion Radiology Contraindication for MRI? Metal Implants Pacemaker Safety Best modality for Acute Ischemic Stroke most sensitive ? Diffusion Weighted MRI DWI Radiology Metformin management with Contrast? Hold for 48 hours Risk of Lactic Acidosis Pharm Appearance of Meningioma with contrast? Diffuse enhancement + Dural Tail Radiology Gold standard for Aneurysm/AVM? Conventional Angiography DSA Radiology Mechanism of CT? X-ray attenuation Hounsfield Units Physics FirstAid Neurology > Radiology Code Stroke Imaging Non-contrast CT is the first-line radiologic intervention for suspected stroke to rule out hemorrhage. CT Density Rule Hyperdense White = Bone Acute Blood Calcification. n Hypodense Black = Air Fat Water CSF. Metformin & Contrast Metformin must be withheld for 48 hours after iodinated contrast administration to prevent Lactic Acidosis . MRI Safety Strong magnetic fields can dislodge ferromagnetic objects. Pacemakers and metallic implants are absolute contraindications. Gadolinium & Renal Failure Avoid Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 due to the risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . Ring-Enhancing Lesion Classic Differential MAGIC DR Metastasis Abscess Glioblastoma Infarct Contusion Demyelination Radiation."}, {"id": "l50", "title": "Lecture 50: Seizures and Epilepsy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l50 Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy Neurology Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy n n 1. Definitions & Pathophysiology n Story Mode nClassically we think of a seizure as a storm in the brain. Physiologically this is an imbalance between Excitation Glutamate and Inhibition GABA . Too much gas or not enough brakes leads to hypersynchronous aberrant neuronal firing. n Seizure Transient occurrence of signs/symptoms due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity. n Epilepsy A disease of the brain defined by n 1. At least two unprovoked seizures >24 hours apart. n 2. One unprovoked seizure with a high probability >60% of recurrence e.g. structural lesion on MRI . n 3. Diagnosis of an Epilepsy Syndrome. n n 1.1 The VITAMINS of Etiology nCommon causes of seizures to remember n V ascular Stroke especially in elderly n I nfection Meningitis Encephalitis n T rauma TBI n A utoimmune Anti-NMDA n M etabolic Hypoglycemia Hyponatremia n I ngestion/Withdrawal Alcohol withdrawal Benzos n N eoplasm Tumors n S ychogenic Pseudo-seizures / PNES n n--- n n 2. Seizure Semiology & Classification n Story Mode nThe old terms Grand Mal and Petit Mal are out. We now classify based on Onset Focal vs. Generalized and Awareness . n n 2.1 Focal Seizures Start in one hemisphere n Focal Aware Old Simple Partial n Neuron storm is limited to one spot. Patient is Awake/Aware . n Jacksonian March Activity marches along the motor strip Thumb $ rightarrow$ Hand $ rightarrow$ Arm . n Aura A focal aware seizure manifesting as a subjective sensation e.g. chemical smell rising epigastric sensation fear before spreading. n Focal Impaired Awareness Old Complex Partial n Seizure affects consciousness/mentation. n Automatisms Repetitive non-purposeful movements Lip smacking picking at clothes . n Localization Most common in Temporal Lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala . n n 2.2 Generalized Seizures Both hemispheres involved n Generalized Tonic-Clonic GTC n Tonic Phase Stiffening Epileptic Cry air forced through closed cords Cyanosis. n Clonic Phase Rhythmic jerking. n Postictal Limp unresponsive loud snoring airway risk! . n Absence Seizures Old Petit Mal n Clinically Sudden behavioral arrest blank stare <15 seconds. No postictal confusion. n Triggers Hyperventilation . n Mechanism Hyperpolarization of Thalamocortical Relay neurons leads to de-inactivation of T-Type Calcium Channels $ rightarrow$ Burst firing. n Gene CACNA1H . n EEG 3 Hz Spike and Wave Pathognomonic . n Treatment Ethosuximide . n n correlate n Status Epilepticus n Definition n Seizure lasting > 5 minutes convulsive or > 10 minutes non-convulsive . n OR Two seizures without return to baseline in between. n Management n Medical Emergency! Time is Brain Excitotoxicity . n 1st Line Benzodiazepines IV Lorazepam /Ativan . n n n--- n n 3. Epilepsy Syndromes Genetics & Structural n Story Mode nWe are moving away from Idiopathic epilepsy effectively. Advanced genetics now link specific channelopathies to specific syndromes. n n 3.1 Ion Channelopathies n Dravet Syndrome n Gene SCN1A Nav1.1 Sodium Channel loss of function in inhibitory interneurons . n Presentation Febrile seizures in infancy $ rightarrow$ Refractory epilepsy developmental regression. High mortality SUDEP . n EAST Syndrome n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy Kidney . n Gene KCNJ10 Kir4.1 Potassium channel in Astrocytes . n Mechanism Astrocytes can t buffer extracellular K+ $ rightarrow$ Neurons depolarize. n n 3.2 Structural & Neurocutaneous Syndromes n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex TSC n Genes TSC1 Hamartin or TSC2 Tuberin . n Pathway Loss of inhibition of mTOR pathway $ rightarrow$ Uncontrolled cell growth Hamartomas . n Clinical n Brain Cortical Tubers cause epilepsy Subependymal Nodules. n Skin Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented Shagreen Patches Facial Angiofibromas. n Heart Rhabdomyoma. n Kidney Angiomyolipoma. n Sturge-Weber Syndrome n Gene GNAQ Somatic Mosaicism NOT hereditary . n Clinical Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 distribution Leptomeningeal Angioma Glaucoma Seizures. n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome LGS n Presentation Childhood <8yo . Intellectual disability. n Seizures Multiple types Atonic/Drop Attacks Tonic . n EEG Slow Spike & Wave < 2.5 Hz . n Gene GABRB3 GABA receptor subunit . n n 3.3 Autoimmune & Encephalitis n Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis n Antibody IgG against NR1 subunit of NMDA receptor. n Demographics Young women. Associated with Ovarian Teratoma . n Course Viral prodrome $ rightarrow$ Psychosis/Hallucinations $ rightarrow$ Catatonia/Mute $ rightarrow$ Hyperkinetic Orofacial Dyskinesia . n Rasmussen Encephalitis n Pathology T-Cell mediated chronic encephalitis affecting one hemisphere . n Outcome Progressive atrophy and hemiparesis. n Treatment Hemispherectomy Functional hemispherectomy usually in children due to neuroplasticity . n n--- n n 4. Workup & Treatment Modalities n 4.1 Diagnostics n EEG Gold standard. Can use Hyperventilation or Photic Stimulation to provoke. n Imaged n CT Emergency Rule out bleed/mass . Quick. n MRI Elective/Detailed. Looks for Mesial Temporal Sclerosis Cortical Dysplasia Lissencephaly Heterotopia . n n 4.2 Treatments n Pharmacology Goal is seizure freedom with minimal side effects. n Ketogenic Diet High Fat Low Carb. Mechanism specific unclear but effective in peds. n Surgical n Resection Lesionectomy or Lobectomy e.g. for Temporal Lobe Epilepsy . n Corpus Callosotomy Palliative. Prevents spread generalization of seizures protecting against drop attacks. Spares the Splenium to preserve some function. n Neuromodulation n Vagal Nerve Stimulator VNS Pacemaker for the vagus nerve. n RNS Responsive Neurostimulation Closed-loop system. Detects seizure pattern $ rightarrow$ Zaps it to abort. n DBS Deep Brain Stimulation Anterior Nucleus of Thalamus. n n highyield n The Do Not Miss Associations n| Syndrome | Gene/Antibody | Key Feature | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Dravet | SCN1A | Febrile Szs $ rightarrow$ Regression | n| Absence | CACNA1H | 3Hz Spike/Wave Ethosuximide | n| Lennox-Gastaut | GABRB3 | Atonic Drop Szs Slow Spike/Wave | n| Tuberous Sclerosis | TSC1/TSC2 | Cortical Tubers Ash Leaf Spots mTOR | n| Anti-NMDA | Anti-NR1 | Ovarian Teratoma Psychiatric onset | n questions A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his teacher who reports he zones out multiple times a day for 10 seconds. He does not respond to his name during these episodes but immediately resumes activity afterward. Hyperventilation in the office reproduces the event. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this disorder? Loss of GABA-A receptor function SCN1A sodium channel mutation T-type calcium channel hypersynchrony Glutamate excitotoxicity via NMDA receptors Potassium channel accumulation in astrocytes 2 This is Absence Epilepsy . The hallmark is a brief <15s stare triggered by hyperventilation with no postictal state. The pathophysiology involves hyperpolarization of thalamocortical relay neurons leading to rebound burst firing via T-type Calcium Channels . Treatment is Ethosuximide. A 24-year-old female presents with new-onset seizures and acute psychosis. She has been acting strangely with fluctuating catatonia and agitation. Physical exam reveals rhythmic tongue-smacking movements. An MRI of the pelvis is ordered. What is the physician looking for? Renal Angiomyolipoma Ovarian Teratoma Uterine Fibroids Polycystic Ovaries Bladder transitional cell carcinoma 1 This clinical picture Psychosis + Seizures + Orofacial Dyskinesia in a young woman is classic for Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis . It is a paraneoplastic syndrome strongly associated with Ovarian Teratomas . A 4-month-old infant presents with a prolonged febrile seizure. Over the next year he develops multiple seizure types including myoclonic and atonic seizures and shows signs of developmental regression. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in SCN1A. What is the most likely diagnosis? West Syndrome Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Dravet Syndrome Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy Benign Rolandic Epilepsy 2 Dravet Syndrome is a severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy caused by an SCN1A mutation. It classically begins with prolonged febrile seizures in the first year of life and progresses to intractable epilepsy and intellectual disability. A patient with intractable epilepsy undergoes a corpus callosotomy. Which of the following structures is typically spared during this procedure to preserve inter-hemispheric transfer of visual information? Rostrum Genu Body Splenium Anterior Commissure 3 The Splenium posterior portion of the corpus callosum is often spared anterior 2/3rds callosotomy to preserve the transfer of visual information between the occipital lobes maintaining some functional connectivity while preventing the generalization of seizures. Which of the following EEG findings is paired with the correct Epilepsy Syndrome? 3 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Hypsarrhythmia - Childhood Absence Epilepsy Slow <2.5 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Centrotemporal Spikes - Dravet Syndrome Generalized Polyspike - Focal Temporal Lobe Epilepsy 2 Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome is characterized by a Slow Spike and Wave pattern <2.5 Hz on EEG. Note 3 Hz Spike/Wave is Absence. Hypsarrhythmia is West Syndrome. Centrotemporal spikes are Rolandic Epilepsy . What is the pathognomonic EEG finding in Absence Seizures? 3 Hz Spike and Wave complexes Neurology What gene mutation is associated with Dravet Syndrome? SCN1A Sodium Channel Nav1.1 Neurology A young woman presents with psychosis oro-facial dyskinesia and seizures. What tumor should be screened for? Ovarian Teratoma Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis Neurology What cutaneous finding is pathognomonic for Tuberous Sclerosis? Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented macules Neurology What is the mechanism of action of Ethosuximide? Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus Pharmacology First-line treatment for Status Epilepticus? IV Benzodiazepines Lorazepam Pharmacology What epilepsy syndrome is associated with GABRB3 mutation and slow <2.5Hz spike-wave EEG? Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Neurology Gene mutation associated with Sturge-Weber Syndrome? GNAQ Somatic mosaicism Neurology Which antiepileptic drug is contraindicated in pregnancy highly teratogenic ? Valproate Neural Tube Defects Pharmacology What is the Jacksonian March ? Progressive spread of focal seizure activity along the motor homunculus Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Epilepsy BandB Neurology > Seizures > Epilepsy Syndromes Sketchy Path Neurology > Seizures First Aid Neurology > Pathology > Seizures Absence vs Focal Impaired Absence <15s immediate recovery provoked by hyperventilation. n Focal Impaired Awareness >30s postictal confusion automatisms common. Sturge-Weber Triad 1. Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 n2. Leptomeningeal Angioma Seizures n3. Glaucoma Increased IOP Tuberous Sclerosis Mnemonic ASH LEAF A sh leaf spots S hagreen patches H eart rhabdomyoma L ung hamartomas E pilepsy Cortical Tubers A ngiomyolipoma Renal F acial angiofibroma. Status Epilepticus Tx 1st Line Benzodiazepines Lorazepam . 2nd Line Fosphenytoin/Valproate/Levetiracetam. 3rd Line Pentobarbital/Propofol. Jacksonian March Focal motor seizure activity that spreads along the motor homunculus e.g. finger to hand to arm to face reflecting electrical spread in the precentral gyrus."}, {"id": "l51", "title": "Lecture 51: Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l51 Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders Neurology Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders n n 1. Evaluation of Memory Loss n Story Mode nNot all memory loss is dementia. The first step is to distinguish between Delirium acute fluctuating inattention and Dementia chronic progressive . We must also rule out MCI Mild Cognitive Impairment where function is preserved despite memory complaints. n n 1.1 Delirium vs. Dementia n Delirium n Time Acute onset hours to days . n Course Fluctuating. n Key Feature Inattention and altered level of consciousness. n Cause Often underlying medical illness infection metabolic on top of a vulnerable brain dementia . n Dementia n Time Chronic months to years . n Course Progressive decline. n Key Feature Memory/Cognitive loss without altered consciousness until late stages . n n 1.2 Reversible Causes nBefore condemning a patient to a neurodegenerative diagnosis rule out the fixable stuff n Metabolic Hypothyroidism B12 deficiency. n Structural Subdural hematoma Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH . n Infectious Syphilis HIV. n Toxic Alcohol Wernicke s Medications Benzos Anticholinergics . n Psychiatric Pseudodementia Depression . n n--- n n 2. Major Neurodegenerative Dementias n Story Mode nDifferent dementias attack different parts of the brain leading to distinct signatures. Alzheimer’s hits memory Hippocampus . FTD hits personality Frontal lobes . LBD hits the visual cortex and motor system. n n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD n Epidemiology Most common cause. Age is the 1 risk factor. n Genetics n Late Onset APOE e4 allele Risk factor not causative . n Early Onset APP Presenilin-1 Presenilin-2 Autosomal Dominant . n Presentation n Short-term memory loss Hippocampus . n Language deficits Word finding . n Visuospatial issues Getting lost . n Pathology/Imaging n MRI Hippocampal Atrophy Mesial Temporal Lobes . n PET Temporoparietal Hypometabolism . n Treatment n AChE Inhibitors Donepezil Rivastigmine GI side effects . n NMDA Antagonist Memantine Moderate-Severe . n Anti-Amyloid mAbs Lecanemab/Donanemab Risk of ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - bleed/edema . n n 2.2 Vascular Dementia n Mechanism Strokes accumulating over time. n Subcortical Slow processing speed lethargy small vessel disease . n Multi-Infarct Step-wise decline. n Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA n Buildup of amyloid in vessel walls. n Presentation Amyloid Spells Transient focal deficits mimicking TIA and lobar hemorrhages . n n 2.3 Lewy Body Dementia LBD n Core Features n Parkinsonism Rigidity bradykinesia. n Visual Hallucinations detailed often people/animals e.g. Robin Williams case . n Fluctuations Good days/bad days. n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams loss of atonia . n Treatment Caution EXTREME sensitivity to Antipsychotics can cause severe rigidity/death . Avoid Benzos. n n 2.4 Frontotemporal Dementia FTD n Presentation n Behavioral Variant Onset in 50s-60s. Disinhibition apathy loss of empathy hyperorality craving sweets compulsive behaviors Bruce Willis case . Memory is spared early on. n Primary Progressive Aphasia Language loss first. n Genetics MAPT GRN C9ORF72. n Management SSRIs for behavior. AChE inhibitors do NOT help. n n--- n n 3. Other Specific Etiologies n n 3.1 Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH n Triad Wet Wobbly Wacky n Urinary Incontinence Wet . n Gait Apraxia / Magnetic Gait Wobbly . n Dementia Wacky . n Pathophysiology Enlarged ventricles comprising white matter tracts legs/bladder fibers run medially . n Diagnosis/Rx High Volume Lumbar Puncture drain 30cc . If gait improves $ rightarrow$ VP Shunt . n n 3.2 Prion Diseases n Mechanism Conversion of normal PrPc helical to abnormal PrPsc Beta-pleated sheet $ rightarrow$ rapid spongiform degeneration. n Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD n Presentation Rapidly progressive dementia + Startle Myoclonus + Ataxia. Death < 1 year. n Diagnosis MRI Cortical ribboning CSF RT-QuIC is gold standard; 14-3-3 is old/nonspecific . n Fatal Familial Insomnia FFI - The Mystery Case n Alfredo s Case 53yo male with profound insomnia dysautonomia sweating pinpoint pupils tachycardia and dream-enactment. n Course Thalamic degeneration prevents sleep $ rightarrow$ Death. n n 3.3 Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE n Cause Repetitive mild TBI Football Boxing . n Timeline Symptoms start years/decades after sports career. n Presentation n Mood/Behavior Explosivity aggression suicide Aaron Hernandez . n Cognitive Dementia later. n Motor Parkinsonism/ALS-like features. n Pathology Tauopathy. n n 3.4 Wernicke s Encephalopathy n Cause Thiamine B1 deficiency Alcoholics . n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Nystagmus/Lateral Rectus palsy . n Rx IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose to prevent precipitating Korsakoff s . n Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic irreversible amnesia + Confabulation damage to mammillary bodies/thalamus . n n highyield n Dementia Buzzwords n| Diagnosis | Buzzword / Key Feature | n| --- | --- | n| Alzheimer s | Hippocampal Atrophy Amyloid Plaques/Tau Tangles | n| LBD | Visual Hallucinations Parkingonism Neuroleptic Sensitivity | n| Frontotemporal | Disinhibition Hyperorality Young onset 50s | n| NPH | Wet Wobbly Wacky Magnetic Gait | n| CJD | Startle Myoclonus Rapid progression | n| Wernicke s | Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Thiamine | n questions A 72-year-old male is brought in by his family for evaluated of acting strange. They report he has been talking to people who aren t there and sometimes thinks his wife is an imposter. On exam he has mild rigidity in the upper extremities. Which medication should be AVOIDED in this patient? Donepezil Carbidopa-Levodopa Haloperidol Memantine Melatonin 2 The patient has features of Lewy Body Dementia Visual Hallucinations Capgras delusion/imposter Parkinsonism . LBD patients have severe neuroleptic sensitivity . Typical antipsychotics like Haloperidol can precipitate severe rigidity catatonia or even death. A 55-year-old woman is brought by her husband for personality changes. She has been making inappropriate sexual comments to strangers eating only candy bars and spending thousands of dollars on ceramic figurines. Her memory for recent events is relatively preserved. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Bipolar Disorder Vascular Dementia Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 1 This is the classic presentation of Behavioral Variant FTD Early onset <60 Disinhibition Hyperorality dietary changes compulsive behaviors and spared memory early in the course. A 68-year-old male presents with a magnetic gait where his feet appear stuck to the floor. He has also had several episodes of urinary incontinence and mild memory decline. A high-volume lumbar puncture is performed and his walking improves significantly. What is the next best step in management? Start Carbidopa-Levodopa Start Donepezil Refer for Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt Repeat Lumbar Puncture in 1 month Start Finasteride 2 This patient has Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus Wet Wobbly Wacky . The positive response to the large volume LP diagnostic tap predicts a good response to permanent CSF diversion via a VP Shunt . A 45-year-old female presents with rapid cognitive decline over the last 3 months. She has developed severe ataxia and startle myoclonus. An EEG shows generalized periodic sharp wave complexes. What is the gold standard diagnostic test for this condition? CSF 14-3-3 protein CSF RT-QuIC assay Brain Biopsy Serum Ceruloplasmin MRI with contrast 1 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD Rapid dementia myoclonus ataxia . While 14-3-3 was used historically RT-QuIC Real-Time Quaking-Induced Conversion is now the specific gold standard test on CSF to detect the abnormal prion protein. Which of the following findings is most strongly associated with Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy? Subcortical lacunar infarcts Lobar intracerebral hemorrhages Tremor at rest Early gait instability Choroid Plexus Cyst 1 Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA involves amyloid deposition in the vessel walls of the cortex making them friable. This leads to recurrent Lobar Hemorrhages in the hemispheres sparing deep structures and Amyloid Spells. What is the classic triad of Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus? Urinary Incontinence Gait disturbance Dementia Wet Wobbly Wacky Neurology Which dementia is characterized by visual hallucinations and parkinsonism? Lewy Body Dementia Neurology What is the gold standard CSF test for CJD? RT-QuIC Real-time Quaking-Induced Conversion Neurology What MRI finding is associated with Alzheimer s Disease? Hippocampal Atrophy Neurology A patient with dementia develops acute rigidity and obtundation after receiving Haloperidol. What is the diagnosis? Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity in Lewy Body Dementia Pharmacology What vitamin must be replenished before glucose in an alcoholic patient? Thiamine B1 - to prevent Wernicke s precipitation Neurology What specific sleep disorder is a prodrome for synucleinopathies LBD/Parkinson s ? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Neurology What protein accumulates in Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE ? Tau It is a Tauopathy Neurology Side effect concern for new Anti-Amyloid monoclonal antibodies Lecanemab ? ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - Edema/Hemorrhage Pharmacology Genetic risk factor allele for Late-Onset Alzheimer s? APOE e4 Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Dementia BandB Neurology > Dementia Sketchy Path Neurology > Neurodegenerative Delirium vs Dementia Delirium is acute fluctuating and affects ATTENTION. Dementia is chronic progressive and affects MEMORY attention preserved until late . NPH Triad Wet Incontinence Wobbly Magnetic Gait Wacky Dementia . Treat with Shunt. LBD Neuroleptic Sensitivity Avoid typical antipsychotics Haldol in Lewy Body Dementia. They can cause severe parkinsonism and death. Wernicke s Tx Give Thiamine BEFORE Glucose. Glucose first depletes remaining thiamine and precipitates Wernicke s. FTD Features Disinhibition Apathy Hyperorality sweets Compulsive behaviors. Memory often spared early."}, {"id": "l52", "title": "Lecture 52: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs 1", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l52 Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1 Pharmacology Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1 n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 52 n n 1. Introduction to Seizures & Epilepsy n Story Mode nA seizure is essentially an electrical storm in the brain defined as a paroxysmal event due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity. When a patient has recurrent seizures due to a chronic underlying process we diagnose Epilepsy . The goal of pharmacotherapy is to stabilize these neurons either by putting the brakes on excitation blocking Sodium/Calcium channels inhibiting Glutamate or stepping on the clutch enhancing GABA inhibition . n n 1.1 Classification Slide 6 8 n Focal Onset Seizures n Arise from a specific discrete local population of neurons in one hemisphere. n Mechanism Hyperexcitability due to rapidly firing Voltage-Gated Sodium Channels . n Evolution Can evolve into bilateral tonic-clonic seizures. n Primary Generalized Onset Seizures n Arise in both hemispheres simultaneously. n Involve deep structures Thalamus/Brainstem . n Status Epilepticus n Definition >5 minutes of continuous seizure activity OR >2 seizures without recovery of consciousness in between. n n 1.2 Pharmacokinetics Overview Slide 15-22 n Absorption/Distribution All ASDs Anti-Seizure Drugs must cross the BBB. Most are highly protein bound potential for displacement interactions . n Metabolism Most undergo hepatic metabolism. n Inducers Carbamazepine Phenytoin Phenobarbital. n Inhibitor Valproate. n Withdrawal Gradual taper is mandatory. Abrupt cessation can precipitate seizures or status epilepticus. n n correlate nSuicidality in ASDs n n- Pathophysiology Mechanism unclear; controversial FDA warning. n- Presentation New onset suicidal ideation or behavior. n- Lecturer s Point All ASDs carry a warning for increased risk of suicidality. Patients especially young ones should be monitored for mood changes. n n n--- n n 2. Voltage-Gated Sodium Channel Blockers n Story Mode nThe fast sodium current is responsible for the upstroke of the action potential. In a seizure neurons fire repetitively at high frequencies. Sodium channel blockers work by binding to the channel in its Inactivated State preventing it from resetting to the active state. This prolongs the refractory period. Importantly these drugs exhibit Use-Dependence they bind more strongly to neurons that are firing rapidly seizing while sparing normal neurons firing at normal rates. n n 2.1 Mechanism of Action Slide 30-35 n The Cycle Resting Closed $ rightarrow$ Depolarization $ rightarrow$ Open Na+ in $ rightarrow$ Inactivated Gate closed $ rightarrow$ Repolarization $ rightarrow$ Resting. n Fast Inactivation Enhancers n Drugs Phenytoin Carbamazepine Lamotrigine Oxcarbazepine . n Bind to the channel pore during the Open/Inactivated state. n Stabilize the channel in the inactivated state refractory . n Slow Inactivation Enhancer n Drug Lacosamide . n Stabilizes the Slow Inactivation state a conformational change occurring over seconds during prolonged depolarization . n n--- n n 3. Phenytoin PHT & Fosphenytoin n Story Mode nPhenytoin is the classic dirty drug of neurology. It’s effective but it follows Zero-Order Kinetics saturation kinetics meaning small dose increases can lead to exponential spikes in blood levels. It also has a laundry list of cosmetic and teratogenic side effects P-H-E-N-Y-T-O-I-N . Fosphenytoin was developed as a water-soluble prodrug to solve the infusion issues of Phenytoin Phlebitis/Purple Glove . n n 3.1 Pharmacokinetics Slide 40-55 n Absorption n Phenytoin Base Chewable/Suspension Erratic absorption. n Phenytoin Sodium Capsules Better absorption. n Distribution Highly protein bound Albumin . n Hypoalbuminemia Liver failure Nephrotic syndrome Pregnancy Leads to INCREASED Free Phenytoin levels $ rightarrow$ Toxicity. n Displacement Uremia kidney failure displaces PHT from albumin. n Metabolism CRITICAL n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymes CYP2C9/2C19 become saturated at therapeutic levels. n Elimination is constant amount per time not constant fraction . n Consequence Small dose increase $ rightarrow$ Disproportionately LARGE increase in serum concentration. n Induction Potent INDUCER of CYP3A4 UGT etc. Eats up other drugs . n n 3.2 Formulations Phenytoin vs. Fosphenytoin Slide 43-45 n| Feature | Phenytoin IV | Fosphenytoin IV/IM | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Solubility | Poor Lipophilic | High Water soluble Prodrug | n| Solvent | Propylene Glycol + Ethanol pH 12 | Water based | n| Adverse Effects | Hypotension/Arrhythmias due to Propylene Glycol Phlebitis | Less Hypotension Minimal irritation | n| Infusion Rate | Must be SLOW risk of chemical burn | Can be faster | n| IM Use | CONTRAINDICATED Tissue necrosis | Safe for IM | n n 3.3 Adverse Effects Slide 61-68 n mnemonic n PHENYTOIN Side Effects n PHENYTOIN n n- P P -450 Inducer / P eripheral Neuropathy / P urple Glove n- H H irsutism excess hair n- E E nlarged Gums Gingival Hyperplasia / E rythema Multiforme SJS n- N N ystagmus / Neurotoxicity Ataxia Slurred speech n- Y Y ellowing Hepatotoxicity - rare n- T T eratogenicity Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome n- O O steomalacia Vit D deficiency from enzyme induction n- I I nterference with Folate Megaloblastic Anemia n- N N europathy n n n correlate nPurple Glove Syndrome n n- Pathophysiology Extravasation of IV Phenytoin highly alkaline + propylene glycol . n- Presentation Distal limb edema discoloration purple/blue pain. n- Complications Skin necrosis compartment syndrome amputation. n- Prevention Use Fosphenytoin instead. n n n correlate nFetal Hydantoin Syndrome n n- Pathophysiology Teratogenic effect of Phenytoin and Carbamazepine/Valproate . n- Presentation Cleft lip/palate Microcephaly Hypoplastic fingernails IUGR. n- Risk 2-3x risk of major malformations. n- Management Folic acid supplementation though may not fully prevent . n n n--- n n 4. Carbamazepine CBZ & Pharmacogenetics n Story Mode nCarbamazepine is chemically related to Tricyclic Antidepressants. It is a potent sodium channel blocker used for focal seizures. Its claim to fame is Auto-Induction it induces its own metabolism so half-life decreases over the first few weeks and severe genetically determined skin reactions. n n 4.1 Pharmacogenetics Slide 23-28 nScreening is MANDATORY in at-risk populations before starting Carbamazepine and chemically related Oxcarbazepine/Phneytoin/Lamotrigine . n n HLA-B 1502 n Population Asian ancestry Han Chinese South Asian . n Risk SJS / TEN Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis . n Action SCREEN all Asian patients. If positive $ rightarrow$ AVOID Carbamazepine. n HLA-A 3101 n Population European Japanese Korean. n Risk DRESS Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms . n n highyield n Auto-Induction nCarbamazepine induces CYP3A4 the very enzyme that metabolizes it. n Effect Serum levels DROP over the first few weeks of therapy despite constant dosing. n Management Dose may need to be increased after 3-4 weeks once auto-induction is maximal. n n n--- n n 5. Lamotrigine LTG n Story Mode nLamotrigine is the Clean broad-spectrum agent. It blocks Sodium channels AND inhibits Glutamate release. It is effective for almost everything Focal Generalized Absence Bipolar . Its major Achilles heel is the Life-Threatening Rash SJS which is why we must titrate it painfully slowly Start low go slow . n n 5.1 Pharmacokinetics & Dosing Slide 69-75 n Interaction with Valproate n Valproate inhibits UGT glucuronidation . n Effect DOUBLES Lamotrigine half-life 60 hours vs 24 hours . n Risk Massive increase in SJS/Rash risk . n Management reduce dose by 50% also titrate even SLOWER every other day dosing initially . n Interaction with Inducers Phenytoin/Carbamazepine n Decreases Lamotrigine half-life. n Management Higher loading/maintenance doses needed. n n 5.2 Clinical Uses n Broad Spectrum Focal Generalized Tonic-Clonic Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Absence 2nd line . n Bipolar Disorder Maintenance treatment prevents Depressive episodes ; NOT for acute Mania. n n--- n n 6. Lacosamide LCM n Story Mode nLacosamide is the newer cleaner cousin. Instead of fast inactivation it enhances Slow Inactivation . It has excellent bioavailability 100% minimal protein binding and renal excretion fewer drug interactions . The one specific catch is the heart. n n 6.1 Mechanism & Side Effects Slide 80-87 n Mechanism Enhances Slow Inactivation of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels. n Key Toxicity PR Interval Prolongation . n Risk Bradycardia AV Block. n Action Get a baseline ECG in patients with known cardiac conduction issues. n Pharmacokinetics n Renally excreted 40% unchanged . n No major CYP interactions unlike Phenytoin/Carbamazepine . n n--- n n 7. Summary Tables n n 7.1 Sodium Channel Blockers Comparison n| Drug | Mechanism | Key PK Feature | Key Toxicity | Clinical Pearl | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Phenytoin | Fast Inactivation | Zero-Order Kinetics ; Potent Inducer | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Purple Glove Osteomalacia | Fosphenytoin is safer for IV. | n| Carbamazepine | Fast Inactivation | Auto-Inducer | SJS HLA-B 1502 DRESS Hyponatremia SIADH | Screen Asians for HLA-B 1502. | n| Lamotrigine | Fast Inactivation + Glutamate Inhibit | Valproate Doubles Half-life | SJS Rash | Start Low Go Slow titration. | n| Lacosamide | Slow Inactivation | Renal Elimination | PR Prolongation | Get ECG aimed at cardiac patients. | n n highyield n Teratogenicity Hierarchy n1. Valproate HIGHEST Risk Neural Tube Defects/Spina Bifida - AVOID. n2. Phenytoin/Carbamazepine High Risk Cleft palate cardiac defects . n3. Lamotrigine/Levetiracetam Safest options for pregnancy. n questions A 35-year-old female with a history of epilepsy is well-controlled on Valproate. She recently started a new medication as add-on therapy for breakthrough seizures. Four weeks later she presents to the ER with a severe peeling rash affecting her face and trunk. Which medication was most likely added? Levetiracetam Gabapentin Lamotrigine Lacosamide Topiramate 2 This case describes Stevens-Johnson Syndrome/Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis. Lamotrigine carries a Black Box Warning for serious skin rashes. Valproate inhibits the glucuronidation of Lamotrigine increasing its half-life and serum levels which drastically increases the risk of this rash if the dose isn t significantly reduced and titrated slowly. A patient is started on an anti-seizure medication that follows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic concentrations. Which of the following best describes the pharmacokinetic consequence of this property? Half-life is constant regardless of plasma concentration A small increase in dose can lead to a disproportionately large increase in serum concentration The drug is eliminated by the kidneys unchanged The drug induces its own metabolism A constant fraction of the drug is eliminated per unit time 1 Phenytoin follows Zero-Order Saturation Kinetics at therapeutic levels. This means metabolic enzymes are saturated and a constant amount not fraction of drug is eliminated per unit time. Consequently once saturation is reached even small dose increases can cause massive spikes in toxicity. Which of the following patients should be screened for the HLA-B 1502 allele prior to starting Carbamazepine? A 50-year-old Caucasian male with kidney failure A 25-year-old African American female with hypertension A 30-year-old Han Chinese male with no other medical history A 70-year-old Hispanic female with liver disease A 40-year-old of Northern European descent 2 The HLA-B 1502 allele is strongly associated with Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis TEN induced by Carbamazepine and Phenytoin/Lamotrigine in patients of Asian ancestry particularly Han Chinese Thai Malaysian and South Asian . Screening is mandatory in these populations. A 6-year-old child presents with gingival hyperplasia overgrown gums and hirsutism. Which anti-seizure medication is the child most likely taking? Ethosuximide Phenytoin Valproic Acid Gabapentin Levetiracetam 1 Gingival Hyperplasia and Hirsutism are classic cosmetic side effects of Phenytoin . They are not typically seen with the other agents listed. A patient requiring IV phenytoin for status epilepticus is mistakenly given the drug via a rapid IV push into a small hand vein. The hand subsequently becomes swollen painful and turns purple. What is the mechanism of this reaction? Allergic reaction to the sulfa moiety Precipitation of phenytoin crystals in the tissue due to high pH and propylene glycol Vasoconstriction caused by sodium channel blockade Clotting factor inhibition Autoimmune vasculitis 1 This is Purple Glove Syndrome . Phenytoin is poorly soluble and requires Propylene Glycol and a high pH 12 solvent. Rapid infusion or extravasation causes severe focal toxicity crystal precipitation and vasoconstriction/thrombosis leading to necrosis. Fosphenytoin avoids this risk. What is the specific HLA allele associated with SJS/TEN in Asian patients taking Carbamazepine? HLA-B 1502 Pharmacogenetics Which anti-seizure drug follows Zero-Order kinetics at therapeutic levels? Phenytoin Pharmacology What effect does Valproate have on Lamotrigine metabolism? Inhibits glucuronidation -> Doubles Lamotrigine half-life Risk of Rash Pharmacokinetics Name two cosmetic side effects specific to Phenytoin. Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Adverse Effects What is the mechanism of action of Lacosamide? Enhances Slow Inactivation of Na+ channels Mechanism Why is Fosphenytoin preferred over Phenytoin for IV loads? Water soluble less risk of hypotension/bradycardia/Purple Glove Syndrome Clinical What is the classic adverse effect of Carbamazepine that requires blood monitoring besides agranulocytosis ? Hyponatremia SIADH Adverse Effects Which ASD is an Auto-Inducer? Carbamazepine Pharmacokinetics What cardiac adverse effect is associated with Lacosamide? PR Interval Prolongation Bradycardia/Heart Block Adverse Effects Which vitamin deficiency is caused by Phenytoin induction? Folate Megaloblastic anemia and Vitamin D Osteomalacia Nutrition Physeo Pharmacology > Neurology > Antiepileptics BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Antiepileptic drugs Sketchy Pharm Neuro/Psych > Antiepileptics HLA-B 1502 Warning Always screen Asian patients for HLA-B 1502 before starting Carbamazepine. Positive = High Risk for SJS/TEN. Zero-Order Kinetics Phenytoin metabolism saturates. Small dose increase = Huge blood level spike. Lamotrigine + Valproate Valproate DOUBLES Lamotrigine levels. You must cut the Lamotrigine dose in half and titrate very slowly to avoid THE RASH. Purple Glove Syndrome Caused by Phenytoin extravasation propylene glycol/pH 12 . Use Fosphenytoin to avoid it. Gingival Hyperplasia Classic side effect of Phenytoin along with Hirsutism ."}, {"id": "l53", "title": "Lecture 53: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs, Part 2", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l53 Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2 Pharmacology Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2 n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 53 n n 1. Broad Spectrum Agents n Story Mode nBroad-spectrum anti-seizure drugs ASDs are the utility players of epilepsy treatment. Because they target multiple mechanisms u2014often hitting sodium channels to stop firing and enhancing GABA to increase inhibition u2014they are effective against a wide variety of seizure types including focal generalized tonic-clonic and absence seizures. This makes them an excellent choice when the exact seizure type is unclear. n n 1.1 Valproate Divalproex n Mechanisms The definition of Broad Spectrum. n Blocks voltage-dependent Sodium $Na^+$ channels . n Blocks T-type Calcium $Ca^ 2+ $ channels Treats Absence . n Enhances GABA activity inhibitory . n Therapeutic Uses n Seizure First-line for many types Focal Generalized Absence Myoclonic. Used for Status Epilepticus off-label . n Non-Seizure Bipolar Disorder Acute Mania and Migraine Prophylaxis . n Pharmacokinetics n Mitochondrial metabolism Beta-oxidation . n Inhibitor of CYP enzymes increases levels of other drugs like Lamotrigine . n Adverse Effects n GI Nausea/Vomiting common limit by taking with food or ER formulations . n Cosmetic Weight Gain Hair Loss Alopecia Tremor. n Serious Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs Pancreatitis Thrombocytopenia . n Teratogenicity Neural Tube Defects Spina Bifida . Avoid in pregnancy if possible. n n correlate n Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy n The Mechanism nValproate is metabolized in the mitochondria and can interfere with the Urea Cycle. n Result Accumulation of Ammonia $NH 3$ in the blood. n Presentation Acute change in mental status confusion lethargy in a patient taking Valproate even with normal liver enzymes. n Risk Factor Concurrent use with Topiramate synergistic risk . n n n 1.2 Topiramate Topamax n Mechanisms n Blocks $Na^+$ channels. n Enhances GABA-A activity. n Antagonizes AMPA/Kainate Glutamate receptors. n Weak Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitor . n Therapeutic Uses n Seizure Focal Generalized Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome. n Non-Seizure Migraine Prophylaxis Weight Loss in combination with Phentermine . n Adverse Effects n CNS Dope-a-max u2013 Cognitive slowing word-finding difficulties sedation paresthesias tingling . n Weight Loss contrast with Valproate . n Renal Kidney Stones Calcium Phosphate stones due to alkaline urine from carbonic anhydrase inhibition . n Ocular Acute Myopia/Angle Closure Glaucoma. n n correlate n Hypohidrosis & Hyperthermia n The Mechanism nTopiramate s carbonic anhydrase inhibition can impair sweat gland function. n Target Children are most vulnerable. n Risk Reduced sweating $ downarrow$ heat dissipation leads to dangerous overheating Hyperthermia during play or hot weather. n Management Counsel parents to monitor for lack of sweating during exertion. n n n 1.3 Levetiracetam Keppra n Mechanism Unique. Binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Protein 2A . n Prevents vesicle docking and neurotransmitter release. n Pharmacokinetics n Excellent Profile 100% Bioavailability Renal excretion unchanged No drug-drug interactions . n Adverse Effects n Generally well-tolerated sedation dizziness . n Neuropsychiatric Keppra Rage u2013 Irritability agitation aggression or psychosis rare . n n--- n n 2. Narrow Spectrum Agents n Story Mode nUnlike the broad-spectrum agents these drugs have a specific lane. Ethosuximide is a sniper it hits one target T-type channels to fix one problem Absence seizures . Gabapentinoids are structural mimics of GABA that don t actually touch GABA receptors but block calcium channels to stop neuropathic pain and focal seizures. n n 2.1 Ethosuximide n Mechanism Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus. n disrupts the 3Hz spike-and-wave rhythm of absence seizures. n Therapeutic Use Absence Seizures ONLY Childhood Absence Epilepsy . n Note Ineffective for tonic-clonic seizures. n Adverse Effects n GI Nausea/Vomiting/Pain very common . n CNS Fatigue Dizziness. n Rare SJS/TEN Aplastic Anemia. n n 2.2 Gabapentinoids Gabapentin & Pregabalin n Mechanism Bind to the $ alpha 2 delta$ subunit of voltage-gated Calcium channels presynaptic . n Reduces calcium influx $ rightarrow$ reduces glutamate release. n Note Do NOT bind GABA receptors despite the name. n Therapeutic Uses n Neuropathic Pain Diabetic neuropathy Post-herpetic neuralgia Fibromyalgia. n Seizure Focal Seizures Adjunct . Exacerbates Generalized seizures. n Adverse Effects Sedation Peripheral Edema Weight gain. n n--- n n 3. GABA Modulators Barbiturates & Benzos n Story Mode nThese drugs work directly on the GABA-A receptor the brain s primary off switch. By opening the chloride channel they hyperpolarize neurons. The distinction is in how they open it Benzos increase Frequency Benzos = Frenz while Barbiturates increase Duration Barbi-durates . n n 3.1 Phenobarbital n Mechanism Barbiturate. Binds allosteric site on GABA-A. n Increases Duration of $Cl^-$ channel opening. n Danger At high doses can open the channel without GABA direct agonist $ rightarrow$ Coma/Death. n Pharmacokinetics n Potent Inducer of CYP450 enzymes eats up other drugs . n Long half-life days . n Therapeutic Uses n Neonatal Seizures First line . n Status Epilepticus Refractory . n Adverse Effects Profound sedation Respiratory Depression Hyperactivity in children paradoxical . n n 3.2 Benzodiazepines Diazepam Lorazepam Midazolam n Mechanism Binds allosteric site on GABA-A. n Increases Frequency of $Cl^-$ channel opening. n Safety Require GABA to work Ceiling effect . n Therapeutic Uses n Acute Status Epilepticus First Line - Abortive therapy . n Chronic Rarely used due to Tolerance . n Specific Agents n Lorazepam IV Preferred for Status stays in brain longer . n Diazepam IV/Rectal Highly lipid soluble $ rightarrow$ rapid entry $ rightarrow$ rapid Redistribution to fat/muscle short duration of action . n Midazolam IM/Buccal/Nasal Used when IV access is not available e.g. EMS . n n correlate n Floppy Baby Syndrome n Benzos in Pregnancy nChronic use of benzodiazepines near delivery can cause neonatal withdrawal and hypotonia. n Presentation Neonate with severe hypotonia rag doll difficulty suckling apnea and hypothermia. n Management Supportive care; avoid benzos in pregnancy if possible. n n n--- n n 4. Other Agents n n 4.1 Cannabidiol Epidiolex n Mechanism Unknown does not act on CB1/CB2 receptors . n Uses Severe childhood epilepsy syndromes n Dravet Syndrome n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex n Adverse Effects Somnolence elevated liver enzymes LFTs . n n--- n n 5. Summary Tables n n highyield n Drug Selection by Seizure Type n| Seizure Type | First Line / Effective | Contraindicated / Ineffective | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Focal | Carbamazepine Phenytoin Lamotrigine Levetiracetam | -- | n| Generalized Tonic-Clonic | Valproate Levetiracetam Lamotrigine Topiramate | Carbamazepine Phenytoin can worsen some generalized types | n| Absence | Ethosuximide Valproate | Phenytoin Carbamazepine Gabapentin | n| Status Epilepticus | Benzodiazepines Lorazepam $ rightarrow$ Fosphenytoin/Valproate | -- | n n n highyield n Key Side Effects & Buzzwords n| Drug | Buzzword / Key Side Effect | n| --- | --- | n| Valproate | Neural Tube Defects Hepatotoxicity Pancreatitis Hyperammonemia | n| Topiramate | Weight Loss Kidney Stones Mental dulling Dopeamax | n| Levetiracetam | Agitation/Aggression Keppra Rage | n| Ethosuximide | GI Distress Pain/Nausea | n| Phenytoin | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Zero-order kinetics | n| Lamotrigine | Stevens-Johnson Syndrome Rash | n questions A 7-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for frequent staring spells where he is unresponsive for 5-10 seconds. An EEG shows a 3-Hz spike-and-wave pattern. You prescribe the first-line finding for this condition. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug? Binds to SV2A vesicle protein Blocks voltage-gated Sodium channels Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels Enhances GABA-A receptor frequency Blocks Alpha-2-Delta Calcium subunit 2 The clinical presentation staring spells and EEG 3Hz spike-and-wave are classic for Absence Seizures . The drug of choice is Ethosuximide which works by blocking T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus. A 25-year-old male with a history of obesity and migraines presents for management of his focal seizures. He is interested in a medication that might also help him lose weight. Which agent would be most appropriate? Valproate Gabapentin Topiramate Phenytoin Carbamazepine 2 Topiramate is associated with weight loss and is also used for migraine prophylaxis making it an excellent choice for this patient. In contrast Valproate and Gabapentin are associated with weight gain. A neonate is born with severe hypotonia difficulty feeding and respiratory depression. The mother reports taking an anti-seizure medication for anxiety during her pregnancy. Which mechanism best explains the infant s presentation? Sodium channel blockade T-type calcium channel blockade Increased frequency of GABA-A channel opening Folate antagonism SV2A binding 2 This is Floppy Baby Syndrome caused by benzodiazepine withdrawal/effect in the neonate. Benzodiazepines work by increasing the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening. Phenytoin Folate antagonist causes Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome cleft lip/palate not acute hypotonia. A patient with refractory seizures is started on Valproate. Two weeks later he presents with confusion and lethargy. LFTs are normal but ammonia levels are elevated. Prior to this he was taking another ASD. Which co-medication most likely increased the risk of this complication? Levetiracetam Topiramate Lamotrigine Gabapentin Ethosuximide 1 Topiramate and Valproate both can cause hyperammonemia and their concurrent use significantly increases the risk of Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy . Which of the following anti-seizure medications functions by binding to the SV2A synaptic vesicle protein? Phenobarbital Lamotrigine Levetiracetam Tiagabine Vigabatrin 2 Levetiracetam has a unique mechanism of action it binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Glycoprotein 2A to prevent neurotransmitter release."}, {"id": "l54", "title": "Lecture 54: Aphasia and Common Strokes", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l54 Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes Neurology Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 54 n n 1. Aphasia Syndromes n Story Mode nAphasia is a disorder of language not speech interruption of the mind-to-mouth connection . To diagnose the type you simply need to check three boxes Fluency Is speech smooth or broken? Comprehension Do they understand you? and Repetition Can they say No ifs ands or buts ? . n n 1.1 The Perisylvian Aphasias Repetition is Impaired nThese occur around the Sylvian fissure where the main language arc resides. n Broca s Aphasia Expressive n Lesion Left Inferior Frontal Gyrus . usually superior division of Left MCA . n Features n Non-Fluent Speech is broken effortful telegraphic Car... go... store . n Comprehension Intact They know what they want to say and get frustrated . n Repetition Impaired. n Associated Contralateral Right face/arm weakness. n Wernicke s Aphasia Receptive n Lesion Left Superior Temporal Gyrus . Inferior division of Left MCA . n Features n Fluent Speech is voluminous but meaningless Word Salad . n Comprehension Impaired They don t realize they aren t making sense . n Repetition Impaired. n Associated Right Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the sky . n Global Aphasia n Lesion Large Left MCA infarct knocking out both Broca s and Wernicke s areas. n Features All Imapired Non-fluent No comprehension No repetition . n Conduction Aphasia n Lesion Arcuate Fasciculus The bridge between Broca and Wernicke . n Features n Fluent speech Intact comprehension. n Repetition IMPAIRED The connection is broken . They act like Wernicke s but know they are making mistakes and try to correct them. n n 1.2 The Transcortical Aphasias Repetition is SPARED nThese lesions are around the language centers watershed zones but spare the core Arcuate Fasciculus loop. n Transcortical Motor Like Broca s Non-fluent Good Comp but Repetition is Intact . ACA/MCA Watershed . n Transcortical Sensory Like Wernicke s Fluent Poor Comp but Repetition is Intact . MCA/PCA Watershed . They may exhibit Echolalia parroting back words . n n--- n n 2. Ischemic Stroke Syndromes by Territory n n 2.1 Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA n Territory Medial surface of the brain Leg homunculus area . n Presentation n Contralateral Lower Extremity Weakness Leg > Arm . n Abulia / Akinetic Mutism Lack of will to move or speak Frontal lobe damage . n Urinary Incontinence. n n 2.2 Middle Cerebral Artery MCA n Territory Lateral surface of the brain Face/Arm homunculus area . n Presentation n Contralateral Face and Arm Weakness Face/Arm > Leg . n Gaze Preference Eyes look Toward the lesion away from the weakness . n Dominant Left Hemi Aphasia. n Non-Dominant Right Hemi Hemineflect ignoring the left side of the world . n n 2.3 Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA n Territory Occipital lobe and Inferior Temporal lobe. n Presentation n Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field cut with Macular Sparing . n Alexia without Agraphia Left PCA + Splenium of Corpus Callosum . Patient can write but cannot read even what they just wrote . n Gerstmann Syndrome Dominant Angular Gyrus . n 1. Agraphia Can t write . n 2. Acalculia Can t do math . n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t identify fingers . n 4. Left-Right Disorientation. n n 2.4 Internal Carotid Artery ICA n Mechanism Occlusion of the source pipe for both ACA and MCA. n Presentation Combination of ACA + MCA symptoms Dense hemiplegia face/arm/leg . n Clinical Pearl Amaurosis Fugax Curtain coming down . Transient monocular blindness due to embolus to the Ophthalmic artery first branch of ICA . n n 2.5 Watershed Infarcts n Mechanism Systemic hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension . The areas between vascular territories ACA-MCA or MCA-PCA borders die first. n Presentation Man-in-a-Barrel Syndrome . n Bilateral proximal arm and weakness Shoulders/Thighs . n spares the hands and feet and face. n n--- n n 3. Lacunar Strokes n Story Mode nThese are small vessel strokes lenticulostriate arteries caused by Hypertension lipohyalinosis . They affect deep structures Internal Capsule Thalamus Pons and have very specific Pure presentations. n Pure Motor Posterior Limb of Internal Capsule. Contralateral weakness only . n Pure Sensory VPL of Thalamus. Contralateral numbness only . n Dysarthria-Clumsy Hand Genu of Internal Capsule or Pons. n Ataxic Hemiparesis Pons or Internal Capsule. n n--- n n 4. Brainstem Syndromes n Story Mode nThe brainstem is high-value real estate. A small stroke here causes massive problems. The rule of thumb Crossed Signs Ipsilateral Cranial Nerve deficit + Contralateral Body deficit . n n 4.1 Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome n Vessel PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Remember Don t Pica a horse hoarseness . n Key Features n Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X . Loss of Gag reflex. n Vertigo / Nystagmus Vestibular Nuclei. n Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Sympathetic fibers. n Ipsilateral Facial Numbness / Contralateral Body Numbness Pain/Temp . n Ipsilateral Ataxia. n n 4.2 Locked-In Syndrome n Vessel Basilar Artery thrombosis. n Lesion Bilateral Ventral Pons. n Presentation n Quadriplegia all motor tracts cut . n Anarthria cannot speak . n Consciousness is Preserved Reticular formation is spared . n Communication Vertical Eye Movements and Blinking spared midbrain centers . n n--- n n 5. Hemorrhagic Strokes n n| Type | Etiology | CT Appearance | Clinical Pearl | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Epidural Hematoma | Trauma Temporal bone fx $ rightarrow$ Middle Meningeal Artery rupture. | Lens-shaped Biconvex . Does NOT cross suture lines. | Talk and Die Lucid interval followed by rapid deterioration. | n| Subdural Hematoma | Trauma Falls/Elderly/Alcoholics $ rightarrow$ Bridging Veins rupture. | Crescent-shaped . Crosses suture lines. | Slow onset. associated with cerebral atrophy. | n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage | Aneurysm Rupture Berry Aneurysm or Trauma. | Blood in cisterns/sulci Starfish pattern . | Worst Headache of my Life Thunderclap . Risk of vasospasm days later. | n n correlate n Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST n The Story nA stroke that doesn t follow arterial rules. Think of this in a Hypercoagulable patient Pregnancy OCP use Cancer Factor V Leiden . n Mechanism Clot in the Dural Venous Sinuses e.g. Sagittal Sinus prevents blood drainage $ rightarrow$ Congestion $ rightarrow$ Hemorrhage. n Presentation Headache High ICP Seizures Papilledema. n Diagnosis MR Venogram MRV or CT Venogram. n Treatment Anticoagulation even if there is some hemorrhage you must clear the clot . n n n highyield n Arterial Dissection n Patient Young patient with neck trauma chiropractic manipulation or spontaneous. n Presentation n Neck Pain + Headache. n Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral The sympathetic fibers run along the carotid artery. Enlargement of the artery compresses them. n TIA/Stroke symptoms from emboli. n n questions A 72-year-old male presents with right-sided face and arm weakness but with relative sparing of the leg. He is also found to have gaze deviation to the left. Which vascular territory is most likely affected? Left Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Left Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Right Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Left Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Basilar Artery 1 Left MCA Stroke . The MCA supplies the lateral surface of the cortex Face/Arm area . Weakness is contralateral Right . Gaze deviation is toward the lesion Left away from the hemiparesis. A patient presents with word salad speech. They speak fluently and with normal rhythm but the content is meaningless. They cannot repeat phrases or follow commands. Where is the most likely lesion? Inferior Frontal Gyrus Superior Temporal Gyrus Arcuate Fasciculus Angular Gyrus Occipital Lobe 1 This is Wernicke s Aphasia Fluent Impaired Comprehension Impaired Repetition . The lesion is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus Wernicke s Area of the dominant hemisphere. A 25-year-old female presents with severe headache and specialized imaging reveals a thrombosis of the superior sagittal sinus. Which of the following is a key risk factor for this condition? Hypertension Atrial Fibrillation Tobacco Use Oral Contraceptive Use Diabetes Mellitus 3 Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST is associated with hypercoagulable states. Oral Contraceptive Use is a major risk factor especially in young women. HTN/DM/Smoking are risks for arterial strokes. A patient is able to speak fluently and understand commands perfectly but cannot repeat the phrase No ifs ands or buts . They are aware of their errors and try to correct them. What is the diagnosis? Broca s Aphasia Wernicke s Aphasia Conduction Aphasia Global Aphasia Transcortical Motor Aphasia 2 Conduction Aphasia is characterized by Fluent speech + Intact Comprehension + Impaired Repetition . It is caused by a lesion to the Arcuate Fasciculus . A patient presents with sudden onset of the worst headache of my life . A non-contrast CT shows a starfish pattern of hyperdensity in the basal cisterns. What represents the most likely etiology? Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery Rupture of Bridging Veins Rupture of Berry Aneurysm Hypertensive lipohyalinosis Carotid Dissection 2 This is a Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Thunderclap headache + Starfish pattern . The most common non-traumatic cause is the rupture of a Saccular Berry Aneurysm ."}, {"id": "l55", "title": "Lecture 55: Cerebrovascular Diseases", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l55 Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases Neurology Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases n n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. n Session Lecture 55 n n 1. Ischemic Stroke Pathology n Story Mode nIschemia is a lack of blood flow which means lack of Oxygen Hypoxia and lack of Glucose Hypoglycemia . The brain is incredibly hungry and has zero reserves. n n 1.1 The Ischemic Cascade Excitotoxicity nWhen neurons run out of energy ATP their ion pumps fail. n1. Depolarization Sodium/Calcium flow IN Potassium flows OUT. n2. Glutamate Dump The dying neuron releases a massive amount of Glutamate excitatory neurotransmitter . n3. Calcium Influx Glutamate opens NMDA channels on neighbors $ rightarrow$ Massive Calcium influx. n4. Enzyme Activation Calcium activates executioner enzymes proteases lipases that chew up the cell from the inside. n5. Result Liquefactive Necrosis . n n 1.2 Timeline of Ischemic Changes n| Time | Microscopic Change | Macroscopic Change | n| --- | --- | --- | n| 0-12 Hours | Red Neurons Eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei . First visible sign. | Normal or slight blurring of gray-white junction. | n| 12-24 Hours | Neutrophils infiltrate Acute inflammation . | Tissue becomes pale and soft. | n| 1-3 Days | Macrophages Microglia arrive to clean up the mess. | Swelling/Edema Risk of Herniation . | n| 1-2 Weeks | Reactive Gliosis Astrocytes proliferate + Vascular proliferation. | Liquefactive Necrosis Mushy cavity . | n| > 2 Weeks | Glial Scar formation Astrocytic border . | Cystic cavity hole in the brain . | n n--- n n 2. Stroke Classifications n 2.1 Thrombotic Stroke n Mechanism Clot forms locally at the site of infarction. n Cause Ruptured Atherosclerotic plaque. n Key Locations Carotid bifurcation MCA origin Basilar artery. n Presentation Stuttering onset symptoms wax and wane . n n 2.2 Embolic Stroke n Mechanism Clot travels from elsewhere . n Causes n Cardiogenic Atrial Fibrillation Left Atrial Appendage Post-MI thrombus Vegetation Endocarditis . n Fat Emboli Long bone fractures femur . Triad Hypoxemia + Petechial Rash + Confusion . n Key Features Sudden maximal deficit at onset. Affects multiple vascular territories Embolic Shower . n n 2.3 Global Ischemia Watershed Infarcts n Mechanism Systemic Hypotension Shock Cardiac Arrest . n Location Border zones between ACA/MCA or MCA/PCA. n Histology Laminar Necrosis Pyramidal neurons of Hippocampus and Cortex layers 3 5 & 6 are most vulnerable . n n 2.4 Lacunar Strokes n Mechanism Lipohyalinosis Hyaline Arteriolosclerosis of small penetrating vessels due to Chronic Hypertension . n Vessels Lenticulostriate arteries. n Location Basal Ganglia Thalamus Internal Capsule Pons. n Clinical Pure Motor or Pure Sensory deficits. No cortical signs No aphasia no neglect . n n--- n n 3. Hemorrhagic Strokes & Vascular Malformations n n| Condition | Etiology | Key Features | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Intracerebral Hemorrhage ICH | Hypertension Deep bleeds Basal Ganglia/Thalamus via Charcot-Bouchard Microaneurysms . <br> Amyloid Angiopathy Lobar/Cortical bleeds in elderly . | Rapid onset focal deficit + Headache + Nausea/Vomiting High ICP . | n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH | Ruptured Berry Aneurysm ACA-Comm is 1 site . Associated with PCKD Ehlers-Danlos. | Thunderclap Headache . Nuchal rigidity. Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia yellow CSF . | n| A-V Malformation AVM | Congenital tangle of arteries-to-veins no capillaries . High flow shunting. | present in Young Adults 10-30yo with Seizures or Hemorrhage. | n n--- n n 4. Traumatic Brain Injury TBI n n 4.1 Skull Fractures n Linear Simple crack. n Depressed Bone fragment pushed inward requires surgery . n Diastatic Separation of cranial sutures. n Basilar Fracture of the skull base. n Signs Raccoon Eyes Periorbital ecchymosis Battle Sign Mastoid ecchymosis CSF Rhinorrhea/Otorrhea Halo sign . n n 4.2 Parenchymal Injuries n Concussion Reversible altered mental status amnesia/confusion with NO structural damage on CT/MRI. n Contusion Bruising of brain tissue Hemorrhage + Edema . n Coup Injury at site of impact Head hits wall . n Contrecoup Injury opposite to impact Brain sloshes back . Common in frontal/temporal poles. n Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI n Mechanism Shearing/Rotational forces e.g. High-speed car crash . n Pathology Stretching/tearing of axons in deep white matter Corpus Callosum/Brainstem . n Imaging Multiple punctate hemorrhages on MRI SWI sequence . CT often normal. n Clinical Instant coma. Poor prognosis. n Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE Accumulation of Tau protein neurofibrillary tangles after repetitive mild TBI Boxers Football . Dementia aggression parkinsonism. n n--- n n 5. Herniation Syndromes n The Story nThe skull is a closed box. If you add volume Blood Tumor Edema the brain has nowhere to go but... squished against rigid membranes or out the holes. n n 5.1 Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation n Movement Cingulate gyrus pushed under the Falx Cerebri. n Compression Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA . n Clinical Contralateral Leg Weakness . n n 5.2 Transtentorial Uncal Herniation n Movement Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe pushed under the Tentorium Cerebelli. n Compression n 1. CN III Ipsilateral Blown Pupil Dilated/Fixed + Down and Out eye. First sign . n 2. Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Cortical Blindness . n 3. Cerebral Peduncle Contralateral Hemiparesis or Ipsilateral if Kernohan s Notch phenomenon . n 4. Duret Hemorrhages Tearing of paramedian basilar arteries $ rightarrow$ Brainstem bleed $ rightarrow$ Death. n n 5.3 Tonsillar Herniation n Movement Cerebellar Tonsils pushed through the Foramen Magnum. n Compression Brainstem Medulla . n Clinical Cardiorespiratory Arrest Coning . n n--- n n 6. High Yield Renders n n highyield n Stroke Syndromes Cheat Sheet n| Vessel | Territory | Key Deficit | Buzzword | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| ACA | Medial Surface | Leg > Arm Weakness | Abulia Lack of will | n| MCA | Lateral Surface | Face/Arm > Leg Weakness | Aphasia Left Neglect Right | n| PCA | Occipital Lobe | Homonymous Hemianopsia | Macular Sparing | n| Lacunar | Deep Structures | Pure Motor or Pure Sensory | Hypertension No Cortical Signs | n n n highyield n Herniation Syndromes n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus under Falx $ rightarrow$ ACA compression $ rightarrow$ Leg weakness. n Uncal Medial temporal lobe under Tentorium $ rightarrow$ CN III compression $ rightarrow$ Down & Out Pupil. n Tonsillar Cerebellar tonsils into Foramen Magnum $ rightarrow$ Brainstem compression $ rightarrow$ Death. n n questions A patient suffers a severe ischemic stroke and dies 6 hours later. An autopsy is performed. What is the most likely microscopic finding in the affected brain tissue? Neutrophilic infiltration Macrophage infiltration Red Neurons Reactive Gliosis Liquefactive Necrosis 2 Red Neurons eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei are the earliest visible microscopic change appearing within 12-24 hours sometimes as early as 6 hours . Neutrophils arrive at 24 hours. A 65-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden onset pure motor hemiparesis. MRI reveals a small fluid-filled cavity in the internal capsule. What is the underlying pathophysiology? Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Amyloid Angiopathy Lipohyalinosis Cardiogenic Embolism Global Ischemia 2 This is a Lacunar Stroke . The mechanism is Lipohyalinosis thickening/destabilization of the small penetrating lenticulostriate arteries due to chronic Hypertension . A patient involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident is comatose on arrival. CT scan is unremarkable but MRI shows multiple punctate hemorrhages in the corpus callosum and brainstem. What is the diagnosis? Subdural Hematoma Epidural Hematoma Diffuse Axonal Injury Coup-Contrecoup Injury Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy 2 Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI is caused by shearing forces in high-speed trauma. It affects deep white matter tracts. It is often invisible on CT but shows as microhemorrhages on MRI SWI . During a brain herniation event a patient develops a dilated fixed pupil on the right side. Which structure is being compressed? Optic Nerve Oculomotor Nerve CN III Trochlear Nerve CN IV Abducens Nerve CN VI Sympathetic chain 1 This is Uncal Herniation . The uncus of the temporal lobe herniates under the tentorium compressing the ipsilateral Oculomotor Nerve CN III causing a blown pupil . What is the primary neurotransmitter responsible for excitotoxicity and subsequent neuronal death in ischemic stroke? GABA Dopamine Serotonin Glutamate Acetylcholine 3 Glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter. In ischemia it is dumped into the synapse overstimulating NMDA receptors and causing massive Calcium influx which kills the cell."}, {"id": "l56", "title": "Lecture 56: Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l56 Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy Neurology Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy n n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. n Session Lecture 56 n n 1. General Principles n Common Theme Accumulation of protein aggregates inclusions resistant to degradation. n Toxicity Aggregates elicit stress responses damaging neurons. n n--- n n 2. Information on Specific Diseases n n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD n Epidemiology Most common cause of dementia in elderly. n Clinical Slow onset memory loss $ rightarrow$ disorientation $ rightarrow$ severe variability/immobility. n Pathology n Gross Global Atrophy Widened sulci Narrowed gyri . Hydrocephalus ex vacuo ventricles dilate to fill space . n Microscopic n 1. Senile Neuritic Plaques Extracellular. Core of Beta-Amyloid A$ beta$ . n Derived from Amyloid Precursor Protein APP cleavage by $ beta$-secretase and $ gamma$-secretase. n A$ beta$42 is the toxic aggregation-prone form. n 2. Neurofibrillary Tangles NFTs Intracellular. Hyperphosphorylated Tau protein microtubule-associated . n Correlation Number of Tangles correlates better with degree of dementia than plaques. n Genetics n APP Chr 21 Down Syndrome patients get AD by age 40 Trisomy 21 = extra APP gene . n Presenilin 1 Chr 14 & 2 Chr 1 Early-onset familial AD increase A$ beta$42 . n ApoE4 Chr 19 Increased risk for sporadic late-onset AD. n n 2.2 Frontotemporal Lobar Degeneration Pick Disease n Clinical Signification behavioral/personality changes disinhibition precede memory loss. Aphasia. n Gross Knife-Edge Atrophy of Frontal and Temporal lobes. Spares Parietal/Occipital. n Microscopic Tau inclusions Pick Bodies in some variants FTLD-Tau . Spheroidal inclusions. n Note FTLD-TDP43 is another variant Ubiquitin/TDP-43 inclusions associated with ALS. n n 2.3 Parkinson s Disease PD n Pathophysiology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta . n Clinical TRAP n T remor Resting Pill-rolling . n R igidity Cogwheel . n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement shuffling gait masked facies . n P ostural Instability. n Microscopic Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions with halo . n Composed of $ alpha$-Synuclein . n MPTP Contaminant in illicit opioids $ rightarrow$ Acute Parkinsonism destroys Substantia Nigra . n n 2.4 Huntington s Disease HD n Genetics Autosomal Dominant. CAG Trinucleotide Repeat expansion in HTT gene Chr 4 . n Anticipation Repeats strictly increase during spermatogenesis Paternal transmission $ rightarrow$ earlier onset in next gen . n Pathology Atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and Putamen . Loss of GABAergic neurons. n Clinical Chorea jerky purposeless movements Dementia Depression/Suicide. Presents in 30s-40s. n n 2.5 Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS n Definition degeneration of Upper Motor Neurons UMN in cortex and Lower Motor Neurons LMN in spinal cord/brainstem. n Clinical n LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations. n UMN signs Hyperreflexia Spasticity Babinski sign. n Sensation is intact. n Genetics Familial cases often linked to SOD1 mutation Superoxide Dismutase 1 . n n 2.6 Prion Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease - CJD n Mechanism Conversion of normal Prion protein PrP$^ c $ $ alpha$-helix to pathogenic form PrP$^ sc $ $ beta$-sheet . n PrP$^ sc $ induces conformational change in normal PrP$^ c $ chain reaction . n Pathology Spongiform Encephalopathy vacuoles in gray matter . No inflammation. n Clinical Rapidly Progressive Dementia Startle Myoclonus. Death < 1 year. n Types Sporadic most common Variant Mad Cow/BSE Familial Iatrogenic. n n--- n n 3. Peripheral Neuropathies n n 3.1 Charcot-Marie-Tooth CMT Disease n Etiology Hereditary Motor and Sensory Neuropathy HMSN . Auto Dominant PMP22 duplication is common . n Clinical Distal muscle atrophy Stork leg deformity / Inverted/Champagne bottle legs Pes Cavus High arch Hammer toes. n Microscopic Repetitive Demyelination/Remyelination $ rightarrow$ Onion Bulb formation. Palpable nerves. n n 3.2 Diabetic Neuropathy n Patterns n 1. Distal Symmetric Sensorimotor Stocking and Glove sensory loss/paresthesia. Ulcers. n 2. Autonomic Orthostatic hypotension erectile dysfunction urinary retention neurogenic bladder . n Pathogenesis High glucose $ rightarrow$ AGEs Advanced Glycation End-products $ rightarrow$ Microvascular damage vasa nervorum + Sorbitol accumulation osmotic damage . n n 3.3 Traumatic/Compression Neuropathies n Traumatic Neuroma Tangled mass of axons/Schwann cells after nerve transection failed regeneration . Painful. n Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Compression of Median Nerve at wrist. Numbness in thumb/index/middle fingers. Thenar atrophy. n Morton s Neuroma Compression of interdigital nerve usually 3rd/4th toe interspace . Related to high heels. n n--- n n 4. High Yield Renders n n highyield n Neurodegenerative Diseases The Protein & Location Match n| Disease | Protein Aggregate | Location of Atrophy | Key Clinical Feature | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Alzheimer s | Beta-Amyloid Plaques Tau Tangles | Temporoparietal / Global | Memory Loss | n| Pick s FTD | Tau Pick Bodies | Frontal / Temporal Knife-edge | Personality Change > Memory | n| Parkinson s | Alpha-Synuclein Lewy Bodies | Substantia Nigra | TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia | n| Huntington s | Huntingtin CAG Repeats | Caudate Nucleus | Chorea + Dementia | n| ALS | TDP-43 / SOD1 | UMN Cortex + LMN spinal cord | Weakness + Spasticity No sensory loss | n| CJD | Prion $PrP^ sc $ | Gray Matter Spongiform | Rapid Dementia + Myoclonus | n n questions A 55-year-old male presents with changes in personality and inappropriate social behavior. His family reports he has become disinhibited and apathetic. Memory is relatively preserved. MRI shows significant atrophy of the frontal and temporal lobes. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Huntington s Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 2 This is Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia . The key features are early behavioral/personality changes disinhibition sparing memory initially and Knife-edge atrophy of frontal/temporal lobes. Histology often shows Tau inclusions Pick bodies . A 40-year-old male presents with choreiform movements and progressive dementia. His father had similar symptoms starting at age 50. Genetic testing reveals an expanded CAG repeat sequence on Chromosome 4. Which brain structure is most likely atophied? Substantia Nigra Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus Cerebellum Motor Cortex 2 This is Huntington s Disease CAG repeats Autosomal Dominant Anticipation . The characteristic pathology is atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and putamen leading to chorea. A 70-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand rigidity and a shuffling gait. Histological examination of his brain post-mortem would most likely reveal which finding in the Substantia Nigra? Neurofibrillary Tangles Senile Plaques Negri Bodies Lewy Bodies Spongiform change 3 This is Parkinson s Disease TRAP symptoms . The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein inclusions in the Substantia Nigra . A 60-year-old female presents with rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus. She dies 4 months later. Autopsy reveals spongiform changes in the gray matter with no inflammation. What is the underlying pathogenic mechanism? Accumulation of Beta-Amyloid Loss of Dopaminergic neurons Conversion of PrPc to PrPsc CAG repeat expansion SOD1 mutation 2 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD a Prion disease. The mechanism is the conversion of normal alpha-helical PrPc to the pathological beta-sheet rich PrPsc which aggregates and causes spongiform encephalopathy. A patient with long-standing diabetes mellitus develops numbness and tingling in both feet that has progressed up to the ankles. This stocking-and-glove distribution is characteristic of which type of neuropathy? Mononeuritis Multiplex Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy Autonomic Neuropathy Radiculopathy Traumatic Neuroma 1 Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy is the most common form of diabetic neuropathy. It presents as a sensory loss/paresthesia starting in the toes and moving proximally Stocking distribution and later affecting hands Glove ."}, {"id": "l57", "title": "Lecture 57: Parkinson", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l57 Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants Neurology Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 57 n n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD n Story Mode nParkinson s is a disease of Too Little Dopamine movement is stuck . It s a synucleinopathy meaning the garbage protein dumping into the cells is Alpha-Synuclein. The classic patient is elderly slow stiff and has a tremor. n n 1.1 Clinical Features TRAP n T remor Resting tremor 4-6 Hz . Pill-rolling . Usually Asymmetric at onset. n R igidity Cogwheel rigidity ratchet-like resistance to passive movement . n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slowness of movement. n Masked Facies Hypomimia . n Micrographia Small handwriting . n Shuffling Gait Festinating gait with reduced arm swing. n Freezing Feet stuck to floor especially in doorways . n P ostural Instability Loss of balance frequent falls usually a Late finding . n n 1.2 Non-Motor Symptoms nOften precede motor symptoms by years. n Anosmia Loss of smell. n Constipation. n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams punching/kicking due to loss of REM atonia. n Depression/Anxiety. n n 1.3 Pathology & Genetics n Pathology n Loss of Dopaminergic Neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta . n Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions composed of Alpha-Synuclein . n Genetics n GBA Glucocerebrosidase Risk factor for PD and Lewy Body Dementia. Linked to Gaucher s Disease Ashkenazi Jews . n LRRK2 Common cause of familial PD Ashkenazi Jews North African Berbers . Slower progression. n Parkin Early-onset PD <40 yo . Autosomal Recessive. n Environmental n Risks Pesticides Paraquat Rotenone Well water Head trauma. n Protective Smoking Caffeine. n n--- n n 2. Atypical Parkinsonism Parkinson-Plus Syndromes n Story Mode nThese look like Parkinson s but Something is Wrong. They don t respond well to Levodopa progress faster and have unique Red Flags. n n 2.1 Multiple System Atrophy MSA n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Glial Cytoplasmic Inclusions . n Key Features Parkinsonism + n 1. Autonomic Dysfunction Dysautonomia Severe Orthostatic Hypotension fainting when standing Urinary Incontinence/Retention Erectile Dysfunction. n 2. Cerebellar Signs Ataxia. n Clinical Pearl Inspiratory Stridor. n n 2.2 Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP n Pathology Tauopathy Tau protein accumulation . n Key Features n Vertical Gaze Palsy Cannot look Down trouble reading going down stairs . n Early Falls Often falling Backwards . n Pseudobulbar Palsy Dysphagia dysarthria growling speech . n n 2.3 Corticobasal Degeneration CBD n Pathology Tauopathy. n Key Features n Markedly Asymmetric symptoms. n Alien Limb Phenomenon One hand acts on its own e.g. unbuttoning shirt while other buttons it . n Apraxia Inability to perform complex motor tasks despite motor strength. n n 2.4 Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Cortical Lewy Bodies . n Key Features n Dementia within 1 year of motor symptoms or before . n Visual Hallucinations Early vivid often children or animals. n Fluctuating Cognition Good days and bad days. n Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity Antipsychotics cause severe worsening of parkinsonism/death. n n--- n n 3. Pharmacology of Parkinson s n Strategy Increase Dopamine Levodopa Agonists MAO/COMT inhibitors or Decrease Acetylcholine Anticholinergics . n n 3.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa n Mechanism n Levodopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses Blood-Brain Barrier Dopamine cannot . n Carbidopa DOPA Decarboxylase Inhibitor . Does NOT cross BBB. Prevents peripheral conversion of L-DOPA $ rightarrow$ Dopamine reduces systemic side effects like nausea/arrhythmias . n Use Gold Standard. Best for motor symptoms bradykinesia/rigidity . n Issues n Wearing Off Effects last shorter over time. n Dyskinesias Chorea-like movements at peak dose. n On-Off Phenomenon Unpredictable fluctuations. n n 3.2 Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Rotigotine n Mechanism Directly stimulate Dopamine receptors D2/D3 . n Side Effects n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Excessive Spending. n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep danger while driving . n Hallucinations. n n 3.3 Enzyme Inhibitors n MAO-B Inhibitors Selegiline Rasagiline n Inhibit breakdown of Dopamine in the brain. n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine $ rightarrow$ Insomnia don t take at night . n COMT Inhibitors Entacapone Opicapone n Inhibit peripheral breakdown of Levodopa methyl-transferase . n Must be given with Levodopa . n Side Effect Orange/Dark Urine . n n 3.4 Amantadine n Mechanism Antiviral. NMDA Antagonist + Increases Dopamine release. n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias . n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Lacy purplish discoloration of skin on legs . n n 3.5 Anticholinergics Trihexyphenidyl Benztropine n Mechanism Restores Dopamine/ACh balance. n Use Predominantly for Tremor in younger patients Shake & Bake . n Side Effects Anti-SLUDGE Dry mouth urinary retention constipation confusion . Avoid in Elderly . n n--- n n 4. High Yield Renders n n highyield n Parkinsonism Differential Diagnosis n| Condition | Key Clinical Feature The Red Flag | Pathology | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Parkinson s Disease | Asymmetric Resting Tremor Good L-DOPA response | Synuclein Lewy Body | n| MSA | Autonomic Failure Orthostasis ED + Cerebellar signs | Synuclein Glial Inclusion | n| PSP | Vertical Gaze Palsy Can t look down + Early Falls | Tau | n| Lewy Body Dementia | Visual Hallucinations + Dementia early <1 yr | Synuclein Cortical Lewy Body | n| Corticobasal CBD | Alien Limb + Apraxia + Severe Asymmetry | Tau | n n n highyield n Parkinson s Pharmacology Buzzwords n| Drug Class | Examples | Buzzword Side Effect | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Gambling / Hypersexuality Sleep Attacks | n| Amantadine | -- | Livedo Reticularis Lacy skin rash | n| COMT Inhibitors | Entacapone | Orange Urine | n| MAO-B Inhibitors | Selegiline | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia | n| Levodopa | -- | Dyskinesia Wriggling movements | n n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand that improves with movement. He also has a shuffling gait and decreased facial expression. Which of the following pathological findings is most likely present in his brain? Neurofibrillary tangles in the Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus atrophy Loss of pigmented neurons in the Substantia Nigra Spongiform change in the Cortex Demyelination of the Periventricular White Matter 2 This characteristic presentation of Parkinson s Disease TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia Postural Instability is caused by the loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta . A 72-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on a new medication. Two months later his wife reports that he has gambled away their life savings and has become hypersexual. Which class of medication is most likely responsible for these behaviors? MAO-B Inhibitor Selegiline Dopamine Agonist Pramipexole COMT Inhibitor Entacapone Anticholinergic Benztropine Amantadine 1 Dopamine Agonists like Pramipexole and Ropinirole are known to cause Impulse Control Disorders such as pathological gambling hypersexuality and excessive shopping due to their stimulation of the mesolimbic reward pathway. A 65-year-old female presents with frequent falls often falling backwards. On exam she has difficulty looking down vertical gaze palsy and her speech is slurred. She has not responded well to Levodopa. What is the most likely diagnosis? Idiopathic Parkinson s Disease Multiple System Atrophy MSA Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP Corticobasal Degeneration CBD Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB 2 This is Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP . The classic triad is Vertical Gaze Palsy trouble looking down early Falls especially backwards and Pseudobulbar palsy. It is a Tauopathy. Levodopa is almost always administered with Carbidopa. What is the primary function of Carbidopa? It increases the breakdown of Levodopa in the brain It facilitates transport of Levodopa across the Blood-Brain Barrier It inhibits DOPA decarboxylase in the periphery It directly stimulates dopamine receptors It prevents the reuptake of dopamine 2 Carbidopa is a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor . It cannot cross the BBB so it inhibits the conversion of Levodopa to Dopamine in the periphery increasing the amount of Levodopa that reaches the brain and reducing systemic side effects. A 45-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish male presents with symptoms of Parkinson s disease. He mentions that his cousin had Gaucher s disease. Which genetic mutation is most likely involved? SNCA Alpha-Synuclein LRRK2 GBA Glucocerebrosidase Parkin PINK1 2 Mutations in the GBA gene which encodes Glucocerebrosidase are the most common genetic risk factor for Parkinson s disease especially in the Ashkenazi Jewish population and are linked to Gaucher s Disease ."}, {"id": "l58", "title": "Lecture 58: Pharmacology: Parkinson and Alzheimer", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l58 Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer Neurology Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer n n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. n Session Lecture 58 n n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD Pharmacology n Goal Restore Dopamine DA levels or reduce Acetylcholine ACh activity to fix the DA/ACh imbalance. n n 1.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa Sinemet n Mechanism n Levodopa L-Dopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses the Blood-Brain Barrier BBB via neutral amino acid transporter. Converted to DA by Dopa Decarboxylase DDC . n Carbidopa Inhibits Peripheral DDC . Does NOT cross BBB. n Why Combine? Without Carbidopa only 1-3% of L-Dopa enters the brain most converted peripherally $ rightarrow$ side effects . With Carbidopa 10% enters brain. n Adverse Effects n Peripheral Nausea/Vomiting CTZ stimulation Orthostatic Hypotension Cardiac Arrhythmias. n Central Dyskinesias writhing movements after long-term use Psychosis Hallucinations/Delusions . n Drug Interactions n High Protein Meals Amino acids compete for transporter $ rightarrow$ Decrease efficacy. n Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Increases peripheral breakdown of L-Dopa Avoid high doses . n n 1.2 COMT Inhibitors -capone n Mechanism Inhibit Catechol-O-Methyltransferase COMT blocking conversion of L-Dopa to 3-O-Methyldopa 3-OMD . Prolongs L-Dopa half-life On time . n Agents n Entacapone Peripheral inhibition only. n Tolcapone Central & Peripheral. Black Box Warning Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs . n Opicapone Long-acting Once daily . n Side Effect Orange/Brown discoloration of urine . n n 1.3 MAO-B Inhibitors -giline n Mechanism Inhibit Monoamine Oxidase B MAO-B preventing central breakdown of Dopamine. n Agents n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine/Methamphetamine $ rightarrow$ Insomnia Do not take late at night . n Rasagiline Irreversible inhibitor. n Safinamide Reversible MAO-B inhibitor + Glutamate modulation. n Neuroprotection May prevent MPTP conversion to toxic MPP+ preventing mitochondrial damage . n Interactions Risk of Serotonin Syndrome if combined with SSRIs/TCAs though risk is lower with selective MAO-B vs MAO-A . n n 1.4 Dopamine Agonists n Mechanism Directly stimulate Post-synaptic Dopamine Receptors D2/D3 . No conversion needed. n Agents n Pramipexole Mirapex D3 > D2. Antioxidant properties. Also for Restless Leg Syndrome RLS . n Ropinirole Requip D2/D3. Also for RLS. n Rotigotine Transdermal Patch stable levels . n Adverse Effects n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Shopping. n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep without warning. n n 1.5 Rescue Therapy Apomorphine n Use Acute subcutaneous injection for severe Off episodes Freezing . n Adverse Effect Severe Nausea/Vomiting. n Management Pre-treat with Trimethobenzamide . n Contraindication Do NOT use 5-HT3 Antagonists Ondansetron/Zofran $ rightarrow$ Causes profound Hypotension and Syncope. n n 1.6 Amantadine n Mechanism Antiviral Flu A - M2 channel blocker . In PD NMDA Receptor Antagonist + increases DA release. n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias . n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Purplish net-like skin rash . n n 1.7 Adenosine Antagonist Istradefylline n Mechanism Adenosine A2A receptor antagonist in Basal Ganglia. Reduces Off time. n Side Effects Insomnia Structurally related to Caffeine . n n 1.8 Anticholinergics Benztropine Trihexyphenidyl n Use Tremor predominant PD. n Adverse Effect Antimuscarinic Dry mouth urinary retention confusion . Avoid in Geriatrics Beers List . n n--- n n 2. Alzheimer s Disease AD Pharmacology n Goal Increase Acetylcholine ACh or reduce Glutamate Excitotoxicity. n n 2.1 Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors AChEIs n Mechanism Inhibit breakdown of ACh in the synaptic cleft. n Agents n Donepezil Aricept . n Rivastigmine Inhibits AChE and Butyrylcholinesterase. Patch available. n Galantamine Also acts as a nicotinic receptor agonist. n Adverse Effects Cholinergic Excess SLUDGE . n Nausea Diarrhea Vomiting. n Bradycardia Risk of syncope Caution with Beta-blockers . n Bronchoconstriction Caution in asthma/COPD . n n 2.2 NMDA Receptor Antagonist n Agent Memantine . n Mechanism Uncompetitive NMDA Antagonist . Blocks tonic/excessive glutamate stimulation excitotoxicity while allowing physiologic signals. n Use Moderate to Severe AD. Can be combined with AChEIs. n n 2.3 Anti-Amyloid Monoclonal Antibodies n Mechanism Bind and clear Amyloid Beta plaques. n Agents n Lecanemab Targets Protofibrils. n Donanemab. n Adverse Effects ARIA Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities . n ARIA-E Edema/Effusion. n ARIA-H Micro-Hemorrhage/Hemosiderin. n Monitoring Requires serial MRIs . n n--- n n 3. High Yield Renders n n highyield n Parkinson s Drug Toxicities & Pearls n| Drug | Key Mechanism/Fact | The Board Exam Side Effect | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Carbidopa | Peripheral DDC Inhibitor | Reduces Peripheral side effects Nausea/Ortho | n| Tolcapone | COMT Inhibitor Central/Periph | Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs | n| Entacapone | COMT Inhibitor Peripheral | Orange Urine | n| Selegiline | MAO-B Inhibitor | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia | n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Impulse Control Gambling Sleep Attacks | n| Apomorphine | Rescue Injection | Severe Nausea NO Zofran! $ rightarrow$ Hypotension | n| Amantadine | NMDA Antagonist | Livedo Reticularis Lacy rash | n n n highyield n Alzheimer s Drug Toxicities n| Class | Agents | Key Side Effects | n| --- | --- | --- | n| AChE Inhibitors | Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine | Bradycardia Falls/Syncope Diarrhea Nausea | n| NMDA Antagonist | Memantine | Dizziness Confusion Use in Mod-Severe AD | n| Monoclonals | Lecanemab Donanemab | ARIA Edema/Hemorrhage on MRI | n n questions A 75-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on Selegiline as an adjunct to his regimen. He complains of difficulty falling asleep. This side effect is most likely due to the metabolism of the drug into which substance? Dopamine Norepinephrine Amphetamine Serotonin Histamine 2 Selegiline an MAO-B inhibitor is metabolized into l-methamphetamine and l-amphetamine which have stimulant properties and can cause insomnia . It should be taken earlier in the day. A patient with Parkinson s disease experiences severe off episodes where he freezes and cannot move. His physician prescribes a subcutaneous rescue medication. Which antiemetic is CONTRAINDICATED in this patient due to the risk of profound hypotension? Trimethobenzamide Ondansetron Promethazine Prochlorperazine Metoclopramide 1 The rescue medication is likely Apomorphine . It causes severe nausea but it is CONTRAINDICATED to use 5-HT3 antagonists like Ondansetron Zofran because the combination causes severe hypotension and loss of consciousness. Trimethobenzamide is the preferred antiemetic. A 68-year-old female is prescribed Entacapone to help manage her motor fluctuations. She calls the clinic in a panic because her urine has changed color. What color is her urine likely to be? Blue Red Orange/Brown Green Black 2 Entacapone a COMT inhibitor is known to cause a harmless orange or brownish discoloration of the urine . A patient with Alzheimer s disease is started on Donepezil. During a follow-up visit his heart rate is noted to be 48 bpm. This bradycardia is a result of Blockade of Nicotinic receptors Increased Acetylcholine activity at the SA node Inhibition of Calcium channels Stimulation of Beta-1 receptors Blockade of Muscarinic receptors 1 Donepezil is an Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitor which increases levels of Acetylcholine. Excess ACh acts on M2 muscarinic receptors in the heart parasympathetic activation leading to Bradycardia which can increase the risk of syncope and falls. A patient with early Alzheimer s disease is started on Lecanemab. Two months later a surveillance MRI is performed. It shows areas of edema ARIA-E . What is the mechanism of action of this drug? Uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor Monoclonal antibody against Amyloid Beta Muscarinic receptor agonist Inhibitor of Tau phosphorylation 2 Lecanemab is a monoclonal antibody that targets Amyloid Beta protofibrils. A known adverse effect of this class is Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities ARIA which can present as edema ARIA-E or hemorrhage ARIA-H ."}, {"id": "l6", "title": "Lecture #6: Anatomy of the Spinal Cord", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l6 Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Gross Anatomy of the Spinal Cord Slides 5-8 nThe spinal cord is a cylindrical structure that serves as the main information superhighway between the brain and the body. It is flattened dorsoventrally and resides within the protective vertebral column. nCrucially the spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column. In adults it ends at the L1-L2 vertebral level forming a tapered tip called the Conus Medullaris . Below this level the spinal canal contains a collection of nerve roots called the Cauda Equina horse s tail floating in the Lumbar Cistern of CSF. n n 2. Meninges and Vascular Supply Slides 10-11 40-41 nThe spinal cord is wrapped in three meningeal layers n1. Dura Mater The tough outer layer. Unlike the cranial dura which is double-layered the spinal dura is single-layered . Between the dura and the vertebrae lies the Epidural Space a real space containing fat and veins Venous Plexus of Batson . n2. Arachnoid Mater The middle spider-web-like layer. n3. Pia Mater The delicate inner layer attached directly to the cord. It forms lateral projections called Denticulate Ligaments that anchor the cord to the dura. n n Blood Supply nThe cord is supplied by one Anterior Spinal Artery ASA and two Posterior Spinal Arteries PSA . n ASA Supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord including the Ventral Horns/Motor and STT/Pain . n PSA Supplies the posterior 1/3 Dorsal Columns/Sensory . n n correlate n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Syndrome n- Pathophysiology Infarction of the ASA often from aortic surgery or occlusion . n- Presentation Sudden onset of complete motor paralysis Corticospinal tract and loss of pain/temperature sensation Spinothalamic tract below the lesion. n- Key Distinction Proprioception and Vibration are SPARED because the Dorsal Columns are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries. n n n 3. Internal Topography and Tracts Slides 29-39 nThe internal structure consists of a central H-shaped butterfly of Gray Matter cell bodies surrounded by White Matter axons . n n The Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Pathway nThis ascending pathway conveys fine touch vibration and proprioception . nPrimary afferents enter the cord and ascend ipsilaterally in the Dorsal Columns Fasciculus Gracilis for legs Cuneatus for arms . They do not synapse until they reach the Medulla Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus . There they decussate Internal Arcuate Fibers and ascend to the Thalamus. n n The Spinothalamic Tract STT - Anterolateral System nThis pathway conveys pain temperature and crude touch . nPrimary afferents enter via Lissauer s Tract and synapse immediately in the Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . The second-order neurons decussate immediately via the Anterior White Commissure and ascend contralaterally. n n The Corticospinal Tract CST nThis is the primary descending motor pathway for voluntary movement. nFibers originate in the Motor Cortex descend through the brainstem and decussate in the Medulla Pyramids . They descend in the spinal cord as the Lateral Corticospinal Tract and synapse on Anterior Horn Cells Lower Motor Neurons . n n 4. Spinal Reflexes Slides 23-27 nReflexes are involuntary motor responses to stimuli mediated by local spinal circuits. n n Stretch Reflex Myotatic nThis is the simplest reflex e.g. Knee Jerk . It is Monosynaptic . n- Mechanism Muscle stretch stimulates specific sensory receptors Muscle Spindles → Ia afferent fires → Direct synapse on Alpha Motor Neuron → Muscle contracts. n- Function Maintains muscle tone and posture. n n Flexor Withdrawal & Crossed-Extensor Reflexes nThese are Polysynaptic . n- Flexor Withdrawal A painful stimulus invokes pulling the limb away Flexion . n- Crossed-Extensor Simultaneously the opposite limb extends to support body weight preventing falling . n n correlate n Tetanus n- Pathophysiology Clostridium tetani toxin Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely from a wound to the spinal cord. It cleaves SNARE proteins in Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons . n- Mechanism Prevents release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine . n- Presentation Unchecked excitation of motor neurons leads to spastic paralysis lockjaw trismus and opisthotonos arching back . n n n 5. Clinical Correlates Additional n n correlate n Shingles Herpes Zoster n- Pathophysiology Reactivation of Varicella Zoster Virus VZV which lies dormant in the Dorsal Root Ganglia DRG . n- Presentation Painful vesicular rash in a specific dermatomal distribution does not cross midline . n n n correlate n Disc Herniation Slides 14-15 n- Pathophysiology The soft Nucleus Pulposus herniates through a tear in the tough Annulus Fibrosus . n- Location Posterolateral herniation is most common and typically compresses the spinal nerve root below the level of the disc e.g. L4/L5 disc compresses L5 root . n- Presentation Radiculopathy shooting pain sensory loss and weakness in the affected nerve distribution. n Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 6 prev l5 next l7 pdf pdfs/Lecture 6. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf Spinal Cord n Tracts n Ascending Sensory n DCML Touch/Prop. Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla n STT Pain/Temp. Contralateral in cord → Decussates Cord n Descending Motor n CST Motor Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla n Reflexes n Stretch Monosynaptic DTRs n Withdrawal Polysynaptic Pain n Pathology n ASA Syndrome Spares Dorsal Columns n Brown-Sequard Hemi-section n Ipsi Motor Proprioception n Contra Pain/Temp n Tetanus Blocks Inhibition questions 1. A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in the right leg and spastic paralysis and loss of vibration sense in the left leg. This Dissociated Sensory Loss pattern Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome is caused by a hemisection lesion on which side of the spinal cord? Right side Ipsilateral to pain loss Left side Ipsilateral to motor/vibration loss Bilateral central lesion Dorsal Columns only Anterior Horn only 1 Brown-S u00e9quard Hemisection <br>- Ipsilateral signs Motor CST decussated above + Vibration DCML decussates above .<br>- Contralateral signs Pain/Temp STT decussates immediately . <br>Since motor/vibration loss is on the Left the lesion is on the Left . 2. Tetanus toxin causes spastic paralysis by blocking the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine from which specific spinal cord cells? Alpha Motor Neurons Gamma Motor Neurons Renshaw Cells Dorsal Root Ganglion Cells Clarke s Nucleus Cells 2 From Slide 22 Tetanospasmin blocks the release of GABA/Glycine from Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons leading to unchecked excitation tetany . 3. An infarction of the Anterior Spinal Artery ASA would result in all of the following deficits below the lesion level EXCEPT Loss of pain sensation STT Loss of temperature sensation STT Spastic Paralysis CST Loss of vibration and proprioception DCML Loss of crude touch 3 From Slide 41 The ASA supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord STT CST . The Dorsal Columns DCML are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries and are SPARED in ASA syndrome. 4. Which spinal tract carries fine touch and proprioception and ascends ipsilaterally within the spinal cord? Lateral Corticospinal Tract Spinothalamic Tract Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML Rubrospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract 2 The DCML enters the cord and ascends in the posterior columns on the SAME side ipsilateral until it reaches the medulla where it decussates. 5. In the stretch reflex e.g. Knee Jerk the primary afferent neuron synapses directly onto the alpha-motor neuron. This type of reflex is classified as Polysynaptic Disynaptic Monosynaptic Intersegmental Contralateral 2 From Slide 24 The stretch reflex is the simplest reflex and is Monosynaptic one synapse between sensory and motor neuron . Function of Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML ? Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception Neuro Function of Spinothalamic Tract STT ? Pain and Temperature Neuro Function of Corticospinal Tract CST ? Voluntary Motor Control Neuro Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuro Where does STT decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuro Where does CST decussate? Medulla Pyramids Neuro What is spard in Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome? Dorsal Columns Proprioception/Vibration Pathology Virus dormant in DRG causing Shingles? Varicella Zoster Virus VZV Micro Toxin blocking Renshaw Cells GABA/Glycine ? Tetanus Toxin Micro Reflex level for Patellar Knee Jerk? L3-L4 Clinical BandB Neurology > Somatosensory Systems Lumbar Puncture LP Because the spinal cord ends at L1-L2 a Lumbar Puncture is safely performed at the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace to avoid damaging the cord. Vascular Watershed The mid-thoracic region T4-T8 is a vascular watershed area and is most susceptible to infarction during hypotension. DCML Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the DCML Ipsilaterally because it doesn t cross until the medulla . STT Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the STT Contralaterally typically 1-2 segments below the lesion due to Lissauer s tract ascent . CST Rule Lesions in the spinal cord cause Ipsilateral Spastic Paralysis UMN signs below the lesion because it already crossed in the medulla . Renshaw Cells Renshaw cells are the brakes of the motor system. Without them e.g. Tetanus toxin blockade motor neurons fire uncontrollably."}, {"id": "l61", "title": "Lecture #61: Gait and Movement", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l61 Lecture 61 Gait and Movement lecturer Adena Leder D.O. Neurology session Lecture 61 prev l60 next l62 pdf pdfs/Lecture 61. A. Leder D.O. PPT - updated 1.29.26 .pdf highYieldPdf content/L61 HighYield Render.pdf transcript Lecture 61 Gait and Movement n n Lecturer Adena Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 61 n n Introduction to Movement Disorders Slide 2 n nMovement disorders represent a complex interplay between the basal ganglia cerebellum and motor cortex. The approach to a patient with a suspected movement disorder such as Parkinson s Disease PD requires a systematic evaluation of their history observation Look passive tone Feel and active strength/gait Move . Understanding the distinction between hyperkinetic and hypokinetic states is fundamental to narrowing the differential diagnosis. n n Core Assessement Objectives n- History Establish onset progression and risk factors. n- Observation Look for tremors posture and involuntary movements. n- Tone Feel for rigidity lead-pipe vs. cogwheel . n- Function Assess strength and observed gait mechanics. n n History Taking The Foundations Slide 3-8 n nA detailed history is the cornerstone of diagnosis as many movement disorders are clinical diagnoses without definitive biomarkers. n n Demographics and Onset Slide 3 n- Age Early vs. Late onset changes the differential. n- Handedness Asymmetry is a hallmark of idiopathic PD symptoms often start on the dominant side . n- Timeline Establishing the Year 0 of symptom onset. n- Prodromal Phase Non-motor symptoms often precede motor symptoms by years e.g. loss of smell constipation . n n Medical and Medication History Slide 4-5 n- Secondary Parkinsonism Rule out drug-induced causes. n - Anti-emetics Metoclopramide Reglan used long-term. n - Anti-psychotics Dopamine blockers Haloperidol Risperidone . n - Other Offenders Lithium and Valproic Acid Depakote can cause tremor. n- Comorbidities Cardiovascular disease Diabetes Cancer history. n n correlate nDrug-Induced Parkinsonism n n- Pathophysiology Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway. n- Presentation Symmetric parkinsonism unlike asymmetric idiopathic PD often without resting tremor. n- Diagnosis Temporal relationship with offending agent; lack of response to Levodopa. n- Treatment Stop the offending agent. n- Risk Factors Elderly high potency antipsychotics anti-emetics. n n n Environmental Exposures Slide 8 nCertain toxins are selectively toxic to dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra. n n| Exposure | Context | Mechanism/Notes | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Pesticides | Farm/Vineyard work | Rotenone Paraquat Glyphosate Roundup | n| Well Water | Rural living | Heavy metals/Pesticide runoff | n| Agent Orange | Vietnam Veterans | Increased risk of PD | n| MPTP | Synthetic Heroin | Acute permanent destruction of Substantia Nigra | n| Head Trauma | Boxers/Football | Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE overlap | n n Genetics Slide 6-7 nWhile most PD is sporadic idiopathic genetics play a role especially in young-onset cases. n- Prevalence 15% of patients have a family history. n- Risk Group Ashkenazi Jewish descent Higher carrier rate . n- Major Genes n - LRRK2 Common autosomal dominant. n - GBA1 Associated with Gaucher s carrier state. n - PRKN Parkin Early onset recessive. n n Review of Systems The Hidden Signs Slide 11-12 n nParkinson s Disease is a whole-body synucleinopathy not just a brain disease. Autonomic and sensory dysfunction often constitute the Prodrome. n n highyield n The Parkinson s Prodrome nSymptoms that can precede motor signs by 10-20 years n1. Anosmia Loss of smell. n2. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out vivid dreams injuring bed partner due to loss of REM atonia. n3. Constipation Slow colonic transit. n4. Autonomic Dysfunction Orthostatic hypotension urinary frequency/urgency. n n n correlate nSeborrheic Dermatitis n n- Pathophysiology Dysregulation of autonomic control of sebaceous glands sebum production . n- Presentation Greasy scaly erythematous rash typically on the face brow nasolabial folds and scalp. n- Lecturer s Point A common dermatologic finding in PD patients. n n n The Physical Examination Slide 14-17 n n Cranial Nerves and face n- Hypomimia Masked Facies u2013 reduction in facial expressivity and blinking. n- Vertical Gaze Limitation suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP an atypical parkinsonism. n- Glabellar Tap Persistent blinking when tapping the forehead Myerson s sign . n n Motor Tone Assessment nExamination of tone differentiates spasticity corticospinal from rigidity extrapyramidal . n- Lead-Pipe Rigidity Uniform constant resistance throughout range of motion. n- Cogwheel Rigidity Ratchet-like resistance Rigidity + Tremor . n- Asymmetry Idiopathic PD typically starts unilaterally . n n Tremor Analysis n- Resting Tremor Pill-rolling 4-6 Hz . Suppressed with movement. Classic for PD. n- Postural/Action Tremor Occurs when holding a pose against gravity. Classic for Essential Tremor . n n correlate nEssential Tremor n n- Pathophysiology Cerebellar-thalamic circuit oscillation. n- Presentation Bilateral symmetric action tremor hands voice head . improves with alcohol. n- Diagnosis Clinical. Spiral drawing test shows shaky axis. n- Treatment Propranolol Beta-blocker or Primidone. n- Differentiation PD is Resting; ET is Action. n n n Gait Assessment Slide 18 nThe Get Up and Go or observable gait provides high-yield diagnostic clues. n- Shuffling Short steps marche u00e0 petits pas . n- Fenestration Accelerating steps to keep balance. n- Freezing Feet feel stuck to the floor especially in doorways/turning. n- En Bloc Turning Turning like a statue/log rather than pivoting segments. n- Reduced Arm Swing Often the first motor sign early asymmetric . n n Diagnosis and Management Slide 19 n nDiagnosis remains primarily clinical requiring Bradykinesia plus either Rigidity or Resting Tremor. n n Diagnostic Tests n- DaTscan Dopamine transporter SPECT scan. Shows loss of uptake in striatum comma shape becomes a period . n- Levodopa Challenge Significant improvement in symptoms confirms Dopaminergic deficiency responsive in PD non-responsive in Parkinson-Plus syndromes . n- Skin Biopsy For Alpha-synuclein deposits. n n mnemonic n The S Signs of Parkinson s Disease n S-WORDS Slide 20 n n- S hake Resting Tremor n- S tiffness Rigidity n- S low Bradykinesia n- S tate Masked Facies n- S huffle Gait n- S mall writing Micrographia n- S mell loss Anosmia n- S leep REM behavior disorder n- S oft speech Hypophonia n- S eborrhea Dermatitis n- S adness Depression n n questions 1. A 62-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand and general slowness of movement. His wife notes that he has been acting out violent dreams and shouting in his sleep for the past 5 years. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Essential Tremor Parkinson s Disease Drug-Induced Parkinsonism Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 1 Parkinson s Disease is characterized by the triad of resting tremor bradykinesia and rigidity. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD is a strong prodromal sign that can precede motor symptoms by years. 2. Which of the following clinical findings is REQUIRED for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor Bradykinesia Postural Instability Cogwheel Rigidity Anosmia 1 Bradykinesia slowness of movement is the central and required feature for the diagnosis of parkinsonism. While tremor is common some patients present with the akinetic-rigid variant without tremor. 3. A 25-year-old male presents with new-onset resting tremor and rigidity. He has a history of Schizophrenia and recently started a new medication. Which mechanism explains his motor symptoms? Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Blockade of Dopamine D2 receptors Depletion of Acetylcholine in the Striatum Cerebellar atrophy Autoimmune attack on the neuromuscular junction 1 Blockade of D2 receptors by antipsychotic medications e.g. Haloperidol causes Drug-Induced Parkinsonism . This is a functional blockade not the structural cell death seen in idiopathic PD. 4. During a gait examination a patient takes multiple small shuffling steps and turns his body as a single rigid unit. This turning pattern is best described as Ataxic turning En bloc turning Magnetic gait Scissoring gait Steppage gait 1 En bloc turning is the classic description of a PD patient turning like a statue or log requiring multiple small steps to reorient due to axial rigidity and bradykinesia. 5. Which environmental exposure is historically linked to the rapid destruction of the Substantia Nigra and acute parkinsonism? Lead paint MPTP Asbestos Silica dust Radon 1 MPTP is a neurotoxin found in synthetic heroin contaminants that selectively destroys dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra serving as a model for MPTP-induced parkinsonism. What is the cardinal motor feature required for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Bradykinesia plus Rigidity OR Tremor Diagnosis What type of tremor is characteristic of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor 4-6 Hz Pill-rolling Clinical What dermatologic condition is commonly associated with Parkinson s Disease? Seborrheic Dermatitis Clinical What sleep disorder is a strong prodromal sign of synucleinopathies like PD? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Clinical What is the mechanism of Drug-Induced Parkinsonism? Dopamine D2 receptor blockade e.g. Antipsychotics Metoclopramide Pathology What genetic mutation is most common in Ashkenazi Jewish patients with PD? LRRK2 or GBA1 Genetics What physical exam finding suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP rather than PD? Vertical Gaze Palsy Inability to look up/down Diagnosis What is En bloc turning? Turning the body as a single rigid unit/log requiring multiple steps Clinical What effect does Cigarette Smoking have on Parkinson s risk? It is associated with a DECREASED risk protective Epidemiology What differentiates Essential Tremor from Parkinson s Tremor? Essential Tremor is Action/Postural and symmetric; PD is Resting and asymmetric Diagnosis BandB Neurology > Movement Disorders Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Disorders > Parkinson Disease SketchyPath Neuro > Degenerative > Parkinson FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Parkinson Disease The Parkinson s Prodrome Non-motor symptoms like Anosmia Constipation and REM Sleep Behavior Disorder can appear 10-20 years before the first tremor. Asymmetry is Key Idiopathic PD almost always starts asymmetrically one hand/side . Symmetric onset should raise suspicion for Drug-Induced Parkinsonism or Atypical syndromes. Tremor vs. Bradykinesia Bradykinesia is the disability-causing symptom. Tremor is the cosmetic symptom. Some patients are Akinetic-Rigid and never develop a tremor. Arm Swing Reduced arm swing on one side is often the earliest detectable motor sign of PD during gait analysis. Gait and Movement Disorders n n Parkinson s Disease TRAP n- T remor Resting Pill-rolling Asymmetric n- R igidity Cogwheel vs Lead-pipe n- A kinesia/Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked facies Decreased blink n- P ostural Instability Late finding Pull test n n Etiology n- Idiopathic Most common Age > 60 n- Genetic LRRK2 GBA1 Ashkenazi Jewish n- Environmental Pesticides MPTP Agent Orange n- Drug-Induced Antipsychotics Metoclopramide D2 Blockers n n Prodromal Signs n- Sensory Anosmia Smell loss n- Sleep REM Sleep Behavior Disorder n- Autonomic Constipation Orthostasis n n Differential Diagnosis n- Essential Tremor Action tremor Improves w/ Alcohol Bilateral n- PSP Vertical Gaze Palsy Early Falls n- MSA Severe Autonomic Dysfunction"}, {"id": "l62", "title": "Lecture #62: Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l62 Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D. Neurology session Lecture 62 prev l61 next l63 pdf pdfs/Lecture 62. M. Mihlbachler Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L62 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L62 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies n n Lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D. n Session Lecture 62 n n Introduction to Peripheral Nerve Injuries Slide 2-3 n nUnderstanding peripheral nerve injuries requires a solid grasp of the brachial plexus architecture and the relationship between spinal nerves dermatomes and myotomes. A nerve is a bundle of axons and injury can be partial or complete. The clinical presentation u2014whether it s a motor deficit weakness/paralysis or sensory deficit numbness/tingling u2014depends entirely on the location of the lesion. We differentiate between injuries at the Root/Trunk level affecting multiple nerves and Peripheral Nerve level affecting specific muscles/skin areas . n n Key Principles n- Roots vs. Peripheral Nerves Lesions at the root level affect myotomes groups of muscles and dermatomes skin strip . Peripheral nerve lesions affect the specific muscles and skin supplied by that nerve. n- Variation Anatomical variations exist; not every patient presents with textbook symptoms. n- Scope This lecture focuses on classic well-documented neuropathies. n n Brachial Plexus Trunk Lesions n nInjuries to the trunks of the brachial plexus cause widespread deficits because they affect fibers destined for multiple peripheral nerves. These are often associated with traumatic traction injuries during birth or severe trauma in adults. n n Superior Trunk Lesion C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Palsy Slide 35-40 n nExcessive separation of the neck and shoulder e.g. shoulder dystocia during birth motorcycle accident tears the C5 and C6 roots of the Superior Trunk. This devastates the muscles of the shoulder and arm supplied by these roots. n n- Nerves Affected n - Musculocutaneous n. Loss of Biceps Brachii Flexion Supination . n - Axillary n. Loss of Deltoid Abduction and Teres Minor Lateral Rotation . n - Suprascapular n. Loss of Supraspinatus Abduction initiation and Infraspinatus Lateral Rotation . n- Resulting Posture The Waiter s Tip position. The arm is adducted loss of deltoid/supraspinatus medially rotated loss of lateral rotators and extended/pronated loss of biceps . n n correlate nErb-Duchenne Palsy Erb s Palsy n n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Superior Trunk C5-C6 . n- Presentation Waiter s Tip position n - Adducted shoulder n - Medially Rotated shoulder n - Extended elbow n - Pronated forearm n- Sensory Loss Lateral aspect of arm and forearm C5-C6 dermatomes . n- Risk Factors Shoulder dystocia macrosomia motorcycle accidents. n n n mnemonic n Erb-Duchenne Palsy n Waiter s Tip n n- Imagine a waiter subtly accepting a tip behind their back Arm hangs by side rotated internally palm facing back. n n n Inferior Trunk Lesion C8-T1 Klumpke s Palsy Slide 43-49 n nHyper-abduction of the arm e.g. catching a tree branch while falling pulling an infant s arm during birth tears the C8 and T1 roots. This primarily affects the intrinsic muscles of the hand. n n- Nerves Affected n - Ulnar n. C8-T1 All intrinsic hand muscles it supplies. n - Median n. C8-T1 component Intrinsic hand muscles Thumbnail lateral lumbricals . n- Key Deficit Loss of ALL intrinsic hand muscles Lumbricals and Interossei . n - Function of Lumbricals Flex MCP Extend PIP/DIP. n - Loss Extension of MCP by unopposed extensor digitorum Flexion of PIP/DIP by unopposed flexors . n- Result Total Claw Hand . n n correlate nKlumpke s Palsy n n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Inferior Trunk C8-T1 . n- Presentation Total Claw Hand Slide 49 . n - Clawing Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP in ALL digits 2-5 . n - Atrophy Wasting of intrinsic hand muscles interossei guttering . n- Sensory Loss Medial aspect of arm forearm and hand C8-T1 dermatomes . n- Lecturer s Point Distinguish from isolated Ulnar Claw which spares digits 2-3 . Klumpke affects ALL digits because C8-T1 feeds both Ulnar and Median intrinsic muscles. n n n Peripheral Nerve Injuries The Scapula and Shoulder n nMoving distally isolated nerve injuries present with more specific functional losses. n n Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C7 Slide 52 nLesion causes paralysis of the Serratus Anterior which anchors the scapula to the thoracic wall. n n correlate nWinged Scapula n n- Pathophysiology Damage to Long Thoracic Nerve Saltatory/Stab wound Radical Mastectomy . n- Presentation Medial border of scapula protrudes posteriorly wings when patient pushes against a wall. n- Functional Deficit Inability to abduct arm above horizontal loss of scapular rotation . n n n Axillary Nerve C5-C6 Slide 54-55 nCommonly injured in Surgical Neck Fractures of the humerus or Anterior-Inferior Dislocation of the shoulder. n n highyield n Axillary Nerve Injury n- Motor Loss of Abduction >15 degrees due to Deltoid paralysis Slide 54 . n- Sensory Loss of sensation over the Regimental Badge area Lateral shoulder . n n n Radial Nerve Injuries Posterior Cord n nThe Radial nerve supplies the Extensors of the arm forearm and hand. Presentation depends heavily on the level of injury. n n Axilla Level Saturday Night Palsy Slide 57-58 nCompression in the axilla e.g. drunk arm over chair crutches . n- Deficits n - Triceps Loss of elbow extension often spared if gravity assists . n - Wrist/Fingers Loss of extension -> Wrist Drop . n - Sensory Posterior arm forearm and dorsum of hand. n n correlate nSaturday Night Palsy Radial Neuropathy at Axilla n n- Pathophysiology Compression of Radial Nerve in the axilla. n- Presentation Wrist Drop inability to extend wrist/fingers . n- Sensory Numbness on posterior arm/forearm/hand. n- Differentiation Triceps reflex may be diminished unlike distal lesions . n n n Mid-Shaft Humerus Fracture Radial Groove Slide 60-61 nThe radial nerve spirals around the humerus in the radial groove. n- Deficits n - Triceps SPARED Branches to triceps arise proximal to the groove. Elbow extension is intact. n - Wrist/Fingers Wrist Drop persists extensors of forearm are distal . n - Sensory Posterior Arm sensory SPARED . Numbness is limited to the dorsum of the hand. n n highyield n Radial Nerve Axilla vs. Spiral Groove n- Axilla Saturday Night Triceps weak/paralyzed + Wrist Drop + Posterior Arm Numbness. n- Spiral Groove Humerus Fracture Triceps SPARED + Wrist Drop + NO Posterior Arm Numbness. n n n Median Nerve Injuries Lateral/Medial Cords n nThe Median nerve supplies the Flexors of the forearm except FCU and ulnar half of FDP and the Thenar muscles OAF Oppose Abduct Flex thumb and Lateral 2 Lumbricals . n n Lesion at Elbow Supracondylar Fracture Slide 65-68 n- Motor Loss n - Forearm Flexors Loss of Pronation Wrist Flexion weak Digit Flexion Digits 1-3 . n - Hand Loss of Thenar muscles No opposition . n- Presentation n - Hand of Benediction occurs when asked to MAKE A FIST . Digits 1 2 3 cannot flex. Digits 4 5 flex Ulnar nerve intact . n - Ape Hand Atrophy of Thenar eminence thumb lies flat . n n correlate nHand of Benediction Benediction Sign n n- Pathophysiology High Median Nerve Injury at Elbow/Proximal . n- Presentation When asked to MAKE A FIST index and middle fingers remain extended; thumb remains extended. Ring and little fingers flex. n- Mechanism Loss of FDS all and FDP lateral half . FDP medial half supplied by Ulnar n. remains intact. n- Lecturer s Point Do not confuse with Ulnar Claw! Benediction is an ACTIVE sign asking to make a fist . n n n Lesion at Wrist Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / Slash Slide 69-70 n- Motor Loss LOAF muscles Lateral 2 Lumbricals Opponens Abductor pollicis brevis Flexor pollicis brevis . n - Forearm Flexors SPARED Patient CAN flex fingers can make a fist . NO Hand of Benediction. n- Sensory Loss Lateral 3.5 digits. n - Carpal Tunnel Palmar cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to tunnel . Palm sensation intact. n - Suicide Slash Palmar cutaneous branch cut. Palm numb. n n correlate nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome n n- Pathophysiology Compression of Median Nerve in carpal tunnel. n- Presentation Paresthesia in lateral 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy late . n- Spare Palmar Cutaneous Branch sensation over thenar eminence is INTACT . n- Risk Factors Repetitive use pregnancy hypothyroidism. n n n Ulnar Nerve Injuries Medial Cord n nThe Ulnar nerve supplies FCU Medial half of FDP and most Intrinsic Hand Muscles Hypothenar Interossei Medial 2 Lumbricals Adductor Pollicis . n n Lesion at Elbow Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Slide 72 n- Motor Loss n - FCU & Medial FDP Weak wrist flexion Loss of DIP flexion in digits 4-5. n - Intrinsics Loss of abduction/adduction Interossei Loss of lumbricals 3-4. n- Presentation Ulnar Claw when opening hand . n- Ulnar Paradox Clawing is LESS severe at the elbow than at the wrist. Why? Because the FDP flexor to digits 4-5 is ALSO paralyzed so the fingers aren t pulled into as much flexion. n n correlate nCubital Tunnel Syndrome n n- Pathophysiology Compression of Ulnar Nerve at medial epicondyle funny bone . n- Presentation Numbness in medial 1.5 digits. Weak grasp. Mild Clawing. n- Ulnar Paradox High lesion = Less Clawing. Low lesion = More Clawing. n n n Lesion at Wrist Guyon s Canal Syndrome Slide 76-78 n- Motor Loss Intrinsic muscles ONLY. Forearm flexors FDP are SPARED. n- Presentation Severe Ulnar Claw . n - Since FDP is working it pulls the DIP joints of digits 4-5 into flexion against the paralyzed lumbricals/interossei. n n correlate nGuyon s Canal Syndrome Ulnar Tunnel n n- Pathophysiology Compression at wrist e.g. cyclist handlebars . n- Presentation Ulnar Claw Severe . n - Claw Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP Digits 4-5 . n- Sensory Medial 1.5 digits. Dorsal cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to wrist -> Dorsal hand sensation intact. n n n highyield n The Hand Signs Summary Slide 80 n n| Sign | Nerve | Level | Action/Context | Feature | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Wrist Drop | Radial | Axilla/Humerus | Resting/Extension | Cannot extend wrist | n| Hand of Benediction | Median | Elbow High | Making a Fist | Digits 1-3 won t flex | n| Ape Hand | Median | Any | Resting | Thenar atrophy | n| Ulnar Claw | Ulnar | Wrist Low | Extending Fingers | Digits 4-5 clawed | n| Total Claw | C8-T1 Klumpke | Trunk | Resting | All digits clawed | n n questions 1. A 21-year-old wrestler complains of weakness in his hand and tingling in his 5th digit. Examination reveals weakness in finger adduction and abduction. He has tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Which diagnosis is most likely? Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Guyon s Canal Syndrome Erb-Duchenne Palsy Pronator Teres Syndrome 1 Cubital Tunnel Syndrome is compression of the ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle. It causes paresthesia in the ulnar distribution and weakness of intrinsic hand muscles interossei leading to difficulty holding a piece of paper adduction . 2. A newborn presents with the right arm adducted medially rotated elbow extended and forearm pronated Waiter s Tip position . Where is the lesion? C8-T1 Roots Inferior Trunk C5-C6 Roots Superior Trunk Posterior Cord Axillary Nerve Long Thoracic Nerve 1 Erb-Duchenne Palsy involves the Superior Trunk C5-C6 . Loss of abductors C5 lateral rotators C5 and flexors/supinators C5-C6 leads to the classic posture. 3. A patient with a mid-shaft humeral fracture presents with wrist drop. Detailed sensation testing reveals numbness on the dorsum of the hand but sensation on the posterior arm is INT. Which nerve structure is spared? Deep branch of Radial Nerve Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve Superficial branch of Radial Nerve Axillary Nerve Musculocutaneous Nerve 1 In a Spiral Groove lesion mid-shaft fracture the Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve supplying posterior arm skin is spared because it branches off the Radial nerve proximal to the groove. 4. When asked to make a fist a patient s index and middle fingers remain extended while the ring and little fingers flex normally. The thumb also fails to flex. This presentation is best described as Ulnar Claw Total Claw Hand Hand of Benediction Wrist Drop Dupuytren s Contracture 2 Hand of Benediction is a sign of High Median Nerve Injury at the elbow . It is an active sign seen when attempting to make a fist due to paralysis of FDS and lateral FDP. 5. A patient presents with winging of the right scapula when pushing against a wall. The injured nerve originates from which spinal levels? C3 C4 C5 C5 C6 C7 C8 T1 C5 C6 C6 C7 C8 1 The Long Thoracic Nerve which innervates the Serratus Anterior originates from roots C5 C6 C7 C5-6-7 wings your heaven . What is the classic posture of Erb-Duchenne Palsy? Waiter s Tip Adducted Medially Rotated Extended Pronated Clinical Which nerve roots are damaged in Klumpke s Palsy? C8-T1 Inferior Trunk Anatomy What physical exam finding is characteristic of Long Thoracic Nerve injury? Winged Scapula medial border protrudes Diagnosis What area of sensation is lost in Axillary Nerve injury? Lateral shoulder Regimental Badge area Clinical Distinguish Radial Nerve injury at Axilla vs. Mid-Humerus regarding Triceps function. Axilla Triceps weak/paralyzed. Mid-Humerus Triceps SPARED. Diagnosis What nerve injury causes Hand of Benediction ? High Median Nerve injury at elbow Clinical Why is sensation over the thenar eminence spared in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome? The Palmar Cutaneous branch of the Median nerves branches PROXIMAL to the carpal tunnel. Anatomy What is the Ulnar Paradox ? High ulnar lesions cause LESS clawing than low lesions because FDP to digits 4-5 is also paralyzed . Concept Which deformity results from combined Combined Median and Ulnar intrinsic paralysis e.g. Klumpke s ? Total Claw Hand Clawing of all digits Clinical What is the common cause of Saturday Night Palsy ? Compression of the Radial Nerve in the axilla e.g. arm over chair . Etiology BandB Neurology > Peripheral Nervous System > Brachial Plexus FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Brachial Plexus Lesions Pathoma N/A Benediction vs. Claw Benediction is an ACTIVE sign High Median injury asked to make fist . Ulnar Claw is a PASSIVE resting deformity Low Ulnar injury . C5-6-7 Wings Back to Heaven Mnemonic for Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C6-C7 -> Serratus Anterior -> Winged Scapula. The Palmar Cutaneous Exception Both Median and Ulnar nerves have palmar cutaneous branches that arise PROXIMAL to the wrist tunnels. If the PALM is numb the lesion is NOT in the tunnel Carpal/Guyon . Brachial Plexus Injuries n n Trunk Lesions n- Superior Trunk C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Waiter s Tip n- Inferior Trunk C8-T1 Klumpke s Total Claw n n Peripheral Nerves n- Long Thoracic Winged Scapula C5-7 n- Axillary Deltoid paralysis Surgical neck n- Radial Wrist Drop Saturday Night Palsy n- Median n - High Hand of Benediction n - Low Ape Hand / Carpal Tunnel n- Ulnar n - High Cubital Tunnel n - Low Ulnar Claw Guyon s Canal"}, {"id": "l63", "title": "Lecture #63: Headache", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l63 Lecture 63 Headache lecturer Adam Reno D.O. Neurology session Lecture 63 prev l62 next l64 pdf pdfs/Lecture 63. A. Reno DO MPH PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L63 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L63 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 63 Headache n n Lecturer Adam Reno D.O. n Session Lecture 63 n n Introduction and Assessment Slide 2-16 n nHeadaches are a massive burden on patients leading to lost work social isolation and suffering. The primary goal of assessment is to exclude urgent/emergent causes Secondary Headaches and accurately diagnose benign recurrent ones Primary Headaches . Diagnosis relies heavily on History u2014imaging is often unnecessary unless Red Flags are present. n n Primary vs. Secondary n- Primary Benign recurrent the headache is the disease Migraine Tension Cluster . n- Secondary Symptom of underlying pathology Tumor Bleed Infection Giant Cell Arteritis . n n Red Flags SNOOP Equivalents Slide 55-59 nThe lecturer emphasizes several Red Flags that demand immediate imaging/workup n- Sudden Onset Thunderclap or Worst headache of my life Subarachnoid Hemorrhage . n- New Onset > 50 Giant Cell Arteritis or Tumor. n- Systemic Signs Fever Weight Loss HIV Cancer history. n- Neurological Deficits Altered mental status focal signs. n- Positional Worse when standing Low pressure or laying/bending High pressure . n n Primary Headaches n n Migraine Headache Slide 23-35 nMigraine is a neuronal dysfunction not just vascular . It involves Cortical Spreading Depression and activation of the Trigeminal-Vascular system. n n Phases n1. Prodrome Irritability food cravings frequent urination hours/days before . n2. Aura 20-30% Transient neurological deficits Visual > Sensory > Motor . Lasts 5-60 mins. n3. Headache Unilateral throbbing nausea photophobia. n4. Postdrome Migraine Hangover tired washed out . n n correlate nMigraine Headache n n- Pathophysiology Cortical Spreading Depression + Trigeminal activation + CGRP release. n- Presentation n - Unilateral 60-70% n - Throbbing/Pulsating n - Aggravated by activity n - Nausea/Vomiting n - Photophobia/Phonophobia n- Diagnosis Clinical 5 attacks lasting 4-72h . n- Triggers Stress Estrogen Menses/OCPs Chocolate Cheese Sleep changes. n- Acute Treatment NSAIDs Triptans 5-HT agonists Antiemetics Metoclopramide . n- Prophylaxis Beta-blockers Propranolol Topiramate Amitriptyline CGRP antagonists. n n n highyield n Migraine Diagnostic Criteria Visual Mnemonic n POUND n- P ulsating n- O ne-day duration 4-72 hours n- U nilateral n- N ausea/Vomiting n- D isabling intensity n n n Tension-Type Headache Slide 37-41 nThe most common headache type. Often associated with stress anxiety and depression. n n correlate nTension-Type Headache n n- Pathophysiology Pericranial muscle tenderness/sensitization. n- Presentation n - Bilateral Band-like squeezing . n - Non-pulsating Pressing/Tightening . n - Mild-Moderate intensity. n - NO Nausea/Vomiting. n - NO aggravation by activity. n- Treatment n - Episodic NSAIDs Acetaminophen OMT. n - Chronic >15 days/mo Amitriptyline TCA Stress management. n n n Cluster Headache Slide 42 nA Trigeminal Autonomic Cephalalgia. Severe suicide-inducing pain. n n correlate nCluster Headache n n- Pathophysiology Hypothalamic activation. n- Presentation n - Male > Female 20-50s . n - Strictly Unilateral Peri-orbital Ice pick in eye . n - Autonomic Signs Ipsilateral Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Miosis Conjunctival injection. n - Behavior Agitated pacing unlike Migraineurs who lie still . n - Timing Attacks occur in clusters daily for weeks often at night. n- Acute Treatment 100% Oxygen Non-rebreather Sumatriptan Subcutaneous . n- Prophylaxis Verapamil. n n n Secondary Headaches Slide 55-59 n nThese encompass life-threatening conditions requiring urgent intervention. n n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH n- Cause Rupture of Saccular Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis . n- Presentation Thunderclap Headache Maximum intensity in seconds Worst headache of my life . n- Workup Non-contrast CT Head first line . If negative but suspicion high -> Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia . n n Meningitis / Encephalitis n- Triad Headache + Fever + Nuchal Rigidity Stiff Neck . n- Encephalitis Add Altered Mental Status and Seizures. n n Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis n- Pathophysiology Vasculitis of medium/large arteries branches of External Carotid . n- Demographics age > 50 Female > Male. n- Presentation Unilateral temporal headache Jaw Claudication Scalp tenderness. n- Complication Blindness Ophthalmic artery occlusion . This is a medical emergency. n- Workup/Tx Elevated ESR/CRP . Start High-Dose Steroids immediately before biopsy confirms . n n correlate nGiant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis n n- Presentation Elderly patient >50 with new unilateral headache jaw pain when chewing and tender temporal artery. n- Risk Irreversible Blindness . n- Management Start Steroids Prednisone IMMEDIATELY . Do not wait for temporal artery biopsy. n n n Intracranial Mass Tumor n- Red Flag Headache that is worse in the morning due to hypoventilation/increased CO2/vasodilation during sleep or worse with Valsalva coughing bending . n n Post-Dural Puncture Headache Lumbar Puncture Headache n- Cause CSF leak after LP or Epidural e.g. C-section . n- Presentation Positional Headache Worse when standing/sitting RELIEVED by lying flat . n- Treatment Blood Patch Caffeine. n n highyield n Headache Comparison Table n n| Type | Location | Quality | Duration | Associated Sx | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Migraine | Unilateral | Throbbing | 4-72 hrs | Nausea Photo/Phonophobia Aura | n| Tension | Bilateral | Tight Band | 30m - 7d | Muscle tenderness No Nausea | n| Cluster | Unilateral Eye | Boring/Stabbing | 15-180 min | Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Autonomic | n n n questions 1. A 30-year-old male presents with severe lancinating pain behind his right eye. He is pacing around the room and appears agitated. Exam reveals right-sided ptosis miosis and rhinorrhea. Treatment with 100% Oxygen provides relief. What is the diagnosis? Migraine with Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Trigeminal Neuralgia Giant Cell Arteritis 2 Cluster Headache presents in males with severe unilateral periorbital pain and autonomic symptoms tearing rhinorrhea Horner s syndrome . Patients are agitated pacing . 100% Oxygen is the first-line abortive therapy. 2. A 72-year-old female presents with a new onset right-sided headache and pain in her jaw when chewing steak. She reports some blurring of vision in her right eye. What is the most appropriate immediate next step? Temporal Artery Biopsy Start High-Dose Corticosteroids Non-contrast CT Head Prescribe Sumatriptan Lumbar Puncture 1 This patient has symptoms of Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis Age >50 Headache Jaw Claudication Visual changes . The risk of permanent blindness is high. You must Start Corticosteroids immediately to prevent vision loss; do not delay treatment for the biopsy. 3. A 25-year-old female complains of a tight band squeezing her head bilaterally. The pain is mild-moderate and does not worsen with routine physical activity. She denies nausea or vomiting. What is the most likely diagnosis? Migraine without Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Pseudotumor Cerebri Sinusitis 1 Tension-Type Headache is bilateral non-pulsating band-like and notably lacks the nausea/vomiting and aggravation by activity seen in migraines. 4. A 45-year-old male presents to the ER screaming This is the worst headache of my life! It started instantly like a thunderclap while he was lifting weights. He has no fever but has some nuchal rigidity. The most urgent diagnostic test is MRI Brain Non-contrast CT Head Lumbar Puncture EEG Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate ESR 1 Subarachnoid Hemorrhage presents as a Thunderclap headache. The initial test of choice is a Non-contrast CT Head to look for blood in the subarachnoid space. 5. A patient who recently underwent a C-section with epidural anesthesia complains of a severe headache that is relieved when lying flat but returns immediately upon standing. What is the mechanism? Corebral Venous Thrombosis CSF Leak Low Pressure Meningitis Preeclampsia Postpartum Migraine 1 Post-Dural Puncture Headache is caused by a persistent CSF leak lowering intracranial pressure. The hallmark is the postural nature worse standing better lying down . What are the POUND criteria for Migraine? Pulsating One-day 4-72h Unilateral Nausea/Vomiting Disabling. Diagnosis What are the autonomic features of Cluster Headache? Ptosis Miosis Lacrimation Rhinorrhea Conjunctival Injection all ipsilateral . Clinical What is the first-line ABORTIVE treatment for Cluster Headache? 100% Oxygen and Sumatriptan . Pharmacology How do you distinguish Tension Headache from Migraine? Tension is Bilateral Non-pulsating and has NO Nausea/Vomiting or Aura. Diagnosis What is the most feared complication of Giant Cell Arteritis? Permanent Blindness need immediate steroids . Clinical Describe the headache of a Brain Tumor. Worse in the morning awakening or with Valsalva coughing/bending . Clinical What is a Thunderclap headache suggestive of? Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm . Clinical What characteristic defines a Post-Dural Puncture Headache? Positional Worse standing Improved lying flat. Diagnosis Which population is most affected by Cluster Headaches? Males 20-50s often smokers. Epidemiology What is the classic presentation of Temporal Arteritis? Elderly >50 Unilateral Temporal Headache Jaw Claudication Elevated ESR. Clinical BandB Neurology > Headache FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Headache Pathoma N/A Migraine vs. Cluster Behavior A Migraine patient wants to lie still in a dark quiet room. A Cluster Headache patient is agitated restless and paces around the room. Treat First Biopsy Later If you suspect Giant Cell Arteritis start Steroids immediately to save vision. Do not wait for the temporal artery biopsy results. SNOOP for Red Flags Systemic Ssx Fever/Weight Loss Neurologic Ssx Onset Sudden/Thunderclap Older age >50 new onset Pattern change Positional/Valsalva . Headaches n n Primary Benign Recurrent n- Migraine Unilateral Pulsating Nausea Aura. Tx Triptans n- Tension Bilateral Band-like No Nausea. Tx NSAIDs n- Cluster Unilateral Eye Autonomic Ssx Restless. Tx 100% O2 n n Secondary Red Flags n- Vascular n - SAH Thunderclap n - Temporal Arteritis Jaw Claudication Blindness risk n- Infection Meningitis Fever Stiff Neck n- Structural n - Tumor Morning headache n - Low Pressure Post-LP Positional"}, {"id": "l64", "title": "Lecture #64: Introduction to Neuropharmacology", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l64 Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 64 prev l63 next l65 pdf pdfs/Lecture 64. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L64 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L64 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology General Anesthetics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 64 n n Inhaled Anesthetics Pharmacokinetics n nThe goal of inhaled anesthesia is to achieve equilibrium between the partial pressure of the gas in the alveoli FA and the brain FCNS . The speed of induction depends on the rate at which FA approaches the inspired concentration FI . n n Key Factors Affecting Uptake Slide 10 28-36 n n1. Solubility Blood Gas Partition Coefficient n - Low Solubility e.g. Nitrous Oxide Desflurane The gas does not want to dissolve in blood. It quickly saturates the blood and raises partial pressure -> Rapid Induction and Rapid Recovery . n - High Solubility e.g. Halothane Isoflurane The gas dissolves readily in blood like sugar in tea . It takes a lot of gas to saturate the blood before partial pressure rises -> Slow Induction and Slow Recovery . n n2. Cardiac Output n - Increased CO Removes gas from alveoli faster -> Slows the rise of FA -> Slower Induction . Counter-intuitive but high yield . n - Decreased CO Speeds induction especially for soluble agents . n n3. Alveolar Ventilation Hyperventilation speeds induction. n n highyield n Solubility & Induction Speed n- Low Blood Gas Coefficient N2O Desflurane = FAST Onset. n- High Blood Gas Coefficient Halothane = SLOW Onset. n n n MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration Slide 52 n- Definition The alveolar concentration that prevents movement in 50% of patients in response to surgical incision Standardized Stimulus . n- Potency MAC is inversely related to potency. Low MAC = High Potency . n - Halothane MAC 0.75% Very Potent. n - Nitrous Oxide MAC >100% Weak anesthetic cannot achieve surgical anesthesia alone . n- Additivity MAC values are additive 0.5 MAC N2O + 0.5 MAC Isoflurane = 1 MAC . n n Inhaled Anesthetics Agents & Toxicity n n Nitrous Oxide N2O n- Properties Gas at room temp. Low solubility Fast onset . Weak potency. Good Analgesia. n- Side Effects n - Diffusion Hypoxia On discontinuation N2O rushes into alveoli displacing O2. Prevention Administer 100% O2 for minutes after stopping N2O. n - Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces Contraindicated in Pneumothorax Bowel Obstruction Middle Ear surgery. n - Vitamin B12 Inhibition Inhibits Methionine Synthase -> Megaloblastic Anemia Neuropathy. n n Halogenated Agents Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Halothane n- Mechanism GABA-A agonism NMDA antagonism. n- Organ Effects n - CV Vasodilation -> Hypotension. Desflurane causes transient Tachycardia Sympathetic stim . n - Resp Respiratory Depression Bronchodilation Sevoflurane is best for asthmatics . Desflurane is Pungent airway irritation . n - CNS Increased Cerebral Blood Flow -> Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . n- Toxicities n - Malignant Hyperthermia Genetic disorder Ryanodine receptor . Triggered by Halogenated agents + Succinylcholine. Tx Dantrolene . n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune-mediated liver necrosis Rare . n - Nephrotoxicity Sevoflurane -> Compound A but rare in humans . n n correlate nMalignant Hyperthermia n n- Trigger Volatile Anesthetics Isoflurane Sevoflurane etc. + Succinylcholine. n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from Sarcoplasmic Reticulum. n- Signs Muscle Rigidity Masseter spasm Hyperthermia Hypercapnia High CO2 Tachycardia Acidosis. n- Treatment Stop agent Give Dantrolene . n n n Intravenous Anesthetics Slide 62-70 n nUsed for Induction Rapid onset due to high lipophilicity . Action is terminated by Redistribution Blood -> Brain -> Muscle/Fat NOT metabolism. n n Propofol n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist. n- Clinical Use Induction Maintenance TIVA ICU Sedation. Milk of Amnesia . n- Pros Anti-emetic prevents nausea Rapid recovery. n- Cons Hypotension Vasodilation Respiratory Depression Pain on Injection described as burning. n- Notes Formulated in egg/soybean emulsion bacterial growth risk allergy . n n Etomidate n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist. n- Clinical Use Induction in patients with Cardiovascular Instability . n- Pros Hemodynamic Stability Minimal change in BP/HR . n- Cons Adrenocortical Suppression inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase Nausea/Vomiting. n n Ketamine n- Mechanism NMDA Receptor Antagonist . n- Clinical Use Dissociative Anesthesia eyes open but unresponsive Trauma/Shock. n- Pros Sympathomimetic Increases BP HR Cardiac Output Bronchodilation Good for Asthmatics . n- Cons Emergence Delirium Hallucinations Nightmares . n n Dexmedetomidine n- Mechanism Alpha-2 Agonist central . n- Clinical Use ICU Sedation Adjunct. n- Effects Sedation usually without respiratory depression. Causes Bradycardia/Hypotension. n n highyield n IV Anesthetic Choice High Yields n- Patient with Hypotension/Trauma Use Ketamine Boosts BP or Etomidate Stable BP . Avoid Propofol. n- Patient with Asthma Use Ketamine or Sevoflurane Bronchodilators . Avoid Desflurane Pungent . n- Patient with Head Injury High ICP Avoid Ketamine increases ICP and Volatile agents increase ICP . Propofol/Barbiturates are preferred decrease metabolic rate . n- Post-Op Nausea Use Propofol Anti-emetic . Avoid N2O and Opioids. n n questions 1. An anesthesiologist wishes to speed up the induction of anesthesia with a highly soluble volatile agent. Which maneuver would be most effective? Decreasing Alveolar Ventilation Increasing Cardiac Output Increasing Alveolar Ventilation Using an agent with a higher Blood Gas partition coefficient Administering a beta-blocker 2 Increasing Alveolar Ventilation delivers more gas to the alveoli speeding the rise of partial pressure FA and thus speeding induction. Conversely increasing Cardiac Output slows induction by removing gas from the alveoli too quickly. 2. A trauma patient with severe hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent is preferred due to its ability to increase blood pressure and heart rate? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Sevoflurane 2 Ketamine is a sympathomimetic that increases BP HR and cardiac output making it ideal for hemodynamically unstable patients shock . Propofol causes hypotension. 3. Following a surgery using Nitrous Oxide the patient is extubated and allowed to breathe room air. Minutes later she becomes hypoxic. What is the cause? Malignant Hyperthermia Diffusion Hypoxia Laryngospasm Pneumothorax expansion Opioid overdose 1 Diffusion Hypoxia occurs when N2O is stopped abruptly; the gas rushes from the blood into the alveoli displacing oxygen. It is prevented by administering 100% O2 for several minutes post-anesthesia. 4. Which anesthetic agent is associated with adrenocortical suppression via inhibition of 11-beta-hydroxylase? Ketamine Propofol Midazolam Etomidate Dexmedetomidine 3 Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. It is generally safe for single-dose induction but typically avoided for long-term infusions in septic patients. 5. Comparing two inhaled anesthetics Drug A has a Blood Gas partition coefficient of 0.42. Drug B has a coefficient of 2.30. Which statement is true? Drug A has a slower onset of action than Drug B Drug B has a faster recovery time than Drug A Drug A is more soluble in blood than Drug B Drug A has a faster onset of action than Drug B Drug B is less potent than Drug A 3 Typically Low Solubility Low Blood Gas coefficient correlates with Fast Onset . Drug A 0.42 is less soluble than Drug B 2.30 so Drug A will have a faster onset . What does a LOW Blood Gas partition coefficient indicate about induction speed? Fast Induction and Fast Recovery . Pharmacokinetics What is the measure of Potency for inhaled anesthetics? MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration . Low MAC = High Potency. Pharmacokinetics Which induction agent causes Adrenocortical Suppression? Etomidate. Pharmacology Which induction agent is Dissociative and increases Cardiac Output? Ketamine. Pharmacology What terminates the action of a single bolus of Propofol? Redistribution not metabolism . Pharmacokinetics What is the trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia? Halogenated Anesthetics + Succinylcholine. Pathology What is the antidote for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene. Pharmacology Why is Nitrous Oxide contraindicated in Pneumothorax? It expands air-filled spaces. Safety Which volatile anesthetic is associated with Nephrotoxicity Compound A ? Sevoflurane. Pharmacology Which IV anesthetic is known as an Anti-emetic? Propofol. Pharmacology Physeo Pharmacology > Neuro > General Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Solubility and Speed Think of Solubility like a sink . If the blood sink is huge high solubility it takes forever to fill up rise in partial pressure before it spills over into the brain. Low solubility = tiny sink = fills fast = fast anesthesia. Ketamine & Asthma Ketamine is a bronchodilator. It s an excellent choice for induction in an asthmatic patient who is actively wheezing. Propofol Burn Propofol hurts on injection vascular irritation . Lidocaine is often mixed with it to blunt the pain. General Anesthetics n n Inhaled n- Pharmacokinetics n - Low Solubility -> Fast Onset Desflurane N2O n - High Solubility -> Slow Onset Halothane Isoflurane n - Increased CO -> Slower Onset n- Agents n - N2O Weak Fast Diffusion Hypoxia. n - Halogenated Potent MH risk Hypotension. n - Sevoflurane Bronchodilation. n - Desflurane Pungent Tachycardia. n n Intravenous n- Propofol Anti-emetic Hypotension Standard induction. n- Etomidate Cardiac Stability Adrenal suppression. n- Ketamine Sympathomimetic High BP Dissociative Bronchodilator. n- Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 agonist Sedation w/o Resp Depression . n- Thiopental Barbiturate High ICP control ."}, {"id": "l65", "title": "Lecture #65: Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l65 Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 65 prev l64 next l66 pdf pdfs/Lecture 65. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L65 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L65 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2 n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 65 n n--- n n Effect of Inhaled Anesthetics on Organ Systems n n Cardiovascular Effects nMost inhaled anesthetics cause a dose-dependent decrease in blood pressure but the mechanisms differ. n- Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Primarily decrease blood pressure by decreasing systemic vascular resistance vasodilation while preserving cardiac output. n - Note This preservation of cardiac output makes them preferable for patients with impaired myocardial function compared to halothane. n- Halothane & Enflurane Decrease blood pressure primarily by depressing myocardial contractility leading to decreased cardiac output. n- Nitrous Oxide N2O Has negligible cardiovascular effects overall because its mild sympathetic stimulation offsets any direct myocardial depression. n- Baroreflex Generally depressed by volatile anesthetics. n - Desflurane Exception Causes transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia hypertension when the concentration is increased rapidly. n - correlate n Coronary Artery Disease CAD n Limit the use of Desflurane or increase concentration slowly in patients with CAD as the resulting tachycardia and hypertension can increase myocardial oxygen demand and induce ischemia or infarction . n n n Respiratory Effects nVolatile anesthetics are respiratory depressants. n- Ventilation Decrease tidal volume and increase respiratory rate rapid shallow breathing . The overall effect is a decrease in minute ventilation. n- CO2 Response They blunt the ventilatory response to hypercapnia increased PaCO2 . n- Airway Irritation Desflurane and Isoflurane are pungent and can cause coughing breath-holding or laryngospasm poor for mask induction . n- Bronchodilation All volatiles cause bronchodilation. Sevoflurane is non-pungent and a potent bronchodilator making it the drug of choice for inhalational induction especially in patients with reactive airways e.g. asthma . n n Cerebral Effects n- Uncoupling Volatile anesthetics increase cerebral blood flow CBF vasodilation and intracranial pressure ICP while simultaneously decreasing cerebral metabolic rate of oxygen CMRO2 . n - Note Nitrous oxide increases both CBF and CMRO2. n - Note Sevoflurane increases ICP less than others. n- correlate n Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP n Inhaled anesthetics should be used with caution in patients with space-occupying lesions tumors or head trauma as the increase in CBF can dangerously elevate intracranial pressure . Hyperventilation to lower PaCO2 can help counteract this vasodilation. n n n Neuromuscular & Renal/Hepatic n- Muscle Volatiles relax skeletal muscle and potentiate the effects of neuromuscular blockers. N2O does not . n- Kidney/Liver Decrease renal and hepatic blood flow dose-dependent . n- Uterus Volatiles relax uterine smooth muscle can lead to uterine atony/hemorrhage . N2O does not. n n--- n n Toxicities of Inhaled Anesthetics n n Malignant Hyperthermia MH nA life-threatening hypermetabolic state triggered by all halogenated volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine in genetically susceptible individuals Ryanodine receptor mutations . n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled calcium release causing sustained muscle contraction. n- Signs Hypercapnia early sign tachycardia muscle rigidity hyperthermia late sign metabolic acidosis hyperkalemia. n- Treatment Stop agent hyperventilate give Dantrolene . n- Important Nitrous Oxide does NOT trigger MH. n n Hepatotoxicity n- Halothane Hepatitis A specific immune-mediated necrosis caused by the formation of trifluoroacetyl chloride from CYP2E1 metabolism which haptenates liver proteins. n - Risk factors Female obesity prior exposure. n - Note Desflurane Isoflurane and Sevoflurane have minimal metabolism and very low risk. n n Nephrotoxicity n- Compound A Sevoflurane interacts with soda lime CO2 absorbers to form Compound A which is nephrotoxic in rats. No proven renal injury in humans but valid theoretical concern use adequate fresh gas flow . n n Carbon Monoxide CO Production n Desflurane most > Isoflurane > Sevoflurane can produce CO when passed through desiccated dry CO2 absorbers . n- Prevent by replacing absorbents regularly. n n Nitrous Oxide N2O Specific Toxicities n1. Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces N2O is 35x more soluble than N2. It diffuses into air cavities faster than N2 leaves increasing pressure/volume. n - correlate n N2O Contraindications n Avoid N2O in patients with pneumothorax bowel obstruction air embolism or during middle ear surgery as expansion of these spaces can cause catastrophic rupture or pressure damage. n n2. Diffusion Hypoxia Upon discontinuation N2O rushes from blood into alveoli diluting O2. Reversed by giving 100% O2 for several minutes after stopping N2O. n3. Vitamin B12 Inhibition N2O oxidizes the cobalt in B12 inhibiting methionine synthase . n - correlate n Megaloblastic Anemia & Neuropathy n Prolonged exposure or use in patients with preexisting deficiency can lead to megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord peripheral neuropathy . Avoid in patients with B12 deficiency/malnutrition. n n4. Second Gas Effect High uptake of N2O concentrates the remaining gases in the alveoli speeding up induction of co-administered agents. n n--- n n Intravenous Anesthetics n n Propofol nThe most common induction agent. n- Mechanism Potentiates chloride current through the GABA-A receptor. n- Effects Rapid induction and recovery clear headed . Antiemetic Anti-pruritic Bronchodilation . Decreases CBF ICP and CMRO2. n- Adverse Effects n - Pain on injection mitigate with lidocaine . n - Hypotension Causes profound vasodilation and myocardial depression. n - Respiratory Depression Significant apnea. n- correlate n Propofol Infusion Syndrome PRIS n Rare but fatal reaction to high-dose/long-term infusion. Signs include metabolic acidosis rhabdomyolysis hyperkalemia hepatomegaly and cardiac failure. n n- Formulation Egg lecithin/soybean oil emulsion risk of bacterial growth careful in egg/soy allergy—though most reactions are to egg white protein not lecithin . n n Etomidate n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist. n- Key Feature Hemodynamic Stability . Minimal change in HR BP or CO. n- Indication Induction agent of choice for patients with cardiovascular instability or shock . n- Adverse Effects n - Adrenal Suppression Inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. Avoid in septic shock patients who need their adrenal reserve. n - Myoclonus Involuntary muscle movements. n - PONV High incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting. n n Ketamine n- Mechanism NMDA receptor antagonist blocks glutamate . n- Effects Dissociative anesthesia catatonia amnesia analgesia . n - Cardiovascular Causes Sympathetic Stimulation increase HR BP CO via catecholamine release. Good for hypovolemic shock. n - Respiratory Maintains airway reflexes and respiratory drive. Potent bronchodilator good for severe asthma induction . n - Cerebral Increases CMRO2 CBF and ICP. n- Adverse Effects n - Emergence Delirium Hallucinations vivid dreams disorientation. Prevent with benzodiazepines. n - correlate n Contraindications for Ketamine n Avoid in patients with severe hypertension angina or active ischemia due to increased myocardial oxygen demand. Also historically avoided in head trauma due to ICP though this is debated. n n n Dexmedetomidine Precedex n- Mechanism Highly selective Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist central action . n- Effects Sedation mimics natural sleep easily rousable Anxiolysis Analgesia . NO respiratory depression. n- Adverse Effects Bradycardia and Hypotension . n n Benzodiazepines Midazolam n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist. n- Use Preoperative anxiolysis amnesia anterograde . n- Reversal Flumazenil . n n--- n n Adjuncts n- Opioids Fentanyl etc. Provide analgesia. Reversal Naloxone . n- Lidocaine IV bolus blunts airway reflexes coughing during intubation. n n highyield n Quick Drug Selection Guide n Asthma/Bronchospasm Sevoflurane inhaled Ketamine IV Propofol IV . n Hemodynamic Instability/Shock Etomidate IV Ketamine IV . n Head Injury High ICP Propofol Barbiturates Etomidate . Avoid Ketamine Volatiles unless hyperventilated . n Reactive Airway/Smoking Sevoflurane. Avoid Desflurane. n History of PONV Propofol TIVA . Avoid Etomidate N2O Opioids. n Bowel Obstruction/Pneumothorax Avoid Nitrous Oxide. n CAD/Ischemia Avoid Ketamine Desflurane tachycardia . n n questions 1. A 55-year-old male with a history of severe coronary artery disease presents for coronary artery bypass grafting. During induction the anesthesiologist chooses an agent known for maintaining hemodynamic stability but causing adrenal suppression. Which drug was likely used? Propofol Ketamine Etomidate Midazolam Sevoflurane 2 Etomidate is known for its excellent hemodynamic stability minimal effect on HR/BP but has the unique side effect of inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase leading to adrenal suppression. 2. A 24-year-old male undergoes surgery for a bowel obstruction. Upon induction with a gas anesthetic the bowel rapidly distends complicating the surgery. Which agent is the most likely cause? Sevoflurane Isoflurane Desflurane Nitrous Oxide Propofol 3 Nitrous Oxide N2O is much more soluble than nitrogen. It diffuses into air-filled spaces like an obstructed bowel faster than nitrogen can leave causing rapid expansion of the space. It is contraindicated in bowel obstruction pneumothorax and middle ear surgery. 3. A child undergoing a brief procedure with sevoflurane anesthesia develops rapid breathing tachycardia muscle rigidity and a rapidly rising body temperature. What is the mechanism of the antidote for this condition? Blockade of NMDA receptors Inhibition of calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum Activation of GABA-A receptors Competitive antagonism of acetylcholine receptors Agonism of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors 1 The presentation is Malignant Hyperthermia . The antidote is Dantrolene which works by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via ryanodine receptors stopping the uncontrolled contraction. 4. A trauma patient with significant blood loss and hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent would best support this patient s blood pressure via sympathetic stimulation? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Midazolam 2 Ketamine stimulates the sympathetic nervous system increasing HR and BP making it an excellent induction agent for patients in hypovolemic shock who need hemodynamic support. Propofol causes hypotension. 5. A patient with a known deficiency of Vitamin B12 undergoes a lengthy procedure using a specific inhaled anesthetic. Postoperatively they develop signs of megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy. This effect is due to inhibition of which enzyme? 11-beta-hydroxylase Methionine synthase Ala-synthase Monoamine oxidase Pseudocholinesterase 1 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom of Vitamin B12 which inhibits Methionine Synthase . This can lead to megaloblastic anemia and neurologic sequelae subacute combined degeneration especially in B12-deficient patients. Which IV anesthetic is known for its hemodynamic stability and minimal respiratory depression? Etomidate Clinical What is the primary mechanism of action of Ketamine? NMDA receptor antagonist Pharmacology Which inhaled anesthetic should be avoided in patients with pneumothorax or bowel obstruction? Nitrous Oxide N2O Contraindications What drug is the specific treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene Antidote Which IV anesthetic causes adrenal suppression by inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase? Etomidate Adverse Effects What is a unique adverse effect of Ketamine during recovery? Emergence delirium hallucinations vivid dreams Adverse Effects Which inhaled anesthetic causes the most airway irritation and coughing? Desflurane Isoflurane is also pungent Adverse Effects Which IV anesthetic has antiemetic properties? Propofol Clinical What potentially nephrotoxic substance is formed when Sevoflurane degrades in soda lime? Compound A Toxicology Which induction agent increases intracranial pressure and cerebral blood flow? Ketamine Physiology BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Desflurane & CAD Desflurane can cause transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia/HTN making it risky for patients with CAD. Sevoflurane Induction Sevoflurane is the best agent for inhalational induction due to its lack of pungency and potent bronchodilation. N2O in Pneumothorax Nitrous Oxide is contraindicated in pneumothorax and bowel obstruction because it diffuses into air-filled spaces 35x faster than nitrogen leaves causing expansion. Etomidate & Shock Etomidate is the drug of choice for induction in hemodynamically unstable patients but causes adrenal suppression. Ketamine & ICP Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic that increases sympathetic tone good for shock but increases ICP bad for head trauma . Propofol & PONV Propofol has antiemetic properties making it ideal for TIVA to reduce PONV. MH Trigger Malignant Hyperthermia is triggered by halogenated volatiles and succinylcholine; treat with Dantrolene. n- Inhaled Anesthetics n - Mechanism Unknown globally but likely GABA potentiators / NMDA inhibitors. n - PK MAC potency Solubility speed . n - Organ Effects n - CV Vasodilation Iso/Des/Sevo Myocardial Depression Halothane . n - Resp Depression Bronchodilation Sevo ++ . n - Brain Increased CBF/ICP Decreased CMRO2. n - Toxicities n - Malignant Hyperthermia All volatiles . n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune . n - Nephrotoxicity Sevo -> Cmpd A . n - N2O Specifics Expansion of air spaces B12 inhibition Diffusion Hypoxia. n- Intravenous Anesthetics n - Propofol GABA Standard induction. Hypotension/Apnea. Antiemetic. n - Etomidate GABA CV Stability. Adrenal suppression. Myoclonus. n - Ketamine NMDA Dissociative. Sympathetic boost Shock . Hallucinations. n - Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 Sedation without resp depression. n"}, {"id": "l66", "title": "Lecture #66: Pharmacology: Local Anesthetics", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l66 Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics lecturer L. Goldstein Neuropharmacology session Lecture 66 prev l65 next l67 pdf pdfs/Lecture 66. L. Goldstein.pdf highYieldPdf content/L66 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L66 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein n Session Lecture 66 n n--- n n Introduction & Mechanism of Action n nLocal anesthetics LAs are diverse agents used to block sensation in a specific area of the body without loss of consciousness. Their primary utility lies in preventing pain transmission during surgical or dental procedures. To understand how they work we must first appreciate the physiology of the voltage-gated sodium channel. Nerves transmit signals via action potentials which are propagated by the rapid influx of sodium ions through these channels. n nLocal anesthetics function by diffusing through the nerve membrane into the axoplasm where they bind to the intracellular portion of voltage-gated sodium channels. Crucially they bind preferentially to channels in the open and inactivated states—a phenomenon known as state-dependent block . By stabilizing the inactivated state they prevent the channel from resetting to the resting state thereby halting the propagation of action potentials. This blockade is also use-dependent meaning nerves that are firing rapidly such as pain fibers are blocked more effectively than resting nerves. n n highyield n Mechanism of Action n- Target Intracellular portion of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels . n- Binding State High affinity for Open and Inactivated states State-Dependent . n- Effect Stabilizes the inactivated state $ rightarrow$ prevents Na+ influx $ rightarrow$ blocks action potential propagation. n- Use-Dependence Higher firing frequency = Stronger block Pain fibers > Motor fibers . n n n Susceptibility to Blockade nNot all nerve fibers are blocked equally. The sensitivity of a nerve fiber to LA blockade is determined by its diameter myelination and firing frequency. In general smaller diameter and higher firing frequency increase sensitivity. n n- Order of Blockade n 1. Small myelinated Sympathetic fibers - Blocked First n 2. Small unmyelinated Pain/Temp - C fibers & Small myelinated Pain/Temp - A$ delta$ fibers n 3. Large myelinated Touch Pressure Motor - Blocked Last n n mnemonic n Fiber Blockade Order n Silly Puns Make Me Laugh n- S iopathetic Sympathetic n- P ain Pain/Temp n- T ouch Touch/Pressure n- M otor Motor n n n Structure & Pharmacokinetics n nLocal anesthetics consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring connected to a hydrophilic amine by an intermediate chain. This intermediate chain defines the two major classes Esters and Amides . This distinction is critical for determining metabolism and allergic potential. n n Esters vs. Amides n- Esters Metabolized by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA para-aminobenzoic acid . PABA is a common allergen making hypersensitivity reactions more common in this class. n- Amides Metabolized in the liver by CYP450 enzymes. Hypersensitivity is extremely rare. n n mnemonic n Amides have Two I s n- Esters One i Procaine Cocaine Tetracaine Benzocaine n- Amides Two i s L i doca i ne Mep i vaca i ne Bup i vaca i ne Rop i vaca i ne Pr i loca i ne n n n pH and pKa The Onset of Action nLocal anesthetics are weak bases pKa 7.5–9.0 . They exist in equilibrium between a non-ionized lipid-soluble form and an ionized water-soluble charged form. n- Non-Ionized Form Crosses the nerve membrane Lipid soluble . n- Ionized Form Binds to the receptor inside the channel Active form . n n The Infection Concept nInfected tissue is acidic low pH . In an acidic environment the equilibrium shifts towards the ionized form $LA + H^+ rightarrow LAH^+$ . Since the ionized form cannot cross the membrane the drug cannot reach its target site. Therefore local anesthetics are less effective in infected/acidic tissue. n n Potency Duration and Absorption n- Potency Defined by Lipid Solubility . Highly lipophilic drugs e.g. Bupivacaine penetrate membranes easily $ rightarrow$ High Potency. n- Duration Defined by Protein Binding and Vascularity . n - High Protein Binding = Long Duration Drug stays at receptor . n - High Vascularity = Short Duration Drug washed away . n- Vasoconstrictors Epinephrine is often added to LAs to cause vasoconstriction. This decreases systemic absorption reduces toxicity and prolongs the duration of action by keeping the drug at the site. n n correlate n Vasoconstrictor Contraindication n Epinephrine should NOT be used in tissues with end-arterial supply Fingers Toes Penis Nose due to risk of ischemia and necrosis. n Obstetrics Epinephrine is avoided in labor epidurals as vasoconstriction can compromise uteroplacental blood flow and cause maternal hypertension. n n n Clinical Pharmacology of Agents n n Ester Local Anesthetics n1. Procaine Prototype short duration. Metabolized to PABA. Rarely used now. n2. Chloroprocaine Rapid onset short duration. Used in epidurals for C-sections. n3. Tetracaine Long duration high potency. Used for spinal anesthesia and ophthalmology. Note CSF has little pseudocholinesterase prolonged action Slide 36 . n4. Benzocaine Topical only low pKa very lipophilic . Used for mucous membranes e.g. teething endoscopy . n5. Cocaine Only LA with intrinsic vasoconstrictive properties blocks NE reuptake . Used Topically for ENT procedures nose/throat to shrink mucosa and reduce bleeding. n n correlate n Methemoglobinemia Benzocaine n Benzocaine spray/gel can cause oxidation of iron in hemoglobin $Fe^ 2+ rightarrow Fe^ 3+ $ leading to Methemoglobinemia . n- Presentation Chocolate brown blood cyanosis shortness of breath. n- Treatment Methylene Blue. n n n Amide Local Anesthetics n1. Lidocaine The Standard . Rapid onset medium duration. Broad utility local spinal epidural IV antiarrhythmic . n2. Mepivacaine Similar to Lidocaine but less vasodilation. Used in dentistry. n3. Bupivacaine High potency long duration. Preferred for epidurals post-op pain. High Cardiotoxicity. n4. Ropivacaine Similar to Bupivacaine but less cardiotoxic and less motor block good for labor— walking epidural . n5. Prilocaine Medium duration. Risk of methemoglobinemia. n n Toxicity Systemic & Neural n nLocal Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST occurs when drug levels rise excessively in the blood usually due to accidental IV injection or excessive dose. The toxicity targets the CNS and Cardiovascular systems. n n Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST n- Phase 1 CNS Excitation Perioral numbness metallic taste tinnitus agitation seizures. n- Phase 2 CNS Depression Coma respiratory arrest. n- Phase 3 CV Collapse Hypotension arrhythmias cardiac arrest. n n highyield n LAST progression nNumbness/Tinnitus $ rightarrow$ Seizures $ rightarrow$ Coma $ rightarrow$ Cardiac Arrest. n Key Concept CNS signs usually precede CV signs Warning sign! EXCEPT with Bupivacaine where they can happen simultaneously. n n n correlate n Bupivacaine Cardiotoxicity n Bupivacaine has high affinity for cardiac Na+ channels and dissociates slowly Fast In Slow Out . n- Risk Severe refractory arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse. n- Avoid Do not use for IV regional anesthesia Bier Block . n- Rescue Intralipid Emulsion Lipid Rescue acts as a lipid sink to soak up the lipophilic drug. n n n Specific Neural Toxicities n1. Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal . n - Presentation Pain/dysesthesia in buttocks/legs resolving in days. n - Prognosis Full recovery no permanent damage. n2. Cauda Equina Syndrome n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal Continuous Microcatheters - historical . n - Presentation Bowel/bladder dysfunction saddle anesthesia paralysis. n - Prognosis Potential for permanent damage. n n Hematologic Toxicity n- Methemoglobinemia Caused by Prilocaine metabolite o-toluidine and Benzocaine . n n Allergies n- Esters High risk due to PABA . Cross-reactivity exists among esters. n- Amides Very rare. No cross-reactivity with esters. If allergic to one amide may try another preservative-free . n questions 1. A 25-year-old woman in labor requests an epidural. The anesthesiologist selects an agent that provides adequate sensory analgesia but minimizes motor blockade to allow her to participate in labor. Which agent was most likely chosen? Tetracaine Ropivacaine Procaine Cocaine Benzocaine 1 Ropivacaine is a long-acting amide local anesthetic similar to bupivacaine but with less motor blockade at analgesic doses. 2. A patient undergoing a dental procedure receives a potentially toxic dose of a local anesthetic. Which early sign would most likely alert the dentist to impending systemic toxicity? Cardiovascular collapse Respiratory depression Perioral numbness and metallic taste Profound hypotension Bradycardia 2 CNS toxicity typically precedes CV toxicity. Early symptoms include circumoral numbness tinnitus and metallic taste. 3. Which local anesthetic is an ester that is metabolized to a product known to cause hypersensitivity reactions? Lidocaine Bupivacaine Mepivacaine Tetracaine Ropivacaine 3 Tetracaine is an ester one i . Esters are metabolized to PABA which is immunogenic. 4. A child is brought to the ER with cyanosis and shortness of breath after his parents applied a generous amount of a teething gel. Blood appears chocolate-brown. Which agent is implicated? Lidocaine Benzocaine Bupivacaine Ropivacaine Procaine 1 Benzocaine can cause Methemoglobinemia oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+ presenting with cyanosis and chocolate-brown blood. 5. Why is epinephrine often co-administered with lidocaine for infiltration anesthesia? To increase the pH of the solution To preventing oxidation of lidocaine To reduce the pain of injection To prolong the duration of action via vasoconstriction To block potassium channels 3 Epinephrine is a vasoconstrictor; it keeps the drug at the site longer prolonging duration and reducing systemic absorption. Which class of local anesthetics has a higher risk of allergic reactions? Esters due to PABA metabolite Concept What is the mechanism of action of local anesthetics? Block intracellular voltage-gated Na+ channels Open/Inactivated states Concept Which nerve fibers are blocked first? Small myelinated fibers Sympathetic Concept What is the treatment for Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST ? IV Lipid Emulsion Intralipid Clinical Which local anesthetic causes methemoglobinemia? Prilocaine and Benzocaine Clinical Which local anesthetic is the most cardiotoxic? Bupivacaine Clinical Why is epinephrine added to local anesthetics? To cause vasoconstriction prolong duration and decrease systemic absorption Concept What is the primary metabolism of Amide anesthetics? Hepatic CYP450 Concept What is the primary metabolism of Ester anesthetics? Plasma pseudocholinesterase Concept Why are local anesthetics less effective in infected tissue? Acidic pH causes ionization; ionized drug cannot cross membrane Concept What is a specific side effect of spinal Lidocaine? Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS Clinical Which ion channel state do LAs bind to most strongly? Inactivated state and Open Concept BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Local Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics > Local Anesthetics SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Local Anesthetics Pathoma Systemic > Pharmacology Esters vs Amides Rule If the drug name has two i s it s an Amide Lidocaine Bupivacaine . If it has one i it s an Ester Procaine Cocaine . Infection and pH Local anesthetics are ineffective in infected tissue because the acidic environment ionizes the drug preventing it from crossing the nerve membrane. Bupivacaine Toxicity Bupivacaine is severely cardiotoxic. Resuscitation is difficult; use Lipid Rescue Intralipid . Order of Block Small myelinated > Small unmyelinated > Large myelinated > Large unmyelinated. Pain is lost before Touch which is lost before Motor. Cocaine s Unique Feature Cocaine is the only LA that causes vasoconstriction blocks NE reuptake . All others are vasodilators often require Epinephrine . n- Mechanism n - Block Na+ channels Intracellular . n - Prefer Open/Inactivated states. n - Use-Dependent High frequency = more block . n- Structure n - Esters 1 i PABA metabolite Allergy . Pseudocholinesterase. n - Amides 2 i s Hepatic metabolism. n- Pharmacokinetics n - pH Acidic tissue infection = Ionized drug = No effect. n - Potency = Lipid Solubility. n - Duration = Protein Binding + Vasoconstrictors Epi . n- Toxicity LAST n - CNS Numbness -> Seizures. n - CV Hypotension -> Arrest. n - Bupivacaine Cardiotoxic Lipid Rescue . n - Benzocaine/Prilocaine Methemoglobinemia. n"}, {"id": "l67", "title": "Lecture #67: Opioids", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l67 Lecture 67 Opioids lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 67 prev l66 next l68 pdf pdfs/Lecture 67. M. Pino Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L67 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L67 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 67 Pharmacology of Opioids n n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. n Session Lecture 67 n n Mechanism and Receptors n nOpioids bind to G-protein coupled receptors Gi/o to inhibit neuronal transmission. n1. Presynaptic Close voltage-gated Ca2+ channels -> Decreased neurotransmitter release Glutamate Substance P . n2. Postsynaptic Open K+ channels -> K+ efflux -> Hyperpolarization . n n Receptor Subtypes n- Mu u00b5 Analgesia Respiratory Depression Constipation Euphoria Sedation Miosis. Most clinically used opioids target this . n- Kappa u03ba Analgesia Sedation Dysphoria/Hallucinations Psychotomimetic effects . n- Delta u03b4 Analgesia. n n Classification and Key Drugs n n 1. Strong Agonists Full Mu Agonists n- Morphine The prototype. n - Metabolism Glucuronidated to Morphine-6-Glucuronide M6G Active/Potent and M3G Neuroexcitatory . M6G accumulates in renal failure -> Toxicity. n - Histamine Release Causes pruritus hypotension bronchoconstriction Pretreat with antihistamines . n- Fentanyl Highly lipophilic 100x more potent than morphine. Used in anesthesia and chronic pain Patch . No histamine release Cardiac stable . n- Meperidine n - Antimuscarinic Causes Mydriasis dilated pupils and Tachycardia unlike other opioids . n - Metabolite Normeperidine accumulates esp. in renal failure -> Seizures . n - Interaction Serotonin Syndrome if mixed with MAOIs/SSRIs blocks 5-HT reuptake . n- Methadone n - Long t1/2 Used for maintenance/withdrawal prevents cravings without high . n - Pharmacology Mu agonist + NMDA Antagonist + 5-HT/NE reuptake inhibitor. n - Toxicity QT Prolongation Torsades . n n 2. Weak/Moderate Agonists n- Codeine Prodrug. Converted to Morphine by CYP2D6 . n - Pharmacogenetics n - Poor Metabolizers No pain relief. n - Ultrarapid Metabolizers Rapid conversion -> Respiratory Depression Danger in children/breastfeeding . n- Tramadol Weak Mu agonist + Inhibits 5-HT/NE reuptake SNRI-like . n - Toxicity Seizures lowers threshold Serotonin Syndrome . n- Loperamide / Diphenoxylate Antidiarrheals. Low abuse potential Loperamide doesn t cross BBB; Diphenoxylate mixed with Atropine . n n 3. Mixed Agonist-Antagonists n- Buprenorphine Partial Mu Agonist / Kappa Antagonist. High affinity. n - Use Opioid Use Disorder Suboxone = Buprenorphine + Naloxone . Precipitates withdrawal if full agonist is present. n- Pentazocine Nalbuphine Butorphanol Kappa Agonist / Mu Antagonist or partial . n - Effect Ceiling effect on respiration. Dysphoria Kappa . n n 4. Antagonists n- Naloxone n - IV/Intranasal. Rapid onset. Short duration. n - Use Acute Overdose . May need repeat dosing. n- Naltrexone Oral. Long duration. Used for Relapse Prevention Opioid and Alcohol . n- Methylnaltrexone Peripheral Mu antagonist Does not cross BBB . n - Use Opioid-Induced Constipation . n n Adverse Effects & Toxicity n n Acute Toxicity Overdose Triad n1. Coma n2. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils n3. Respiratory Depression Cause of death n n Tolerance n- Develops to Analgesia Euphoria Sedation Respiratory Depression. n- NO TOLERANCE to Constipation and Miosis . Patients on chronic opioids always need a bowel regimen . n n Withdrawal n- Not life-threatening unlike Alcohol/Benzo withdrawal but miserable. n- Symptoms Autonomic Hyperactivity -> Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning Piloerection Cold Turkey Dilated Pupils Diarrhea Nausea/Vomiting. n- Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome NAS High-pitched cry seizures diarrhea. Treat with Morphine/Methadone. n n mnemonic n Opioids LOCK everything down n- Miosis Pinpoint pupils n- Constipation Locked bowels n- Respiratory Depression n n Withdrawal UNLOCKS the floodgates n- Mydriasis Dilated pupils n- Diarrhea n- Rhinorrhea / Lacrimation Runny nose/eyes n n questions 1. A patient with end-stage renal disease requires severe pain management. Which opioid metabolite is most likely to accumulate and cause neurotoxicity seizures in this patient? Morphine-6-Glucuronide Normeperidine 6-Monoacetylmorphine Codeine-6-Glucuronide Noroxycodone 1 While Morphine-6-Glucuronide accumulates Normeperidine metabolite of Meperidine is famously associated with causing seizures in patients with renal failure. Morphine-6-Glucuronide causes respiratory depression/sedation. The question asks for neurotoxicity/seizures making Normeperidine the classic answer Meperidine is contraindicated in renal failure . 2. A breastfeeding mother is prescribed Codeine for pain. Her infant becomes lethargic and develops respiratory depression. Genetic testing would likely reveal the mother has which phenotype? CYP2D6 Poor Metabolizer CYP2D6 Ultrarapid Metabolizer CYP3A4 Poor Metabolizer CYP2C19 Extensive Metabolizer P-glycoprotein deficiency 1 Codeine is a prodrug converted to Morphine by CYP2D6 . An Ultrarapid Metabolizer converts codeine to levels of morphine much higher than expected which is then passed in breast milk causing toxicity in the infant. 3. Which opioid lacks the characteristic miosis pinpoint pupils in overdose and may instead cause mydriasis and tachycardia? Morphine Fentanyl Methadone Meperidine Heroin 3 Meperidine has antimuscarinic atropine-like effects. Therefore it can cause mydriasis dilated pupils and tachycardia distinguishing its toxidrome from other opioids. 4. A patient on chronic opioid therapy for cancer pain complains of severe constipation. Which agent treats this specifically without reversing analgesia? Naloxone Naltrexone Methylnaltrexone Buprenorphine Methadone 2 Methylnaltrexone is a peripherally acting mu-opioid antagonist. It has a quaternary amine structure that prevents it from crossing the blood-brain barrier so it relieves constipation peripheral without affecting analgesia central . 5. A patient taking an MAO Inhibitor for depression is given an opioid for shivering and develops hyperthermia muscle rigidity and confusion Serotonin Syndrome . Which opioid was likely administered? Morphine Codeine Meperidine Hydromorphone Oxymorphone 2 Meperidine and Tramadol Dextromethorphan has weak serotonin reuptake inhibitory effects. When combined with MAOIs it can precipitate life-threatening Serotonin Syndrome . What is the classic triad of Opioid Overdose? 1. Coma n2. Respiratory Depression n3. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils Clinical Signs To which two opioid effects does tolerance NOT develop? 1. Constipation n2. Miosis Adverse Effects Which opioid metabolite causes seizures? Normeperidine metabolite of Meperidine . Toxicology Which opioid has antimuscarinic effects tachycardia mydriasis ? Meperidine. Pharmacology What is the mechanism of Methylnaltrexone? Peripheral Mu Antagonist Block GI receptors spares CNS analgesia . Mechanism Which enzyme converts Codeine to Morphine? CYP2D6. Metabolism What is the mechanism of Opioid receptors cellular ? Close Presynaptic Ca2+ channels; Open Postsynaptic K+ channels. Mechanism Which mixed agonist acts as a Kappa agonist and Mu antagonist? Pentazocine can cause dysphoria/hallucinations . Pharmacology Why is Methadone used for opioid withdrawal? Long half-life allows for tapering and suppression of withdrawal symptoms. Treatment What drug combination is used for maintenance to prevent misuse by injection? Buprenorphine + Naloxone Suboxone . Naloxone is inactive orally but active if injected. Treatment Physeo Pharmacology > Neuro > Opioids FirstAid Pharmacology > Opioids No Tolerance You never get used to the constipation or the pinpoint pupils. You can be on heroin for 20 years and you will still be constipated and have miosis. Gallbladder Attacks Opioids cause contraction of the Sphincter of Oddi . This can worsen biliary colic. Meperidine was traditionally said to have less of this effect but that s debated. Morphine is definitely a culprit. The Meperidine Exception Meperidine is the weird opioid. It breaks the rules 1. Causes Mydriasis not miosis . 2. Causes Tachycardia not bradycardia . 3. Causes Seizures Normeperidine . 4. Causes Serotonin Syndrome . Opioids n n Receptors Gi coupled n- Mu Analgesia Resp Depression Constipation Euphoria. n- Kappa Dysphoria Sedation. n n Full Agonists n- Morphine Histamine release M6G accumulation. n- Meperidine Seizures Serotonin Syndrome Mydriasis. n- Methadone Long acting QT prolong. n- Fentanyl High potency Lipid soluble. n n Partial/Weak n- Codeine CYP2D6 -> Morphine. n- Tramadol SNRI + Mu. Seizures. n n Antagonists n- Naloxone Acute overdose. n- Naltrexone Maintenance Anti-craving . n- Methylnaltrexone Constipation Peripheral . n n Toxicity n- Overdose Coma Miosis Resp Depression. n- Side Effects Constipation No tolerance ."}, {"id": "l68", "title": "Lecture #68: Psychopharmacology I (Drugs of Abuse)", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l68 Lecture 68 Psychopharmacology I Drugs of Abuse lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 68 prev l67 next l69 pdf pdfs/Lecture 68. G. Otazu Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L68 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L68 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 68 Psychopharmacology I Drugs of Abuse n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 68 n n The Reward Pathway n n Mesolimbic Dopamine Pathway n- Origin Ventral Tegmental Area VTA in the Midbrain. n- Target Nucleus Accumbens NAc in the Limbic System Basal Ganglia . n- Function Reward Prediction Error . release dopamine when a reward is unexpected or better than expected . n- Drugs of Abuse Hijack this system by causing a massive non-adapting release of Dopamine in the NAc. n n Stimulants n n 1. Cocaine n- Mechanism Blocks DAT Dopamine Transporter -> Inhibits reuptake of DA NE and 5-HT. n- Clinical Effects Euphoria Agitation Mydriasis Dilated pupils Hypertension Tachycardia. n- Toicity n - Coronary Vasospasm Causes MI / Chest pain. n - Nasal Septum Perforation Due to chronic vasoconstriction. n- Treatment of Cocaine Chest Pain n - Use Benzodiazepines and Alpha-blockers Phentolamine . n - NEVER use Beta-blockers alone Leads to unopposed alpha stimulation -> Worsens hypertension and vasoconstriction. n n 2. Amphetamines n- Mechanism n 1. Reverses DAT Pumps Dopamine out of the nerve terminal. n 2. Inhibits VMAT Displaces Dopamine from vesicles. n- Clinical Similar to cocaine but longer duration. n n 3. MDMA Ecstasy n- Mechanism Reverses SERT Serotonin Transporter -> Massive 5-HT release and DA/NE . n- Toxicity n - Hyperthermia Dance parties + dehydration . n - Hyponatremia Due to excessive water intake + SIADH . n - Serotonin Syndrome . n n 4. Nicotine n- Mechanism Agonist at Nicotinic ACh Receptors nAChR on VTA neurons -> Depolarization -> DA release. n n Depressants n n 1. Alcohol Ethanol n- Mechanism Enhances GABA-A currents Blocks NMDA Glutamate receptors. n- Kinetics Zero-order metabolism. n- Withdrawal Life-Threatening n - 6-12h Tremors Anxiety Insomnia. n - 12-48h Alcoholic Hallucinosis Visual stable vitals . n - 2-4 days Delirium Tremens DTs Agitation fever tachycardia hallucinations. High Mortality . n - Treatment Benzodiazepines Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . n n 2. Benzodiazepines & Barbiturates n- Target Allosteric site on GABA-A Receptor Cl- channel . n- Benzodiazepines Increase Frequency of opening. F renzodiazepines . n - Safety Harder to overdose alone unless mixed with alcohol . Treat OD with Flumazenil . n- Barbiturates Increase Duration of opening. BarbiDURAT es . n - Safety Low therapeutic index Linear dose-response . Can cause fatal respiratory depression directly. n n 3. Opioids n- Mechanism Mu agonists. Disinhibit VTA dopamine neurons by inhibiting GABAergic interneurons. n n Hallucinogens n n 1. Marijuana Cannabinoids n- Active Agent THC. n- Target CB1 Receptor Gi coupled . Inhibits GABA release -> Disinhibits Dopamine. n- Clinical Conjunctival injection Red eyes Increased appetite Munchies Dry mouth. n n 2. PCP Phencyclidine n- Mechanism NMDA Receptor Antagonist . n- Clinical Violence Agitation Nystagmus Vertical/Horizontal Analgesia. n- Street Name Angel Dust. n n 3. LSD n- Mechanism 5-HT2A Receptor Agonist. n questions 1. A 24-year-old male is brought to the ED with severe agitation chest pain and dilated pupils. His BP is 180/110. He admits to using cocaine. Which medication is CONTRAINDICATED? Lorazepam Nitroglycerin Phentolamine Metoprolol Verapamil 3 Beta-blockers Metoprolol are contraindicated in cocaine induced chest pain due to unopposed alpha vasoconstriction . Blocking beta-2 vasodilation leaves alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed potentially worsening hypertension and coronary spasm. 2. Which agent works by increasing the FREQUENCY of GABA-A chloride channel opening? Phenobarbital Diazepam Ethanol Baclofen Zolpidem 1 Benzodiazepines Diazepam increase the Frequency of channel opening. Barbiturates Phenobarbital increase the Duration . 3. A patient is brought in violent agitated and insensitive to pain. Physical exam reveals vertical and horizontal nystagmus. Which drug mechanism explains this presentation? NMDA Antagonist CB1 Agonist Mu Opioid Agonist Dopamine Reuptake Inhibitor 5-HT2A Agonist 0 This is classic PCP intoxication Violence Nystagmus Analgesia . PCP is an NMDA Receptor Antagonist . 4. 72 hours after his last drink a chronic alcoholic develops severe agitation global confusion fever 103 u00b0F and tachycardia. What is the diagnosis? Alcoholic Hallucinosis Delirium Tremens Wernicke s Encephalopathy Korsakoff s Psychosis Hepatic Encephalopathy 1 Delirium Tremens DTs is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal occurring 2-4 days after cessation. It is characterized by autonomic instability fever tachycardia and delirium. It has a high mortality rate. 5. MDMA Ecstasy uniquely reverses the transporter for which neurotransmitter leading to distinct risks of hyperthermia and hyponatremia? Dopamine Norepinephrine Serotonin Acetylcholine GABA 2 While MDMA affects all monoamines it has a high affinity for SERT Serotonin Transporter causing massive serotonin release. This contributes to the risk of Serotonin Syndrome hyperthermia and SIADH hyponatremia . What is the specific target of the reward pathway? Nucleus Accumbens NAc . Neuroanatomy Why are Beta-blockers contraindicated in Cocaine toxicity? Unopposed Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction. Clinical Pharmacology Mechanism of Amphetamines? 1. Reverse DAT Pump DA out . n2. Inhibit VMAT Displace DA from vesicles . Mechanism Mechanism of Benzodiazepines vs Barbiturates? Benzos Increase Frequency . nBarbs Increase Duration . Mechanism What is the drug of choice for Alcohol Withdrawal / DTs? Benzodiazepines e.g. Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . Treatment Drug associated with Vertical Nystagmus and Violence? PCP Phencyclidine . Clinical Signs Which receptor does Marijuana THC bind to? CB1 Receptor. Mechanism Mechanism of MDMA Ecstasy ? Reverses SERT -> Massive Serotonin release. Mechanism Severe complication of Alcohol Withdrawal 2-4 days ? Delirium Tremens DTs - Autonomic instability high mortality. Clinical Signs Mechanism of Cocaine? Blocks Dopamine Reuptake DAT inhibitor . Mechanism Physeo Psychiatry > Substance Use Disorder FirstAid Psychiatry > Substance Use Benzos vs Barbs Ben -zodiaze- pines increase Fre -quency Ben likes it Fre -quently . n Barbi -turates increase Dura -tion Barbi likes it to last a long Dura -tion . Cocaine and Beta Blockers Imagine a hose blood vessel . Alpha constricts it Beta-2 dilates it. Cocaine turns both on. If you block Beta dilate you are left with pure Alpha constrict . The hose clamps shut. Hypertension crisis. The Alcoholic Timeline 6-12h Shakes/Anxiety. n12-48h Bugs crawling Hallucinosis - but they know it s not real/vitals stable . n48-96h DELIRIUM TREMENS Confused Fever Tachycardia - They think it s real and they might die . Drugs of Abuse n n Stimulants n- Mechanism Increase Dopamine Reward Pathway VTA -> Nucleus Accumbens . n- Cocaine Blocks DAT. Chest Pain No Beta Blockers . n- Amphetamine Reverses DAT/VMAT. n- MDMA Reverses SERT. Hyperthermia Hyponatremia. n n Depressants n- Alcohol GABA agonist NMDA antag. DTs Fatal withdrawal . n- Benzos GABA-A Frequency. n- Barbs GABA-A Duration. n- Opioids Mu agonist. Disinhibit VTA. n n Hallucinogens n- PCP NMDA Antagonist. Violence Nystagmus. n- LSD 5-HT2A Agonist. n- Marijuana CB1 Agonist."}, {"id": "l69", "title": "Lecture #69: Substance Use Disorders", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l69 Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 69 prev l68 next l70 pdf pdfs/Lecture 69. A. Coladner D.O. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L69 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L69 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 69 Psychopharmacology II Substance Use Disorders n n Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. n Session Lecture 69 n n Substance Use Disorder SUD n Definition Recurrent use causes clinical impairment. n Severity Mild 2-3 criteria Moderate 4-5 Severe 6+ . n n The 11 Criteria Grouped n1. Impaired Control Taking more than intended inability to cut down time spent seeking/using cravings. n2. Social Impairment Failure to fulfill obligations work/school giving up activities interpersonal problems. n3. Risky Use Hazardous situations driving continued use despite physical/psychological problems. n4. Pharmacological Tolerance and Withdrawal . n Note Tolerance/Withdrawal do NOT count for diagnosis if taking medications as prescribed under medical supervision. n n Specific Substances Intoxication & Withdrawal n n 1. Alcohol n- Intoxication Slurred speech Incoordination Nystagmus Impaired judgment. n - Poisoning Respiratory depression Coma. n- Withdrawal n - Minor 6-12h Tremors Anxiety Insomnia. n - Severe 48-96h Delirium Tremens DTs -> Agitation Hallucinations Autonomic Instability Fever Tachycardia . Life-Threatening. n- Treatment n - Benzodiazepines Diazepam/Chlordiazepoxide for withdrawal. n - Thiamine before glucose to prevent Wernicke s Encephalopathy. n n 2. Opioids n- Intoxication Miosis Pinpoint pupils Respiratory Depression Coma Constipation. n- Withdrawal Flu-like illness. n - Rhinorrhea Runny nose Lacrimation Tearing . n - Yawning . n - Piloerection Gooseflesh -> Cold Turkey . n - Mydriasis Dilated pupils . n - Not life-threatening. n n 3. Stimulants Cocaine/Amphetamines n- Intoxication Mydriasis Dilated pupils Tachycardia Agitation Chest Pain . n- Withdrawal The Crash n - Dysphoria / Depression. n - Fatigue / Hypersomnia Vivid unpleasant dreams . n - Increased Appetite . n n 4. Cannabis n- Intoxication Conjunctival Injection Red eyes Increased Appetite Munchies Dry mouth Tachycardia. n- Withdrawal Irritability Anxiety Decreased appetite Insomnia. n n 5. Nicotine n- Withdrawal Irritability Anxiety Increased Appetite Weight gain Difficulty concentrating. n- Treatment n - Varenicline Chantix Partial Agonist at a4b2 nicotinic receptor. Reduces craving AND reduces reward from smoking. Most effective. n - Bupropion DA/NE reuptake inhibitor. n - NRT Patch/Gum. n n 6. Hallucinogens PCP n- Intoxication n - Violent behavior . n - Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal . n - Analgesia Superhuman strength feeling . n n 7. Sedative-Hypnotics Benzos/Barbs n- Withdrawal Similar to Alcohol . Can cause Seizures and Death . n questions 1. A patient presents with agitation violent behavior and decreased sensitivity to pain. Physical exam reveals vertical nystagmus. Which substance is most likely involved? Cocaine Heroin Phencyclidine PCP LSD Marijuana 2 PCP intoxication is characterized by aggression/violence medical/physical nystagmus vertical is specific and analgesia . 2. Which set of symptoms best characterizes Opioid Withdrawal? Seizures Tremors Tachycardia Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning Dilated Pupils Increased Appetite Hypersomnia Depression Red eyes Dry mouth Euphoria Nystagmus Ataxia Slurred Speech 1 Opioid withdrawal is a Flu-like illness Runny nose Rhinorrhea Tearing Lacrimation Yawning loose stools. Sympathetic rebound causes Dilated Pupils Mydriasis and Piloerection. 3. A heavy smoker wants to quit. Which medication is a partial agonist at the nicotinic receptor and is considered the most effective pharmacotherapy? Bupropion Nicotine Patch Varenicline Clonidine Nortriptyline 2 Varenicline is a partial agonist at the alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptor. It reduces cravings agonist effect while blocking the rewarding effects of nicotine if the patient smokes antagonist effect . 4. 48 hours after hospital admission for a surgery a patient develops confusion severe tremors diaphoresis hypertension 170/100 and tachycardia 120 bpm . He claims he sees spiders . What is the treatment of choice? Haloperidol Benzodiazepines Thiamine alone Propofol Carbamazepine 1 This patient is in Delirium Tremens DTs Alcohol Withdrawal Delirium . The first-line treatment is Benzodiazepines e.g. Diazepam Lorazepam to bind GABA-A receptors and calm the autonomic hyperactivity. 5. What is the characteristic sign of Cannabis intoxication? Miosis Nystagmus Conjunctival Injection Hypersalivation Bradycardia 2 Conjunctival Injection Red eyes is the hallmark sign of Cannabis use. Other signs include dry mouth cotton mouth tachycardia and increased appetite. What is the hallmark physical exam finding of PCP intoxication? Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal . Clinical Signs What are the classic signs of Opioid Withdrawal? Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning Piloerection Dilated Pupils. Clinical Signs Which withdrawal syndrome is life-threatening? Alcohol and Sedative-Hypnotic withdrawal Risk of Seizures/DTs . Clinical Signs What characterizes Stimulant Withdrawal? The Crash Depression Fatigue/Sleep Increased Appetite. Clinical Signs Mechanism of Varenicline? Partial Agonist at Nicotinic Receptors. Mechanism Sign of Cannabis Intoxication? Conjunctival Injection Red Eyes . Clinical Signs Pupil size in Opioid Intoxication vs Withdrawal? Intoxication Constricted Miosis . nWithdrawal Dilated Mydriasis . Clinical Signs Pupil size in Stimulant Intoxication? Dilated Mydriasis . Clinical Signs Treatment for Delirium Tremens? Benzodiazepines. Treatment Why is Thiamine given in alcoholics? To prevent Wernicke s Encephalopathy Ataxia Confusion Ophthalmoplegia . Treatment Physeo Psychiatry > Substance Use Disorder FirstAid Psychiatry > Substance Use Pupils Don t Lie Pinpoint = Opioid Overdose or Cholinergics . n Dilated = Stimulants Cocaine/Amphetamines OR Opioid Withdrawal OR Anticholinergics. Wet vs Dry Opioid Withdrawal makes you Wet Tears Runny nose Diarrhea Sweat . nAnticholinergic Toxicity makes you Dry Bone dry . The Yawn If a patient on the wards is complaining of 10/10 pain and they are yawning excessively... check for opioid withdrawal. Yawning is a very specific sign. Substance Use Disorders n n Alcohol/Depressants n- Intoxication Slurred speech Ataxia. n- Withdrawal Tremors -> Seizures -> DTs Fatal . n- Rx Benzos. n n Opioids n- Intoxication Miosis Resp Depression. n- Withdrawal Flu-like Mydriasis Yawning Rhino/Lacrinnorrhea. n n Stimulants n- Intoxication Mydriasis Tachycardia Chest Pain. n- Withdrawal Sleep Hunger Depression. n n Hallucinogens n- PCP Violence Nystagmus. n- Cannabis Red eyes Munchies."}, {"id": "l7", "title": "Lecture #7: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l7 Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Overview of Brainstem Divisions Slides 4-5 nThe brainstem is divided into three major regions Rostral to Caudal n1. Midbrain Mesencephalon The most superior portion. n2. Pons Metencephalon The middle bulbous portion. n3. Medulla Myelencephalon The most inferior portion continuous with the spinal cord. n n 2. External Anatomy Ventral View Slide 6 n Midbrain Characterized by the Cerebral Peduncles Crus Cerebri massive stalks containing descending motor fibers from the cortex. n Pons Characterized by the Basilar Pons a bulbous structure with transverse fibers. n Medulla n Pyramids Two medial columns containing the Corticospinal Tract . n Pyramidal Decussation The crossing point of motor fibers at the caudal medulla border with spinal cord . n Olive A lateral bump produced by the Inferior Olivary Nucleus motor coordination . n n 3. External Anatomy Dorsal View Slide 7-8 n Midbrain Tectum n Superior Colliculi Visual reflexes/saccades. n Inferior Colliculi Auditory pathway. n Pons n Cerebellar Peduncles n Superior SCP Main output from cerebellum. n Middle MCP Connects Pons to Cerebellum massive input . n Inferior ICP Connects Medulla to Cerebellum. n Floor of 4th Ventricle The open posterior aspect. n Medulla n Gracile Tubercle Medial bump Leg sensory . n Cuneate Tubercle Lateral bump Arm sensory . n Obex The caudal point where the 4th ventricle closes. n n 4. Internal Anatomy Cross-Sectional Zones Slide 10 nIn cross-section the brainstem is divided anterior-to-posterior n1. Basilar Region Ventral Contains descending motor tracts Corticospinal Corticobulbar . n2. Tegmentum Middle The core containing Cranial Nerve Nuclei Reticular Formation and sensory tracts. n3. Tectum Dorsal Found only in the Midbrain Colliculi . n n 5. Key Nuclei by Level Slides 15-26 n n Medulla n Gracile & Cuneate Nuclei Dorsal column nuclei Touch/Proprioception . n Inferior Olivary Nucleus Large crumpled sac appearance. Motor learning Climbing fibers to cerebellum . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain/Temp for the face extends down to C2 . n n Pons n Pontine Nuclei Scattered in the basilar pons. Relay cortex information to the cerebellum via the MCP. n Facial Nucleus CN VII Motor to face. n Abducens Nucleus CN VI Lateral eye movement. n n Midbrain n Substantia Nigra Dark pigmented line Dopamine . Degenerates in Parkinson s. n Red Nucleus Motor coordination Rubrospinal tract . n Periaqueductal Gray PAG Pain modulation Opioid receptors . n Superior & Inferior Colliculi. n n correlate n Cranial Nerve Exit Points Rule of 4s n Midbrain CN 3 4 Oculomotor interpeduncular fossa Trochlear dorsal exit! . n Pons CN 5 6 7 8 Trigeminal lateral pons Abducens/Facial/Vestibulocochlear pontomedullary junction . n Medulla CN 9 10 11 12 Glossopharyngeal/Vagus/Accessory retro-olivary Hypoglossal pre-olivary sulcus . n Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 7 prev l6 next l8 pdf pdfs/Lecture 7. I. Kurtzer Ph.D. PPT .pdf Brainstem Anatomy I n Midbrain n Ventral Cerebral Peduncles Motor n Dorsal Tectum Sup/Inf Colliculi n Internal Aqueduct Substantia Nigra Red Nucleus n CN III IV n Pons n Ventral Basilar Pons Transverse fibers n Dorsal 4th Ventricle Floor n Internal Pontine Nuclei MCP n CN V VI VII VIII n Medulla n Ventral Pyramids Olive n Dorsal Gracile/Cuneate Tubercles n Internal Inf. Olive DC Nuclei n CN IX X XI XII questions 1. Which structure is a hallmark feature of the dorsal midbrain? Pyramids Inferior Olive Superior Colliculus Fourth Ventricle Gracile Tubercle 2 The Superior Colliculus is part of the Tectum found on the dorsal aspect of the midbrain. The Pyramids and Olive are ventral medulla structures. 2. The pyramidal decussation marks the caudal boundary of which brainstem division? Midbrain Pons Medulla Diencephalon Spinal Cord 2 The Pyramidal Decussation is found at the junction of the Medulla and the spinal cord. 3. Which cranial nerve is the only one to exit from the dorsal aspect of the brainstem? CN III Oculomotor CN IV Trochlear CN V Trigeminal CN VI Abducens CN VII Facial 1 CN IV Trochlear exits dorsally from the midbrain tectum. All other cranial nerves exit ventrally or laterally. 4. Damage to the Substantia Nigra in the midbrain is most commonly associated with which disorder? Huntington s Disease Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 2 The Substantia Nigra contains dopaminergic neurons. Their degeneration leads to the motor symptoms of Parkinson s Disease . 5. Which ventricular space is located between the Pons/Medulla and the Cerebellum? Lateral Ventricle Third Ventricle Cerebral Aqueduct Fourth Ventricle Central Canal 3 The Fourth Ventricle is the open space bounded anteriorly by the Pons/Medulla and posteriorly by the Cerebellum. Three divisions of Brainstem Rostral→Caudal ? Midbrain → Pons → Medulla Anatomy Hallmark dorsal structure of Midbrain? Tectum Corpora Quadrigemina / Colliculi Anatomy Ventral columns of Medulla contain? Corticospinal Tract Pyramids Anatomy Only CN to exit dorsally? CN IV Trochlear Anatomy What marks the Medulla-Spinal Cord border? Pyramidal Decussation Anatomy Function of Inferior Colliculus? Auditory Processing Physiology Function of Superior Colliculus? Visual/Saccadic Eye Movements Physiology Function of Substantia Nigra? Dopamine production Motor Control Physiology Nucleus responsible for Olive bump? Inferior Olivary Nucleus Anatomy Ventricular space of the Midbrain? Cerebral Aqueduct Anatomy Tectum Location The Tectum Roof exists ONLY in the Midbrain . It consists of the Superior and Inferior Colliculi. Cerebral Aqueduct The Cerebral Aqueduct is a hallmark of the Midbrain . The 4th Ventricle is associated with the Pons and Medulla. Decussation Rule The Pyramidal Decussation crossing of motor fibers marks the transition from Medulla to Spinal Cord. CN IV Uniqueness The Trochlear Nerve CN IV is the ONLY cranial nerve to exit from the Dorsal aspect of the brainstem. Inferior Olive Function The Inferior Olive Medulla is a MAJOR source of input to the cerebellum Climbing Fibers for motor learning. Substantia Nigra Located in the Midbrain cerebral peduncles. Contains Dopaminergic neurons. Loss = Parkinson s Disease."}, {"id": "l70", "title": "Lecture #70: Pediatric H&P I", "module": "Pediatrics", "content": "l70 Lecture 70 Pediatric H&P I lecturer A. Patel D.O. Pediatrics session Lecture 70 prev l69 next l71 pdf pdfs/Lecture 70. A. Patel D.O. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L70 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L70 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 70 Pediatric History & Physical Exam I Newborn - 5 Years n n Lecturer A. Patel D.O. n Session Lecture 70 n n The Newborn Exam n n 1. Vital Signs & Growth n- APGAR Score At 1 and 5 minutes. Appearance Pulse >100=2 Grimace Activity Respiration . n - Score 7-10 Excellent. n- Weight n - Lose up to 10% in first few days. Regain birth weight by 2 weeks . n - Double birth weight by 4 months . n - Triple birth weight by 1 year . n- Head Circumference Anterior Fontanelle closes by 12-18 months . Posterior by 1-2 months. n n 2. HEENT n- Head Swellings n - Caput Succedaneum Crosses suture lines Cap wears like a hat . Resolves days. n - Cephalohematoma Does NOT cross suture lines Blood under periosteum . Resolves weeks. Risk of Jaundice. n - Subgaleal Hemorrhage Crosses sutures massive blood loss. Dangerous . n- Eyes n - Red Reflex White reflex Leukocoria = Cataracts or Retinoblastoma . n - Conjunctivitis n - Gonococcal Day 2-5. Purulent. Risk of blindness. Tx IM Ceftriaxone. Prevention Erythromycin ointment. n - Chlamydial Day 5-14. Watery/Mucopurulent. Tx Oral Macrolide Risk of Pyloric Stenosis . n- Ears Low set = Down Syndrome. n- Mouth Epstein Pearls Benign cysts . n n 3. Chest & Abdomen n- Clavicles Check for fx crepitus/step-off from birth trauma. n- Umbilical Cord Should have 2 Arteries 1 Vein . Single artery -> Renal/Cardiac anomalies . n - Granuloma Treat with Silver Nitrate. n- Hernias Umbilical Observe until age 4-5 . n n 4. Genitourinary GU n- Hydrocele Fluid in scrotum. Transilluminates . n- Cryptorchidism Undescended testes. n- Hypospadias Urethra on ventral surface. Contraindication to Circumcision Foreskin needed for repair . n n 5. Musculoskeletal Hips n- Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH Risk factors Breech Female Family Hx. n- Barlow B ack and In Adduct & push Posterior -> Dislocates . n- Ortolani O ut and Anterior Abduct & pull Anterior -> Reduces Clunk . n n 6. Skin n- Milia Tiny white cysts on nose Benign . n- Erythema Toxicum blotchy red rash with central papule/pustule. Flea-bitten appearance. Benign. Eosinophils. n- Mongolian Spots Congenital Dermal Melanocytosis Blue-grey macules on back/buttocks. Benign. n- Hemangioma Vascular tumor. Grows then regresses. Tx Propranolol only if visual axis or airway compromised. n n Development & Screening n- Screening n - Metabolic Newborn screen PKU Hypothyroid etc . n - Autism M-CHAT at 18 and 24 months . n - Lead 12 and 24 months. n- Milestones Rule of Thumb n - 2 mo Lift head. n - 4 mo Roll over. n - 6 mo Sit unassisted. n - 9 mo Crawl Pincer grasp. n - 12 mo Walk First words. n questions 1. A newborn has a scalp swelling that does NOT cross suture lines. What is the most likely diagnosis? Caput Succedaneum Cephalohematoma Subgaleal Hemorrhage Hydrocephalus Meningocele 1 Cephalohematoma is a subperiosteal hemorrhage that is limited by the suture lines of the skull bones. Caput crosses sutures edema . 2. During a newborn exam you note a clunk when abducting the infant s hip while lifting the trochanter anteriorly. Which maneuver is this? Barlow Ortolani Galeazzi Moro Babinski 1 Ortolani maneuver reduces a dislocated hip. You abduct O ut and lift feeling a clunk as the head returns to the acetabulum. Barlow dislocates an unstable hip. 3. A 4-day-old infant presents with copious purulent eye discharge and eyelid swelling. Gram stain shows gram-negative diplococci. What is the treatment? Oral Erythromycin Topical Gentamicin IM Ceftriaxone Oral Acyclovir Observation 2 This is Gonococcal Conjunctivitis Onset 2-5 days Purulent . It requires systemic treatment with IM Ceftriaxone to prevent corneal perforation and blindness. 4. By what age should an infant expect to double their birth weight? 2 weeks 2 months 4 months 6 months 1 year 2 Infants typically double their birth weight by 4 months and triple it by 1 year . 5. You notice a scrotal mass in a male newborn. It is painless and transilluminates brightly with a flashlight. What is the diagnosis? Inguinal Hernia Hydrocele Testicular Torsion Varicocele Cryptorchidism 1 Hydrocele is a fluid collection in the tunica vaginalis. The hallmark finding is Transillumination . Hernias usually do not transilluminate unless fluid-filled but usually bowel . What is the difference between Caput Succedaneum and Cephalohematoma regarding suture lines? Caput Crosses sutures. Cephalohematoma does NOT . Clinical Signs When does birth weight double and triple? Double 4 months. nTriple 1 year. Growth Maneuver to REDUCE a dislocated hip? Ortolani Abduct/Out + Lift . Physical Exam Maneuver to DISLOCATE an unstable hip? Barlow Adduct/Back + Push . Physical Exam Timing of Gonococcal vs Chlamydial Conjunctivitis? Gonococcal 2-5 Days. nChlamydial 5-14 Days. Infectious Disease How many vessels in a normal umbilical cord? 3 Vessels 2 Arteries 1 Vein . Anatomy Diagnosis of scrotal mass that transilluminates? Hydrocele. Diagnosis Rash with flea-bitten appearance red blotch with central pustule in newborn? Erythema Toxicum Neonatorum Benign . Dermatology White reflex Leukocoria suggests? Retinoblastoma or Congenital Cataracts. Diagnosis When does the Anterior Fontanelle close? 12-18 Months. Growth Physeo Pediatrics > Newborn FirstAid Public Health > Pediatrics Caput vs Cephalo Caput wears a Cap Caps cover the whole head crossing sutures . n Cephalo -hematoma is clearly defined by the bone Does not cross . Barlow vs Ortolani B arlow goes B ack Posterior to dislocate. n O rtolani goes O ut Abduct to reduce. Conjunctivitis Timing C hemical 24h n G onococcal 2-5d n C hlamydial 5-14d n H erpes 2-4w Pediatric H&P I n n Newborn n- APGAR 1 & 5 min. Appearance Pulse Grimace Activity Resp. n- Growth Double 4mo Triple 1yr. n- Head Caput Crosses vs Cephalo No . n- Eyes Leukocoria Retinoblastoma . Gono 2-5d vs Chlam 5-14d . n n Exam n- Hips Barlow Dislocate Ortolani Reduce . n- GU Hydrocele Transilluminate . Hypospadias No circ . n- Reflexes Moro Rooting Gone by 4-6mo . n n Skin n- Benign Milia E-Tox Mongolian Spots. n- Hemangioma Tx Prop if visual axis."}, {"id": "l8", "title": "Lecture 8: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l8 Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 8 n n 1. Major Tracts of the Brainstem Slide 3-17 n nThe brainstem serves as a major conduit for ascending and descending pathways connecting the spinal cord cerebellum and cerebrum. Understanding the relative positions of these tracts at different levels Midbrain Pons Medulla is crucial for localizing lesions. n n Descending Tracts n Corticospinal Tract n Function Voluntary motor control. n Pathway Descends from the cerebral cortex. n Trajectory It is always the most ventral structure throughout the brainstem. n Decussation Most fibers decussate at the caudal medulla pyramidal decussation to form the lateral corticospinal tract in the spinal cord. n n Ascending Tracts n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML n Function Fine touch vibration and proprioception for the body. n Pathway n Spinal Cord Ascends centrally as the Dorsal Columns Gracile and Cuneate fasciculi . n Medulla Synapses in the Dorsal Column Nuclei Gracile and Cuneate nuclei . n Decussation Axons form Internal Arcuate Fibers which decussate in the caudal medulla Slide 16 . n Brainstem Ascent Ascends as the Medial Lemniscus . n Trajectory It is always medial to the spinothalamic tract. Its orientation changes from vertical medulla to horizontal midbrain as it ascends. n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System n Function Pain and temperature sensation. n Pathway Decussates immediately in the spinal cord. n Trajectory Ascends through the brainstem in a lateral position. It remains lateral to the DCML at all levels Slide 17 . n n 2. Cerebellar Peduncles Slide 18-20 n nThe cerebellum communicates with the brainstem via three paired peduncles located at specific levels n n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle ICP n Level Medulla and lower Pons. n Function Primarily afferent input to the cerebellum from the spinal cord e.g. dorsal spinocerebellar tract and medulla. n Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP n Level Pons. n Function Entirely afferent input from the pontine nuclei corticopontine pathway . It is the largest peduncle. n Superior Cerebellar Peduncle SCP n Level Upper Pons and lower Midbrain. n Function Primarily efferent output from the deep cerebellar nuclei to the red nucleus and thalamus. n n 3. Cranial Nerve Nuclei Organization Slide 22-26 n nThe organization of cranial nerve nuclei follows a rational developmental logic based on the neural tube plates alar = sensory basal = motor . n n Functional Columns nNuclei are arranged in longitudinal columns. A general rule for the brainstem is Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . n n Motor Columns Medial to Lateral n 1. Somatic Motor GSE Most medial near the midline. Innervates myotome-derived muscles tongue eye . n Nuclei Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Abducens VI Hypoglossal XII . n 2. Branchial Motor SVE Ventrolateral. Innervates pharyngeal arch muscles. n Nuclei Trigeminal Motor V Facial Motor VII Nucleus Ambiguus IX X Spinal Accessory XI . n 3. Visceral Motor GVE Parasympathetic. n Nuclei Edinger-Westphal III Superior Salivatory VII Inferior Salivatory IX Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus X . n n Sensory Columns Medial to Lateral n 1. Visceral Sensory GVA/SVA n Nuclei Nucleus Solitarius VII IX X - taste and cardiorespiratory . n 2. Somatic Sensory GSA General sensation from face. n Nuclei Trigeminal Nuclei Mesencephalic Pontine Spinal . n 3. Special Sensory SSA Most lateral. n Nuclei Vestibular and Cochlear Nuclei VIII . n n mnemonic n Cranial Nerves Sensory/Motor/Both n Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More n n S ome I - Sensory n S ay II - Sensory n M arry III - Motor n M oney IV - Motor n B ut V - Both n M y VI - Motor n B rother VII - Both n S ays VIII - Sensory n B ig IX - Both n B rains X - Both n M atter XI - Motor n M ore XII - Motor n Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei Neuroscience questions A patient presents with loss of vibration and proprioception on the left side of the body. A lesion is located in the medulla. Which structure is most likely affected? Left Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneate Right Medial Lemniscus Left Medial Lemniscus Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Lateral Corticospinal Tract 1 The DCML decussates in the caudal medulla as internal arcuate fibers. After decussation the fibers ascend as the Medial Lemniscus on the contralateral side. Therefore a lesion in the medulla affecting the Right Medial Lemniscus would cause loss of vibration/proprioception on the Left side. Which cranial nerve nucleus column is located most laterally in the brainstem? Somatic Motor Branchial Motor Visceral Motor Somatic Sensory Special Sensory 4 Following the Motor Medial Sensory Lateral rule the Special Sensory column hearing and balance CN VIII is the most lateral column. The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle conveys fibers primarily from which location? Spinal Cord Medulla Pons Midbrain Deep Cerebellar Nuclei 2 The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP connects the Pons to the Cerebellum and conveys afferent fibers from pontine nuclei. Where does the Corticospinal tract decussate? Midbrain Upper Pons Lower Pons Upper Medulla Caudal Medulla 4 The Corticospinal tract descends ventrally and decussates at the Pyramids in the Caudal lower Medulla. Which nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil constriction ? Oculomotor Nucleus Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Trochlear Nucleus Abducens Nucleus Superior Salivatory Nucleus 1 The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the Visceral Motor parasympathetic component of CN III Oculomotor responsible for pupil constriction. Decussation level of Corticospinal Tract Caudal Medulla pyramids Neuroanatomy Decussation level of DCML Caudal Medulla internal arcuate fibers Neuroanatomy Decussation level of Spinothalamic Tract Spinal Cord anterior white commissure Neuroanatomy Relative position of DCML vs Spinothalamic in brainstem DCML is always MEDIAL to Spinothalamic Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Medulla and Lower Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Middle Cerebellar Peduncle Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Superior Cerebellar Peduncle Upper Pons and Lower Midbrain Neuroanatomy General rule for CN Nuclei location Medial-Lateral Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral Neuroanatomy Nucleus responsible for taste CN VII IX X Nucleus Solitarius Gustatory nucleus Neuroanatomy Nucleus supplying pharyngeal muscles swallowing Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Brainstem Pathoma CNS Tract Trajectories Rule The Corticospinal tract is always the most ventral structure. The DCML is always medial to the Spinothalamic tract. The Spinothalamic tract is always lateral . Medial vs. Lateral Rule In the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei organization Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . Decussation Levels Corticospinal Caudal Medulla Pyramids . n DCML Caudal Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic Spinal Cord immediately on entry . Peduncle Levels Inferior Medulla/Lower Pons Afferent . n Middle Pons Afferent . n Superior Upper Pons/Midbrain Efferent . Nucleus Ambiguus The Nucleus Ambiguus supplies branchial motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx swallowing gag reflex via CN IX and X."}, {"id": "l9", "title": "Lecture 9: Reticular Formation", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l9 Lecture 9 Reticular Formation n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 9 n n 1. Reticular Formation Overview Slide 6-7 n nThe Reticular Formation RF is a phylogenetically old set of nuclei located throughout the brainstem core extending from the caudal medulla to the lateral hypothalamus. It is not a single anatomical structure but a diffuse network responsible for life-sustaining functions. n n Structure Composed of short interneurons reflexes and long projection neurons neuromodulation . n Key Concept A single neuron can synapse with 25 000+ others allowing diffuse modulation of the entire CNS. n n 2. Neurochemistry of the Reticular Formation Slide 8-13 n nThe RF houses the nuclei for major neuromodulatory systems. You MUST know the location and neurotransmitter for each n n Serotonin 5-HT n Nuclei Raphe Nuclei midline of entire brainstem . n Functions Mood Arousal Pain modulation Raphe Magnus Sleep. n Norepinephrine NE n Nuclei Locus Coeruleus pons/midbrain junction and Lateral Tegmental Area. n Functions Arousal RAS Attention Fight or Flight Pain modulation. n Dopamine DA n Nuclei Ventral Tegmental Area VTA and Substantia Nigra Midbrain . n Pathways n Nigrostriatal Coordination of movement degenerate in Parkinson s . n Mesolimbic Reward/Addiction Schizophrenia positive symptoms . n Mesocortical Cognition Schizophrenia negative symptoms . n Acetylcholine ACh n Nuclei Pedunculopontine Tegmental Nucleus PPT Brainstem and Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Forebrain . n Functions Arousal REM sleep generation Memory degenerate in Alzheimer s . n Histamine n Nuclei Tuberomammillary Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus . n Function Arousal/Wakefulness Antihistamines cause drowsiness . n n 3. Reticular Activating System ARAS & Consciousness Slide 16-24 n nThe Ascending Reticular Activating System ARAS projects to the cortex to maintain Wakefulness and Alertness . n n Disorders of Consciousness n Coma Low arousal no awareness. Dysfunction of ARAS or bilateral cortex. n Persistent Vegetative State Unresponsive Wakefulness . Arousal is present eyes open sleep-wake cycles but NO awareness of environment. n Minimally Conscious State Intermittent inconsistent awareness. n n correlate n Locked-in Syndrome n Pathophysiology Ischemic stroke of the Ventral Pons Basilar Artery . n Presentation Quadriplegia corticospinal disruption and anarthria corticobulbar disruption . n Key Feature The ARAS is spared tegmentum so the patient is Fully Awake and Aware but cannot move. n Communication Vertical eye movements controlled by midbrain and blinking are usually preserved. n n n 4. Sleep Physiology Slide 25-33 n n Stages n N1-N2 Light sleep Sleep Spindles K-Complexes . n N3 Slow Wave Sleep Delta waves - Deep restorative sleep. n REM Rapid Eye Movement dreaming atonia. EEG looks like Awake Beta/Sawtooth . n Neurochemistry n Wakefulness Promoters NE 5-HT Histamine ACh Hypocretin Orexin . n Sleep Promoters GABA VLPO nucleus and Melatonin. n n correlate n Narcolepsy n Pathophysiology Loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. n Presentation n Excessive Daytime Sleepiness. n Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion. n Sleep Paralysis and Hypnagogic Hallucinations . n Treatment Stimulants Amphetamines to increase NE/DA. n n n 5. Descending Motor & Pain Control Slide 37-39 n n Motor Rigidity Posturing n Decorticate Posturing Flexion of upper limbs. Lesion Above Red Nucleus midbrain . Rubrospinal tract intact. n Decerebrate Posturing Extension of all limbs. Lesion Below Red Nucleus . Vestibulospinal tracts dominate. Worse prognosis. n n Descending Pain Modulation nThe brainstem can inhibit incoming pain signals Pain Inhibits Pain . n Pathway Periaqueductal Gray PAG → Rostral Ventromedial Medulla RVM Raphe Magnus → Dorsal Horn block pain transmission via Enkephalins/Serotonin/NE . n Clinical Antidepressants like SnRIs Duloxetine or TCAs Amitriptyline treat chronic pain by boosting NE/5-HT in this inhibitory pathway. Lecture 9 Reticular Formation Neuroscience questions A patient presents with locked-in syndrome following a stroke. They are fully aware but cannot move their limbs or speak. Which structure is most likely spared preserving their consciousness? Basis Pontis Corticospinal Tracts Reticular Activating System Tegmentum Medullary Pyramids Internal Capsule 2 Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion to the ventral pons basis pontis destroying corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Consciousness is preserved because the Reticular Activating System ARAS located in the dorsal tegmentum is spared. Which neurotransmitter is synthesized primarily in the Locus Coeruleus? Serotonin Dopamine Acetylcholine Norepinephrine Histamine 3 The Locus Coeruleus is the primary site of Norepinephrine NE synthesis in the brainstem involved in arousal and attention. A comatose patient exhibits abnormal posturing with extension of both upper and lower extremities to painful stimuli. This Decerebrate rigidity suggests a lesion at which level? Cortex Above the Red Nucleus At or Below the Red Nucleus Spinal Cord Thalamus 2 Decerebrate rigidity extension of all limbs implies a lesion at or below the Red Nucleus midbrain/upper pons removing rubrospinal influence and leaving vestibulospinal extension unchecked. A 25-year-old male complains of excessive daytime sleepiness and episodes of sudden muscle weakness when laughing cataplexy . A deficiency in which neuropeptide is likely? Serotonin GABA Hypocretin Orexin Dopamine Melatonin 2 Narcolepsy with cataplexy is classically caused by a loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Which drug class treats chronic neuropathic pain by enhancing the descending pain inhibitory pathway from the brainstem? SSRIs Fluoxetine Benzodiazepines SNRIs Duloxetine Dopamine Agonists Antihistamines 2 SNRIs like Duloxetine and TCAs are effective for neuropathic pain because they inhibit reuptake of Norepinephrine and Serotonin enhancing the descending inhibitory signals from the RVM/PAG to the spinal cord. Location of Serotonin 5-HT cell bodies Raphe Nuclei Neurochemistry Location of Norepinephrine NE cell bodies Locus Coeruleus Neurochemistry Location of Dopamine DA cell bodies Midbrain Substantia Nigra & Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Neurochemistry Location of Histamine cell bodies Tuberomammillary Nucleus Hypothalamus Neurochemistry Lesion location for Decorticate Rigidity Above the Red Nucleus Midbrain Neurology Lesion location for Decerebrate Rigidity At or Below the Red Nucleus Neurology Which is worse Decorticate or Decerebrate? Decerebrate indicates deeper brainstem damage Neurology Neurotransmitter deficiency in Narcolepsy Hypocretin Orexin Neurology Area spared in Locked-in Syndrome Reticular Activating System Tegmentum & Vertical Eye Movements Neurology Structure responsible for Descending Pain Modulation Periaqueductal Gray PAG Neurology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Coma & Sleep Pathoma CNS Decorticate vs. Decerebrate Decorticate Flexor posturing Hands to C ore . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. n Decerebrate Extensor posturing worse . Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Locating Neurotransmitters Raphe Nuclei = Serotonin 5-HT . n Locus Coeruleus = Norepinephrine NE . n Substantia Nigra = Dopamine DA . n Tuberomammillary Nucleus = Histamine. Locked-in Syndrome Caused by a Ventral Pontine stroke Basilar artery . The ARAS is spared Tegmentum so the patient is fully awake/aware but quadriplegic. Can only blink or move eyes vertically. Narcolepsy Mechanism Caused by a deficiency of Hypocretin Orexin producing neurons in the Lateral Hypothalamus . Pain Modulation Pathway PAG Midbrain → RVM Medulla → Spinal Cord Dorsal Horn. Mediated by Opioids Serotonin and NE."}];